(## Abstractions/Spectrum of Consistency Models/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                                                     strict consistency) Tj
T*
(                                    sequential) Tj
T*
(                      causal       consistency           |) Tj
T*
(eventual             consistency        |                |) Tj
T*
(consistency             |               |                |) Tj
T*
(   |                    |               |                |) Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
(weakest                                              strongest) Tj
T*
(consistency                                          consistency) Tj
T*
(model                                                model) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Eventual consistency) Tj
T*
(Eventual consistency is the weakest consistency model. The applications that don’t have strict) Tj
T*
(ordering requirements and don’t require reads to return the latest write choose this model.) Tj
T*
(Eventual consistency ensures that all the replicas will eventually return the same value to the) Tj
T*
(read request, but the returned value isn’t meant to be the latest value. However, the value) Tj
T*
(will finally reach its latest state.) Tj
T*
(Example) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The domain name system is a highly available system that enables name lookups to a hundred) Tj
T*
(million devices across the Internet. It uses an eventual consistency model and doesn’t) Tj
T*
(necessarily reflect the latest values.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Cassandra is a highly available NoSQL database that provides eventual consistency.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Causal consistency) Tj
T*
(This model doesn’t ensure ordering for the operations that are not causally related. These) Tj
T*
(operations can be seen in different possible orders.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Causal consistency is weaker overall, but stronger than the eventual consistency model. It’s) Tj
T*
(used to prevent non-intuitive behaviors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The causal consistency model is used in a commenting system. For example, for the replies to a) Tj
T*
(comment on a Facebook post, we want to display comments after the comment it replies to. This) Tj
T*
(is because there is a cause-and-effect relationship between a comment and its replies.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Sequential consistency) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Sequential consistency is stronger than the causal consistency model. It preserves the ordering) Tj
T*
(specified by each client’s program. However, sequential consistency doesn’t ensure that the) Tj
T*
(writes are visible instantaneously or in the same order as they occurred according to some) Tj
T*
(global clock.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In social networking applications, we usually don’t care about the order in which some of our) Tj
T*
(friends’ posts appear. However, we still anticipate a single friend’s posts to appear in the) Tj
T*
(correct order in which they were created\). Similarly, we expect our friends’ comments in a post) Tj
T*
ET
