(## Strict consistency) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A  strict consistency or linearizability is the strongest consistency model. This model ensures) Tj
T*
(that a read request from any replicas will get the latest write value. Once the client receives) Tj
T*
(the acknowledgment that the write operation has been performed, other clients can read that) Tj
T*
(value.) Tj
T*
(high consistency=>low availability requests延遲處理) Tj
T*
(Example) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Updating an account’s password requires strict consistency. For example, if we suspect) Tj
T*
(suspicious activity on our bank account, we immediately change our password so that no) Tj
T*
(unauthorized users can access our account. If it were possible to access our account using an) Tj
T*
(old password due to a lack of strict consistency, then changing passwords would be a useless) Tj
T*
(security strategy.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Amazon Aurora provides strong consistency.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Abstractions/The Spectrum of Failure Models/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(                                                                  Byzantine) Tj
T*
(                                                     Temporal           |) Tj
T*
(                                   Omission              |              |) Tj
T*
(                     Crash              |                |              |) Tj
T*
(Fail-stop               |               |                |              |) Tj
T*
(   |                    |               |                |              |) Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
(weakest                                                          strongest) Tj
T*
(consistency                                                     consistency) Tj
T*
(model                                                              model) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
