T*
(## Fail-stop) Tj
T*
(In this type of failure, a node in the distributed system halts permanently. However, the other) Tj
T*
(nodes can still detect that node by communicating with it.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(From the perspective of someone who builds distributed systems, fail-stop failures are the) Tj
T*
(simplest and the most convenient.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Crash) Tj
T*
(In this type of failure, a node in the distributed system halts silently, and the other nodes) Tj
T*
(can’t detect that the node has stopped working.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Omission) Tj
T*
(In omission failures, the node fails to send or receive messages. There are two types of) Tj
T*
(omission failures. If the node fails to respond to the incoming request, it’s said to be a send) Tj
T*
(omission failure. If the node fails to receive the request and thus can’t acknowledge it, it’s) Tj
T*
(said to be a receive omission failure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Temporal) Tj
T*
(In temporal failures, the node generates correct results, but is too late to be useful. This) Tj
T*
(failure could be due to bad algorithms, a bad design strategy, or a loss of synchronization) Tj
T*
(between the processor clocks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Byzantine) Tj
T*
ET
