(# Examples of Resource Estimation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Introduction) Tj
T*
(Now that we’ve set the foundation for resource estimation, let’s make use of our background) Tj
T*
(knowledge to estimate resources like servers, storage, and bandwidth. Below, we consider a) Tj
T*
(scenario and a service, make assumptions, and based on those assumptions, we make estimations.) Tj
T*
(Let’s jump right in!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Number of servers required) Tj
T*
(Let’s make the following assumptions about a Twitter-like service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There are 500 million \(M\) daily active users \(DAU\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A single user makes 20 requests per day on average.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Recall that a single server can handle 8,000 RPS.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Total requests per second 500M x 20/86400=115K requests per second which is the RPS for our) Tj
T*
(system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For the number of servers, \(Number of Request/sec\) / RPS of server=115k/8k = 15  servers to) Tj
T*
(handle all requests.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Estimation of Number of Servers]\(./estimating_the_number_of_servers.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Indeed, the number above doesn’t seem right. If we only need 15 commodity servers to serve 500M) Tj
T*
(daily users, then why do big services use millions of servers in a data center? The primary) Tj
T*
(reason for this is that the RPS is not enough to estimate the number of servers required to) Tj
T*
(provide a service. Also, we made some underlying assumptions in the calculations above. One of) Tj
T*
(the assumptions was that a request is handled by one server only. In reality, requests go) Tj
T*
(through to web servers that may interact with application servers that may also request data) Tj
T*
(from storage servers. Each server may take a different amount of time to handle each request.) Tj
T*
(Furthermore, each request may be handled differently depending upon the state of the data) Tj
T*
(center, the application, and the request itself. Remember that we have a variety of servers for) Tj
T*
(providing various services within a data center.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Therefore, we approximate the number of servers by depicting how many clients a server handles) Tj
T*
(on a given day:) Tj
T*
(In this case, it’s equal to 500M / 8000 = 62,500) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: This may not be an accurate estimation, but it’s a realistic one. Therefore, we use this) Tj
T*
(approach in estimating the number of servers in our design problems. Informally, the equation) Tj
T*
(given above assumes that one server can handle 8,000 users. We use this reference in the rest) Tj
T*
(of the course as well.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
