T*
(# Put Back-of-the-envelope Numbers in Perspective) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Why do we use back-of-the-envelope calculations?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A distributed system has compute nodes connected via a network. There’s a wide variety of) Tj
T*
(available compute nodes and they can be connected in many different ways. Back-of-the-envelope) Tj
T*
(calculations help us ignore the nitty-gritty details of the system \(at least at the design) Tj
T*
(level\) and focus on more important aspects.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Some examples of a back-of-the-envelope calculation could be:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The number of concurrent TCP connections a server can support.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The number of requests per second \(RPS\) a web, database, or cache server can handle.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The storage requirements of a service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Choosing an unreasonable number for such calculations can lead to a flawed design. Since we) Tj
T*
(need good estimations in many design problems, we’ll discuss all the relevant concepts in) Tj
T*
(detail in this lesson. These concepts include:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The types of data center servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The realistic access latencies of different components.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The estimation of RPS that a server can handle.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Examples of bandwidth, servers, and storage estimation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Types of data center servers) Tj
T*
(Data centers don’t have a single type of server. Enterprise solutions use commodity hardware to) Tj
T*
ET
