(save cost and develop scalable solutions. Below, we discuss the types of servers that are) Tj
T*
(commonly used within a data center to handle different workloads.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Web servers) Tj
T*
(For scalability, the web servers are decoupled from the application servers. Web servers are) Tj
T*
(the first point of contact after load balancers. Data centers have racks full of web servers) Tj
T*
(that usually handle API calls from the clients. Depending on the service that’s offered, the) Tj
T*
(memory and storage resources in web servers can be small to medium. However, such servers) Tj
T*
(require good computational resources. For example, Facebook has used a web server with 32 GB of) Tj
T*
(RAM and 500 GB of storage space. But for its high-end computational needs, it partnered with) Tj
T*
(Intel to build a custom 16-core processor.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Many numbers quoted in this lesson are obtained from the data center design that Facebook) Tj
T*
(open-sourced in 2011. Due to the slowing of Moore’s law-induced performance circa 2004, the) Tj
T*
(numbers are not stale.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Application servers) Tj
T*
(Application servers run the core application software and business logic. The difference) Tj
T*
(between web servers and application servers is somewhat fuzzy. Application servers primarily) Tj
T*
(provide dynamic content, whereas web servers mostly serve static content to the client, which) Tj
T*
(is mostly a web browser. They can require extensive computational and storage resources.) Tj
T*
(Storage resources can be volatile and non-volatile. Facebook has used application servers with) Tj
T*
(a RAM of up to 256 GB and two types of storage—traditional rotating disks and flash—with a) Tj
T*
(capacity of up to 6.5 TB.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Storage servers) Tj
T*
(With the explosive growth of Internet users, the amount of data stored by giant services has) Tj
T*
(multiplied. Additionally, various types of data are now being stored in different storage) Tj
T*
(units. For instance, YouTube uses the following datastores:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Blob storage for its encoded videos.) Tj
T*
() Tj
