(## Requests estimation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This section discusses the number of requests a typical server can handle in a second. Within a) Tj
T*
(server, there are limited resources and depending on the type of client requests, different) Tj
T*
(resources can become a bottleneck. Let’s understand two types of requests.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(CPU-bound requests: These are the type of requests where the limiting factor is the CPU.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Memory-bound requests: These are the types of requests that are limited by the amount of memory) Tj
T*
(a machine has.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Let’s approximate the RPS for each type of request. But before that, we need to assume the) Tj
T*
(following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Our server has the specifications of the typical server that we defined in the table above.) Tj
T*
(Operating systems and other auxiliary processes have consumed a total of 16 GB of RAM.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each worker consumes 300 MBs of RAM storage to complete a request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For simplicity, we assume that the CPU obtains data from the RAM. Therefore, a caching system) Tj
T*
(ensures that all the required content is available for serving, without there being a need to) Tj
T*
(access the storage layer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each CPU-bound request takes 200 milliseconds, whereas a memory-bound request takes 50) Tj
T*
(milliseconds to complete.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s do the computation for each type of request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**CPU bound**: A simple formula used to calculate the RPS for CPU-bound requests is:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(RPS cpu = NUM cpu / TASK time) Tj
T*
(where) Tj
T*
(RPS cpu: CPU bound RPS) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(NUM cpu: Number of CPU threads, which are also called hardware threads) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
