<< /Length 3418 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
(TASK time: The time each task takes to complete) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Then, RPS cpu = 72/200ms=360 RPS) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The rationale for the calculation shown above is that we can visualize one second as a box and) Tj
T*
(we calculate how many mini-boxes \(tasks\) can fit inside the big box—that is, the number of) Tj
T*
(tasks that can be completed in one second by a number of CPUs. So, a higher number of) Tj
T*
(CPUs/threads will result in a higher RPS.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Memory-bound requests**: For memory-bound requests, we use the following formula:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(RPS memory= RAM size / WORKDER memory x 1 / TASK time) Tj
T*
(where) Tj
T*
(RPS memory: Memory bound RPS) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(RAM size: Total size of RAM) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(WORKER memory: A worker in memory that manages a request) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Then, RPS memory=240GB/300MB x 1/50 ms= 16,000 RPS) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Continuing our box analogy from the explanation of CPU-bound processes, here we first calculate) Tj
T*
(the number of boxes there are \(how many memory-bound processes a server can host\) and then how) Tj
T*
(many mini-boxes \(tasks\) we can fit in each of the bigger boxes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A service receives both the CPU-bound and memory-bound requests. Considering the case that half) Tj
T*
(the requests are CPU-bound and the other half memory-bound, we can handle a total of) Tj
T*
(360/2+16,000/2=8180, around 8000 RPS.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The calculations above are only an approximation for developing an understanding of the basic) Tj
T*
(factors involved in estimating RPS. In reality, a lot of other factors come into play. For) Tj
T*
(instance, latency is required to do a disk seek in case the data is not readily available in) Tj
T*
(RAM or if a request is made to the database server, which will also include the database and) Tj
T*
(network latency. Additionally, the type of query also matters. Of course, faults, bugs in code,) Tj
T*
(node failures, power outages, network disruptions, and more are inevitable factors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(On a typical day, various types of requests arrive, and a powerful server that only serves) Tj
T*
(static content from the RAM might handle as many as 500k RPS. On the other end of the spectrum,) Tj
T*
(computational-intensive tasks like image processing may only allow a maximum of 50 RPS.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: In reality, capacity estimation is a hard problem, and organizations learn how to improve) Tj
T*
(it over the years. A monitoring system keeps an eye on all parts of our infrastructure to give) Tj
T*
(us early warnings about overloading servers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Blob Store/Design Considerations of a Blob Store/README.md) Tj
T*
