(Besides storing the actual blob data, we have to maintain some metadata for managing the blob) Tj
T*
(storage. Let’s see what that data is.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Blob metadata) Tj
T*
(When a user uploads a blob, it’s split into small-sized chunks in order to be able to support) Tj
T*
(the storage of large files that can’t fit in one contiguous location, in one data node, or in) Tj
T*
(one block of a disk associated with that data node. The chunks for a single blob are then) Tj
T*
(stored on different data nodes that have enough storage space available to store these chunks.) Tj
T*
(There are billions of blobs that are kept in storage. The master node has to store all the) Tj
T*
(information about the blob’s chunks and where they are stored, so that it can retrieve the) Tj
T*
(chunks on reads. The master node assigns an ID to each chunk.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The information about a blob consists of chunk IDs and the name of the assigned data node for) Tj
T*
(each chunk. We split the blobs into equal-sized chunks. Chunks are replicated to enable them to) Tj
T*
(deal with data node failure. Hence, we also store the replica IDs for each chunk. We have) Tj
T*
(access to all this information pertaining to each blob.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s say we have a blob of 128 MB, and we split it into two chunks of 64 MB each. The metadata) Tj
T*
(for this blob is shown in the following table:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Chunk: A chunk is the minimum unit of data for writing and reading.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Blob Metadata) Tj
T*
(Chunk           Datanode ID          Replica 1 ID         Replica 2 ID            Replica 3 ID) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1                d1b1                 r1b1                  r2b1                     r3b1) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2                d1b2                 r1b2                  r2b2                     r3b2) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: To avoid complexity, the chunk size is fixed for all the blobs in a blob store. The chunk) Tj
T*
(size depends on the performance requirements of a blob store. We desire a larger chunk size to) Tj
T*
(maintain small metadata at the master node because a large chunk size results in a higher disk) Tj
T*
(latency, which leads to slower performance. Interestingly, disks can have almost the same) Tj
T*
(latency for reading and writing a range of data. For example, a disk can have similar latency) Tj
T*
(for writing MBs in the range of 4–8. Additionally, they can have similar latency for writing) Tj
T*
(data in the range of 9–20 MBs. This is due to the use of contiguous sectors on the disks, and) Tj
T*
(caching on the disk and on the server by its OS.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A chunk is the minimum unit of data for writing and reading.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(We maintain three replicas for each block. When writing a blob, the master node identifies the) Tj
T*
(data and the replica nodes using its free space management system. Besides handling data node) Tj
T*
(failure, the replica nodes are also used to serve read/write requests, so that the primary node) Tj
T*
(is not overloaded.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the example above, the blob size is a multiple of the chunk size, so the master node can) Tj
T*
(determine how many Bytes to read for each chunk.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What if the blob size isn’t a multiple of our configured chunk size? How does the master node) Tj
T*
(know how many Bytes to read for the last chunk?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(If the blob size isn’t a multiple of the chunk size, the last chunk won’t be full.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The master node also keeps the size of each blob to determine the number of Bytes to read for) Tj
T*
ET
