(## Partition data) Tj
T*
(We talked about the different levels of abstraction in a blob store—the account layer, the) Tj
T*
(container layer, and the blob layer. There are billions of blobs that are stored and read.) Tj
T*
(There is a large number of data nodes on which we store these blobs. If we look for the data) Tj
T*
(nodes that contain specific blobs out of all of the data nodes, it would be a very slow) Tj
T*
(process. Instead, we can group data nodes and call each group a partition. We maintain a) Tj
T*
(partition map table that contains a list of all the blobs in each partition. If we distribute) Tj
T*
(the blobs on different partitions independent of their container IDs and account IDs, we) Tj
T*
(encounter a problem, as shown in the following illustration:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Billions of blobs]\(./blobpath.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Range partitioning based on blob IDs]\(./range_partition.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Partitioning based on the blob IDs causes certain problems. For example, the blobs under a) Tj
T*
(specific container or account may reside in different partitions that add overhead while) Tj
T*
(reading or listing the blobs linked to a particular account or a particular container.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To overcome the problem described above, we can partition the blobs based on the complete path) Tj
T*
(of the blob. The partition key here is the combination of the account ID, container ID, and) Tj
T*
(blob ID. This helps in co-locating the blobs for a single user on the same partition server,) Tj
T*
(which enhances performance.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: The partition mappings are maintained by the master node, and these mappings are stored) Tj
T*
(in the distributed metadata storage.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Blob indexing) Tj
T*
(Finding specific blobs in a sea of blobs becomes more difficult and time-consuming with an) Tj
T*
(increase in the number of blobs that are uploaded to the storage. The blob index solves the) Tj
T*
(problem of blob management and querying.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To populate the blob index, we define key-value tag attributes on the blobs while uploading the) Tj
T*
(blobs. We use multiple tags, like container name, blob name, upload date and time, and some) Tj
T*
(other categories like the image or video blob, and so on.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As shown in the following illustration, a blob indexing engine reads the new tags, indexes) Tj
T*
(them, and exposes them to a searchable blob index:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Indexing and searching blobs]\(./indexing.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can categorize blobs as well as sort blobs using indexing. Let’s see how we utilize indexing) Tj
T*
(in pagination.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Pagination for listing) Tj
T*
(Listing is about returning a list of blobs to the user, depending on the user’s entered prefix.) Tj
T*
(A prefix is a character or string that returns the blobs whose name begins with that particular) Tj
T*
(character or string.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Pagination]\(./list_blobs.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Users may want to list all the blobs associated with a specific account, all the blobs present) Tj
T*
(inside a specific container, or they may want to list some public blobs based on a prefix. The) Tj
T*
(problem is that this list could be very long. We can’t return the whole list to the user in one) Tj
T*
(go. So, we have to return the list of the blobs in parts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s say a user wants a list of blobs associated with their account and there are a total of) Tj
T*
