(### Asynchronous replication across data centers and region) Tj
T*
(The blob store’s data centers are present in different regions—for example, Asia-Pacific,) Tj
T*
(Europe, eastern US, and more. In each region, we have more than one data center placed at) Tj
T*
(different locations, so that if one data center goes down, we have other data centers in the) Tj
T*
(same region to step in and serve the user requests. There are a minimum of three data centers) Tj
T*
(within each region, each separated by miles to protect against local events like fires, floods,) Tj
T*
(and so on.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The number of copies of a blob is called the replication factor. Most of the time, a) Tj
T*
(replication factor of three is sufficient.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([These are the regions and availability zones. Dark yellow is the primary data and light yellow) Tj
T*
(are the replicas]\(./replication.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We keep four copies of a blob. One is the local copy within the data center in the primary) Tj
T*
(region to protect against server rack and drive failures. The second copy of the blob is placed) Tj
T*
(in the other data center within the same region to protect against fire or flooding in the data) Tj
T*
(center. The third copy is placed in the data center of a different region to protect against) Tj
T*
(regional disasters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Garbage collection while deleting a blob) Tj
T*
(Since the blob chunks are placed at different data nodes, deleting from many different nodes) Tj
T*
(takes time, and holding a client until that’s done is not a viable option. Due to real-time) Tj
T*
(latency optimization, we don’t actually remove the blob from the blob store against a delete) Tj
T*
(request. Instead, we just mark a blob as “DELETED” in the metadata to make it inaccessible to) Tj
T*
(the user. The blob is removed later after responding to the user’s delete request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Marking the blob as deleted, but not actually deleting it at the moment, causes internal) Tj
T*
(metadata inconsistencies, meaning that things continue to take up storage space that should be) Tj
T*
(free. These metadata inconsistencies have no impact on the user. For example, for a blob marked) Tj
T*
(as deleted in the metadata, we still have the entries for that blob’s chunks in the metadata.) Tj
T*
(The data nodes are still holding on to that blob’s chunks. Therefore, we have a service called) Tj
T*
(a garbage collector that cleans up metadata inconsistencies later. The deletion of a blob) Tj
T*
(causes the chunks associated with that blob to be freed. However, there could be an appreciable) Tj
T*
(time delay between the time a blob is deleted by a user and the time of the corresponding) Tj
T*
(increase in free space in the blob store. We can bear this appreciable time delay because, in) Tj
T*
(return, we have a real-time fast response benefit for the user’s delete blob request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The whole deletion process is shown in the following illustration:) Tj
T*
([Garbage collection]\(./gc\)) Tj
T*
