(their mappings \(data nodes\) to the client.) Tj
T*
(4. The client then reads the chunk data from the data nodes.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: The metadata information for reading the blob is cached at the client machine, so that) Tj
T*
(the next time a client wants to read the same blob, we won’t have to burden the master node.) Tj
T*
(Additionally, the client’s read operation will be faster the next time.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Suppose the master node moves data from one data node to another because of an impending disk) Tj
T*
(failure. The user will now have stale information if they use the cached metadata to access the) Tj
T*
(data. How do we handle such situations?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(In such cases, the client calls fail. The client then flushes the cache and fetches new) Tj
T*
(metadata information from the master node.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Deleting a blob** Upon receiving a delete blob request, the master node marks that blob as) Tj
T*
(deleted in the metadata, and frees up the space later using a garbage collector. We learn more) Tj
T*
(about garbage collectors in the next lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Can the master node be considered a single point of failure? If yes, then how can we cope with) Tj
T*
(this problem?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Yes, because the master node is the central point of a blob store and is a single point of) Tj
T*
(failure. Therefore, we have to have a backup or shadow server in place of a master node.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The technique that we use for this is called checkpointing, meaning we take snapshots of the) Tj
T*
(data at different time intervals. A snapshot captures the state, data, hardware configuration) Tj
T*
(of the running master node, and messages in transit between master and data nodes. It maintains) Tj
T*
(the operation log in an external storage area or snapshot repository. If the master node fails,) Tj
T*
(an automated system or the administrator uses the snapshot to restart that master node from the) Tj
T*
(state it failed at and replays the operation log.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Operation log: A logical timeline, defining the serialized order of concurrent operations) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
