(## Resource estimation) Tj
T*
(Let’s estimate the total number of servers, storage, and bandwidth required by a blob storage) Tj
T*
(system. Because blobs can have all sorts of data, mentioning all of those types of data in our) Tj
T*
(estimation may not be practical. Therefore, we’ll use YouTube as an example, which stores) Tj
T*
(videos and thumbnails on the blob store. Furthermore, we’ll make the following assumptions to) Tj
T*
(complete our estimations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Assumptions:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The number of daily active users who upload or watch videos is five million.) Tj
T*
(- The number of requests per second that a single blob store server can handle is 500. This) Tj
T*
(number can be significantly higher, depending upon the blob store. For example, Microsoft Azure) Tj
T*
(can handle a maximum of 20,000 IOPS.) Tj
T*
(- The average size of a video is 50 MB.) Tj
T*
(- The average size of a thumbnail is 20 KB.) Tj
T*
(- The number of videos uploaded per day is 250,000.) Tj
T*
(- The number of read requests by a single user per day is 20.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Number of servers estimation) Tj
T*
(From our assumptions, we use the number of daily active users \(DAUs\) and queries a blob store) Tj
T*
(server can handle per second. The number of servers that we require is calculated using the) Tj
T*
(formula given below:) Tj
T*
(Number of active users/Queries handled per server = 10 K servers) Tj
T*
(### Storage estimation) Tj
T*
(Considering the assumptions written above, we use the formula given below to compute the total) Tj
T*
(storage required by YouTube in one day:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total_{storage/day} = No. of videos per day x \(Storage per video +Storage per thumbnail\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Putting the numbers from above into the formula gives us 12.51 TB /day, which is the) Tj
T*
(approximate storage required by YouTube per day for keeping a single copy of the uploaded video) Tj
T*
(in a single resolution.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(                      Total Storage Required to Store Videos and Thumbnails Uploaded Per Day on) Tj
T*
(YouTube) Tj
T*
( No. of videos per day          Storage per video \(MB\)        Storage per thumbnail \(KB\)) Tj
T*
(Total storage per day \(TB\)) Tj
T*
(  250000                                     50                            20) Tj
T*
(12.51) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(### Bandwidth estimation) Tj
T*
(Let’s estimate the bandwidth required for uploading data to and retrieving data from the blob) Tj
T*
(store.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Incoming traffic: To estimate the bandwidth required for incoming traffic, we consider the) Tj
T*
(total data uploaded per day, which indirectly means the total storage needed per day that we) Tj
T*
(calculated above. The amount of data transferred to the servers per second can be computed) Tj
T*
(using the following formula:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(       Total bandwidth = Total storage per day / \(24x60x60\)) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(                 Bandwidth Required for Uploading Videos on YouTube) Tj
T*
( Total storage per day \(TB\)             Seconds in a day        Bandwidth \(Gb/s\)) Tj
T*
( 12.51                        86400                  1.16) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Outgoing traffic: Since the blob store is a read-intensive store, most of the bandwidth is) Tj
T*
(required for outgoing traffic. Considering the aforementioned assumptions, we calculate the) Tj
T*
(bandwidth required for outgoing traffic using the following formula:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
