50 780 Td
(define its scope. Requirements include understanding what the service is, how it works, and) Tj
T*
(what its main features are. Our goal in this step is to gather the functional and non-) Tj
T*
(functional requirements of a service we are about to design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Estimation**: As the name suggests, this step estimates the resources required to provide the) Tj
T*
(service to a defined number of users. By resources, we mean the hardware or infrastructural) Tj
T*
(resources. Some sample estimation questions are the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. How many servers will we require to provide smooth services to 500 million daily active) Tj
T*
(users \(DAU\)?) Tj
T*
(2. How much storage do we need if we have to store 125 million tweets per day, and 20% of) Tj
T*
(tweets contain media?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Estimations are important because they help us understand the scale of the system we’ll design.) Tj
T*
(We’ll make key decisions based on the estimate. For example, we’ll decide what type of database) Tj
T*
(to use for storing our data, which data structure will give optimal performance, and so on.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Storage schema \(optional\)**: This step involves articulating our data model—that is, we) Tj
T*
(define which tables we need and what type of fields are part of each table. However, this is an) Tj
T*
(optional step, and we may not exercise this effort in every design problem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**High-level design**: This step involves identifying the main components and building blocks) Tj
T*
(we’ll use to design our desired system. We do this by getting inspiration from our functional) Tj
T*
(and non-functional requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This is considered the first step toward the complete design of our system and therefore) Tj
T*
(requires further iteration and improvement. Primarily, this section will focus on fulfilling) Tj
T*
(the functional requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**API design**: The goal in this phase is to build interfaces for our service. Using these) Tj
T*
(interfaces, users can call various services within our system. These interfaces are in the form) Tj
T*
(of API calls and are generally a translation of our functional requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Detailed design**: The detailed design starts by recognizing the limitations of the high-) Tj
T*
(level design. We’ll capitalize on these limitations to evolve our design. During this step,) Tj
T*
(we’ll finalize our design by mentioning all the components and building blocks that we’ll use.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We also define the workflow of our design and its usage of different technologies. The detailed) Tj
T*
(design aims to fulfill the functional as well as non-functional requirements of the problem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Evaluation**: This step will measure the effectiveness of our solution. In other words, we) Tj
T*
(justify how our design fulfills the functional and non-functional requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We discuss different trade-offs we made in our solution and also identify room for improvement.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Distinctive component/feature**: At the start of this lesson, we discussed how no one-size-) Tj
T*
(fits-all solution exists. This step is to identify a unique aspect for each design problem and) Tj
T*
(discuss it. For example, the Uber design problem has payment service and fraud detection as its) Tj
T*
(unique feature. In contrast, Google Docs has concurrency control, which is required when) Tj
T*
(different users want to edit the same section of a document simultaneously.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
