(# Evaluation of CDN's Design) Tj
T*
(## Evaluation) Tj
T*
(Here, we see how our design fulfills the requirements we discussed in the previous lessons. Our) Tj
T*
(main requirements are high performance, availability, scalability, reliability, and security.) Tj
T*
(Let’s discuss them all one by one.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Performance) Tj
T*
(CDN achieves high performance by minimizing latency. Some of the key design decisions that) Tj
T*
(minimize latency are as follows:) Tj
T*
(- Proxy servers usually serve content from the RAM.) Tj
T*
(- CDN proxy servers are placed near the users to provide faster access to content.) Tj
T*
(- A CDN can also be the provider of proxy servers located in the ISP or Internet exchange) Tj
T*
(points \(IXPs\) to handle high traffic.) Tj
T*
(- The request routing system ensures that users are directed to the nearest proxy servers.) Tj
T*
(We’ll have a detailed discussion on the request routing system in the next lesson.) Tj
T*
(- The proxy servers have long-tail content stored in nonvolatile storage systems like SSD or) Tj
T*
(HDD. Serving from these resources results in a more negligible latency than we’d see from) Tj
T*
(serving content from origin servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
