BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Long-tail) Tj
T*
(If we monitor the access frequency of our content over some time, we can see a trend where some) Tj
T*
(content is read by many clients \(for example, 20% of content asked by 80% of clients\), and then) Tj
T*
(there is a long list of the content that very few clients ask for. Such an access distribution) Tj
T*
(is called a long-tailed distribution.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
([Long-tail]\(./longtail.jpg\)) Tj
T*
(- As was discussed previously, proxy servers can be implemented in layers where if one layer) Tj
T*
(doesn’t have the content, the request can be entertained by the next layer of proxy servers.) Tj
T*
(For example, the edge proxy servers can request the parent proxy servers. Placing proxy servers) Tj
T*
(at specific ISPs could be the best option when most traffic comes from those ISP regions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Availability) Tj
T*
(A CDN can deal with massive traffic due to its distributed nature. A CDN ensures availability) Tj
T*
(through its cached content that serves as a backup whenever the origin servers fail. Moreover,) Tj
T*
(if one or more proxy servers in the CDN stop working, other operational proxy servers step in) Tj
T*
(and continue to drive the web traffic. In addition, edge proxy servers can be made available) Tj
T*
(through redundancy by replicating data to as many proxy servers as needed to avoid a single) Tj
T*
(point of failure and to meet the request load. Finally, we can use a load balancer to) Tj
T*
(distribute the users’ requests to nearby active proxy servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Scalability) Tj
T*
(The design of CDN facilitates scalability in the following ways:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- It brings content closer to the user and removes the requirement of high bandwidth, thereby) Tj
T*
(ensuring scalability.) Tj
T*
(- Horizontal scalability is possible by adding the number of reading replicas in the form of) Tj
T*
(edge proxy servers.) Tj
T*
(- The limitations with horizontal scalability and storage capacity of an individual proxy) Tj
T*
(server can be dealt with using the layered architecture of the proxy servers we described) Tj
T*
(above.) Tj
T*
(### Reliability and security) Tj
T*
(A CDN ensures no single failure point by carefully implementing maintenance cycles and) Tj
T*
(integrating additional hardware and software when required. Apart from failures, the CDN) Tj
T*
(handles massive traffic loads by equally distributing the load to the edge proxy servers. We) Tj
T*
(can use scrubber servers to prevent DDoS attacks and securely host content. Moreover, we can) Tj
T*
(use the heartbeat protocol to monitor the health of servers and omit faulty servers. Real-time) Tj
T*
(applications also build their own specified CDNs to prevent content leakage problems and) Tj
T*
(securely serve content to their end users.) Tj
T*
(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(Since its inception in the 1990s, the CDN has played a vital role in providing high) Tj
T*
(availability and low-latency content delivery. Nowadays, CDNs are considered a key player in) Tj
T*
(improving the overall performance of giant services.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Content Delivery Network \(CDN\)/In-depth Investigation of CDN Part 1/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# In-depth Investigation of CDN: Part 1) Tj
T*
(In this lesson, we’ll go into the details of certain concepts, such as CDN models and multi-) Tj
T*
(tier/layered CDN architecture, that we mentioned in the previous lessons. We’ll also introduce) Tj
T*
(some new concepts, including dynamic content caching optimization and various techniques to) Tj
T*
(discover the nearby proxy servers in CDNs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Content caching strategies in CDN) Tj
T*
(Identifying content to cache is important in delivering up-to-date and popular web content. To) Tj
T*
(ensure timely updates, two classifications of CDNs are used to get the content from the origin) Tj
T*
(servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Push CDN) Tj
T*
(Content gets sent automatically to the CDN proxy servers from the origin server in the push CDN) Tj
T*
ET
