/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(model. The content delivery to the CDN proxy servers is the content provider’s responsibility.) Tj
T*
(Push CDN is appropriate for static content delivery, where the origin server decides which) Tj
T*
(content to deliver to users using the CDN. The content is pushed to proxy servers in various) Tj
T*
(locations according to the content’s popularity. If the content is rapidly changing, the push) Tj
T*
(model might struggle to keep up and will do redundant content pushes.) Tj
T*
([Push content to PoPs]\(./push.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Pull CDN) Tj
T*
(A CDN pulls the unavailable data from origin servers when requested by a user. The proxy) Tj
T*
(servers keep the files for a specified amount of time and then remove them from the cache if) Tj
T*
(they’re no longer requested to balance capacity and cost.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(When users request web content in the pull CDN model, the CDN itself is responsible for pulling) Tj
T*
(the requested content from the origin server and serving it to the users. Therefore, this type) Tj
T*
(of CDN is more suited for serving dynamic content.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Content pull from origin server to the CDN PoPs]\(./pull.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As stated, the push CDN is mostly used for serving static content. Since static content is) Tj
T*
(served to a wide range of users for longer than dynamic content, the push CDN scheme maintains) Tj
T*
(more replicas than the pull CDN, thus improving availability. On the other hand, the pull CDN) Tj
T*
(is favored for frequently changing content and a high traffic load. Low storage consumption is) Tj
T*
(one of the main benefits of the pull CDN.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Most content providers use both pull and push CDN caching approaches to get the benefits) Tj
T*
(of both.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Dynamic content caching optimization) Tj
T*
(Since dynamic content often changes, it’s a good idea to cache it optimally. This section deals) Tj
T*
(with the optimization of frequently changing content.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Certain dynamic content creation requires the execution of scripts that can be executed at) Tj
T*
(proxy servers instead of running on the origin server. Dynamic data can be generated using) Tj
T*
(various parameters, which can be beneficial if executed at the proxy servers. For example, we) Tj
T*
(can generate dynamic content based on user location, time of day at a location, third-party) Tj
T*
(APIs specific to a location \(for instance, weather API\), and so on. So, it’s optimal to run the) Tj
T*
(scripts at proxy servers instead of the origin servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To reduce the communication between the origin server and proxy servers and storage) Tj
T*
(requirements at proxy servers, it’s useful to employ compression techniques as well. For) Tj
T*
(example, Cloudflare uses Railgun to compress dynamic content.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Another popular approach for dynamic data compression is Edge Side Includes \(ESI\) markup) Tj
T*
(language. Usually, a small portion of the web pages changes in a certain time. It means) Tj
T*
(fetching a full web page on each small change contains a lot of redundant data. To resolve this) Tj
T*
(performance penalty, ESI specifies where content was changed so that the rest of the web page) Tj
T*
(content can be cached. It assembles dynamic content at the CDN edge server or client browser.) Tj
T*
(ESI isn’t standardized yet by the World Wide Web Consortium \(W3C\), but many CDN providers use) Tj
T*
(it.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Dynamic Adaptive Streaming one HTTP \(DASH\) uses a manifest file with URIs of the video) Tj
T*
(with different resolutions so that the client can fetch whatever is appropriate as per) Tj
T*
(prevailing network and end node conditions. Netflix uses a proprietary DASH version with a) Tj
T*
(Byte-range in the URL for further content request and delivery optimization.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Multi-tier CDN architecture) Tj
T*
(The content provider sends the content to a large number of clients through a CDN. The task of) Tj
T*
