(distributing data to all the CDN proxy servers simultaneously is challenging and burdens the) Tj
T*
(origin server significantly. CDNs follow a tree-like structure to ease the data distribution) Tj
T*
(process for the origin server. The edge proxy servers have some peer servers that belong to the) Tj
T*
(same hierarchy. This set of servers receives data from the parent nodes in the tree, which) Tj
T*
(eventually receive data from the origin servers. The data is copied from the origin server to) Tj
T*
(the proxy servers by following different paths in the tree.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The tree structure for data distribution allows us to scale our system for increasing users by) Tj
T*
(adding more server nodes to the tree. It also reduces the burden on the origin server for data) Tj
T*
(distribution. A CDN typically has one or two tiers of proxy servers \(caches\). The following) Tj
T*
(illustration shows the two tiers of proxy servers:) Tj
T*
([Data distribution among CDN proxy servers]\(./distribution.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Whenever a new proxy server enters the tree of a CDN, it requests the control core, which) Tj
T*
(maintains information on all the proxy servers in the CDN and provides initial content with the) Tj
T*
(configuration data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The control core is a set of servers that manage the proxy servers within the PoP.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Research shows that many contents have long-tail distribution. This means that, at some point,) Tj
T*
(only a handful of content is very popular, and then we have a long tail of less popular) Tj
T*
(content. Here, a multi-layer cache might be used to handle long-tail content.) Tj
T*
([Many kinds of data exhibit the long-tailed phenomenon]\(./statistics.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What happens if a child or parent proxy server fails or if the origin server fails?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Each PoP contains a collection of CDN proxy servers. When any child proxy server stops working) Tj
T*
(due to any failures, DNS can route clients to a different child-level proxy server. Each child) Tj
T*
(proxy server knows many upper-layer parent servers, and if one fails, it can go to the other) Tj
T*
(one. The origin server is a set of servers with a hot backup\(s\), and content is in replicated) Tj
T*
(store. If any of the origin servers fail, other servers take the load.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Now that we’ve seen a way to distribute content from the origin server to all the proxy servers) Tj
T*
(of the CDN, we should also educate ourselves on how users can use these proxy servers to get) Tj
T*
(the data more efficiently. We’ll discuss how the nearest proxy server is chosen when clients) Tj
T*
(make requests and how the CDN is located in the upcoming sections of this lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Find the nearest proxy server to fetch the data) Tj
T*
(It’s vital for the user to fetch data from the nearest proxy server because the CDN aims to) Tj
T*
(reduce user-perceived latency by bringing the data close to the user. However, the question) Tj
T*
(remains of how users worldwide request data from the nearest proxy server. The goal of this) Tj
T*
(section is to answer that question.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Important factors that affect the proximity of the proxy server) Tj
T*
(There are two important factors that are relevant to finding the nearest proxy server to the) Tj
T*
(user:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Network distance between the user and the proxy server is crucial. This is a function of the) Tj
T*
(following two things:) Tj
T*
(    - The first is the length of the network path.) Tj
T*
(    - The second is the capacity \(bandwidth\) limits along the network path.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The shortest network path with the highest capacity \(bandwidth\) is the nearest proxy server to) Tj
T*
(the user in question. This path helps the user download content more quickly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
