(data to local CDN components only once, whereas local CDN components can provide data to) Tj
T*
(different users individually. No user will have to download their own copy of data from the) Tj
T*
(origin servers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Various streaming protocols are used to deliver dynamic content by the CDN providers. For) Tj
T*
(example, CDNsun uses the Real-time Messaging Protocol \(RTMP\), HTTP Live Streaming \(HLS\), Real-) Tj
T*
(time Streaming Protocol \(RTSP\), and many more to deliver dynamic content.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(- Scarcity of data center resources: A CDN is used to serve popular content. Due to this) Tj
T*
(reason, most of the traffic is handled at the CDN instead of the origin servers. So, different) Tj
T*
(local or distributed CDN components share the load on origin servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: A few well-known CDN providers are Akamai, StackPath, Cloudflare, Rackspace, Amazon) Tj
T*
(CloudFront, and Google Cloud CDN.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Does a CDN cache all content from the origin server?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Not likely. A CDN caches a considerable portion of the content depending on its capabilities,) Tj
T*
(and it mostly caches the static content.) Tj
T*
(static content: The content that isn’t frequently changed is known as static content.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(It also depends on the size of the content. For example, it might be possible for Netflix to) Tj
T*
(store more than 90% of its movies in the CDN, while this might not be feasible for a service) Tj
T*
(like YouTube due to the humongous volume of content.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Requirements) Tj
T*
(Let’s look at the functional and non-functional requirements that we expect from a CDN.) Tj
T*
(### Functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- Retrieve: Depending upon the type of CDN models--push or pull, a CDN should be able to) Tj
T*
(retrieve content from the origin servers. We’ll cover CDN models in the coming lesson.) Tj
T*
(- Request: Content delivery from the proxy server is made upon the user’s request. CDN proxy) Tj
T*
(servers should be able to respond to each user’s request in this regard.) Tj
T*
(- Deliver: In the case of the push model, the origin servers should be able to send the content) Tj
T*
(to the CDN proxy servers.) Tj
T*
(- Search: The CDN should be able to execute a search against a user query for cached or) Tj
T*
(otherwise stored content within the CDN infrastructure.) Tj
T*
(- Update: In most cases, content comes from the origin server, but if we run script in CDN, the) Tj
T*
(CDN should be able to update the content within peer CDN proxy servers in a PoP.) Tj
T*
(- Delete: Depending upon the type of content \(static or dynamic\), it should be possible to) Tj
T*
(delete cached entries from the CDN servers after a certain period.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Functional requirements of a CDN]\(./req.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A Point of Presence \(PoP\) is a physical place that allows two or more networks or devices to) Tj
T*
(communicate with each other. Typically, each CDN PoP has a large number of cache servers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- Performance: Minimizing latency is one of the core missions of a CDN. The proposed design) Tj
T*
(should have minimum possible latency.) Tj
T*
(- Availability: CDNs are expected to be available at all times because of their effectiveness.) Tj
T*
(Availability includes protection against attacks like DDoS.) Tj
T*
(- Scalability: An increasing number of users will request content from CDNs. Our proposed CDN) Tj
T*
(design should be able to scale horizontally as the requirements increase.) Tj
T*
ET
