(## Content Delivery Network \(CDN\)/System Design The Content Delivery Network \(CDN\)/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: The Content Delivery Network \(CDN\)) Tj
T*
(## Problem statement) Tj
T*
(Let’s start with a question: If millions of users worldwide use our data-intensive) Tj
T*
(applications, and our service is deployed in a single data center to serve the users’ requests,) Tj
T*
(what possible problems can arise?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following problems can arise:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- High latency: The user-perceived latency will be high due to the physical distance from the) Tj
T*
(serving data center. User-perceived latency has many components, such as transmission delays \(a) Tj
T*
(function of available bandwidth\), propagation delays \(a function of distance\), queuing delays) Tj
T*
(\(a function of network congestion\), and nodal processing delays. Therefore, data transmission) Tj
T*
(over a large distance results in higher latency. Real-time applications require a latency below) Tj
T*
(200 milliseconds \(ms\) in general. For the Voice over Internet Protocol \(VoIP\), latency should) Tj
T*
(not be more than 150 ms, whereas video streaming applications cannot tolerate a latency above a) Tj
T*
(few seconds.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: According to one of the readings taken on December 21, 2021, the average latency from US) Tj
T*
(East \(N. Virginia\) to US West \(N. California\) was 62.9 ms. Across continents—for example, from) Tj
T*
(the US East \(N. Virginia\) to Africa \(Cape Town\)—was 225.63 ms. This is two-way latency, known) Tj
T*
(as round-trip latency.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
([Origin data center entertaining users' requests across the globe]\(./latency.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Data-intensive applications: Data-intensive applications require transferring large traffic.) Tj
T*
(Over a longer distance, this could be a problem due to the network path stretching through) Tj
T*
(different kinds of ISPs. Because of some smaller Path message transmission unit \(MTU\) links,) Tj
T*
(the throughput of applications on the network might be reduced. Similarly, different portions) Tj
T*
(of the network path might have different congestion characteristics. The problem multiplies as) Tj
T*
(the number of users grows because the origin servers will have to provide the data individually) Tj
T*
(to each user. That is, the primary data center will need to send out a lot of redundant data) Tj
T*
(when multiple clients ask for it. However, applications that use streaming services are both) Tj
T*
(data-intensive and dynamic in nature.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A Path MTU refers to the smallest data unit that can traverse from source to destination) Tj
T*
(without the need for splitting.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: According to a survey, 78% of the United States consumers use streaming services, which) Tj
T*
(is an increase of 25% in five years.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(- Scarcity of data center resources: Important data center resources like computational) Tj
T*
(capacity and bandwidth become a limitation when the number of users of a service increases) Tj
T*
(significantly. Services engaging millions of users simultaneously need scaling. Even if scaling) Tj
T*
(is achieved in a single data center, it can still suffer from becoming a single point of) Tj
T*
(failure when the data center goes offline due to natural calamity or connectivity issues with) Tj
T*
(the Internet.) Tj
T*
([User growth over the years for Facebook and YouTube applications]\(./users.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: According to one study, YouTube, Netflix, and Amazon Prime collectively generated 80% of) Tj
T*
