(Internet traffic in 2020. Circa 2016, the CDN provider Akamai served 15% to 30% of web traffic) Tj
T*
(\(about 30 terabits per second\). For 90% of Internet users, Akamai was just one hop away.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, we have strong reasons to optimize the delivery and consumption of this data without) Tj
T*
(making the Internet core a bottleneck.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## How will we design a CDN?) Tj
T*
(We’ve divided the design of CDN into six lessons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Introduction to a CDN: We’ll provide a thorough introduction to CDNs and identify the) Tj
T*
(functional and non-functional requirements.) Tj
T*
(- Design of a CDN: We’ll explain how to design the CDN. We’ll also briefly describe the API) Tj
T*
(design.) Tj
T*
(- In-depth Investigation of CDN: Part 1: This lesson explains caching strategies and CDN) Tj
T*
(architecture. Also, we’ll discuss various approaches to finding the nearest proxy server.) Tj
T*
(- In-depth Investigation of CDN: Part 2: We’ll discuss how to make content consistent in a CDN) Tj
T*
(and the deployment of proxy servers. We’ll also cover the custom and specialized CDN in detail.) Tj
T*
(- Evaluation of CDN: This lesson will provide an evaluation of our proposed design.) Tj
T*
(- Quiz on CDN System Design: We’ll reinforce major concepts of CDN design with a quiz.) Tj
T*
(Let’s think about the solution to the discussed issues in the next lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Databases/Data Partitioning/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Data Partitioning) Tj
T*
(## Why do we partition data?) Tj
T*
(Data is an asset for any organization. Increasing data and concurrent read/write traffic to the) Tj
T*
(data put scalability pressure on traditional databases. As a result, the latency and throughput) Tj
T*
(are affected. Traditional databases are attractive due to their properties such as range) Tj
T*
(queries, secondary indices, and transactions with the ACID properties.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(At some point, a single node-based database isn’t enough to tackle the load. We might need to) Tj
T*
(distribute the data over many nodes but still export all the nice properties of relational) Tj
T*
(databases. In practice, it has proved challenging to provide single-node database-like) Tj
T*
(properties over a distributed database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(One solution is to move data to a NoSQL-like system. However, the historical codebase and its) Tj
T*
(close cohesion with traditional databases make it an expensive problem to tackle.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Organizations might scale traditional databases by using a third-party solution. But often,) Tj
T*
(integrating a third-party solution has its complexities. More importantly, there are abundant) Tj
T*
(opportunities to optimize for the specific problem at hand and get much better performance than) Tj
T*
(a general-purpose solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Data partitioning \(or sharding\) enables us to use multiple nodes where each node manages some) Tj
T*
(part of the whole data. To handle increasing query rates and data amounts, we strive for) Tj
T*
(balanced partitions and balanced read/write load.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll discuss different ways to partition data, related challenges, and their solutions in this) Tj
T*
(lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A range query is a common database operation that retrieves all records where some value is) Tj
T*
(between an upper and lower boundary.) Tj
T*
() Tj
