(## Sharding) Tj
T*
(To divide load among multiple nodes, we need to partition the data by a phenomenon known as) Tj
T*
(partitioning or sharding. In this approach, we split a large dataset into smaller chunks of) Tj
T*
(data stored at different nodes on our network.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The partitioning must be balanced so that each partition receives about the same amount of) Tj
T*
(data. If partitioning is unbalanced, the majority of queries will fall into a few partitions.) Tj
T*
(Partitions that are heavily loaded will create a system bottleneck. The efficacy of) Tj
T*
(partitioning will be harmed because a significant portion of data retrieval queries will be) Tj
T*
(sent to the nodes that carry the highly congested partitions. Such partitions are known as) Tj
T*
(hotspots. Generally, we use the following two ways to shard the data:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Vertical sharding) Tj
T*
(Horizontal sharding) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(### Vertical sharding) Tj
T*
(We can put different tables in various database instances, which might be running on a) Tj
T*
(different physical server. We might break a table into multiple tables so that some columns are) Tj
T*
(in one table while the rest are in the other. We should be careful if there are joins between) Tj
T*
(multiple tables. We may like to keep such tables together on one shard.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Often, vertical sharding is used to increase the speed of data retrieval from a table) Tj
T*
(consisting of columns with very wide text or a binary large object \(blob\). In this case, the) Tj
T*
(column with large text or a blob is split into a different table.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As shown in the figure a couple paragraphs below, the Employee table is divided into two) Tj
T*
(tables: a reduced Employee table and an EmployeePicture table. The EmployePicture table has) Tj
T*
(just two columns, EmployeID and Picture, separated from the original table. Moreover, the) Tj
T*
(primary key EmpoloyeeID of the Employee table is added in both partitioned tables. This makes) Tj
T*
(the data read and write easier, and the reconstruction of the table is performed efficiently.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Vertical sharding has its intricacies and is more amenable to manual partitioning, where) Tj
T*
(stakeholders carefully decide how to partition data. In comparison, horizontal sharding is) Tj
T*
(suitable to automate even under dynamic conditions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Vertical partitioning]\(./vertical.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Horizontal sharding) Tj
T*
(At times, some tables in the databases become too big and affect read/write latency. Horizontal) Tj
T*
(sharding or partitioning is used to divide a table into multiple tables by splitting data row-) Tj
T*
(wise, as shown in the figure in the next section. Each partition of the original table) Tj
T*
(distributed over database servers is called a shard. Usually, there are two strategies) Tj
T*
(available:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Key-range based sharding) Tj
T*
(Hash based sharding) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Key-range based sharding) Tj
T*
(In the key-range based sharding, each partition is assigned a continuous range of keys.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the following figure, horizontal partitioning on the Invoice table is performed using the) Tj
T*
(key-range based sharding with Customer_Id as the partition key. The two different colored) Tj
T*
(tables represent the partitions.) Tj
T*
([Horizontal partitioning]\(./horizontal.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Sometimes, a database consists of multiple tables bound by foreign key relationships. In such a) Tj
T*
(case, the horizontal partition is performed using the same partition key on all tables in a) Tj
T*
(relation. Tables \(or subtables\) that belong to the same partition key are distributed to one) Tj
T*
(database shard. The following figure shows that several tables with the same partition key are) Tj
T*
