(Elasticsearch, Riak, and many more.) Tj
T*
(#### Dynamic partitioning) Tj
T*
(In this approach, when the size of a partition reaches the threshold, it’s split equally into) Tj
T*
(two partitions. One of the two split partitions is assigned to one node and the other one to) Tj
T*
(another node. In this way, the load is divided equally. The number of partitions adapts to the) Tj
T*
(overall data amount, which is an advantage of dynamic partitioning.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(However, there’s a downside to this approach. It’s difficult to apply dynamic rebalancing while) Tj
T*
(serving the reads and writes. This approach is used in HBase and MongoDB.) Tj
T*
(#### Partition proportionally to nodes) Tj
T*
(In this approach, the number of partitions is proportionate to the number of nodes, which means) Tj
T*
(every node has fixed partitions. In earlier approaches, the number of partitions was dependent) Tj
T*
(on the size of the dataset. That isn’t the case here. While the number of nodes remains) Tj
T*
(constant, the size of each partition rises according to the dataset size. However, as the) Tj
T*
(number of nodes increases, the partitions shrink. When a new node enters the network, it splits) Tj
T*
(a certain number of current partitions at random, then takes one half of the split and leaves) Tj
T*
(the other half alone. This can result in an unfair split. This approach is used by Cassandra) Tj
T*
(and Ketama.) Tj
