(## Request routing) Tj
T*
(We’ve learned how to partition our data. However, one question arises here: How does a client) Tj
T*
(know which node to connect to while making a request? The allocation of partitions to nodes) Tj
T*
(varies after rebalancing. If we want to read a specific key, how do we know which IP address we) Tj
T*
(need to connect to read?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This problem is also known as service discovery. Following are a few approaches to this) Tj
T*
(problem:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Allow the clients to request any node in the network. If that node doesn’t contain the) Tj
T*
(requested data, it forwards that request to the node that does contain the related data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The second approach contains a routing tier. All the requests are first forwarded to the) Tj
T*
(routing tier, and it determines which node to connect to fulfill the request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The clients already have the information related to partitioning and which partition is) Tj
T*
(connected to which node. So, they can directly contact the node that contains the data they) Tj
T*
(need.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(In all of these approaches, the main challenge is to determine how these components know about) Tj
T*
(updates in the partitioning of the nodes.) Tj
