(## Replication) Tj
T*
(Replication refers to keeping multiple copies of the data at various nodes \(preferably) Tj
T*
(geographically distributed\) to achieve availability, scalability, and performance. In this) Tj
T*
(lesson, we assume that a single node is enough to hold our entire data. We won’t use this) Tj
T*
(assumption while discussing the partitioning of data in multiple nodes. Often, the concepts of) Tj
T*
(replication and partitioning go together.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(However, with many benefits, like availability, replication comes with its complexities.) Tj
T*
(Replication is relatively simple if the replicated data doesn’t require frequent changes. The) Tj
T*
(main problem in replication arises when we have to maintain changes in the replicated data over) Tj
T*
(time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Additional complexities that could arise due to replication are as follows:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(How do we keep multiple copies of data consistent with each other?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(How do we deal with failed replica nodes?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Should we replicate synchronously or asynchronously?) Tj
T*
(    |) Tj
T*
(    +How do we deal with replication lag in case of asynchronous replication?) Tj
T*
(How do we handle concurrent writes?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(What consistency model needs to be exposed to the end programmers?) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(We’ll explore the answer to these questions in this lesson.) Tj
T*
([Replication in action]\(./replicas.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Before we explain the different types of replication, let’s understand the synchronous and) Tj
T*
(asynchronous approaches of replication.) Tj
T*
(## Synchronous versus asynchronous replication) Tj
T*
(There are two ways to disseminate changes to the replica nodes:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Synchronous replication) Tj
T*
(Asynchronous replication) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(In synchronous replication, the primary node waits for acknowledgments from secondary nodes) Tj
T*
(about updating the data. After receiving acknowledgment from all secondary nodes, the primary) Tj
T*
(node reports success to the client. Whereas in asynchronous replication, the primary node) Tj
T*
(doesn’t wait for the acknowledgment from the secondary nodes and reports success to the client) Tj
T*
(after updating itself.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The advantage of synchronous replication is that all the secondary nodes are completely up to) Tj
T*
(date with the primary node. However, there’s a disadvantage to this approach. If one of the) Tj
T*
(secondary nodes doesn’t acknowledge due to failure or fault in the network, the primary node) Tj
T*
(would be unable to acknowledge the client until it receives the successful acknowledgment from) Tj
T*
(the crashed node. This causes high latency in the response from the primary node to the client.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(On the other hand, the advantage of asynchronous replication is that the primary node can) Tj
T*
(continue its work even if all the secondary nodes are down. However, if the primary node fails,) Tj
T*
(the writes that weren’t copied to the secondary nodes will be lost.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The above paragraph explains a trade-off between data consistency and availability when) Tj
