(Now, let’s discuss various mechanisms of data replication. In this section, we’ll discuss the) Tj
T*
(following models along with their strengths and weaknesses:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Single leader or primary-secondary replication) Tj
T*
(Multi-leader replication) Tj
T*
(Peer-to-peer or leaderless replication) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Single leader/primary-secondary replication) Tj
T*
(In primary-secondary replication, data is replicated across multiple nodes. One node is) Tj
T*
(designated as the primary. It’s responsible for processing any writes to data stored on the) Tj
T*
(cluster. It also sends all the writes to the secondary nodes and keeps them in sync.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Primary-secondary replication is appropriate when our workload is read-heavy. To better scale) Tj
T*
(with increasing readers, we can add more followers and distribute the read load across the) Tj
T*
(available followers. However, replicating data to many followers can make a primary bottleneck.) Tj
T*
(Additionally, primary-secondary replication is inappropriate if our workload is write-heavy.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Another advantage of primary-secondary replication is that it’s read resilient. Secondary nodes) Tj
T*
(can still handle read requests in case of primary node failure. Therefore, it’s a helpful) Tj
T*
(approach for read-intensive applications.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Replication via this approach comes with inconsistency if we use asynchronous replication.) Tj
T*
(Clients reading from different replicas may see inconsistent data in the case of failure of the) Tj
T*
(primary node that couldn’t propagate updated data to the secondary nodes. So, if the primary) Tj
T*
(node fails, any missed updates not passed on to the secondary nodes can be lost.) Tj
T*
([Primary-secondary data replication model where data is replicated from primary to) Tj
T*
(secondary]\(./1primary.jpg\)) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What happens when the primary node fails?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(In case of failure of the primary node, a secondary node can be appointed as a primary node,) Tj
T*
(which speeds up the process of recovering the initial primary node. There are two approaches to) Tj
T*
(select the new primary node: manual and automatic.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In a manual approach, an operator decides which node should be the primary node and notifies) Tj
T*
(all secondary nodes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In an automatic approach, when secondary nodes find out that the primary node has failed, they) Tj
T*
(appoint the new primary node by conducting an election known as a leader election. A leader is) Tj
T*
(typically assigned based on a factor, such as choosing the processor with the highest) Tj
T*
(identifier.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(#### Primary-secondary replication methods) Tj
T*
(There are many different replication methods in primary-secondary replication:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Statement-based replication) Tj
T*
(Write-ahead log \(WAL\) shipping) Tj
T*
(Logical \(row-based\) log replication) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Let’s discuss each of them in detail.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(##### Statement-based replication) Tj
T*
(In the statement-based replication approach, the primary node saves all statements that it) Tj
T*
(executes, like insert, delete, update, and so on, and sends them to the secondary nodes to) Tj
T*
(perform. This type of replication was used in MySQL before version 5.1.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This type of approach seems good, but it has its disadvantages. For example, any) Tj
T*
