(nondeterministic function \(such as NOW\(\)\) might result in distinct writes on the follower and) Tj
T*
(leader. Furthermore, if a write statement is dependent on a prior write, and both of them reach) Tj
T*
(the follower in the wrong order, the outcome on the follower node will be uncertain.) Tj
T*
(##### Write-ahead log \(WAL\) shipping) Tj
T*
(In the write-ahead log \(WAL\) shipping approach, the primary node saves the query before) Tj
T*
(executing it in a log file known as a write-ahead log file. It then uses these logs to copy the) Tj
T*
(data onto the secondary nodes. This is used in PostgreSQL and Oracle. The problem with WAL is) Tj
T*
(that it only defines data at a very low level. It’s tightly coupled with the inner structure of) Tj
T*
(the database engine, which makes upgrading software on the leader and followers complicated.) Tj
T*
(##### Logical \(row-based\) log replication) Tj
T*
(In the logical \(row-based\) log replication approach, all secondary nodes replicate the actual) Tj
T*
(data changes. For example, if a row is inserted or deleted in a table, the secondary nodes will) Tj
T*
(replicate that change in that specific table. The binary log records change to database tables) Tj
T*
(on the primary node at the record level. To create a replica of the primary node, the secondary) Tj
T*
(node reads this data and changes its records accordingly. Row-based replication doesn’t have) Tj
T*
(the same difficulties as WAL because it doesn’t require information about data layout inside) Tj
T*
(the database engine.) Tj
T*
(### Multi-leader replication) Tj
T*
(As discussed above, single leader replication using asynchronous replication has a drawback.) Tj
T*
(There’s only one primary node, and all the writes have to go through it, which limits the) Tj
T*
(performance. In case of failure of the primary node, the secondary nodes may not have the) Tj
T*
(updated database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Multi-leader replication is an alternative to single leader replication. There are multiple) Tj
T*
(primary nodes that process the writes and send them to all other primary and secondary nodes to) Tj
T*
(replicate. This type of replication is used in databases along with external tools like the) Tj
T*
(Tungsten Replicator for MySQL.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This kind of replication is quite useful in applications in which we can continue work even if) Tj
T*
(we’re offline—for example, a calendar application in which we can set our meetings even if we) Tj
T*
(don’t have access to the internet. Once we’re online, it replicates its changes from our local) Tj
T*
(database \(our mobile phone or laptop acts as a primary node\) to other nodes.) Tj
T*
(#### Conflict) Tj
T*
(Multi-leader replication gives better performance and scalability than single leader) Tj
T*
(replication, but it also has a significant disadvantage. Since all the primary nodes) Tj
T*
(concurrently deal with the write requests, they may modify the same data, which can create a) Tj
T*
(conflict between them. For example, suppose the same data is edited by two clients) Tj
T*
(simultaneously. In that case, their writes will be successful in their associated primary) Tj
T*
(nodes, but when they reach the other primary nodes asynchronously, it creates a conflict.) Tj
T*
(#### Handle conflicts) Tj
T*
(Conflicts can result in different data at different nodes. These should be handled efficiently) Tj
T*
(without losing any data. Let’s discuss some of the approaches to handle conflicts:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(##### Conflict avoidance) Tj
T*
(A simple strategy to deal with conflicts is to prevent them from happening in the first place.) Tj
T*
(Conflicts can be avoided if the application can verify that all writes for a given record go) Tj
T*
(via the same leader.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(However, the conflict may still occur if a user moves to a different location and is now near a) Tj
T*
(different data center. If that happens, we need to reroute the traffic. In such scenarios, the) Tj
T*
(conflict avoidance approach fails and results in concurrent writes.) Tj
T*
(##### Last-write-wins) Tj
T*
(Using their local clock, all nodes assign a timestamp to each update. When a conflict occurs,) Tj
T*
(the update with the latest timestamp is selected.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This approach can also create difficulty because the clock synchronization across nodes is) Tj
T*
(challenging in distributed systems. There’s clock skew that can result in data loss.) Tj
T*
(##### Custom logic) Tj
T*
(In this approach, we can write our own logic to handle conflicts according to the needs of our) Tj
T*
(application. This custom logic can be executed on both reads and writes. When the system) Tj
T*
