T*
() Tj
T*
(- Restrict Brand at site B to Wolf \(called projection\) and move the result to site A:) Tj
T*
(T=0.1 + \(10×200\)/50,000,000 = approximately 0.1 seconds) Tj
T*
(Here, 0.1 is the access delay of the Product table. The number of the Wolf brand is 10, hence) Tj
T*
(the number of tuples.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(When we compare the three approaches, the third approach provides us the least latency \(0.1) Tj
T*
(seconds\). This example shows that careful query optimization is also critical in the) Tj
T*
(distributed database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Conclusion) Tj
T*
(Data distribution \(vertical and horizontal sharding\) across multiple nodes aims to improve the) Tj
T*
(following features, considering that the queries are optimized:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Reliability \(fault-tolerance\)) Tj
T*
(- Performance) Tj
T*
(- Balanced storage capacity and dollar costs) Tj
T*
(Both centralized and distributed databases have their pros and cons. We should choose them) Tj
T*
(according to the needs of our application.) Tj
