() Tj
T*
(#### Integration) Tj
T*
(The process of aggregating data from multiple sources is a common practice in enterprise) Tj
T*
(applications. A common way to perform this aggregation is to integrate a shared database where) Tj
T*
(multiple applications store their data. This way, all the applications can easily access each) Tj
T*
(other’s data while the concurrency control measures handle the access of multiple applications.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Backup and disaster recovery) Tj
T*
(Relational databases guarantee the state of data is consistent at any time. The export and) Tj
T*
(import operations make backup and restoration easier. Most cloud-based relational databases) Tj
T*
(perform continuous mirroring to avoid loss of data and make the restoration process easier and) Tj
T*
(quicker.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Drawback) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Impedance mismatch) Tj
T*
(Impedance mismatch is the difference between the relational model and the in-memory data) Tj
T*
(structures. The relational model organizes data into a tabular structure with relations and) Tj
T*
(tuples. SQL operation on this structured data yields relations aligned with relational algebra.) Tj
T*
(However, it has some limitations. In particular, the values in a table take simple values that) Tj
T*
(can’t be a structure or a list. The case is different for in-memory, where a complex data) Tj
T*
(structure can be stored. To make the complex structures compatible with the relations, we would) Tj
T*
(need a translation of the data in light of relational algebra. So, the impedance mismatch) Tj
T*
(requires translation between two representations, as denoted in the following figure:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([A single aggregated value in the view is composed of several rows and tables in the relational) Tj
T*
(database]\(./mismatch.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Why non-relational \(NoSQL\) databases?) Tj
T*
(A NoSQL database is designed for a variety of data models to access and manage data. There are) Tj
T*
(various types of NoSQL databases, which we’ll explain in the next section. These databases are) Tj
T*
(used in applications that require a large volume of semi-structured and unstructured data, low) Tj
T*
(latency, and flexible data models. This can be achieved by relaxing some of the data) Tj
T*
(consistency restrictions of other databases. Following are some characteristics of the NoSQL) Tj
T*
(database:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Simple design: Unlike relational databases, NoSQL doesn’t require dealing with the impedance) Tj
T*
(mismatch—for example, storing all the employees’ data in one document instead of multiple) Tj
T*
(tables that require join operations. This strategy makes it simple and easier to write less) Tj
T*
(code, debug, and maintain.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Horizontal scaling: Primarily, NoSQL is preferred due to its ability to run databases on a) Tj
T*
(large cluster. This solves the problem when the number of concurrent users increases. NoSQL) Tj
T*
(makes it easier to scale out since the data related to a specific employee is stored in one) Tj
T*
(document instead of multiple tables over nodes. NoSQL databases often spread data across) Tj
T*
(multiple nodes and balance data and queries across nodes automatically. In case of a node) Tj
T*
(failure, it can be transparently replaced without any application disruption.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Availability: To enhance the availability of data, node replacement can be performed without) Tj
T*
(application downtime. Most of the non-relational databases’ variants support data replication) Tj
T*
(to ensure high availability and disaster recovery.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Support for unstructured and semi-structured data: Many NoSQL databases work with data that) Tj
T*
(doesn’t have schema at the time of database configuration or data writes. For example, document) Tj
T*
(databases are structureless; they allow documents \(JSON, XML, BSON, and so on\) to have) Tj
T*
(different fields. For example, one JSON document can have fewer fields than the other.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cost: Licenses for many RDBMSs are pretty expensive, while many NoSQL databases are open source) Tj
T*
(and freely available. Similarly, some RDBMSs rely on costly proprietary hardware and storage) Tj
T*
ET
