T*
(We’ve now explained the design and how it fulfills the functional requirements for a) Tj
T*
(collaborative document editing service. This lesson will focus on how our design meets the non-) Tj
T*
(functional requirements. In particular, we’ll focus on consistency, latency, scalability, and) Tj
T*
(availability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Consistency) Tj
T*
(We’ve looked at how we’ll achieve strong consistency for conflict resolution in a document) Tj
T*
(through two technologies: operational transformation \(OT\) and Conflict-free Resolution Data) Tj
T*
(Types \(CRDTs\). In addition, a time series database enables us to preserve the order of events.) Tj
T*
(Once OT or CRDT has resolved any conflicts, the final result is saved in the database. This) Tj
T*
(helps us achieve consistency in terms of individual operations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’re also interested in keeping the document state consistent across different servers in a) Tj
T*
(data center. To replicate an updated state of a document within the same data center at the) Tj
T*
(same time, we can use peer-to-peer protocols like Gossip protocol. Not only will this strategy) Tj
T*
(improve consistency, it will also improve availability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Also referred to as epidemic protocol which is used for disseminating data across all nodes in) Tj
T*
(a network to achieve consistency.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Why should we use strong consistency instead of eventual consistency for conflict resolution in) Tj
T*
(a collaborative document editing service?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(From Amazon’s Dynamo system, we learn that if we use eventual consistency for conflict) Tj
T*
(resolution, we might have multiple versions of a document that are eventually reconciled,) Tj
T*
(either automatically or manually. In the case of automatic reconciliation, the document might) Tj
T*
(update abruptly, which defeats the purpose of collaboration. The second case, manual) Tj
T*
(resolution, is tedious labor that we want to avoid.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Therefore, we use strong consistency for conflict resolution, and the logically centralized) Tj
T*
(server provides the final order of events to all clients. We use a replicated operations queue) Tj
T*
(so that even if our ordering service dies, it can easily restart on a new server and resume) Tj
T*
(where it left off. Clients might encounter short service unavailability while the failed) Tj
T*
(component is being respawned.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Latency) Tj
T*
(Latency may feel like a challenge, specifically when two users are distant from each other or) Tj
T*
(the server. However, users maintain a replica of documents at their end while data is being) Tj
T*
(propagated through WebSockets to end-servers. Therefore, user-perceived latency will be low.) Tj
T*
(Apart from this, users mostly tend to write textual data in a document that’s small. Therefore,) Tj
T*
(dissemination of data among different servers within the same facility and among different data) Tj
T*
(centers or zones will be carried out with low latency. Also, files like videos and images can) Tj
T*
(be stored in CDNs for quick serving because this content doesn’t change frequently.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Practically speaking, there will be a limited number of readers and writers of an online) Tj
T*
ET
