(Imagine two students are preparing a report on a project they’ve just completed. Since the) Tj
T*
(students live apart, the first student asks the second student to start writing the report, and) Tj
T*
(the first student will improve the received copy. Although quite motivated, the students soon) Tj
T*
(understand that such a collaboration is disorganized. The following illustration shows how) Tj
T*
(tedious the process can get.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Problems arising when there’s no collaborative document editing service]\(./demo.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The scenario above is one example that leads to time wastage and frustration when users) Tj
T*
(collaborate on a document by exchanging files with each other.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Google Docs) Tj
T*
(To combat the problem above, we can use an online collaborative document editing service like) Tj
T*
(Google Docs. Some advantages of using an online document editing service instead of a desktop) Tj
T*
(application are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Users can review and comment on a document while it’s being edited.) Tj
T*
(- There are no special hardware specifications required to get the latest features. A machine) Tj
T*
(that can run a browser will suffice.) Tj
T*
(- It's possible to work from any location.) Tj
T*
(- Unlike local desktop editors, users can view long-term document history and restore an older) Tj
T*
(version if need be.) Tj
T*
(- The service is free of cost.) Tj
T*
(Other than Google docs, some popular online editing services include Etherpad, Microsoft Office) Tj
T*
(365, Slite, and many others.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Designing Google Docs) Tj
T*
(A collaborative document editing service can be designed in two ways:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- It could be designed as a centralized facility using client-server architecture to provide) Tj
T*
(document editing service to all users.) Tj
T*
(- It could be designed using peer-to-peer technology to collaborate on a single document.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Most commercial solutions focus on client-service architecture to have finer control.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, we’ll focus on designing a service using the client service architecture. Let’s see) Tj
T*
(how we will progress in this chapter.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: According to a survey, 64% of people use Google Docs for document editing at least once a) Tj
T*
(week.) Tj
T*
(See: https://www.statista.com/forecasts/1011649/frequency-of-using-google-docs-in-the-us) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## How will we design Google Docs?) Tj
T*
(We've divided the design problem into four stages:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Requirements for Google Docs’ Design: This lesson will focus on establishing the) Tj
T*
(requirements for designing a collaborative document editing service. We’ll also quantify the) Tj
T*
(infrastructure requirements in this stage.) Tj
T*
(2. Google Docs’ Design: The goal of this lesson is to come up with a design that fulfills the) Tj
T*
(requirements of the service. This lesson will explain why a component is used and how it) Tj
T*
(integrates with other components to fulfill functional requirements.) Tj
T*
(3. Concurrency in Collaborative Editing: Online document editing services have to resolve) Tj
T*
(conflicts between users editing the same portion of a document. This lesson covers the type of) Tj
T*
(problems that can arise and the techniques used to resolve such conflicts.) Tj
T*
(4. Evaluating Google Docs’ Design: The main objective of this lesson is to evaluate our design) Tj
T*
(for non-functional requirements. Mainly, we see if our design is performant, consistent,) Tj
T*
ET
