(available, and scalable.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design Google Maps/Challenges of Google Maps' Design/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Challenges of Google Maps' Design) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Meeting the challenges) Tj
T*
(We listed two challenges in the Introduction lesson: scalability and ETA computation. Let’s see) Tj
T*
(how we meet these challenges.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Scalability) Tj
T*
(Scalability is about the ability to efficiently process a huge road network graph. We have a) Tj
T*
(graph with billions of vertices and edges, and the key challenges are inefficient loading,) Tj
T*
(updating, and performing computations. For example, we have to traverse the whole graph to find) Tj
T*
(the shortest path. This results in increased query time for the user. So, what could be the) Tj
T*
(solution to this problem?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The idea is to break down a large graph into smaller subgraphs, or partitions. The subgraphs) Tj
T*
(can be processed and queried in parallel. As a result, the graph construction and query) Tj
T*
(processing time will be greatly decreased. So, we divide the globe into small parts called) Tj
T*
(segments. Each segment corresponds to a subgraph.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Segment) Tj
T*
(A segment is a small area on which we can work easily. Finding paths within these segments) Tj
T*
(works because the segment’s road network graph is small and can be loaded easily in the main) Tj
T*
(memory, updated, and traversed. A city, for example, can be divided into hundreds of segments,) Tj
T*
(each measuring 5×5 miles.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Traversing is visiting the graph vertices to reach a specific vertex.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: A segment is not necessarily a square. It can also be a polygon. However, we are assuming) Tj
T*
(square segments for ease of explanation.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Each segment has four coordinates that help determine which segment the user is in. Each) Tj
T*
(coordinate consists of two values, the latitude and longitude.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Partitioning the globe into small segments, and each segment has four coordinates with) Tj
T*
(latitude and longitude values]\(./segment.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s talk about finding paths between two locations within a segment. We have a graph) Tj
T*
(representing the road network in that segment. Each intersection/junction acts as a vertex and) Tj
T*
(each road acts as an edge. The graph is weighted, and there could be multiple weights on each) Tj
T*
(edge—such as distance, time, and traffic—to find the optimal path. For a given source and) Tj
T*
(destination, there can be multiple paths. We can use any of the graph algorithms on that) Tj
T*
(segment’s graph to find the shortest paths. The most common shortest path algorithm is the) Tj
T*
(Dijkstra’s algorithm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The traditional Dijkstra algorithm performs a guided search to find the shortest path. However,) Tj
T*
(it comes at the cost of an increased query time. We have to set a limit on the query time) Tj
T*
(because we wouldn’t want the user to wait too long to get their results. So, we’ll go with) Tj
T*
(finding the optimal path within the given time cost.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The A* algorithm is an extension of the Dijkstra algorithm that uses heuristics to guide its) Tj
T*
(search. Instead of finding the shortest path, A* finds the optimal path that is calculated by) Tj
T*
(minimizing the following function.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
