<< /Length 3904 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(f\(n\)=g\(n\)+h\(n\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Here, n is the next node on the path, g\(n\) is the cost of the path from the start node to n,) Tj
T*
(and h\(n\) is a heuristic function that estimates the cost of the cheapest path from n to the) Tj
T*
(goal.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: There are some algorithms that are specifically designed for road networks that focus on) Tj
T*
(achieving shorter query time. These algorithms are A*, Arc flags, contraction hierarchies,) Tj
T*
(transit node routing, reach-based routing, and hub labeling.) Tj
T*
(https://arxiv.org/pdf/1603.01607.pdf) Tj
T*
(http://www.diag.uniroma1.it/challenge9/papers/kohler.pdf) Tj
T*
(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contraction_hierarchies) Tj
T*
(https://www.researchgate.net/publication/6366597_Fast_Routing_in_Road_Networks_with_Transit_Nod) Tj
T*
(es) Tj
T*
(https://archive.siam.org/meetings/alenex04/abstacts/rgutman1.pdf) Tj
T*
(https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2010/12/HL-TR.pdf) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(After running the shortest path algorithm on the segment’s graph, we store the algorithm’s) Tj
T*
(output in a distributed storage to avoid recalculation and cache the most requested routes. The) Tj
T*
(algorithm’s output is the shortest distance in meters or miles between every two vertices in) Tj
T*
(the graph, the time it takes to travel via the shortest path, and the list of vertices along) Tj
T*
(every shortest path. All of the above processing \(running the shortest path algorithm on the) Tj
T*
(segment graph\) is done offline \(not on a user’s critical path\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Finding a path between vertices within a segment]\(./path.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The illustration above shows how we can find the shortest distance in terms of miles between) Tj
T*
(two points. For example, the minimum distance \(m\) between points A and D is 11 miles by taking) Tj
T*
(the path A->C->D. It has an ETA of 15 minutes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ve found the shortest path between two vertices. What if we have to find the path between) Tj
T*
(two points that lie on the edges? What we do is find the vertices of the edge on which the) Tj
T*
(points lie, calculate the distance of the point from the identified vertices, and choose the) Tj
T*
(vertices that make the shorter total distance between the source and the destination. The) Tj
T*
(distance from the source \(and destination\) to the nearest vertices is approximated using) Tj
T*
(latitude/longitude values.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Choosing a path between points that lie on the edge]\(./selection.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’re able to find the shortest paths within the segment. Let’s see what happens when the) Tj
T*
(source and the destination belong to two different segments, and how we connect the segments.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Connect two segments) Tj
T*
(Each segment has a unique name and boundary coordinates. We can easily identify which location) Tj
T*
(\(latitude, longitude\) lies in which segment. Given the source and the destination, we can find) Tj
T*
(the segments in which they lie. For each segment, there are some boundary edges, which we call) Tj
T*
(exit points. In the illustration below, we have four exit points, S4E1, S4E2, S4E3, and S4E4.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Besides the vertices we have inside the segment, we also consider the exit points of a) Tj
T*
(segment as vertices and calculate the shortest path for these exit points. So for each segment,) Tj
T*
(we calculate the shortest path between exit points in addition to the shortest path from the) Tj
T*
