(# Design of Google Maps) Tj
T*
(## High-level design) Tj
T*
(Let’s start with the high-level design of a map system. We split the discussion into two) Tj
T*
(sections:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. The components we’ll need in our design.) Tj
T*
(2. The workflow that interconnects these components.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Components) Tj
T*
(We’ll use the following components in our design:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Location finder: The location finder is a service used to find the user’s current location) Tj
T*
(and show it on the map since we can’t possibly personally remember the latitude and longitude) Tj
T*
(for every place in the world.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Route finder: For the people who are new to a place, it’s difficult to travel because they) Tj
T*
(don’t know the correct routes. The route finder is a service used to find the paths between two) Tj
T*
(locations, or points.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Navigator: Suggesting a route through the route finder is not enough. A user may deviate from) Tj
T*
(the optimal path. In that case, a navigator service is used. This service keeps track of users’) Tj
T*
(journeys and sends updated directions and notifications to the users as soon as they deviate) Tj
T*
(from the suggested route.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- GPS/Wi-Fi/Cellular technology: These are the technologies that we used to find the user’s) Tj
T*
(ground position.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Distributed search: For converting place names to latitude/longitude values, we need a) Tj
T*
(conversion system behind the source and destination fields. A distributed search maintains an) Tj
T*
(index consisting of places names and their mapping to latitude and longitude. User’s entered) Tj
T*
(keywords are searched using this service to find a location on the map.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Area search service: The area search service coordinates between the distributed search and) Tj
T*
(graph processing service to obtain the shortest path against a user query. The area search) Tj
T*
(service will request the distributed search to obtain the locations of the source and) Tj
T*
(destination on the map. Then, it will use the graph processing service to find the optimal path) Tj
T*
(from the source to the destination.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Graph processing service: There can be multiple paths from one place to another. The graph) Tj
T*
(processing service runs the shortest path algorithm on a shorter graph based on the area) Tj
T*
(spanning the source and destination points and helps us determine which path to follow.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Database: As discussed in the previous lesson, we have the road data from various sources) Tj
T*
(stored in the form of a graph. We’ll map this data to a database to develop the road network) Tj
T*
(graph. We’re using a graph database like DataStax Graph to store the graph for our design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Pub-sub system: Users might deviate from the first suggested path. In that case, they’ll need) Tj
T*
(information on a new path to their destination. Pub-sub is a system that listens to various) Tj
T*
(events from a service and triggers another service accordingly. For example, when a user) Tj
T*
(deviates from the suggested path, it pings the area search service to find a new route from the) Tj
T*
(user’s current location to their destination point. It also collects the streams of location) Tj
T*
(data for different users from the navigator. This data can be processed later to find traffic) Tj
T*
(patterns on different roads at different times. We’ll use Kafka as a pub-sub system in our) Tj
T*
(design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Third-party road data: How can we build a map system if we don’t have the road networks data?) Tj
T*
(We need to collect the road data from third-party resources and preprocess the collected data) Tj
T*
(to bring it into a single format that can be utilized to build the graph.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Graph building: We’ll use a service that builds the graph from the given data, either) Tj
T*
ET
