(### Workflow) Tj
T*
(We explain the workflow by assuming that a user has to travel between two points but doesn’t) Tj
T*
(know their current location or how to get to their destination. So, let’s see how the maps) Tj
T*
(system helps.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(For this exercise, we assume that the data about road networks and maps has already been) Tj
T*
(collected from the third parties and the graph is built and stored in the Graph DB.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(- In a maps system, the user has to enter their starting point and their destination to create) Tj
T*
(a path between the two. For the starting source point, the user uses the current location) Tj
T*
(service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The location finder determines the current location by maintaining a persistent connection) Tj
T*
(with the user. The user will provide an updated location using GPS, Wi-Fi, and cellular) Tj
T*
(technology. This will be the user’s source point.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- For the destination point, the user types an address in text format. It’s a good idea to make) Tj
T*
(use of our Typeahead service here to provide useful suggestions and avoid spelling mistakes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- After entering the source and the destination points, the user requests the optimal path.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The user’s path request is forwarded to the route-finder service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  - The route finder forwards the requests to an area search service with the source and the) Tj
T*
(destination points.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  - The area search service uses the distributed search to find the latitude/longitude for the) Tj
T*
(source and the destination. It then calculates the area on the map spanning the two \(source’s) Tj
T*
(and destination’s\) latitude/longitude points.) Tj
T*
(  - After finding the area, the area search service asks the graph processing service to) Tj
T*
(process part of the graph, depending on the area to find the optimal path.) Tj
T*
(  - The graph processing service fetches the edges and nodes within that specified area from) Tj
T*
(the database, finds the shortest path, and returns it to the route-finder service that) Tj
T*
(visualizes the optimal path with the distance and time necessary to comeplete the route. It) Tj
T*
(also displays the steps the user should follow for navigation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Now that the user can visualize the shortest path on the map, they also want to get) Tj
T*
(directions towards the destination. The direction request is handled by the navigator.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The navigator tracks that the user is following the correct path, which it has from the) Tj
T*
(route-finder service. It updates the user’s location on the map while the user is moving, and) Tj
T*
(shows where to turn with the distance. If a user deviates from the path, it generates an event) Tj
T*
(that is fed to Kafka.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Upon receiving the event from the navigator, Kafka updates the subscribed topic of the area) Tj
T*
(search service, which in turn recalculates the optimal path and suggests it to the user. The) Tj
T*
(navigator also provides a stream of live location data to the graph, building it through the) Tj
T*
(pub-sub system. Later, this data can be used to improve route suggestions provided to the) Tj
T*
ET
