([Non-functional requirements fulfilled by our maps system]) Tj
T*
(## Scalability) Tj
T*
(We scaled our system for large road networks. Scalability can be seen in two ways:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The ability of the system to handle an increasing amount of user requests.) Tj
T*
(- The ability of the system to work with more data \(segments\).) Tj
T*
(We divided the world into small segments. Each segment is hosted on a different server in the) Tj
T*
(distributed system. The user requests for different routes are served from the different) Tj
T*
(segment servers. In this way, we can serve millions of user requests.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: It wouldn’t have been possible to serve millions of user requests if we had a single) Tj
T*
(large graph spanning the whole road network. There would have been memory issues loading and) Tj
T*
(processing a huge graph.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(We can also add more segments easily because we don’t have to change the complete graph. We can) Tj
T*
(further improve scalability by non-uniformly selecting the size of a segment—selecting smaller) Tj
T*
(segment sizes in densely connected areas and bigger segments for the outskirts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Smaller response times) Tj
T*
(We’re running the user requests on small subgraphs. Processing a small subgraph of hundreds of) Tj
T*
(vertices is far faster than a graph of millions of vertices. We can cache the processed small) Tj
T*
(subgraph in the main memory and quickly respond to user requests. This is how our system) Tj
T*
(responds to the user in less time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There’s another aspect that helps our system to respond quickly, and that is keeping the) Tj
T*
(segment information in the key-value store. The key-value store helps different services to get) Tj
T*
(the required information quickly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The graph processing service checks for the relevant segments in which the source and the) Tj
T*
(destination latitude/longitude lie by querying the key-value store for the segmentID values.) Tj
T*
(- For load-balancing user requests among different segment servers, the key-value store is) Tj
T*
(queried for the serverID against the segment on which the graph processing should run for a) Tj
T*
(specific request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Accuracy) Tj
T*
(Besides the road data we had initially, we also captured the live location data of users, on) Tj
T*
(which we performed analytics using data science techniques. Our system improves the accuracy of) Tj
T*
(the results \(path, ETA\) using these analytics. Based on the analytics of the traffic patterns,) Tj
T*
(the maps are updated, and the routes and ETA estimations are improved.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(               Meeting Non-functional Requirements) Tj
T*
(Requirements       Techniques) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Availability       Process user queries on small graphs \(segments\).) Tj
T*
(                   Load balance requests across different segment servers.) Tj
T*
(                   Replicate segment servers.) Tj
T*
(Scalability        Partition the large graphs into small graphs to ease segment addition.) Tj
T*
(                   Host the segments on different servers to enable serving more queries per) Tj
T*
(second.) Tj
T*
(                   Less response time) Tj
T*
(                   Cache the processed graphs.) Tj
T*
(                   Use a key-value store to quickly get the required information.) Tj
T*
(Accuracy           Collect live data.) Tj
T*
(                   Perform data analytics.) Tj
T*
ET
