(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(Google Maps is one of the most widely used applications in the world, where users find the) Tj
T*
(shortest route between two locations. A map system models the road network with a graph data) Tj
T*
(structure. To find the route, the shortest path algorithm runs over the graph. We’ve seen) Tj
T*
(scalability issues with a large graph of the road network. We solved the problem by splitting) Tj
T*
(the world into small segments. Each segment consists of a small graph that can be loaded into) Tj
T*
(the memory to find the paths quickly. We’ve also seen that the estimated time of arrival can be) Tj
T*
(improved by analyzing the live location data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design Google Maps/Requirements of Google Maps' Design/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Requirements of Google Maps' Design) Tj
T*
(## Requirements) Tj
T*
(Before we start requirements, let’s clarify that we will design a system like Google Maps by) Tj
T*
(picking a few key features because actual Google Maps is feature-rich and complex.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s list the functional and non-functional requirements of the system under design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Functional requirements) Tj
T*
(The functional requirements of our system are as follows.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Identify the current location: Users should be able to approximate their current location) Tj
T*
(\(latitude and longitude in decimal values\) on the world map.) Tj
T*
(- Recommend the fastest route: Given the source and destination \(place names in text\), the) Tj
T*
(system should recommend the optimal route by distance and time, depending on the type of) Tj
T*
(transportation.) Tj
T*
(- Give directions: Once the user has chosen the route, the system should list directions in) Tj
T*
(text format, where each item in the list guides the user to turn or continue in a specific) Tj
T*
(direction to reach the destination.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Google Maps functional requirements]\(./fq.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(The non-functional requirements of our system are as follows.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Availability: The system should be highly available.) Tj
T*
(- Scalability: It should be scalable because both individuals and other enterprise applications) Tj
T*
(like Uber and Lyft use Google Maps to find appropriate routes.) Tj
T*
(- Less response time: It shouldn’t take more than two or three seconds to calculate the ETA and) Tj
T*
(the route, given the source and the destination points.) Tj
T*
(- Accuracy: The ETA we predict should not deviate too much from the actual travel time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Non-functional requirements of Google maps]\(./nfq.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Note: We’re not getting into the details of how we get the data on roads and layout. Government) Tj
T*
(agencies provide maps, and in some places, Google itself drives mapping vehicles to find roads) Tj
T*
(and their intersection points. Road networks are modeled with a graph data structure, where) Tj
T*
(intersection points are the vertices, and the roads between intersections are the weighted) Tj
T*
(edges.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Challenges) Tj
T*
(Some of the challenges that we need to focus on while designing a system like Google Maps are) Tj
T*
(below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Scalability: Serving millions of queries for different routes in a second, given a graph with) Tj
T*
(billions of nodes and edges spanning over 194 countries, requires robust scalability measures.) Tj
T*
(A simple approach, given the latitude and longitude of the source and destination, would be to) Tj
T*
(apply an algorithm like Dijkstra to find the shortest path between the source and the) Tj
T*
ET
