(## Resource estimation) Tj
T*
(Let’s estimate the total number of servers, storage, and bandwidth required by the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Number of servers estimation) Tj
T*
(To estimate the number of servers, we need to know how many daily active users are using Google) Tj
T*
(Maps and how many requests per second a single Google Maps server can handle. We assume the) Tj
T*
(following numbers:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Daily active users who use Google Maps: 32 million \(about 1 billion monthly users\).) Tj
T*
(- Number of requests a single server can handle per second: 8,000.) Tj
T*
(The number of servers required has been calculated using the below formula:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Number of active users/requests handled per server = 4K servers) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Number of servers required for Google Maps]\(./servers.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Storage estimation) Tj
T*
(Google Maps is essentially a system with a one-time storage requirement. The road data from) Tj
T*
(many countries has already been added, which is over 20 petabytes as of 2022. Since there are) Tj
T*
(minimal changes on the road networks, the daily storage requirement is going to be negligible) Tj
T*
(for Google Maps. Also, short-term changes in the road network is a small amount of data as) Tj
T*
(compared to the full network data. Therefore, our storage needs don’t change rapidly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Bandwidth estimation) Tj
T*
(As a standard practice, we have to estimate the bandwidth required for the incoming and) Tj
T*
(outgoing traffic of our system. Most of the bandwidth requirements for Google Maps are due to) Tj
T*
(requests sent by users. Therefore, we’ve devised the following formula to calculate bandwidth:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total bandwidth = Total requests_second ×Total query_size) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The Total_{requests\\_second} represents the number of requests per second, whereas the) Tj
T*
(Total_{query\\_size} represents the size of each request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Incoming Traffic**) Tj
T*
(To estimate the incoming query traffic bandwidth, we assume the following numbers:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Maximum number of requests by a single user per day: 50.) Tj
T*
(- Request size \(source and destination\): 200 Bytes.) Tj
T*
(Using the assumptions above, we can estimate the total number of requests per second on Google) Tj
T*
(Maps using the following formula:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total requests_second= Daily active users ×  Requests per_user / \(24 x 60 x 60\) = =18,518) Tj
T*
ET
