(## Upload, view, and search a photo) Tj
T*
(The client requests to upload the photo, load balancer passes the request to any of the) Tj
T*
(application servers, which adds an entry to the database. An update that the photo is stored) Tj
T*
(successfully is sent to the user. If an error is encountered, the user is communicated about it) Tj
T*
(as well.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The photo viewing process is also similar to the above-mentioned flow. The client requests to) Tj
T*
(view a photo, and an appropriate photo that matches the request is fetched from the database) Tj
T*
(and shown to the user. The client can also provide a keyword to search for a specific image.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The read requests are more than write requests and it takes time to upload the content in the) Tj
T*
(system. It is efficient if we separate the write \(uploads\) and read services. The multiple) Tj
T*
(services operated by many servers handle the relevant requests. The read service performs the) Tj
T*
(tasks of fetching the required content for the user, while the write service helps upload) Tj
T*
(content to the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We also need to cache the data to handle millions of reads. It improves the user experience by) Tj
T*
(making the fetching process fast. We’ll also opt for lazy loading, which minimizes the client’s) Tj
T*
(waiting time. It allows us to load the content when the user scrolls and therefore save the) Tj
T*
(bandwidth and focus on loading the content the user is currently viewing. It improves the) Tj
T*
(latency to view or search a particular photo or video on Instagram.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Lazy loading contributes to efficiency in the program’s operation. It is ideal in use cases) Tj
T*
(where network content is accessed and initialization times are to be kept at a minimum, such as) Tj
T*
(in the case of web pages. For example, deferring the loading of images on a web page until they) Tj
T*
(are needed can make the initial display of the web page faster. Source: Wikipedia) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The updated design is as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Various operations on photos]\(./photos.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Generate a timeline) Tj
T*
(Now our task is to generate a user-specific timeline. Let’s explore various approaches and the) Tj
T*
(advantages and disadvantages to opt for the appropriate strategy.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### The pull approach) Tj
T*
(When a user opens their Instagram, we send a request for timeline generation. First, we fetch) Tj
T*
(the list of people the user follows, get the photos they recently posted, store them in queues,) Tj
T*
(and display them to the user. But this approach is slow to respond as we generate a timeline) Tj
T*
(every time the user opens Instagram.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can substantially reduce user-perceived latency by generating the timeline offline. For) Tj
T*
(example, we define a service that fetches the relevant data for the user before, and as the) Tj
T*
(person opens Instagram, it displays the timeline. This decreases the latency rate to show the) Tj
T*
(timeline. Let’s take a look at the slides below to understand the problem and its solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Pull]\(./pull\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
