stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(What are the shortcomings of the pull approach?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Instagram is a read-heavy system. Many people don’t post any photos and instead just view) Tj
T*
(others’ posts. So, the calls we make to fetch the recent posts from every follower will usually) Tj
T*
(return nothing. Hence, we should keep in mind that it is not a write-heavy system and should) Tj
T*
(develop a possible solution to cater to it.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### The push approach) Tj
T*
(In a push approach, every user is responsible for pushing the content they posted to the) Tj
T*
(people’s timelines who are following them. In the previous approach, we pulled the post from) Tj
T*
(each follower, but in the current approach, we push the post to each follower.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now we only need to fetch the data that is pushed towards that particular user to generate the) Tj
T*
(timeline. The push approach has stopped a lot of requests that return empty results when) Tj
T*
(followed users have no post in a specified time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Push-based approach]\(./push.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What are the shortcomings of the push approach?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Consider an account that belongs to a celebrity, like Cristiano Ronaldo, who has over 400) Tj
T*
(million followers. So if he posts a photo or a video, we will push the links of the photo/video) Tj
T*
(to 400 million+ users, which is inefficient.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Hybrid approach) Tj
T*
(Let’s split our users into two categories:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Push-based users: The users who have a followers count of hundreds or thousands.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Pull-based users: The users who are celebrities and have followers count of a hundred) Tj
T*
(thousand or millions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The timeline service pulls the data from pull-based followers and adds it to the user’s) Tj
T*
(timeline. The push-based users push their posts to the timeline service of their followers so) Tj
T*
(the timeline service can add to the user’s timeline.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We have used the method which generates the timeline, but where do we store the timeline? We) Tj
T*
(store a user’s timeline against a userID in a key-value store. Upon request, we fetch the data) Tj
T*
(from the key-value store and show it to the user. The key is userID, while the value is) Tj
T*
(timeline content \(links to photos and videos\). Because the storage size of the value is often) Tj
T*
(limited to a few MegaBytes, we can store the timeline data in a blob and put the link to the) Tj
T*
(blob in the value of the key as we approach the size limit.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can add a new feature called story to our Instagram. In the story feature, the users can add) Tj
T*
(a photo that stays available for others to view for 24 hours only. We can do this by) Tj
T*
(maintaining an option in the table where we can store a story’s duration. We can set it to 24) Tj
T*
(hours, and the task scheduler deletes the entries whose time exceeds the 24 hours limit.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Finalized design) Tj
T*
(We’ll also use CDN \(content delivery network\) in our design. We can keep images and videos of) Tj
T*
(celebrities in CDN which make it easier for the followers to fetch them. The load balancer) Tj
T*
(first routes the read request to the nearest CDN, if the requested content is not available) Tj
T*
(there, then it forwards the request to the particular read application server \(see the “load) Tj
T*
(balancing chapter” for the details\). The CDN helps our system to be available to millions of) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
