(## Disaster recovery) Tj
T*
(Our proposed and detailed design does not cater to the situation of natural disasters. While we) Tj
T*
(have met other non-functional requirements, durability, fault-tolerance, and availability are) Tj
T*
(incomplete without a disaster recovery management plan. This section will explore some) Tj
T*
(mechanisms that provide resilience against disasters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The first and foremost approach of handling a disaster is frequent backups. The frequency of) Tj
T*
(backups depends on the size of the data. Daily backups are suitable for our design because we) Tj
T*
(can backup individual data stores and shards without any hassle. Of course, backups will be) Tj
T*
(stored at remote destinations because natural disasters can wipe out the entire facility at a) Tj
T*
(location.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Natural disasters: Disasters include large-scale floods, earthquakes, fires, and outages on) Tj
T*
(multiple backbone Internet connections taking off a whole data center or multiple data centers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Taking regular backup at remote locations is not enough. Quick restoration of backed-up) Tj
T*
(data in a timely manner completes a disaster recovery plan.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following are important questions for designing a disaster recovery plan:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- What data and systems are considered critical to recover from disasters?) Tj
T*
(- How fast is the restoration from the backup facility?) Tj
T*
(- Can all systems be recovered through backups?) Tj
T*
(- How can we deal with potential loss of data that we couldn’t replicate before the disaster) Tj
T*
(hit?) Tj
T*
(The illustration below shows a simple architecture of how a disaster recovery scheme works:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Disaster recovery management]\(./recovery.jpg\)) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
