T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(What would be considered a good approach for communication between different manager and worker) Tj
T*
(processes within the service hosts?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Two approaches are feasible for communication:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. UNIX sockets) Tj
T*
(2. TCP connections) Tj
T*
(Sockets \(Unix or TCP\) allow data streaming between sender and receiver with appropriate flow) Tj
T*
(control \(and congestion control in the case of TCP\). That means the sender and receiver can) Tj
T*
(send variable-size data in a decoupled fashion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Other interprocess communication techniques like shared memory may not be feasible because they) Tj
T*
(require estimating the size of the required memory segment, which makes the participants more) Tj
T*
(coupled. Also, it will not work across physical servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(So, we prefer sockets due to their high decoupling, flow control, and ability to work for both) Tj
T*
(single servers or over the network.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
(In our detailed design, we have employed Kafka to handle our view counter of answers. Why do) Tj
T*
(you think we made this decision?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Depending on the topic label, many users can view the response to a question at once. This can) Tj
T*
(choke the servers. Instantaneous update of the view counter is also not an important product) Tj
T*
(feature. Therefore, Kafka is suitable for handling these tasks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Quora handles these tasks in two minutes or less.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Nonetheless, we can use sharded counters as an effective solution to the view counter problem.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
