T*
(# Client-side Load Balancer for Twitter) Tj
T*
(## Introduction) Tj
T*
(In the previous lesson, we conceived the design of Twitter using a dedicated load balancer.) Tj
T*
(Although this method works, and we’ve employed it in other designs, it may not be the optimal) Tj
T*
(choice for Twitter. This is because Twitter offers a variety of services on a large scale,) Tj
T*
(using numerous instances and dedicated load-balancers are not a suitable choice for such) Tj
T*
(systems. To understand the concept better, let’s understand the history of Twitter’s design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Twitter’s design history) Tj
T*
(The initial design of Twitter included a monolithic \(Ruby on Rails\) application with a MySQL) Tj
T*
(database. As Twitter scaled, the number of services increased and the MySQL database was) Tj
T*
(sharded. A monolithic application design like this is a disaster because of the following) Tj
T*
(reasons:) Tj
T*
ET
