() Tj
T*
(- A large number of developers work on the same codebase, which makes it difficult to update) Tj
T*
(individual services.) Tj
T*
(- One service’s upgrade process may lead to the breaking of another.) Tj
T*
(- Hardware costs grow because a single machine performs numerous services.) Tj
T*
(- Recovery from failures is both time-consuming and complex.) Tj
T*
(With the way Twitter has evolved, the only way out was many microservices where each service) Tj
T*
(can be served through hundreds or thousands of instances.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Client-side load balancing) Tj
T*
(In a client-side load balancing technique, there’s no dedicated intermediate load-balancing) Tj
T*
(infrastructure between any two services with a large number of instances. A node requesting a) Tj
T*
(set of nodes for a service has a built-in load balancer. We refer to the requesting node or) Tj
T*
(service as a client. The client can use a variety of techniques to select a suitable instance) Tj
T*
(to request the service. The illustration below depicts the concept of client-side load) Tj
T*
(balancing. Every new arriving service or instance will register itself with a service registry) Tj
T*
(so that other services are aware of its existence) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([How client-side load balancing works]\(./clients.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the diagram above, Service A has to choose a relatively less-burdened instance of Service B.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, it will make use of the load balancer component inside it to choose the most) Tj
T*
(suitable instance of Service B. Using the same client-side load balancing method, Service B) Tj
T*
(will talk to other services. It’s clear that Service A is the client when it is calling Service) Tj
T*
(B, whereas Service B is the client when it is talking to Service C and D. As a result, there) Tj
T*
(will be no central entity doing load balancing. Instead, every node will do load balancing of) Tj
T*
(its own.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Advantages: Using client-side load-balancing has the following benefits.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Less hardware infrastructure/layers are required to do load balancing.) Tj
T*
(- Network latency will be reduced because of no intermediate hop.) Tj
T*
(- Client-side load balancers eliminate bandwidth bottlenecks. On the other hand, in a dedicated) Tj
T*
(load balancing layer, all requests go through a single machine that can choke if traffic) Tj
T*
(multiplies.) Tj
T*
(- Fewer points of failure in the overall system.) Tj
T*
(- There is no end users queue waiting for the resource \(server\) for the particular services) Tj
T*
(because many load balancers are routing the traffic. Eventually, it increases the quality of) Tj
T*
(experience \(QoE\).) Tj
T*
(Many real-world applications like Twitter, Yelp, Netflix, and others use client-side load) Tj
T*
(balancing. We’ll discuss the client-side load balancing techniques used by Twitter in the next) Tj
T*
(section.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Client-side load balancing in Twitter) Tj
T*
(Twitter uses a client-side load balancer referred to as deterministic aperture that is part of) Tj
T*
(a larger RPC framework called Finagle. Finagle is a protocol-agnostic, open-source, and) Tj
T*
(asynchronous RPC library.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Aperture specifies the number of sessions to be made with the calling service.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Protocol-agnostic systems are protocol-independent such that it enables the communication) Tj
T*
(between a variety of protocols.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Consider the following question. What does the client-side load balancer of Twitter) Tj
T*
(balance on? That is, what parameters are used to fairly balance the load across different) Tj
T*
(servers?) Tj
T*
ET
