(Twitter primarily uses two distributions to measure the effectiveness of a load balancer:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. The distribution of requests \(OSI layer 7\)) Tj
T*
(2. The distribution of sessions \(OSI layer 5\)) Tj
T*
(Although requests are the key metric, sessions are an important attribute for achieving a fair) Tj
T*
(distribution. Therefore, we’ll develop techniques for fair distribution of requests as well as) Tj
T*
(sessions. Let’s start with the simple technique of Power of Two Random Choices \(P2C\) for) Tj
T*
(request distribution first.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Request distribution using P2C) Tj
T*
(The P2C technique for request distribution gives uniform request distribution as long as) Tj
T*
(sessions are uniformly distributed. In P2C, the system randomly picks two unique instances) Tj
T*
(\(servers\) against each request, and selects the one with the least amount of load. Let’s look) Tj
T*
(at the illustration below to understand P2C.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Power of two random choices]\(./power.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(P2C is based on the simple idea that comparison between two randomly selected nodes provides) Tj
T*
(load distribution that is exponentially better than random selection) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(See “The power of two choices in randomized load balancing” by M. Mitzenmacher.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now that we’ve established how to distribute requests fairly, let’s explore different) Tj
T*
(techniques of session distribution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Session distribution) Tj
T*
(Solution 1: We’ll start our discussion with the Mesh topology. Using this approach, each client) Tj
T*
(\(load balancer\) starts a session with all of the given instances of a service. The illustration) Tj
T*
(below represents the concept of mesh topology.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Obviously, this method is fair because the sessions are evenly distributed. However, the even) Tj
T*
(distribution of sessions comes at a very high cost, especially when scaled to thousands of) Tj
T*
(servers. Apart from that, some unhealthy or stale sessions may lead to failures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Solution 2: To solve the problem of scalability with solution 1, Twitter devised another) Tj
T*
(solution called random aperture. This technique randomly chooses a subset of servers for) Tj
T*
(session establishment.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Random aperture]\(./random_aperture.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Of course, random selection will reduce the number of established sessions as we can see in the) Tj
T*
(diagram above. However, the question is, how many servers will be chosen randomly? This is not) Tj
T*
(an easy question to answer and the answer varies, depending on the request concurrency of a) Tj
T*
(client.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To answer the question above, Twitter installed a feedback controller inside the random) Tj
T*
(aperture that decides the subset size based on the client’s load. As a result, we’re able to) Tj
T*
(reduce the session load and ensure scalability. However, this solution isn’t fair. We can see) Tj
T*
(the imbalance in the illustration above such that Server 4 has no session whereas Server 0 has) Tj
T*
(three sessions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This imbalance creates problems such as idle servers, high load on a few servers, and so on.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, we need a solution that is both fair and scalable.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Solution 3: We learned from solution 2 that we can scale by subsetting the number of session) Tj
T*
(establishments. However, we failed to do the session distribution fairly. To resolve the) Tj
T*
(problem, Twitter came up with a deterministic aperture solution. The key benefits of this) Tj
T*
