(## Cache) Tj
T*
(As we know, caches help to reduce the latency and increase the throughput. Caching is mainly) Tj
T*
(utilized for storage \(heavy read traffic\), computation \(real-time stream processing and machine) Tj
T*
(learning\), and transient data \(rate limiters\). Twitter has been used as multi-tenant \(multiple) Tj
T*
(instances of an application have the shared environment\) Twitter Memcached \(Twemcache\) and) Tj
T*
(Redis \(Nighthawk\) clusters for caching. Due to some issues such as unexpected performance,) Tj
T*
(debugging difficulties, and other operational hassles in the existing cache system \(Twemcache) Tj
T*
(and Nighthawk\), Twitter has started to use the Pelikan cache. This cache gives high-throughput) Tj
T*
(and low latency. Pelikan uses many types of back-end servers such as the peliken_twemcache) Tj
T*
(replacement of Twitter’s Twemcache server, the peliken_slimcache replacement of Twitter’s) Tj
T*
(Memcached/Redis server, and so on. To dive deep, we have a detailed chapter on an In-memory) Tj
T*
(Cache. Let’s have a look at the below illustration representing the relationship of application) Tj
T*
(servers with distributed Pelikan cache.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Distributed Pelikan cache]\(./cache.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Pelikan also introduced another back-end server named Segcache, which is extremely) Tj
T*
(scalable and memory-efficient for small objects. Typically, the median size of the small object) Tj
T*
(is between 200 to 300 bytes in a large-scale application’s cache. Most solutions \(Memcache and) Tj
T*
(Redis\) have a high size \(56 bytes\) of metadata with each object. This signifies that the) Tj
T*
(metadata takes up more than one-third of the memory. Pelikan reduced metadata size per object) Tj
T*
(to 38 bytes. Segcache also received NSDI Community Award and is used as an experimental server) Tj
T*
(as of 2021.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Observability) Tj
T*
(Real-time applications use thousands of servers that provide multiple services. Monitoring) Tj
T*
(resources and their communication inside or outside the system is complex. We can use various) Tj
T*
(tools to monitor services’ health, such as providing alerts and support for multiple issues.) Tj
T*
(Our alert system notifies broken or degraded services triggered by the set metrics. We can also) Tj
T*
(use the dynamic configuration library that deploys and updates the configuration for multiple) Tj
T*
(services without restarting the service. This library leverages the Zookeeper \(discussed later\)) Tj
T*
(for the configuration as a source of truth. Twitter used the LonLens service that delivers) Tj
T*
(visualization and analytics of service logs. Later, it was replaced by Splunk Enterprise, a) Tj
T*
(central logging system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Tracing billions of requests is challenging in large-scale real-time applications. Twitter uses) Tj
T*
(Zipkin, a distributed tracing system, to trace each request \(spent time and request count\) for) Tj
T*
(multiple services. Zipkin selects a portion of all the requests and attaches a lightweight) Tj
T*
(trace identifier. This sampling also reduces the tracing overhead. Zipkin receives data through) Tj
T*
(the Scribe \(real-time log data aggregation\) server and stores it in the key-value stores with) Tj
T*
(few indexes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Observability]\(./observability.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Most real-time applications use Zookeeper to store critical data. It can also provide multiple) Tj
T*
(services such as distributed locking and leader election in the distribution system. Twitter) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
