BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(uses Zookeeper to store service registry, Manhattan clusters’ topology information, metadata,) Tj
T*
(and so on. Twitter also uses it for the leader election on the various systems.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Real-world complex problems) Tj
T*
(Twitter has millions of accounts, and some accounts \(public figures\) have millions of) Tj
T*
(followers. When these accounts post Tweets, millions of followers of the respective account) Tj
T*
(engage with their Tweets in a short time. The problem becomes big when the system handles the) Tj
T*
(billions of interactions \(such as views and likes\) on these Tweets. This problem is also known) Tj
T*
(as the heavy hitter problem. For this, we need millions of counters to count various operations) Tj
T*
(on Tweets.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Moreover, a single counter for each specific operation on the particular Tweet is not enough.) Tj
T*
(It’s challenging to handle millions of increments or decrements requests against a particular) Tj
T*
(Tweet in a single counter. Therefore, we need multiple distributed counters to manage burst) Tj
T*
(write requests \(increments or decrements\) against various interactions on celebrities’ Tweets.) Tj
T*
(Each counter has several shards working on different computational units. These distributed) Tj
T*
(counters are known as sharded counters. These counters also help in another real-time problem) Tj
T*
(named the Top-k problem. Let’s discuss an example of Twitter’s Top-k problems: trends and) Tj
T*
(timeline.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Trends: Twitter shows Top-k trends \(hashtags or keywords\) locally and globally. Here, “locally”) Tj
T*
(refers to when a topic or hashtag is used within the exact location where the requested user is) Tj
T*
(active. Alternatively, “globally” refers to when the particular hashtag is used worldwide.) Tj
T*
(There is a possibility that users from some regions are not using a specific hashtag in their) Tj
T*
(Tweets but get this hashtag in their trends timeline. Hashtags with the maximum frequency) Tj
T*
(\(counts\) become trends both locally and globally. Furthermore, Twitter shows various promoted) Tj
T*
(trends \(known as “paid trends”\) in specified regions under trends. The below slides represent) Tj
T*
(hashtags in the sliding window selected as Top-k trends over time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Trends]\(./topK\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Timeline: Twitter shows two types of timelines: home and user timelines. Here, we’ll discuss) Tj
T*
(the home timeline that displays a stream of Tweets posted by the followed accounts. The) Tj
T*
(decision to show Top-k Tweets in the timeline includes followed accounts Tweets and Tweets that) Tj
T*
(are liked or Retweeted by the followed accounts. There’s also another category of promoted) Tj
T*
(Tweets displayed in the home timeline.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Sharded counters solve the discussed problems efficiently. We can also place shards of the) Tj
T*
(specified counter near the user to reduce latency and increase overall performance like CDN.) Tj
T*
(Another benefit we can get is a frequent response to the users when they interact \(like or) Tj
T*
(view\) with a Tweet. The nearest servers managing various shards of the respective counters are) Tj
T*
(continuously updating the like or view counts with short refresh intervals. We should note,) Tj
T*
(however, that the near real-time counts will update on the Tweets with a long refresh interval.) Tj
T*
(The reason is the application server waits for multiple counts submitted by the various servers) Tj
T*
(placed in different regions. We have a detailed chapter on Sharded Counters, explaining how it) Tj
T*
(works in real-time applications.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## The complete design overview) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This section will discuss what happens in the back-end system when the end users generate) Tj
T*
(multiple requests. The following are the steps:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- First, end users get the address of the nearest load balancer from the local DNS.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Load balancer routes end users’ requests to the appropriate servers according to the) Tj
T*
(requested services. Here, we’ll discuss the Tweet, timeline, and search services.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  - Tweet service: When end users perform any operation, such as posting a Tweet or liking) Tj
T*
(other Tweets, the load balancers forward these requests to the server handling the Tweet) Tj
T*
(service. Consider an example where users post Tweets on Twitter using the /postTweet API. The) Tj
T*
ET
