stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
(### Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- Availability: Many people and organizations use Twitter to communicate time-sensitive) Tj
T*
(information \(service outage messages\). Therefore, our service must be highly available and have) Tj
T*
(a good uptime percentage.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Latency: The latency to show the most recent top Tweets in the home timeline could be a) Tj
T*
(little high. However, near real-time services, such as Tweet distribution to followers, must) Tj
T*
(have low latency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Scalability: The workload on Twitter is read-heavy, where we have many readers and relatively) Tj
T*
(few writers, which eventually requires the scalability of computational resources. Some) Tj
T*
(estimates suggest a 1:1000 write-to-read ratio for Twitter. Although the Tweet is limited to) Tj
T*
(280 characters, and the video clip is limited to 140s by default, we need high storage capacity) Tj
T*
(to store and deliver Tweets posted by public figures to their millions of followers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Reliability: All Tweets remain on Twitter. This means that Twitter never deletes its content.) Tj
T*
(So there should be a promising strategy to prevent the loss or damage of the uploaded content.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Consistency: There’s a possibility that a user on the east coast of the US does not get an) Tj
T*
(immediate status \(like, reply, and so on.\) update on the Tweet, which is liked or Retweeted by) Tj
T*
(a user on the west coast of the US. However, the user on the west coast of the US needs an) Tj
T*
(immediate status update on his like or reply. An effective technique is needed to offer rapid) Tj
T*
(feedback to the user \(who liked someone’s post\), then to other specified users in the same) Tj
T*
(region, and finally to all worldwide users linked to the Tweet.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Non-functional requirements of Twitter]\(./nfr.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Furthermore, we must identify which essential resources must be estimated in the Twitter) Tj
T*
(design. We used Twitter as an example in our Back-of-the-Envelope chapter, so we won’t repeat) Tj
T*
(that exercise here.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Building blocks we will use) Tj
T*
(Twitter’s design utilizes the following building blocks that we discussed in the initial) Tj
T*
(chapters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Building blocks used in Twitter design]\(./bb.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- DNS is the service that maps human-friendly Twitter domain names to machine-readable IP) Tj
T*
(addresses.) Tj
T*
(- Load balancers distribute the read/write requests among the respective services.) Tj
T*
(- Sequencers generate the unique IDs for the Tweets.) Tj
T*
(- Databases store the metadata of Tweets and users.) Tj
T*
(- Blob stores store the images and video clips attached with the Tweets.) Tj
T*
(- Key-value stores are used for multiple purposes such as indexing, identifying the specified) Tj
T*
(counter to update its value, identifying the Tweets of a particular user and many more.) Tj
T*
(- Pub-sub is used for real-time processing such as elimination of redundant data, organizing) Tj
T*
(data, and much more.) Tj
T*
(- Sharded counters help to handle the count of multiple features such as viewing, liking,) Tj
T*
(Retweeting, and so on,. of the accounts with millions of followers.) Tj
T*
(- A cache is used to store the most requested and recent data in RAM to give users a quick) Tj
T*
(response.) Tj
T*
(- CDN helps end users to access the data with low latency.) Tj
T*
(- Monitoring analyses all outgoing and incoming traffic, identifies the redundancy in the) Tj
T*
(storage system, figures out the failed node, and so on.) Tj
T*
(In the next lesson, we’ll discuss the design of the Twitter system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design Twitter/System Design Twitter/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: Twitter) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
