302 0 obj
<< /Length 4055 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
(## Twitter) Tj
T*
(Twitter is a free microblogging social network where registered users post messages called) Tj
T*
(“Tweets”. Users can also like, reply to, and retweet public tweets. Twitter has about 397) Tj
T*
(million users as of 2021 that is proliferating with time. One of the main reasons for its) Tj
T*
(popularity is the vast sharing of the breaking news on the platform. Another important reason) Tj
T*
(is that Twitter allows us to engage with and learn from people belonging to different) Tj
T*
(communities and cultures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([A user performs various operation on Twitter]\(./operations.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The illustration below shows the country-wise userbase of Twitter as of January 2022 \(source:) Tj
T*
(Statista\), where the US is taking the lead. Such statistics are important when we design our) Tj
T*
(infrastructure because it allows us to allocate more capacity to the users of a specific) Tj
T*
(region, and traffic served from near specific users.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Country wise userbase on Twitter]\(./stats.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## How will we design Twitter?) Tj
T*
(We’ll divide Twitter’s design into four sections:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Requirements: This lesson describes the functional and non-functional requirements of) Tj
T*
(Twitter. We’ll also estimate multiple aspects of Twitter, such as storage, bandwidth, and) Tj
T*
(computational resources.) Tj
T*
(2. Design: In this lesson, we’ll discuss the high-level design of Twitter in this lesson. We) Tj
T*
(also briefly explain the API design and identify the significant components of the Twitter) Tj
T*
(architecture. Moreover, we will discuss how to manage the Top-k problem, such as Tweets liked) Tj
T*
(or viewed by millions of users on Twitter.) Tj
T*
(3. Client-side load balancers: This lesson discusses how Twitter performs load balancing for) Tj
T*
(its microservices system to manage billions of requests between various services’ instances.) Tj
T*
(Furthermore, we also see why Twitter uses a customized load-balancing technique instead of) Tj
T*
(other commonly used approaches.) Tj
T*
(4. Quiz: Finally, we’ll reinforce major concepts of Twitter design with a quiz.) Tj
T*
(Let’s begin with defining Twitter’s requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design Typeahead Suggestion/Data Structure for Storing Prefixes/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Data Structure for Storing Prefixes) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## The trie data structure) Tj
T*
(Before we move on to the discussion of the detailed design of the typeahead suggestion system,) Tj
T*
(we must choose an efficient data structure to store the prefixes. Prefixes are the groups of) Tj
T*
(characters a user types. The issue we’re attempting to tackle is that we have many strings that) Tj
T*
(we need to store in a way that allows users to search for them using any prefix. Our service) Tj
T*
(suggests the next words that match the provided prefix. Let’s suppose our database contains the) Tj
T*
(phrases UNITED, UNIQUE, UNIVERSAL, and UNIVERSITY. Our system should suggest “UNIVERSAL” and) Tj
T*
(“UNIVERSITY” when the user types “UNIV.”) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There should be a method that can efficiently store our data and help us conduct fast searches) Tj
T*
(because we have to handle a lot of requests with minimal latency. We can’t rely on a database) Tj
T*
(for this because providing suggestions from the database takes longer as compared to reading) Tj
T*
(suggestions from the RAM. Therefore, we need to store our index in memory in an efficient data) Tj
T*
(structure. However, for durability and availability, this data is stored in the database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Recall from the Back-of-the-Envelope Calculation chapter that reading 1MB of data sequentially) Tj
T*
(from RAM takes 9 microseconds while from persistent storage like SSD takes 200 microseconds.) Tj
