(The riders and drivers are connected to the location manager service. This service shows the) Tj
T*
(nearby drivers to the riders when they open the application. This service also receives) Tj
T*
(location updates from the drivers every four seconds. The location of drivers is then) Tj
T*
(communicated to the quadtree map service to determine which segment the driver belongs to on a) Tj
T*
(map. The location manager saves the last location of all drivers in a database and saves the) Tj
T*
(route followed by the drivers on a trip.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Quadtree map service) Tj
T*
(The quadtree map service updates the location of the drivers. The main problem is how we deal) Tj
T*
(with finding nearby drivers efficiently.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll modify the solution discussed in the Yelp chapter according to our requirements. We used) Tj
T*
(quadtrees on Yelp to find the location. Quadtrees help to divide the map into segments. If the) Tj
T*
(number of drivers exceeds a certain limit, for example, 500, then we split that segment into) Tj
T*
(four more child nodes and divide the drivers into them.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each leaf node in quadtrees contains segments that can’t be divided further. We can use the) Tj
T*
(same quadtrees for finding the drivers. The most significant difference we have now is that our) Tj
T*
(quadtree wasn’t designed with regular upgrades in consideration. So, we have the following) Tj
T*
(issues with our dynamic segment solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We must update our data structures to point out that all active drivers update their location) Tj
T*
(every four seconds. It takes a longer amount of time to modify the quadtree whenever a driver’s) Tj
T*
(position changes. To identify the driver’s new location, we must first find a proper grid) Tj
T*
(depending on the driver’s previous position. If the new location doesn’t match the current) Tj
T*
(grid, we should remove the driver from the current grid and shift it to the correct grid. We) Tj
T*
(have to repartition the new grid if it exceeds the driver limit, which is the number of drivers) Tj
T*
(for each region that we set initially. Furthermore, our platform must tell both the driver and) Tj
T*
(the rider, of the car’s current location while the ride is in progress.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To overcome the above problem, we can use a hash table to store the latest position of the) Tj
T*
(drivers and update our quadtree occasionally, say after 10–15 seconds. We can update the) Tj
T*
(driver’s location in the quadtree around every 15 seconds instead of four seconds, and we use a) Tj
T*
(hash table that updates every four seconds and reflects the drivers’ latest location. By doing) Tj
T*
(this, we use fewer resources and time.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A quadtree is a tree data structure in which each internal node has exactly four children.) Tj
T*
(Quadtrees are the two-dimensional analog of octrees and are most often used to partition a two-) Tj
T*
(dimensional space by recursively subdividing it into four quadrants or regions. The data) Tj
T*
(associated with a leaf cell varies by application, but the leaf cell represents a “unit of) Tj
T*
(interesting spatial information”. Source: Wikipedia) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Updating driver's location]\(./driver's_location.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Request vehicle) Tj
T*
(The rider contacts the request vehicle service to request a ride. The rider adds the drop-off) Tj
T*
(location here. The request vehicle service then communicates with the find driver service to) Tj
T*
(book a vehicle and get the details of the vehicle using the location manager service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Find driver) Tj
T*
(The find driver service finds the driver who can complete the trip. It sends the information of) Tj
T*
(the selected driver and the trip information back to the request vehicle service to communicate) Tj
T*
(the details to the rider. The find driver service also contacts the trip manager to manage the) Tj
T*
(trip information.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Trip manager) Tj
T*
(The trip manager service manages all the trip-related tasks. It creates a trip in the database) Tj
T*
(and stores all the information of the trip in the database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
