(### ETA service) Tj
T*
(The ETA service deals with the estimated time of arrival. It shows riders the pickup ETA when) Tj
T*
(their trip is scheduled. This service considers factors such as route and traffic. The two) Tj
T*
(basic components of predicting an ETA given an origin and destination on a road network are the) Tj
T*
(following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Calculate the shortest route from origin to destination.) Tj
T*
(- Compute the time required to travel the route.) Tj
T*
(The whole road network is represented as a graph. Intersections are represented by nodes, while) Tj
T*
(edges represent road segments. The graph also depicts one-way streets, turn limitations, and) Tj
T*
(speed limits.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Graph representation]\(./presentation.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To identify the shortest path between source and destination, we can utilize routing algorithms) Tj
T*
(such as Dijkstra’s algorithm. However, Dijkstra, or any other algorithm that operates on top of) Tj
T*
(an unprocessed graph, is quite slow for such a system. Therefore, this method is impractical at) Tj
T*
(the scale at which these ride-hailing platforms operate.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To resolve these issues, we can split the whole graph into partitions. We preprocess the) Tj
T*
(optimum path inside partitions using contraction hierarchies and deal with just the partition) Tj
T*
(boundaries. This strategy can considerably reduce the time complexity since it partitions the) Tj
T*
(graph into layers of tiny cells that are largely independent of one another. The preprocessing) Tj
T*
(stage is executed in parallel in the partitions when necessary to increase speed. In the) Tj
T*
(illustration below, all the partitions process the best route in parallel. For example, if each) Tj
T*
(partition takes one second to find the path, we can have the complete path in one second since) Tj
T*
(all partitions work in parallel.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Contraction hierarchies is a speed-up method optimized to exploit the properties of graphs) Tj
T*
(representing road networks. The speed-up is achieved by creating shortcuts in a preprocessing) Tj
T*
(phase, which are then used during a shortest-path query to skip over unimportant vertices.) Tj
T*
(Source: Wikipedia) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Partitioning the graph]\(./partitions.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Once we determine the best route, we calculate the expected time to travel the road segment) Tj
T*
(while accounting for traffic. The traffic data will be the edge weights between the nodes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### DeepETA) Tj
T*
(We use a machine learning component named DeepETA to deliver an immediate improvement to) Tj
T*
(metrics in production. It establishes a model foundation that can be reused for multiple) Tj
T*
(consumer use cases.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We also use a routing engine that uses real-time traffic information and map data to predict an) Tj
T*
(ETA to traverse the best path between the source and the destination. We use a post-processing) Tj
T*
(ML model that considers spatial and temporal parameters, such as the source, destination, time) Tj
T*
(of the request, and knowledge about real-time traffic, to forecast the ETA residual.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([DeepETA]\(./deepeta.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Database) Tj
T*
(Let’s select the database according to our requirements:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The database must be able to scale horizontally. There are new customers and drivers on a) Tj
T*
(regular basis, so we should be able to add more storage without any problems.) Tj
T*
(The database should handle a large number of reads and writes because the location of drivers) Tj
T*
(is updated every four seconds.) Tj
T*
(Our system should never be down.) Tj
T*
