(# Evaluation of Uber’s Design) Tj
T*
(## Fulfill non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(Let’s evaluate how our system fulfills the non-functional requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Availability) Tj
T*
(Our system is highly available. We used WebSocket servers. If a user gets disconnected, the) Tj
T*
(session is recreated via a load balancer with a different server. We’ve used multiple replicas) Tj
T*
(of our databases with a primary-secondary replication model. We have the Cassandra database,) Tj
T*
(which provides highly available services and no single point of failure. We used a CDN, cache,) Tj
T*
(and load balancers, which increase the availability of our system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Scalability) Tj
T*
(Our system is highly scalable. We used many independent services so that we can scale these) Tj
T*
(services horizontally, independent of each other as per our needs. We used quadtrees for) Tj
T*
(searching by dividing the map into smaller segments, which shortens our search space. We used a) Tj
T*
(CDN, which increases the capacity to handle more users. We also used a NoSQL database,) Tj
T*
(Cassandra, which is horizontally scalable. Additionally, we used load balancers, which improve) Tj
T*
(speed by distributing read workload among different servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Reliability) Tj
T*
(Our system is highly reliable. The trip can continue even if the rider’s or driver’s connection) Tj
T*
(is broken. This is achieved by using their phones as local storage. The use of multiple) Tj
T*
(WebSocket servers ensures smooth, nearly real-time operations. If any of the servers fail, the) Tj
T*
