(processor then takes that information from Kafka, processes it, and sends it as intent to) Tj
T*
(Kafka. From that, the order processor again processes and contacts the PSP. The order processor) Tj
T*
(then takes the answer from PSP and transmits it to Kafka as a result. The result is then saved) Tj
T*
(by the order writer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The key capabilities of Kafka that the payment service uses are the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- It works like message queues to publish and subscribe to message queues.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- It stores the records in a way that is fault tolerant.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- It processes the payment records asynchronously.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Fraud detection) Tj
T*
(Fraud detection is a critical operational component of our design. Payment fraud losses are) Tj
T*
(calculated as a percentage of gross amounts. Despite fraud activities accounting for a tiny) Tj
T*
(portion of gross bookings, these losses considerably influence earnings. Moreover, if malicious) Tj
T*
(behavior isn’t promptly detected and handled, it may be further leveraged, which results in) Tj
T*
(significant losses for the business. Earlier, we discussed the risk engine that detects fraud) Tj
T*
(before the trip starts. Here, we’ll focus on the fraud detection that happens during trips or) Tj
T*
(at the end of the trips.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Fraud detection is challenging because many instances of fraud are like detecting zero-day) Tj
T*
(security bugs. Therefore, our system needs intelligence to detect anomalies, and it also needs) Tj
T*
(accountability for automated decisions in the form of human-led audits.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The activities that are considered fraudulent by Uber are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The following list of eight fraudulent activities is taken from:) Tj
T*
(https://www.uber.com/gb/en/drive/driver-app/fraud-activities/) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver deliberately increases the time or distance of a trip in a dishonest manner—for) Tj
T*
(example, if they take a much longer route than necessary to the rider’s destination.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver manipulates the GPS data or uses fake location GPS apps.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver confirms trips with no intention of completing them, forcing riders to cancel.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver creates false driver or rider accounts for fraudulent purposes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver provides incorrect or inaccurate information about themselves when opening their) Tj
T*
(account.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver drives a vehicle that hasn’t been approved by Uber.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver claims fraudulent fees or charges, such as unwarranted cleaning fees.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A driver willfully confirms or completes fraudulent trips.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### RADAR) Tj
T*
(Uber introduced RADAR, a human-assisted AI fraud detection and mitigation solution to handle) Tj
T*
(the discussed scenarios of fraud. The RADAR system detects fraud by analyzing the activity time) Tj
T*
(series of the payment system. It then generates a rule for it and stops it from further) Tj
T*
(processing. This proactive approach can help to detect unseen fraud in real time. This) Tj
T*
(detection model also uses human knowledge for continuous improvements. Here, we’ll briefly) Tj
T*
(discuss how RADAR works:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The RADAR fraud detection solution]\(./radar.jpg\)) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
