(### Bandwidth estimation) Tj
T*
(We’ll only consider drivers’ location updates and trip data for bandwidth calculation since) Tj
T*
(other factors don’t require significant bandwidth. We have 20 million daily rides, which means) Tj
T*
(we have approximately 232 trips per second.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(20000000/86400 ≈232 trips per second) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now, each trip takes around 100 Bytes. So, it takes around 23 KB per second of bandwidth.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(232×100 = 23 KB×8 = 185 kbps) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As already stated, the driver’s location is updated every four seconds. If we receive the) Tj
T*
(driver’s ID \(3 Bytes\) and location \(16 Bytes\), our application will take the following) Tj
T*
(bandwidth:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3M active drivers×\(3+16\)B= 57MB×8 = 456/4  =114 Mbps) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(These bandwidth requirements are modest because we didn’t include bandwidth needs for maps and) Tj
T*
(other components that are present in the real Uber service.) Tj
T*
