T*
(## YouTube search) Tj
T*
(Since YouTube is one of the most visited websites, a large number of users will be using the) Tj
T*
(search feature. Even though we have covered a building block on distributed search, we’ll) Tj
T*
(provide a basic overview of how search inside the YouTube system will work.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each new video uploaded to YouTube will be processed for data extraction. We can use a JSON) Tj
T*
(file to store extracted data, which includes the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Title of the video.) Tj
T*
(- Channel name.) Tj
T*
(- Description of the video.) Tj
T*
(- The content of the video, possibly extracted from the transcripts.) Tj
T*
(- Video length.) Tj
T*
(- Categories.) Tj
T*
(Each of the JSON files can be referred to as a document. Next, keywords will be extracted from) Tj
T*
(the documents and stored in a key-value store. The key in the key-value store will hold all the) Tj
T*
(keywords searched by the users, while the value in the key-value store will contain the) Tj
T*
(occurrence of each key, its frequency, and the location of the occurrence in the different) Tj
T*
(documents. When a user searches for a keyword, the videos with the most relevant keywords will) Tj
T*
(be returned.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([An abstraction of how YouTube search works]\(./search.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The approach above is simplistic, and the relevance of keywords is not the only factor) Tj
T*
(affecting search in YouTube. In reality, a number of other factors will matter. The processing) Tj
T*
(engine will improve the search results by filtering and ranking videos. It will make use of) Tj
T*
(other factors like view count, the watch time of videos, and the context, along with the) Tj
T*
(history of the user, to improve search results.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design Youtube/Evaluation of YouTube's Design/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Evaluation of YouTube's Design) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Fulfilling requirements) Tj
T*
(Our proposed design needs to fulfill the requirements we mentioned in the previous lessons. Our) Tj
T*
(main requirements are smooth streaming \(low latency\), availability, and reliability. Let’s) Tj
T*
(discuss them one by one.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Low latency/Smooth streaming can be achieved through these strategies:) Tj
T*
(  - Geographically distributed cache servers at the ISP level to keep the most viewed content.) Tj
T*
(  - Choosing appropriate storage systems for different types of data. For example, we’ll can) Tj
T*
(use Bigtable for thumbnails, blob storage for videos, and so on.) Tj
T*
(  - Using caching at various layers via a distributed cache management system.) Tj
T*
(  - Utilizing content delivery networks \(CDNs\) that make heavy use of caching and mostly serve) Tj
T*
(videos out of memory. A CDN deploys its services in close vicinity to the end users for low-) Tj
T*
(latency services.) Tj
T*
(2. Scalability: We’ve taken various steps to ensure scalability in our design as depicted in) Tj
T*
(the table below. The horizontal scalability of web and application servers will not be a) Tj
T*
(problem as the users grow. However, MySQL storage cannot scale beyond a certain point. As we’ll) Tj
T*
(see in the coming sections, that may require some restructuring.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Availablity: The system can be made available through redundancy by replicating data to as) Tj
T*
(many servers as possible to avoid a single point of failure. Replicating data across data) Tj
T*
(centers will ensure high availability, even if an entire data center fails because of power or) Tj
T*
(network issues. Furthermore, local load balancers can exclude any dead servers, and global load) Tj
T*
(balancers can steer traffic to a different region if the need arises.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. Reliability: YouTube’s system can be made reliable by using data partitioning and fault-) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
395 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 394 0 R >>
endobj
396 0 obj
<< /Length 3850 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(tolerance techniques. Through data partitioning, the non-availability of one type of data will) Tj
T*
(not affect others. We can use redundant hardware and software components for fault tolerance.) Tj
T*
(Furthermore, we can use the heartbeat protocol to monitor the health of servers and omit) Tj
T*
(servers that are faulty and erroneous. We can use a variant of consistent hashing to add or) Tj
T*
(remove servers seamlessly and reduce the burden on specific servers in case of non-uniform) Tj
T*
(load.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The heartbeat protocol is a way of identifying failures in distributed systems. Using this) Tj
T*
(protocol, every node in a cluster periodically reports its health to a monitoring service.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Variant consistent hashing: Mirrokni, Vahab, Mikkel Thorup, and Morteza Zadimoghaddam.) Tj
T*
(“Consistent hashing with bounded loads.” Proceedings of the Twenty-Ninth Annual ACM-SIAM) Tj
T*
(Symposium on Discrete Algorithms. Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics, 2018.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Isn’t the load balancer a single point of failure \(SPOF\)?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Just like with servers, we can use multiple load balancers. Users can be randomly forwarded to) Tj
T*
(different load balancers from the Domain Name System \(DNS\).) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([How YouTube achieves scalability, availability, and good performance]\(./req.jpg\)) Tj
T*
(## Trade-offs) Tj
T*
(How YouTube achieves scalability, availability, and good performance) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Consistency) Tj
T*
(Our solution prefers high availability and low latency. However, strong consistency can take a) Tj
T*
(hit because of high availability \(see the CAP theorem\). Nonetheless, for a system like YouTube,) Tj
T*
(we can afford to let go of strong consistency. This is because we don’t need to show a) Tj
T*
(consistent feed to all the users. For example, different users subscribed to the same channel) Tj
T*
(may not see a newly uploaded video at the same time. It’s important to mention that we’ll) Tj
T*
(maintain strong consistency of user data. This is another reason why we’ve decoupled user data) Tj
T*
(from video metadata.) Tj
T*
(### Distributed cache) Tj
T*
(We prefer a distributed cache over a centralized cache in our YouTube design. This is because) Tj
T*
(the factors of scalability, availability, and fault-tolerance, which are needed to run YouTube,) Tj
T*
(require a cache that is not a single point of failure. This is why we use a distributed cache.) Tj
T*
(Since YouTube mostly serves static content \(thumbnails and videos\), Memcached is a good choice) Tj
T*
(because it is open source and uses the popular Least Recently Used \(LRU\) algorithm. Since) Tj
T*
(YouTube video access patterns are long-tailed, LRU-like algorithms are suitable for such data) Tj
T*
(sets.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Bigtable versus MySQL) Tj
T*
(Another interesting aspect of our design is the use of different storage technologies for) Tj
T*
(different data sets. Why did we choose MySQL and Bigtable?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The primary reason for the choice is performance and flexibility. The number of users in) Tj
T*
(YouTube may not scale as much as the number of videos and thumbnails do. Moreover, we require) Tj
T*
(storing the user and metadata in structured form for convenient searching. Therefore, MySQL is) Tj
T*
(a suitable choice for such cases.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(However, the number of videos uploaded and the thumbnails for each video would be very large in) Tj
T*
(number. Scalability needs would force us to use a custom or NoSQL type of design for that) Tj
T*
(storage. One could use alternatives to GFS and Bigtable, such as HDFS and Cassandra.) Tj
T*
(### Public versus private CDN) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
397 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 396 0 R >>
endobj
398 0 obj
<< /Length 3646 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Our design relies on CDNs for low latency serving of the content. However, CDNs can be private) Tj
T*
(or public. YouTube can choose between any one of the two options.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This choice is more of a cost issue than a design issue. However, for areas where there is) Tj
T*
(little traffic, YouTube can use the public CDN because of the following reasons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Setting up a private CDN will require a lot of CAPEX.) Tj
T*
(2. For rather little viral traffic in certain regions, there will not be enough time to set up) Tj
T*
(a new CDN.) Tj
T*
(3. There may not be enough users to sustain the business.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Private CDN: A CDN infrastructure that is for internal use only.) Tj
T*
(Public CDN: A CDN infrastructure that is available to the public on a pay-as-you-use basis.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(However, YouTube can consider building its own CDN if the number of users is too high, since) Tj
T*
(public CDNs can prove to be expensive if the traffic is high. Private CDNs can also be) Tj
T*
(optimized for internal usage to better serve customers.) Tj
T*
(### Duplicate videos) Tj
T*
(The current YouTube design doesn’t handle duplicate videos that have been uploaded by a user or) Tj
T*
(spammers. Duplicated videos take extra space, which leads to a trade-off. As a result, we) Tj
T*
(either waste storage space or face an additional complexity to the upload process for handling) Tj
T*
(duplicate videos.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s perform some calculations to resolve this problem. Assume that 50 out of 500 hours of) Tj
T*
(videos uploaded to YouTube are duplicates. Considering that one minute of video requires 6 MB) Tj
T*
(of storage space, the duplicated content will take up the following storage space:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(\(50 x 60\) minutes x 6 MB/min = 18 GB) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(If we avoid video duplication, we can save up to 9.5 petabytes of storage space in a year. The) Tj
T*
(calculations are as follows:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(18 GB/min x \(60 x 24 x 365\) total minutes in an year = 9.5 Peta Bytes) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Storage space being wasted, and other computational costs are not the only issues with) Tj
T*
(duplicate videos. An important aspect of duplicate videos is the copyright issue. No content) Tj
T*
(creator would want their content plagiarized. Therefore, it’s plausible to add the complexity) Tj
T*
(of handling duplicate videos to the design of YouTube.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Duplication can be solved with simple techniques like locality-sensitive hashing. However,) Tj
T*
(there can be complex techniques like Block Matching Algorithms \(BMAs\) and phase correlation to) Tj
T*
(find duplications. Implementing this solution can be quite complex in a huge database of) Tj
T*
(videos. We may have to use technologies like artificial intelligence \(AI\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Future scaling) Tj
T*
(So far, we’ve focused on the design and analysis of the proposed design for YouTube. In) Tj
T*
(reality, the design of YouTube is quite complex and requires advanced systems. In this section,) Tj
T*
(we’ll focus on the pragmatic structure of data stores and the web server.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll begin our discussion with some limitations in terms of scaling YouTube. In particular,) Tj
T*
(we’ll consider what design changes we’ll have to make if the traffic load to our service goes) Tj
T*
(up by, say, a few folds.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We already know that we’ll have to scale our existing infrastructure, which includes the below) Tj
T*
(elements:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Web servers) Tj
T*
(- Application servers) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
