411 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 410 0 R >>
endobj
412 0 obj
<< /Length 3570 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(- Scalability: As the number of users grows, these issues should not become bottlenecks:) Tj
T*
(storage for uploading content, the bandwidth required for simultaneous viewing, and the number) Tj
T*
(of concurrent user requests should not overwhelm our application/web server.) Tj
T*
(- Good performance: A smooth streaming experience leads to better performance overall.) Tj
T*
(- Reliability: Content uploaded to the system should not be lost or damaged.) Tj
T*
(We don’t require strong consistency for YouTube’s design. Consider an example where a creator) Tj
T*
(uploads a video. Not all users subscribed to the creator’s channel should immediately get the) Tj
T*
(notification for uploaded content.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To summarize, the functional requirements are the features and functionalities that the user) Tj
T*
(will get, whereas the non-functional requirements are the expectations in terms of performance) Tj
T*
(from the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Based on the requirements, we’ll estimate the required resources and design of our system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Resource estimation) Tj
T*
(Estimation requires the identification of important resources that we’ll need in the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Hundreds of minutes of video content get uploaded to YouTube every minute. Also, a large number) Tj
T*
(of users will be streaming content at the same time, which means that the following resources) Tj
T*
(will be required:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Storage resources will be needed to store uploaded and processed content.) Tj
T*
(- A large number of requests can be handled by doing concurrent processing. This means) Tj
T*
(web/application servers should be in place to serve these users.) Tj
T*
(- Both upload and download bandwidth will be required to serve millions of users.) Tj
T*
(To convert the above resources into actual numbers, we assume the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Total number of YouTube users: 1.5 billion.) Tj
T*
(- Active daily users \(who watch or upload videos\): 500 million.) Tj
T*
(- Average length of a video: 5 minutes.) Tj
T*
(- Size of an average \(5 minute-long\) video before processing/encoding \(compression, format) Tj
T*
(changes, and so on\): 600 MB.) Tj
T*
(- Size of an average video after encoding \(using different algorithms for different resolutions) Tj
T*
(like MPEG-4 and VP9\): 30 MB.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Storage estimation) Tj
T*
(To find the storage needs of YouTube, we have to estimate the total number of videos and the) Tj
T*
(length of each video uploaded to YouTube per minute. Let’s consider that 500 hours worth of) Tj
T*
(content is uploaded to YouTube in one minute. Since each video of 30 MB is 5 minutes long, we) Tj
T*
(require 30/5 = 6 MB to store 1 minute of video.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s put this in a formula by assuming the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total_{storage} : Total storage requirement.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total_{upload/min} : Total content uploaded \(in minutes\) per minute.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Example: 500 hours worth of video is uploaded in one minute.) Tj
T*
(Storage_{min} : Storage required for each minute of content) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Then, the following formula is used to compute the storage:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total_{storage} = Total_{upload/min} x Storage_{min}) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Below is a calculator to help us estimate our required resources. We’ll look first at the) Tj
T*
(storage required to persist 500 hours of content uploaded per minute, where each minute of) Tj
T*
