(1. Question: It is possible to quantify availability by adding numbers like 99.99% availability) Tj
T*
(or 99.999% availability. In that case, what design changes will be required?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This is a hard question. In reality, such numbers are part of a service’s SLA, and they are) Tj
T*
(generated from models or long-term empirical studies. While it is good to know how the above) Tj
T*
(numbers are obtained and how organizations use monitoring to keep availability high, it might) Tj
T*
(be a better strategy to discuss the fault tolerance of the system—what will happen if there are) Tj
T*
(software faults, server failures, full data center failures, and so on. If the system is) Tj
T*
(resilient against faults, that implies the system will have good availability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Question: We assumed some reasonable numbers to come up with broad resource requirements.) Tj
T*
(For example, we said the average length of a video is five minutes. In this case, are we) Tj
T*
(designing for average behavior? What will happen to users who don’t follow the average profile?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A possible answer could be that the above number will likely change over time. Our system) Tj
T*
(design should be horizontally scalable so that with increasing users, the system keeps) Tj
T*
(functioning adequately. Practically, systems might not scale when some aspect of the system) Tj
T*
(increases by an order of magnitude. When some aspects of a system increase by an order of) Tj
T*
(magnitude \(for example, 10x\), we usually need a new, different design. Cost points of designing) Tj
T*
(10x and 100x scales are very different.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Question: Why didn’t we discuss and estimate resources for video comments and likes?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Concurrent users’ comments on videos are roughly at the same complexity as designing a) Tj
T*
(messaging system. We’ll discuss that problem in System Design: WhatsApp.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. Question: How to manage unexpected spikes in system load?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A possible answer is that because our design is horizontally scalable, we can shed some load on) Tj
T*
(the public cloud due to its elasticity features. However, public clouds are not infinitely) Tj
T*
(scalable. They often need a priori business contracts that might put a limit on maximum,) Tj
T*
(concurrently allowed resource use at different data centers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. Question: How will we deploy a global network to connect data centers and CDN sites?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In practice, YouTube uses Google’s network, which is built for that purpose and peers with many) Tj
T*
(ISPs of the world. It is a fascinating field of study that we have left outside this course for) Tj
T*
(further review.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6. Question: Why isn’t there more detail on audio/video encoding?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There are many audio/video encoding choices, many publicly known and some proprietary. Due to) Tj
T*
(excessive redundancy in multimedia content, encoding is often able to reduce a huge raw format) Tj
T*
(content to a much smaller size \(for example, from 600 MB to 30 MB\). We have left the details of) Tj
T*
(such encoding algorithms to you if you’re interested in further exploration.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(7. Question: Cant we use specialized hardware \(or accelerators like GPUs\) to speed up some) Tj
T*
(aspects of the YouTube computation?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(When we estimated the number of servers, we assumed that any server could fulfill any required) Tj
T*
(functionality. In reality, with the slowing of Moore’s law, we have special-purpose hardware) Tj
T*
(available \(for example, hardware encoders/decoders, machine-learning accelerators like Tensor) Tj
T*
(Processing Units, and many more\). All such platforms need their own courses to do justice to) Tj
T*
(the content. So, we avoided that discussion in this design problem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Moore’s law was presented by Gordon Moore in 1965, who stated that the number of transistors in) Tj
T*
(an integrated circuit \(IC\) doubles every year.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
