() Tj
T*
(## Searching) Tj
T*
(From Google Maps, we were able to connect segments and meet the scalability challenge to) Tj
T*
(process a large graph efficiently. The graph of the world had numerous nodes and vertices, and) Tj
T*
(traversing them was time-consuming. Therefore, we divided the whole world into smaller) Tj
T*
(segments/subgraphs to process and query them simultaneously. The segmentation helps us improve) Tj
T*
(the scalability of the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each segment will be of the size 5×5 miles and will contain a list of places that exist within) Tj
T*
(it. We only search a few segments to locate destinations that are close by. We can use a given) Tj
T*
(location and defined radius to find all the nearby segments and identify sites that are close.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Partitioning the globe into small segments, where each segment has four coordinates \(lat,) Tj
T*
(long\) and all segments hold the coordinates of different places]\(./segment.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(How will we find nearby places if we create a table for storing all places?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(We can make a table of places that have Place_ID as a unique ID and store each place in it. The) Tj
T*
(longitude and latitude columns help us specify the exact location. Indexing both of these) Tj
T*
(columns can help us fetch data efficiently. We can specify each location as a pair of latitude) Tj
T*
(\(M\) and longitude \(N\). We can also search for a place within a given radius of R by finding all) Tj
T*
(the places between the latitude M-R and M+R and the longitude N-R and N+R. We can apply) Tj
T*
(Dijkstra’s algorithm to find the distance between two points.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
(How efficient will our searching be when based on the table?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(We can have multiple lists of places within \(M-R, M+R\) and \(N-R, N+R\). It’ll be a challenge to) Tj
T*
(handle concurrent requests. When queries that are used to fetch the places will be coming at a) Tj
T*
(high rate for different segments, the response time and performance will be affected.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can store all the places in a table and uniquely identify a segment by having a segment_ID.) Tj
T*
(We can index each segment in the database. Now, we have limited the number of segments we need) Tj
T*
(to search, so the query will be optimized and return results quickly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Improve data fetching) Tj
T*
(We use a key-value store for quick access to places in the segments. The key is the segment_ID,) Tj
T*
(while the value contains the list of places in that segment. Let’s estimate how much space we) Tj
T*
(need to store the indexes.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(We can usually store a few MBs in the value of the key-value store. If we assume that each) Tj
T*
(segment has 500 places then it will take up 500×1296 Bytes=0.648MB, and we can easily store it) Tj
T*
(as a value.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The total area of the earth is around 200 million square miles, and the land surface area is) Tj
T*
(about 60 million square miles. If we consider our radius to be ten miles, then we’ll have 6) Tj
T*
(million segments. An 8-Byte identifier will identify each segment.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s calculate how much memory we need.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Total Area of the Earth \(Million Square Miles\)      60) Tj
T*
(Search Radius \(Miles\)                               10) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
