(Number of Segments \(Millions\)                        6) Tj
T*
(Segment_ID \(Bytes\)                                   8) Tj
T*
(Place_ID \(Bytes\)                                     8) Tj
T*
(Number of Places \(Millions\)                        500) Tj
T*
(Total Space \(TB\)                                  4.048) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Search using segments) Tj
T*
(A user may select a radius for searching places that aren’t present in a single segment. So, we) Tj
T*
(need to combine multiple segments by connecting the segments to find locations within the) Tj
T*
(specified radius—say, five miles.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(First, we constrain the number of segments. This reduces the graph size and makes the searching) Tj
T*
(process optimizable. Then, we identify all the relevant locations, compute the distance from) Tj
T*
(the searching point, and show it to the user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Searching for locations using segments]\(./searching.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Can you identify a problem with the current approach?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Our locations are not evenly distributed across segments, and this approach may still take a) Tj
T*
(long time to run on segments with a lot of places. For example, in a city like New York, we can) Tj
T*
(have lots of places even within a small radius because it is a densely-populated area. And for) Tj
T*
(less populated areas, the same radius might not have enough places, and we might need to expand) Tj
T*
(our radius to find more.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Dynamic segments) Tj
T*
(We solve the problem of uneven distribution of places in a segment by dynamically sizing the) Tj
T*
(segments. We do this by focusing on the number of places. We split a segment into four more) Tj
T*
(segments if the number of places reaches a certain limit. We assume 500 places as our limit.) Tj
T*
(While using this approach, we need to decide on the following questions:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(How will we map the segments?) Tj
T*
(How will we connect to other segments?) Tj
T*
(We use a QuadTree to manage our segments. Each node contains the information of a segment. If) Tj
T*
(the number of places exceeds 500, then we split that segment into four more child nodes and) Tj
T*
(divide the places between them. In this way, the leaf nodes will be those segments that can’t) Tj
T*
(be broken down any further. Each leaf node will have a list of places in it too.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A quadtree is a tree data structure in which each internal node has exactly four children.) Tj
T*
(Quadtrees are the two-dimensional analog of octrees and are most often used to partition a two-) Tj
T*
(dimensional space by recursively subdividing it into four quadrants or regions. The data) Tj
T*
(associated with a leaf cell varies by application, but the leaf cell represents a “unit of) Tj
T*
(interesting spatial information”. Source: Wikipedia) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Search using a QuadTree) Tj
T*
(We start searching from the root node and continue to visit the nodes to find our desired) Tj
T*
(segment. We check every node to see if it has more child nodes. If a node has no more children,) Tj
T*
(then we stop our search because that node is the required one. We also connect each child node) Tj
T*
(with its neighboring nodes with a doubly-linked list. All the child nodes of all the parents) Tj
T*
(nodes are connected through the doubly-linked list. This list allows us to find the neighboring) Tj
T*
(segments when we can move forward and backward as per our requirement. After identifying the) Tj
T*
(segments, we have the required PlaceID values of the places and we can search our database to) Tj
T*
(find more details on them.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
