(**Question**) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Is there an alternative approach to find the neighboring segments?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Answer**) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can use the pointers of parent nodes to find the neighboring segments. In each node, we can) Tj
T*
(keep a pointer that points towards the parent node. Every parent node has pointers to its) Tj
T*
(children nodes, so we can use those to find the adjacent leaf nodes. We can extend our search) Tj
T*
(by going up through the parent pointers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the following illustration, node F can find its neighboring node, G, by first going to the) Tj
T*
(parent node, B, and then to G.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Tree]\(./traverse.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following slides show how the process of searching for a place works. If a node has the) Tj
T*
(places we need, we stop there. Otherwise, we explore more nodes until we reach our search) Tj
T*
(radius. After finding the node, we query the database for information related to the places and) Tj
T*
(return the desired ones.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following slides show how the process of searching for a place works. If a node has the) Tj
T*
(places we need, we stop there. Otherwise, we explore more nodes until we reach our search) Tj
T*
(radius. After finding the node, we query the database for information related to the places and) Tj
T*
(return the desired ones.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([steps]\(./quadtree\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Storage space estimation for QuadTrees) Tj
T*
(Let’s calculate the storage we need for keeping QuadTrees:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- We store the PlaceID, Latitude, and Longitude for each place in the node. Each of these) Tj
T*
(values is of the size 8 Bytes. For 500 million places, we need the following amount of storage) Tj
T*
(for all places:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  - \(8+8+8\)×10^6×500=12GB) Tj
T*
(- We’ll have 500 places in a single node. So, for 500 million places, we’ll need 500 M/500 =1M) Tj
T*
(leaf nodes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The QuadTree with 1 million leaf nodes has approximately 1/3rd of the leaf nodes of the) Tj
T*
(internal nodes \(all the nodes up the leaf level\), and each internal node has four pointers to) Tj
T*
(its child nodes. If we’ll assume 8 Bytes for each pointer, then we’ll need 1M * 1/3 * 4 * 8 =) Tj
T*
(10.67 MB of space for the internal nodes.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Source: https://tildesites.bowdoin.edu/~ltoma/teaching/cs3225-GIS/fall16/Lectures/gis_qdt1.pdf) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(We can get the total space needed to store all the internal nodes by adding 12GB and 10.67MB,) Tj
T*
(that is, 12.01GB approximately.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can easily store a QuadTree on a server.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s try the following calculator to calculate the storage needed for a QuadTree:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(PlaceID, Latitude, and Longitude \(Bytes\)              24) Tj
T*
(Total Number of Places \(Millions\)                    500) Tj
T*
(Total Space Needed to Store All Places \(GB\)           12) Tj
T*
