T*
(### Data partitioning) Tj
T*
(Keeping 20% growth per year in mind, the number of places will increase. We can partition data) Tj
T*
(on the following basis:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Regions: We can split our places into regions on the basis of zip codes. This way, all the) Tj
T*
(places that belong to a specific region are stored on a single node. We store information on) Tj
T*
(the region along with the place, so that we can query on the basis of regions too. We can use) Tj
T*
(the user’s region to find the places in that specific region.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This data partitioning comes with a few challenges. For example, if a region becomes popular) Tj
T*
(during tourist season, it can affect the performance of our system. We might have numerous) Tj
T*
(queries on the server that might result in slow responsiveness to user queries.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. PlaceID: We can partition data on the basis of PlaceID instead of the region to avoid the) Tj
T*
(query overload in popular seasons or rush hours. We can use a key-value store to store the) Tj
T*
(places. In this case, the key is the PlaceID and the value contains the server in which that) Tj
T*
(place is stored. This will make the process of fetching places more efficient.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(So, we’ll opt for partitioning on the basis of places. Moreover, we’ll also use caches for) Tj
T*
(popular places. The cache will have information about that particular place.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Ensure availability) Tj
T*
(Consider a scenario where multiple people in the same radius place a search request. If we have) Tj
T*
(a single QuadTree, it’ll affect the availability of the users. So, we can’t rely on a single) Tj
T*
(QuadTree. To cater to this problem, we replicate our QuadTrees on multiple servers to ensure) Tj
T*
(availability. This allows us to distribute the read traffic and decrease the response time.) Tj
T*
(QuadTrees are built on a server, so we can use the server ID as a key to identify the server on) Tj
T*
(which the QuadTree is present. The value is the list of places that the QuadTree holds. The) Tj
T*
(key-value store eases the rebuilding of the QuadTree in case we lose it.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(How will the leader-follower approach help us in replication?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(We’ll have a single leader and two followers. The leader will have the QuadTree and it’ll) Tj
T*
(handle all the write requests. It’ll update the followers about any change made to the) Tj
T*
(QuadTrees synchronously, so there’ll be a chance of delay. The followers will handle all the) Tj
T*
(read requests. In case a follower dies, we’ll choose another follower and replicate the data to) Tj
T*
(it. If the leader is down, we‘ll choose any of the followers to step in as the leader. This) Tj
T*
(way, we’ll be able to replicate our QuadTrees onto three servers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Insert a new place) Tj
T*
(We insert a new place into the database as well as in our QuadTree. We find the segment of the) Tj
T*
(new place if the Quadtree is distributed on different servers, and then we add it to that) Tj
T*
(segment. We split the segment if required and update the QuadTree accordingly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Rank popular places) Tj
T*
(We need a service, a rating calculator, which calculates the overall rating of a service. We) Tj
T*
(can store the rating of a place in the database and also in the QuadTree, along with the ID,) Tj
T*
(latitude, and longitude of the place. The QuadTree returns the top 50 or 100 popular places) Tj
T*
(within the given radius. The aggregator service determines the actual top places and returns) Tj
T*
(them to the user.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
