(## Design) Tj
T*
(Now we’ll discuss the individual building blocks and components used in the design of Yelp and) Tj
T*
(how they work together to complete various functional requirements.) Tj
T*
(### Components) Tj
T*
(These are the components of our system:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Segments producer: This component is responsible for communicating with the third-party world) Tj
T*
(map data services \(for example, Google Maps\). It takes up that data and divides the world into) Tj
T*
(smaller regions called segments. The segment producer helps us narrow down the number of places) Tj
T*
(to be searched.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- QuadTree servers: These are a set of servers that have trees that contain the places in the) Tj
T*
(segments. A QuadTree server finds a list of places based on the given radius and the user’s) Tj
T*
(provided location and returns that list to the user. This component mainly aids the search) Tj
T*
(functionality.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Aggregators: The QuadTrees accumulate all the places and send them to the aggregators. Then,) Tj
T*
(the aggregators aggregate the results and return the search result to the user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Read servers: We use a set of read servers that we use to handle all the read requests. Since) Tj
T*
(we have more read requests, it’s efficient to separate these requests from the write requests.) Tj
T*
(Each read server directs the search requests to the QuadTrees’ servers and returns the results) Tj
T*
(to the user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Write server: We use a set of write servers to handle all the write requests. Each write) Tj
T*
(server handles the write requests of the user and updates the storage accordingly. Examples for) Tj
T*
(write requests include adding a place, writing a comment, rating a place, and so on.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Storage: We’ll use two types of storage to fulfill our diverse needs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  - SQL database: Our system will have different tables like “Users,” “Place,” “Reviews,”) Tj
T*
(“Photos,” and others as described below. The data in these tables is inherently relational and) Tj
T*
(structured. We need to perform queries like places a user visited, reviews they added, or view) Tj
T*
(all the reviews of a specific place. It’s easy to perform such queries in a SQL-based database.) Tj
T*
(We also want all users to have a consistent view of the data, and SQL-based databases are) Tj
T*
(better suited for such use cases. We’ll use reliable and scalable databases, as is discussed in) Tj
T*
(the Database building block.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
