stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(- Mappings of the URLs, that is, the long URLs that are mapped onto short URLs.) Tj
T*
(Our service doesn’t require user registration for the generation of a short URL, so we can skip) Tj
T*
(adding certain data to our database. Additionally, the stored records will have no) Tj
T*
(relationships among themselves other than linking the URL-creating user’s details, so we don’t) Tj
T*
(need structured storage for record-keeping. Considering the reasons above and the fact that our) Tj
T*
(system will be read-heavy, NoSQL is a suitable choice for storing data. In particular, MongoDB) Tj
T*
(is a good choice for the following reasons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. It uses leader-follower protocol, making it possible to use replicas for heavy reading.) Tj
T*
(2. MongoDB ensures atomicity in concurrent write operations and avoids collisions by returning) Tj
T*
(duplicate-key errors for record-duplication issues.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Why are NoSQL databases like Cassandra or Riak not good choices instead of MongoDB?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Since our service is more read-intensive and less write-intensive, MongoDB suits our use case) Tj
T*
(the best for the following reasons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(NoSQL databases like Cassandra, Riak, and DynamoDB need read-repair during the reading stage) Tj
T*
(and hence provide slower reads to write performance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(They are leader-less NoSQL databases that provide weaker atomicity upon concurrent writes.) Tj
T*
(Being a single leader database, MongoDB provides a higher read throughput as we can either read) Tj
T*
(from the leader replica or follower replicas. The write operations have to pass through the) Tj
T*
(leader replica. It ensures our system’s availability for reading-intensive tasks even in cases) Tj
T*
(where the leader dies.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Since Cassandra inherently ensures availability more than MongoDB, choosing MongoDB over) Tj
T*
(Cassandra might make our system look less available. However, the time taken by the leader) Tj
T*
(election algorithm is negligible compared to the time elapsed between short URL generation and) Tj
T*
(its first usage, so it doesn’t hamper our system’s availability.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Short URL generator: Our short URL generator will comprise a building block and an additional) Tj
T*
(component:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A sequencer to generate unique IDs) Tj
T*
(- A Base-58 encoder to enhance the readability of the short URL) Tj
T*
(We built a sequencer in our building blocks section to generate 64-bit unique numeric IDs.) Tj
T*
(However, our proposed design requires 64-bit alphanumeric short URLs in base-58. To convert the) Tj
T*
(numeric \(base-10\) IDs to alphanumeric \(base-58\), we’ll need a base-10 for the base-58 encoder.) Tj
T*
(We’ll explore the rationale behind these decisions alongside the internal working of the) Tj
T*
(base-58 encoder in the next lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Take a look at the diagram below to understand how the overall short URL generation unit will) Tj
T*
(work.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Internal working of a short URL generator]\(./internal.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Other building blocks: Beside the elements mentioned above, we’ll also incorporate other) Tj
T*
(building blocks like load balancers, cache, and rate limiters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Load balancing: We can employ Global Server Load Balancing \(GSLB\) apart from local load) Tj
T*
(balancing to improve availability. Since we have plenty of time between a short URL being) Tj
T*
(generated and subsequently accessed, we can safely assume that our DB is geographically) Tj
T*
(consistent and that distributing requests globally won’t cause any issues.) Tj
T*
(- Cache: For our specific read-intensive design problem, Memcached is the best choice for a) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
