<< /Length 4051 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(### Disadvantages) Tj
T*
(The URL shortening service has some associated drawbacks as well. Some of them are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- We lose the originality of our brand by using a short URL generated by a third-party service.) Tj
T*
(Many different brands using the same service get short URLs containing the same domain.) Tj
T*
(- Since we’re using a third-party service for URL shortening, the possibility of it getting) Tj
T*
(shut down and wiping all our shortened URLs will always be there.) Tj
T*
(- Our business brand image depends on the trustworthiness of a URL shortening service. The) Tj
T*
(wrong business move might negatively impact our business. The competition for acquiring the in-) Tj
T*
(demand custom short URLs is immense, so it might be possible that the best custom URLs are) Tj
T*
(already taken by the time we start generating short URLs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## How will we design a URL shortening service?) Tj
T*
(We’ve divided the URL shortening service design into the following five lessons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Requirements: This lesson discusses the functional and non-functional requirements of the) Tj
T*
(URL shortening service, along with estimating the resources required to achieve these) Tj
T*
(requirements. Moreover, it also lists down the fundamental building blocks needed to build such) Tj
T*
(a service.) Tj
T*
(2. Design and Deployment: This explains the working and usage of each component, the linkage) Tj
T*
(among them, and the overall working mechanism of them as a unit.) Tj
T*
(3. Encoder: This particular lesson unfolds the inner mechanism of the encoder used in the) Tj
T*
(design, stating the reason we use it along with the mathematical explanation.) Tj
T*
(4. Evaluation: Lastly, we test our design by considering different dimensions of our design) Tj
T*
(requirements and include the possibility of improving it.) Tj
T*
(5. Quiz: This lesson will test our understanding of the TinyURL design.) Tj
T*
(Let’s start by defining the requirements for a TinyURL-like URL shortening service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Cache/Background of Distributed Cache/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Background of Distributed Cache) Tj
T*
(The main goal of this chapter is to design a distributed cache. To achieve this goal, we should) Tj
T*
(have substantial background knowledge, mainly on different reading and writing techniques. This) Tj
T*
(lesson will help us build that background knowledge. Let’s look at the structure of this lesson) Tj
T*
(in the table below) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(                                  Structure of This Lesson) Tj
T*
(Section                    Motivation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Writing policies           Data is written to cache and databases. The order in which data) Tj
T*
(writing happens has performance implications. We’ll discuss various writing policies to help) Tj
T*
(decide which writing policy would be suitable for the distributed cache we want to design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Eviction policies          Since the cache is built on limited storage \(RAM\), we ideally want) Tj
T*
(to keep the most frequently accessed data in the cache. Therefore, we’ll discuss different) Tj
T*
(eviction policies to replace less frequently accessed data with most frequently accessed data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cache invalidation         Certain cached data may get outdated. We’ll discuss different) Tj
T*
(invalidation methods to remove stale or outdated entries from the cache in this section.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Storage mechanism          A distributed storage has many servers. We’ll discuss important) Tj
T*
(design considerations, such as which cache entry should be stored in which server and what data) Tj
T*
(structure to use for storage.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cache client               A cache server stores cache entries, but a cache client calls the) Tj
T*
(cache server to request data. We’ll discuss the details of a cache client library in this) Tj
T*
(section.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Writing policies) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
499 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 498 0 R >>
endobj
500 0 obj
<< /Length 3628 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Often, cache stores a copy \(or part\) of data, which is persistently stored in a data store.) Tj
T*
(When we store data to the data store, some important questions arise:) Tj
T*
(- Where do we store the data first? Database or cache?) Tj
T*
(- What will be the implication of each strategy for consistency models?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The short answer is, it depends on the application requirements. Let’s look at the details of) Tj
T*
(different writing policies to understand the concept better:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Write-through cache: The write-through mechanism writes on the cache as well as on the) Tj
T*
(database. Writing on both storages can happen concurrently or one after the other. This) Tj
T*
(increases the write latency but ensures strong consistency between the database and the cache.) Tj
T*
(- Write-back cache: In the write-back cache mechanism, the data is first written to the cache) Tj
T*
(and asynchronously written to the database. Although the cache has updated data, inconsistency) Tj
T*
(is inevitable in scenarios where a client reads stale data from the database. However, systems) Tj
T*
(using this strategy will have small writing latency.) Tj
T*
(- Write-around cache: This strategy involves writing data to the database only. Later, when a) Tj
T*
(read is triggered for the data, it’s written to cache after a cache miss. The database will) Tj
T*
(have updated data, but such a strategy isn’t favorable for reading recently updated data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Eviction policies) Tj
T*
(One of the main reasons caches perform fast is that they’re small. Small caches mean limited) Tj
T*
(storage capacity. Therefore, we need an eviction mechanism to remove less frequently accessed) Tj
T*
(data from the cache.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Several well-known strategies are used to evict data from the cache. The most well-known) Tj
T*
(strategies include the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Least recently used \(LRU\)) Tj
T*
(Most recently used \(MRU\)) Tj
T*
(Least frequently used \(LFU\)) Tj
T*
(Most frequently used \(MFU\)) Tj
T*
(Other strategies like first in, first out \(FIFO\) also exist. The choice of each of these) Tj
T*
(algorithms depends on the system the cache is being developed for.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Data can be classified into three temperature regions depending on the access frequency:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Hot: This is highly accessed data.) Tj
T*
(- Warm: This is less frequently accessed data.) Tj
T*
(- Cold: This is rarely accessed data.) Tj
T*
(Cold data frequently gets evicted from the cache and gets replaced with hot or warm data. The) Tj
T*
(following image shows the spectrum of data temperatures:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Data temperature]\(./temperature.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Cache invalidation) Tj
T*
(Apart from the eviction of less frequently accessed data, some data residing in the cache may) Tj
T*
(become stale or outdated over time. Such cache entries are invalid and must be marked for) Tj
T*
(deletion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The situation demands a question: How do we identify stale entries?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Resolution of the problem requires storing metadata corresponding to each cache entry.) Tj
T*
(Specifically, maintaining a time-to-live \(TTL\) value to deal with outdated cache items.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can use two different approaches to deal with outdated items using TTL:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Active expiration: This method actively checks the TTL of cache entries through a daemon) Tj
T*
(process or thread.) Tj
T*
(- Passive expiration: This method checks the TTL of a cache entry at the time of access.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
