<< /Length 3880 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(TTL is the time after which a cache entry is considered expired.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Each expired item is removed from the cache upon discovery.) Tj
T*
(## Storage mechanism) Tj
T*
(Storing data in the cache isn’t as trivial as it seems because the distributed cache has) Tj
T*
(multiple cache servers. When we use multiple cache servers, the following design questions need) Tj
T*
(to be answered:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Which data should we store in which cache servers?) Tj
T*
(- What data structure should we use to store the data?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The above two questions are important design issues because they’ll decide the performance of) Tj
T*
(our distributed cache, which is the most important requirement for us. We’ll use the following) Tj
T*
(techniques to answer the questions above.) Tj
T*
(### Hash function) Tj
T*
(It’s possible to use hashing in two different scenarios:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Identify the cache server in a distributed cache to store and retrieve data.) Tj
T*
(- Locate cache entries inside each cache server.) Tj
T*
(For the first scenario, we can use different hashing algorithms. However, consistent hashing or) Tj
T*
(its flavors usually perform well in distributed systems because simple hashing won’t be ideal) Tj
T*
(in case of crashes or scaling.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the second scenario, we can use typical hash functions to locate a cache entry to read or) Tj
T*
(write inside a cache server. However, a hash function alone can only locate a cache entry. It) Tj
T*
(doesn’t say anything about managing data within the cache server. That is, it doesn’t say) Tj
T*
(anything about how to implement a strategy to evict less frequently accessed data from the) Tj
T*
(cache server. It also doesn’t say anything about what data structures are used to store the) Tj
T*
(data within the cache servers. This is exactly the second design question of the storage) Tj
T*
(mechanism. Let’s take a look at the data structure next.) Tj
T*
(### Linked list) Tj
T*
(We’ll use a doubly linked list. The main reason is its widespread usage and simplicity.) Tj
T*
(Furthermore, adding and removing data from the doubly linked list in our case will be a) Tj
T*
(constant time operation. This is because we either evict a specific entry from the tail of the) Tj
T*
(linked list or relocate an entry to the head of the doubly linked list. Therefore, no) Tj
T*
(iterations are required.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Bloom filters are an interesting choice for quickly finding if a cache entry doesn’t) Tj
T*
(exist in the cache servers. We can use bloom filters to determine that a cache entry is) Tj
T*
(definitely not present in the cache server, but the possibility of its presence is) Tj
T*
(probabilistic. Bloom filters are quite useful in large caching or database systems.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Sharding in cache clusters) Tj
T*
(To avoid SPOF and high load on a single cache instance, we introduce sharding. Sharding) Tj
T*
(involves splitting up cache data among multiple cache servers. It can be performed in the) Tj
T*
(following two ways.) Tj
T*
(#### Dedicated cache servers) Tj
T*
(In the dedicated cache servers method, we separate the application and web servers from the) Tj
T*
(cache servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The advantages of using dedicated cache servers are the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- There’s flexibility in terms of hardware choices for each functionality.) Tj
T*
(- It’s possible to scale web/application servers and cache servers separately.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Apart from the advantages above, working as a standalone caching service enables other) Tj
T*
(microservices to benefit from them—for example, Cache as a Service. In that case, the caching) Tj
T*
(system will have to be aware of different applications so that their data doesn’t collide.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
