stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
([A depiction of service hosts coordinating with dedicated cache servers]\(./cache_servers1.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Co-located cache) Tj
T*
(The co-located cache embeds cache and service functionality within the same host.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The main advantage of this strategy is the reduction in CAPEX and OPEX of extra hardware.) Tj
T*
(Furthermore, with the scaling of one service, automatic scaling of the other service is) Tj
T*
(obtained. However, the failure of one machine will result in the loss of both services) Tj
T*
(simultaneously.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(CAPEX: Capital expenditures are major investments over assets that are held for a long time.) Tj
T*
(OPEX: Operational Expenditures are day-to-day expenses that keep the company running.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
([Hosting cache and application logic in the same machine]\(./cache_servers2.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Cache client) Tj
T*
(We discussed that the hash functions should be used for the selection of cache servers. But) Tj
T*
(what entity performs these hash calculations?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A cache client is a piece of code residing in hosting servers that do \(hash\) computations to) Tj
T*
(store and retrieve data in the cache servers. Also, cache clients may coordinate with other) Tj
T*
(system components like monitoring and configuration services. All cache clients are programmed) Tj
T*
(in the same way so that the same PUT, and GET operations from different clients return the same) Tj
T*
(results. Some of the characteristics of cache clients are the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Each cache client will know about all the cache servers.) Tj
T*
(- All clients can use well-known transport protocols like TCP or UDP to talk to the cache) Tj
T*
(servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(PUT: Adding data to a cache server.) Tj
T*
(GET: Retrieving data from a cache server.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What will be the behavior of cache clients to an access request if one of the cache servers is) Tj
T*
(dead?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Since the data within the cache servers will no longer be available, cache clients will mark) Tj
T*
(that access request as a cache miss.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(In this lesson, we learned what distributed caches are and highlighted their significance in) Tj
T*
(distributed systems. We also discussed different storage and eviction mechanisms for caches.) Tj
T*
(Caches are vital for any distributed system and are located at different points within the) Tj
T*
(design of a system. It’s important to understand how distributed caches can be designed as part) Tj
T*
(of a large system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Cache/Detailed Design of a Distributed Cache/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Detailed Design of a Distributed Cache) Tj
T*
(This lesson will identify some shortcomings of the high-level design of a distributed cache and) Tj
T*
(improve the design to cover the gaps. Let’s get started.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Find and remove limitations) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
