506 0 obj
<< /Length 4147 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Before we get to the detailed design, we need to understand and overcome some challenges:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- There’s no way for the cache client to realize the addition or failure of a cache server.) Tj
T*
(- The solution will suffer from the problem of single point of failure \(SPOF\) because we have a) Tj
T*
(single cache server for each set of cache data. Not only that, if some of the data on any of) Tj
T*
(the cache servers is frequently accessed \(generally referred to as a hotkey problem\), then our) Tj
T*
(performance will also be slow.) Tj
T*
(- Our solution also didn’t highlight the internals of cache servers. That is, what kind of data) Tj
T*
(structures will it use to store and what eviction policy will it use?) Tj
T*
(### Maintain cache servers list) Tj
T*
(Let’s start by resolving the first problem. We’ll take incremental steps toward the best) Tj
T*
(possible solution. Let’s look at the following slides to get an idea of each of the solutions) Tj
T*
(described below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([[Solution 1] Maintaining a configuration file in each server that the cache client can) Tj
T*
(use]\(./solution1.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([[Solution 2] Maintaining a configuration file on a centralized location]\(./solution2.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([[Solution 3] Using a configuration service to monitor cache servers and keep the cache clients) Tj
T*
(updated]\(./solution3.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Solution 1: It’s possible to have a configuration file in each of the service hosts where the) Tj
T*
(cache clients reside. The configuration file will contain the updated health and metadata) Tj
T*
(required for the cache clients to utilize the cache servers efficiently. Each copy of the) Tj
T*
(configuration service can be updated through a push service by any DevOps tool. The main) Tj
T*
(problem with this strategy is that the configuration file will have to be manually updated and) Tj
T*
(deployed through some DevOps tools.) Tj
T*
(- Solution 2: We can store the configuration file in a centralized location that the cache) Tj
T*
(clients can use to get updated information about cache servers. This solves the deployment) Tj
T*
(issue, but we still need to manually update the configuration file and monitor the health of) Tj
T*
(each server.) Tj
T*
(- Solution 3: An automatic way of handling the issue is to use a configuration service that) Tj
T*
(continuously monitors the health of the cache servers. In addition to that, the cache clients) Tj
T*
(will get notified when a new cache server is added to the cluster. When we use this strategy,) Tj
T*
(no human intervention or monitoring will be required in case of failures or the addition of new) Tj
T*
(nodes. Finally, the cache clients obtain the list of cache servers from the configuration) Tj
T*
(service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The configuration service has the highest operational cost. At the same time, it’s a complex) Tj
T*
(solution. However, it’s the most robust among all the solutions we presented.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Improve availability) Tj
T*
(The second problem relates to cache unavailability if the cache servers fail. A simple solution) Tj
T*
(is the addition of replica nodes. We can start by adding one primary and two backup nodes in a) Tj
T*
(cache shard. With replicas, there’s always a possibility of inconsistency. If our replicas are) Tj
T*
(in close proximity, writing over replicas is performed synchronously to avoid inconsistencies) Tj
T*
(between shard replicas. It’s crucial to divide cache data among shards so that neither the) Tj
T*
(problem of unavailability arises nor any hardware is wasted.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This solution has two main advantages:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- There’s improved availability in case of failures.) Tj
T*
(- Hot shards can have multiple nodes \(primary-secondary\) for reads.) Tj
T*
(Not only will such a solution improve availability, but it will also add to the performance.) Tj
T*
(### Internals of cache server) Tj
T*
(Each cache client should use three mechanisms to store and evict entries from the cache) Tj
T*
(servers:) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
