() Tj
T*
(- Hash map: The cache server uses a hash map to store or locate different entries inside the) Tj
T*
(RAM of cache servers. The illustration below shows that the map contains pointers to each cache) Tj
T*
(value.) Tj
T*
(- Doubly linked list: If we have to evict data from the cache, we require a linked list so that) Tj
T*
(we can order entries according to their frequency of access. The illustration below depicts how) Tj
T*
(entries are connected using a doubly linked list.) Tj
T*
(- Eviction policy: The eviction policy depends on the application requirements. Here, we assume) Tj
T*
(the least recently used \(LRU\) eviction policy.) Tj
T*
(A depiction of a sharded cluster along with a node’s data structure is provided below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([A shard primary and replica, each with the same internal mechanisms]\(./lru.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(It’s evident from the explanation above that we don’t provide a delete API. This is because the) Tj
T*
(eviction \(through eviction algorithm\) and deletion \(of expired entries through TTL\) is done) Tj
T*
(locally at cache servers. Nevertheless, situations can arise where the delete API may be) Tj
T*
(required. For example, when we delete a recently added entry from the database, it should) Tj
T*
(result in the removal of items from the cache for the sake of consistency.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Detailed design) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’re now ready to formalize the detailed design after resolving each of the three previously) Tj
T*
(highlighted problems. Look at the detailed design below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Detailed design of a distributed caching system]\(./caching.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s summarize the proposed detailed design in a few points:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The client’s requests reach the service hosts through the load balancers where the cache) Tj
T*
(clients reside.) Tj
T*
(- Each cache client uses consistent hashing to identify the cache server. Next, the cache) Tj
T*
(client forwards the request to the cache server maintaining a specific shard.) Tj
T*
(- Each cache server has primary and replica servers. Internally, every server uses the same) Tj
T*
(mechanisms to store and evict cache entries.) Tj
T*
(- Configuration service ensures that all the clients see an updated and consistent view of the) Tj
T*
(cache servers.) Tj
T*
(- Monitoring services can be additionally used to log and report different metrics of the) Tj
T*
(caching service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: An important aspect of the design is that cache entries are stored and retrieved from) Tj
T*
(RAM. We discussed the suitability of RAM for designing a caching system in the previous lesson.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(While consistent hashing is a good choice, it may result in unequal distribution of data, and) Tj
T*
(certain servers may get overloaded. How do we resolve this problem?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(A number of consistent hashing algorithms’ flavors have been suggested over time. We can use) Tj
T*
(one such flavor that distributes load uniformly and even makes multiple copies of the same data) Tj
T*
(on different cache servers. Each cache server can have virtual servers inside it, and the) Tj
T*
(number of virtual servers in a machine depends on the machine’s capability. This results in a) Tj
T*
(finer control on the amount of load on a cache server. At the same time, it improves) Tj
T*
(availability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(One example is in DeCandia, Giuseppe, et al. “Dynamo: Amazon’s highly available key-value) Tj
T*
(store.” ACM SIGOPS operating systems review 41.6 \(2007\): 205-220.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
