50 780 Td
(```) Tj
T*
(The numbers above highlight the importance of the eviction algorithm to increase the cache hit) Tj
T*
(rate. Each application should conduct an empirical study to determine the eviction algorithm) Tj
T*
(that gives better results for a specific workload.) Tj
T*
(## Scalability) Tj
T*
(We can create shards based on requirements and changing server loads. While we add new cache) Tj
T*
(servers to the cluster, we also have to do a limited number of rehash computations, thanks to) Tj
T*
(consistent hashing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Adding replicas reduces the load on hot shards. Another way to handle the hotkeys problem is to) Tj
T*
(do further sharding within the range of those keys. Although the scenario where a single key) Tj
T*
(will become hot is rare, it’s possible for the cache client to devise solutions to avoid the) Tj
T*
(single hotkey contention issue. For example, cache clients can intelligently avoid such a) Tj
T*
(situation that a single key becomes a bottleneck, or we can use dynamic replication for) Tj
T*
(specific keys, and so on. Nonetheless, the solutions are complex and beyond the scope of this) Tj
T*
(lesson.) Tj
T*
([Guarantees provided by a distributed cache]) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## High availability) Tj
T*
(We have improved the availability through redundant cache servers. Redundancy adds a layer of) Tj
T*
(reliability and fault tolerance to our design. We also used the leader-follower algorithm to) Tj
T*
(conveniently manage a cluster shard. However, we haven’t achieved high availability because we) Tj
T*
(have two shard replicas, and at the moment, we assume that the replicas are within a data) Tj
T*
(center.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(It’s possible to achieve higher availability by splitting the leader and follower servers among) Tj
T*
(different data centers. But such high availability comes at a price of consistency. We assumed) Tj
T*
(synchronous writes within the same data center. But synchronous writing for strong consistency) Tj
T*
(in different data centers has a serious performance implication that isn’t welcomed in caching) Tj
T*
(systems. We usually use asynchronous replication across data centers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For replication within the data center, we can get strong consistency with good performance. We) Tj
T*
(can compromise strong consistency across data center replication to achieve better availability) Tj
T*
(\(see CAP and PACELC theorems\).) Tj
T*
(## Consistency) Tj
T*
(It’s possible to write data to cache servers in a synchronous or asynchronous mode. In the case) Tj
T*
(of caching, the asynchronous mode is favored for improved performance. Consequently, our) Tj
T*
(caching system suffers from inconsistencies. Alternatively, strong consistency comes from) Tj
T*
(synchronous writing, but this increases the overall latency, and the performance takes a hit.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Inconsistency can also arise from faulty configuration files and services. Imagine a scenario) Tj
T*
(where a cache server is down during a write operation, and a read operation is performed on it) Tj
T*
(just after its recovery. We can avoid such scenarios for any joining or rejoining server by not) Tj
T*
(allowing it to serve requests until it’s reasonably sure that it’s up to date.) Tj
T*
(## Affordability) Tj
T*
(Our proposed design has a low cost because it’s feasible and practical to create such a system) Tj
T*
(using commodity hardware.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(What happens if the leader node fails in the leader-follower protocol?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Two possible solutions can be used in that case:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Use the leader election algorithm to elect a new leader among the set of available followers.) Tj
T*
(Use a separate distributed configuration management service to monitor and select leaders.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
