50 780 Td
(Is it a good idea to introduce synchronous replication to cache servers?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Synchronous replication on the cache clusters will introduce a performance penalty because it) Tj
T*
(takes time to replicate the same data to all the cache servers. Therefore, there’s a trade-off) Tj
T*
(between performance and consistency in the usage of asynchronous versus synchronous) Tj
T*
(replication, respectively.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The answer depends on the use case. In case of cache servers, synchronous replication is a good) Tj
T*
(choice if the replicated servers are within close proximity of the primary server.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 3) Tj
T*
(Assume that a large number of concurrent requests are forwarded to a single shard server. If) Tj
T*
(the server uses the LRU algorithm, some of the requests will result in a cache miss and write a) Tj
T*
(new entry to the cache, while some will result in a cache hit and end up reading from the) Tj
T*
(server. These concurrent requests may compete with each other. Therefore, some locking) Tj
T*
(mechanisms may be required. However, locking an entire data structure for all the reading and) Tj
T*
(writing operations will heavily reduce the performance. How do you think such a problem will be) Tj
T*
(dealt with?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Generally, for concurrent access to shared data, some locking mechanisms like Semaphore,) Tj
T*
(Monitors, Mutex locks, and others are good choices. But as the number of users \(readers in case) Tj
T*
(of cache hit or writers in case of a cache miss\) grows, locking the entire data structure isn’t) Tj
T*
(a suitable solution. In that case, we can consider the following options:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Limited locking: In this strategy, only specific sections of the entire data structure will) Tj
T*
(be locked. While some threads or processes can read from the data structure simultaneously,) Tj
T*
(some threads may temporarily block access to specific sections of the data structure.) Tj
T*
(- Offline eviction: Offline eviction may be a possibility where instead of making actual) Tj
T*
(changes to the data structure, only the required changes will be recorded while performing) Tj
T*
(different operations until it’s necessary to commit the changes. This solution is desirable and) Tj
T*
(easy if the cache hit rate is high because a change to the data structure will likely be) Tj
T*
(required when a cache miss occurs.) Tj
T*
(- Lock-free implementation: Multiple solutions have been proposed which suggest that) Tj
T*
(simultaneous reading and writing over doubly linked list is feasible to support large number of) Tj
T*
(concurrent reading and writing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(locking mechanism: A mechanism by which access to shared data is limited to a single thread) Tj
T*
(only. Thus achieving synchronization.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Summary) Tj
T*
(We studied the basics of the cache and designed a distributed cache that has a good level of) Tj
T*
(availability, high performance, high scalability, and low cost. Our mechanism maintains high) Tj
T*
(availability using replicas, though if all replicas are in one data center, such a scheme won’t) Tj
T*
(tackle full data center failures. Now that we’ve learned about the basics of design, let’s) Tj
T*
(explore popular open-source frameworks like Memcached and Redis.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Cache/High-level Design of a Distributed Cache/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# High-level Design of a Distributed Cache) Tj
T*
(In this lesson, we’ll learn to design a distributed cache. We’ll also discuss the trade-offs) Tj
T*
(and design choices that can occur while we progress in our journey towards developing a) Tj
T*
(solution.) Tj
T*
(## Requirements) Tj
T*
(Let us start by understanding the requirements of our solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
