(Question) Tj
T*
(The API design of the distributed cache looks exactly like the key-value store. What are the) Tj
T*
(possible differences between a key-value store and a distributed cache?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Some of the key differences are the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Key-value stores need to durably store data \(persistence\). A cache is used in addition to) Tj
T*
(persistent storage to increase reading performance.) Tj
T*
(- A cache serves data from the RAM. A key-value store writes data to non-volatile storage.) Tj
T*
(- Key-value stores are robust and should survive failures. However, caches can crash and be) Tj
T*
(populated from scratch after recovery.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design considerations) Tj
T*
(Before designing the distributed cache system, it’s important to consider some design choices.) Tj
T*
(Each of these choices will be purely based on our application requirements. However, we can) Tj
T*
(highlight some key differences here:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Storage hardware) Tj
T*
(If our data is large, we may require sharding and therefore use shard servers for cache) Tj
T*
(partitions. Should these shard servers be specialized or commodity hardware? Specialized) Tj
T*
(hardware will have good performance and storage capacity, but it will cost more. We can build a) Tj
T*
(large cache from commodity servers. In general, the number of shard servers will depend on the) Tj
T*
(cache’s size and access frequency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Furthermore, we can consider storing our data on the secondary storage of these servers for) Tj
T*
(persistence while we still serve data from RAM. Secondary storage may be used in cases where a) Tj
T*
(reboot happens, and cache rebuilding takes a long time. Persistence, however, may not be a) Tj
T*
(requirement in a cache system if there’s a dedicated persistence layer, such as a database.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Facebook built 28 TeraBytes of cache using commodity machines in 2013.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Data structures) Tj
T*
(A vital part of the design has to be the speed of accessing data. Hash tables are data) Tj
T*
(structures that take a constant time on average to store and retrieve data. Furthermore, we) Tj
T*
(need another data structure to enforce an eviction algorithm on the cached data. In particular,) Tj
T*
(linked lists are a good option \(as discussed in the previous lesson\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Also, we need to understand what kind of data structures a cache can store. Even though we) Tj
T*
(discussed in the API design section that we’ll use strings for simplicity, it’s possible to) Tj
T*
(store different data structures or formats, like hash maps, arrays, sets, and so on, within the) Tj
T*
(cache. In the next lesson, we’ll see a practical example of such a cache.) Tj
T*
(### Cache client) Tj
T*
(It’s the client process or library that places the insert and retrieve calls. The location of) Tj
T*
(the client process is a design issue. For example, it’s possible to place the client process) Tj
T*
(within a serving host if the cache is for internal use only. Otherwise, in the case where the) Tj
T*
(caching system is provided as a service for external use, a dedicated cache client can send) Tj
T*
(users’ requests to the cache servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Writing policy) Tj
T*
(The writing strategy over the cache and database has consistency implications. In general,) Tj
T*
(there’s no optimal choice, but depending on our application, the preference of writing policy) Tj
T*
(is significantly important.) Tj
T*
(### Eviction policy) Tj
T*
(By design, the cache provides low-latency reads and writes. To achieve this, data is often) Tj
T*
(served from RAM memory. Usually, we can’t put all the data in the cache due to the limited size) Tj
T*
(of the cache as compared to the full dataset. So, we need to carefully decide what stays in the) Tj
T*
(cache and how to make room for new entries.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
