BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
(With the addition of new data, some of the existing data may have to be evicted from the cache.) Tj
T*
(However, choosing a victim entry depends on the eviction policy. Numerous eviction policies) Tj
T*
(exist, but the choice again depends on the application using it. For instance, least recently) Tj
T*
(used \(LRU\) can be a good choice for social media services where recently uploaded content will) Tj
T*
(likely get the most views.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Apart from the details in the sections above, optimizing the time-to-live \(TTL\) value can play) Tj
T*
(an essential role in reducing the number of cache misses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## High-level design) Tj
T*
(The following figure depicts our high-level design:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([High-level design of distributed cache]\(./design.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The main components in this high-level design are the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Cache client: This library resides in the service application servers. It holds all the) Tj
T*
(information regarding cache servers. The cache client will choose one of the cache servers) Tj
T*
(using a hash and search algorithm for each incoming insert and retrieve request. All the cache) Tj
T*
(clients should have a consistent view of all the cache servers. Also, the resolution technique) Tj
T*
(to move data to and from the cache servers should be the same. Otherwise, different clients) Tj
T*
(will request different servers for the same data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Cache servers: These servers maintain the cache of the data. Each cache server is accessible) Tj
T*
(by all the cache clients. Each server is connected to the database to store or retrieve data.) Tj
T*
(Cache clients use TCP or UDP protocol to perform data transfer to or from the cache servers.) Tj
T*
(However, if any cache server is down, requests to those servers are resolved as a missed cache) Tj
T*
(by the cache clients.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Cache/Memcached versus Redis/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Memcached versus Redis) Tj
T*
(## Introduction) Tj
T*
(This lesson will discuss some of the widely adopted real-world implementations of a distributed) Tj
T*
(cache. Our focus will be on two well-known open-source frameworks: Memcached and Redis. They’re) Tj
T*
(highly scalable, highly performant, and robust caching tools. Both of these techniques follow) Tj
T*
(the client-server model and achieve a latency of sub-millisecond. Let’s discuss each one of) Tj
T*
(them and then compare their usefulness.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Memcached) Tj
T*
(Memcached was introduced in 2003. It’s a key-value store distributed cache designed to store) Tj
T*
(objects very fast. Memcached stores data in the form of a key-value pair. Both the key and the) Tj
T*
(value are strings. This means that any data that has been stored will have to be serialized.) Tj
T*
(So, Memcached doesn’t support and can’t manipulate different data structures.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Serialization is the process of translating data into a format that can be transmitted or) Tj
T*
(stored elsewhere. Later, reconstruction or deserialization of the data should be possible.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Memcached has a client and server component, each of which is necessary to run the system. The) Tj
T*
(system is designed in a way that half the logic is encompassed in the server, whereas the other) Tj
T*
(half is in the client. However, each server follows the shared-nothing architecture. In this) Tj
T*
(architecture, servers are unaware of each other, and there’s no synchronization, data sharing,) Tj
T*
(and communication between the servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Due to the disconnected design, Memcached is able to achieve almost a deterministic query speed) Tj
T*
(\(O\(1\)\) serving millions of keys per second using a high-end system. Therefore, Memcached offers) Tj
T*
(a high throughput and low latency.) Tj
T*
([Design of a typical Memcached cluster]\(./memcached.jpg\)) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
