(As evident from the design of a typical Memcached cluster, Memcached scales well horizontally.) Tj
T*
(The client process is usually maintained with the service host that also interacts with the) Tj
T*
(authoritative storage \(back-end database\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Facebook and Memcached) Tj
T*
(The data access pattern in Facebook requires frequent reads and updates because views are) Tj
T*
(presented to the users on the fly instead of being generated ahead of time. Because Memcached) Tj
T*
(is simple, it was an easy choice for the solution because Memcached started developing in 2003) Tj
T*
(whereas Facebook was developed in 2004. In fact, in some cases, Facebook and Memcached teams) Tj
T*
(worked together to find solutions.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Redis was developed in 2009. So, using Redis wasn’t a possibility at Facebook by then.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Some of the simple commands of Memcached include the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(get <key_1> <key_2> <key_3> ...) Tj
T*
(set <key> <value> ...) Tj
T*
(delete <key>[<time>] ...) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(At Facebook, Memcached sits between the MySQL database and the web layer that uses roughly 28) Tj
T*
(TeraBytes of RAM spread across more than 800 servers \(as of 2013, Facebook and Memcached - Tech) Tj
T*
(Talk\). By an approximation of least recently used \(LRU\) eviction policy, Facebook is able to) Tj
T*
(achieve a cache hit rate of 95%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following illustration shows the high-level design of caching architecture at Facebook. As) Tj
T*
(we can see, out of a total of 50 million requests made by the web layer, only 2.5 million) Tj
T*
(requests reach the persistence layer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Facebook using a layer of Memcached sitting between persistence and web layer]\(./fb.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Redis) Tj
T*
(Redis is a data structure store that can be used as a cache, database, and message broker. It) Tj
T*
(offers rich features at the cost of additional complexity. It has the following features:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Data structure store: Redis understands the different data structures it stores. We don’t) Tj
T*
(have to retrieve data structures from it, manipulate them, and then store them back. We can) Tj
T*
(make in-house changes that save both time and effort.) Tj
T*
(- Database: It can persist all the in-memory blobs on the secondary storage.) Tj
T*
(- Message broker: Asynchronous communication is a vital requirement in distributed systems.) Tj
T*
(Redis can translate millions of messages per second from one component to another in a system.) Tj
T*
(Redis provides a built-in replication mechanism, automatic failover, and different levels of) Tj
T*
(persistence. Apart from that, Redis understands Memcached protocols, and therefore, solutions) Tj
T*
(using Memcached can translate to Redis. A particularly good aspect of Redis is that it) Tj
T*
(separates data access from cluster management. It decouples data and controls the plane. This) Tj
T*
(results in increased reliability and performance. Finally, Redis doesn’t provide strong) Tj
T*
(consistency due to the use of asynchronous replication.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Redis structure supporting automatic failover using redundant secondary) Tj
T*
(replicas]\(./redis_replicas.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Redis cluster) Tj
T*
(Redis has built-in cluster support that provides high availability. This is called Redis) Tj
T*
(Sentinel. A cluster has one or more Redis databases that are queried using multithreaded) Tj
T*
(proxies. Redis clusters perform automatic sharding where each shard has primary and secondary) Tj
T*
(nodes. However, the number of shards in a database or node is configurable to meet the) Tj
T*
(expectations and requirements of an application.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each Redis cluster is maintained by a cluster manager whose job is to detect failures and) Tj
T*
ET
