T*
(### Pipelining in Redis) Tj
T*
(Since Redis uses a client-server model, each request blocks the client until the server) Tj
T*
(receives the result. A Redis client looking to send subsequent requests will have to wait for) Tj
T*
(the server to respond to the first request. So, the overall latency will be higher.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Redis uses pipelining to speed up the process. Pipelining is the process of combining multiple) Tj
T*
(requests from the client side without waiting for a response from the server. As a result, it) Tj
T*
(reduces the number of RTT spans for multiple requests.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The round-trip-time \(RTT\) is the latency for a request to travel from the client to the server) Tj
T*
(and back.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Redis client-server communication without pipelining versus Redis client-server communication) Tj
T*
(with pipelining]\(./redis_communication.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The process of pipelining reduces the latency through RTT and the time to do socket level I/O.) Tj
T*
(Also, mode switching through system calls in the operating system is an expensive operation) Tj
T*
(that’s reduced significantly via pipelining. Pipelining the commands from the client side has) Tj
T*
(no impact on how the server processes these requests.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For example, two requests pipelined by the client reach the server, and the server can’t) Tj
T*
(entertain the second. The server provides a result for the first and returns an error for the) Tj
T*
(second. The client is independent in batching similar commands together to achieve maximum) Tj
T*
(throughput.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Pipelining improves the latency to a minimum of five folds if both the client and server) Tj
T*
(are on the same machine. The request is sent on a loopback address \(127.0.0.1\). The true power) Tj
T*
(of pipelining is highlighted in systems where requests are sent to distant machines.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Memcached versus Redis) Tj
T*
(Even though Memcached and Redis both belong to the NoSQL family, there are subtle aspects that) Tj
T*
(set them apart:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Simplicity: Memcached is simple, but it leaves most of the effort for managing clusters left) Tj
T*
(to the developers of the cluster. This, however, means finer control using Memcached. Redis, on) Tj
T*
(the other hand, automates most of the scalability and data division tasks.) Tj
T*
(- Persistence: Redis provides persistence by properties like append only file \(AOF\) and Redis) Tj
T*
(database \(RDB\) snapshot. There’s no persistence support in Memcached. But this limitation can) Tj
T*
(be catered to by using third-party tools. For example, libmemcached-tools) Tj
T*
(- Data types: Memcached stores objects, whereas Redis supports strings, sorted sets, hash maps,) Tj
T*
(bitmaps, and hyper logs. However, the maximum key or value size is configurable.) Tj
T*
(- Memory usage: Both tools allow us to set a maximum memory size for caching. Memcached uses) Tj
T*
(the slab allocation method for reducing fragmentation. However, when we update the existing) Tj
T*
(entries’ size or store many small objects, there may be a wastage of memory. Nonetheless, there) Tj
T*
(are configuration workarounds to resolve such issues.) Tj
T*
(- Multithreading: Redis runs as a single process using one core, whereas Memcached can) Tj
T*
(efficiently use multicore systems with multithreading technology. We could argue that Redis was) Tj
T*
(designed to be a single-threaded process that reduces the complexity of multithreaded systems.) Tj
T*
(Nonetheless, multiple Redis processes can be executed for concurrency. At the same time, Redis) Tj
T*
(has improved over the years by tweaking its performance. Therefore, Redis can store small data) Tj
T*
(items efficiently. Memcached can be the right choice for file sizes above 100 K.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
