(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(It’s impossible to imagine high-speed large-scale solutions without the use of a caching) Tj
T*
(system. In this chapter, we covered the need for a caching system and its fundamental details,) Tj
T*
(and we orchestrated a basic distributed cache system. We also familiarized ourselves with the) Tj
T*
(design and features of two of the most well-known caching frameworks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Cache/System Design The Distributed Cache/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: The Distributed Cache) Tj
T*
(## Problem statement) Tj
T*
(A typical system consists of the following components:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- It has a client that requests the service.) Tj
T*
(- It has one or more service hosts that entertain client requests.) Tj
T*
(- It has a database used by the service for data storage.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Under normal circumstances, this abstraction performs fine. However, as the number of users) Tj
T*
(increases, the database queries also increase. And as a result, the service providers are) Tj
T*
(overburdened, resulting in slow performance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In such cases, a cache is added to the system to deal with performance deterioration. A cache) Tj
T*
(is a temporary data storage that can serve data faster by keeping data entries in memory.) Tj
T*
(Caches store only the most frequently accessed data. When a request reaches the serving host,) Tj
T*
(it retrieves data from the cache \(cache hit\) and serves the user. However, if the data is) Tj
T*
(unavailable in the cache \(cache miss\), the data will be queried from the database. Also, the) Tj
T*
(cache is populated with the new value to avoid cache misses for the next time.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Cache hit: When the requested data is found in the cache, the server responds with the data) Tj
T*
(immediately.) Tj
T*
(Cache miss: When the requested data isn’t found in the cache, it’s called a cache miss.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Service before using caching]\(./no_cache.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Service using caching to improve performance.]\(./no_cache.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A cache is a nonpersistent storage area used to keep repeatedly read and written data, which) Tj
T*
(provides the end user with lower latency. Therefore, a cache must serve data from a storage) Tj
T*
(component that is fast, has enough storage, and is affordable in terms of dollar cost as we) Tj
T*
(scale the caching service. The following illustration highlights the suitability of RAM as the) Tj
T*
(raw building block for caching:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([An approximation that depicts how RAM is the optimal choice for serving cached) Tj
T*
(data]\(./interest.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We understand the need for a cache and suitable storage hardware, but what is distributed) Tj
T*
(cache? Let’s discuss this next.) Tj
T*
ET
