() Tj
T*
(## What is a distributed cache?) Tj
T*
(A distributed cache is a caching system where multiple cache servers coordinate to store) Tj
T*
(frequently accessed data. Distributed caches are needed in environments where a single cache) Tj
T*
(server isn’t enough to store all the data. At the same time, it’s scalable and guarantees a) Tj
T*
(higher degree of availability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Caches are generally small, frequently accessed, short-term storage with fast read time. Caches) Tj
T*
(use the locality of reference principle.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Locality of reference is the notion that a program will access a particular set of data over a) Tj
T*
(short period of time. Locality can be spatial or temporal.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Generally, distributed caches are beneficial in the following ways:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- They minimize user-perceived latency by precalculating results and storing frequently) Tj
T*
(accessed data.) Tj
T*
(- They pre-generate expensive queries from the database.) Tj
T*
(- They store user session data temporarily.) Tj
T*
(- They serve data from temporary storage even if the data store is down temporarily.) Tj
T*
(- Finally, they reduce network costs by serving data from local resources.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Why distributed cache?) Tj
T*
(When the size of data required in the cache increases, storing the entire data in one system is) Tj
T*
(impractical. This is because of the following three reasons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- It can be a potential single point of failure \(SPOF\).) Tj
T*
(- A system is designed in layers, and each layer should have its caching mechanism to ensure) Tj
T*
(the decoupling of sensitive data from different layers.) Tj
T*
(- Caching at different locations helps reduce the serving latency at that layer.) Tj
T*
(In the table below, we describe how caching at different layers is performed through the use of) Tj
T*
(various technologies. It’s important to note that key-value store components are used in) Tj
T*
(various layers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(                                         Caching at Different Layers of a System) Tj
T*
(System Layer         Technology in Use) Tj
T*
(Usage) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Web                  HTTP cache headers, web accelerators, key-value store, CDNs, and so on) Tj
T*
(Accelerate retrieval of static web content, and manage sessions) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Application          Local cache and key-value data store) Tj
T*
(Accelerate application-level computations and data retrieval) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Database             Database cache, buffers, and key-value data store) Tj
T*
(Reduce data retrieval latency and I/O load from database) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Apart from the three system layers above, caching is also performed at DNS and client-side) Tj
T*
(technologies like browsers or end-devices.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## How will we design distributed cache?) Tj
T*
(We’ll divide the task of designing and reinforcing learning major concepts of distributed cache) Tj
T*
(into five lessons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Background of Distributed Cache: It’s imperative to build the background knowledge necessary) Tj
T*
ET
