(to make critical decisions when designing distributed caches. This lesson will revisit some) Tj
T*
(basic but important concepts.) Tj
T*
(2. High-level Design of a Distributed Cache: We’ll build a high-level design of a distributed) Tj
T*
(cache in this lesson.) Tj
T*
(3. Detailed Design of a Distributed Cache: We’ll identify some limitations of our high-level) Tj
T*
(design and work toward a scalable, affordable, and performant solution.) Tj
T*
(4. Evaluation of a Distributed Cache Design: This lesson will evaluate our design for various) Tj
T*
(non-functional requirements, such as scalability, consistency, availability, and so on.) Tj
T*
(5. Memcached versus Redis: We’ll discuss well-known industrial solutions, namely Memcached and) Tj
T*
(Redis. We’ll also go through their details and compare their features to help us understand) Tj
T*
(their potential use cases and how they relate to our design.) Tj
T*
(Let’s begin by exploring the background of the distributed cache in the next lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Logging/Design of a Distributed Logging Service/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Design of a Distributed Logging Service) Tj
T*
(We’ll design the distributed logging system now. Our logging system should log all activities) Tj
T*
(or messages \(we’ll not incorporate sampling ability into our design\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Requirements) Tj
T*
(Let’s list the requirements for designing a distributed logging system:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Functional requirements) Tj
T*
(The functional requirements of our system are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Writing logs: The services of the distributed system must be able to write into the logging) Tj
T*
(system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Searchable logs: It should be effortless for a system to find logs. Similarly, the) Tj
T*
(application’s flow from end-to-end should also be effortless.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Storing logging: The logs should reside in distributed storage for easy access.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Centralized logging visualizer: The system should provide a unified view of globally) Tj
T*
(separated services.) Tj
T*
(### Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(The non-functional requirements of our system are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Low latency: Logging is an I/O-intensive operation that is often much slower than CPU) Tj
T*
(operations. We need to design the system so that logging is not on an application’s critical) Tj
T*
(path.) Tj
T*
(- Scalability: We want our logging system to be scalable. It should be able to handle the) Tj
T*
(increasing amounts of logs over time and a growing number of concurrent users.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Availability: The logging system should be highly available to log the data.) Tj
T*
(## Building blocks we will use) Tj
T*
(The design of a distributed logging system will utilize the following building blocks:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Pub-sub system: We’ll use a pub-sub- system to handle the huge size of logs.) Tj
T*
(Distributed search: We’ll use distributed search to query the logs efficiently.) Tj
T*
([Building blocks we will use]\(./bb.jpg\)) Tj
T*
(## API design) Tj
T*
(The API design for this problem is given below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
