(Question) Tj
T*
(We learned earlier that a simple user-level API call to a large service might involve hundreds) Tj
T*
(of internal microservices and thousands of nodes. How can we stitch together logs end-to-end) Tj
T*
(for one request with causality intact?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Most complex services use a front-end server to handle an end user’s request. On reception of a) Tj
T*
(request, the front-end server can get a unique identifier using a sequencer. This unique) Tj
T*
(identifier will be appended to all the fanned-out services. Each log message generated anywhere) Tj
T*
(in the system also emits the unique identifier.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Later, we can filter the log \(or preprocess it\) based on the unique identifiers. At this step,) Tj
T*
(we are able to collect all the logs across microservices against a unique request. In the) Tj
T*
(Sequencer building block, we discussed that we can get unique identifiers that maintain) Tj
T*
(happens-before causality. Such an identifier has the property that if ID 1 is less than ID 2,) Tj
T*
(then ID 1 represents a time that occurred before ID 2. Now, each log item can use a time-) Tj
T*
(stamp, and we can sort log entries for a specific request in ascending order.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Correctly ordering the log in a chronological \(or causal\) order simplifies log analyses.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Windows Azure Storage System \(WAS\) uses an extensive logging infrastructure in its) Tj
T*
(development. It stores the logs in local disks, and given a large number of logs, they do not) Tj
T*
(push the logs to the distributed storage. Instead, they use a grep-like utility that works as a) Tj
T*
(distributed search. This way, they have a unified view of globally distributed logs data.) Tj
T*
ET
