(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(- We learned how logging is crucial in understanding the flow of events in a distributed) Tj
T*
(system. It helps to reduce the mean time to repair \(MTTR\) by steering us toward the root causes) Tj
T*
(of issues.) Tj
T*
(- Logging is an I/O-intensive operation that is time-consuming and slow. It is essential to) Tj
T*
(handle it carefully and not affect the critical path of other services’ execution.) Tj
T*
(- Logging is essential for monitoring because the data fetched from logs helps monitor the) Tj
T*
(health of an application. \(Alert and error aggregators serve this purpose.\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Logging/Introduction to Distributed Logging/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Introduction to Distributed Logging) Tj
T*
(## Logging in a distributed system) Tj
T*
(In today’s world, an increasing number of designs are moving to microservice architecture) Tj
T*
(instead of monolithic architecture. In microservice architecture, logs of each microservice are) Tj
T*
(accumulated in the respective machine. If we want to know about a certain event that was) Tj
T*
(processed by several microservices, it is difficult to go into every node, figure out the flow,) Tj
T*
(and view error messages. But, it becomes handy if we can trace the log for any particular flow) Tj
T*
(from end to end.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Moreover, it is also not necessary that a microservice is deployed on only one node. It can be) Tj
T*
(deployed on thousands of nodes. Consider the following example, where hundreds of microservices) Tj
T*
(are interdependent, and failure of one service can result in failures of other services. And if) Tj
T*
(we do not have logs, we might not determine the root cause of failure. This emphasizes the need) Tj
T*
(for logging.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Logging]\(./logging\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Restrain the log size) Tj
T*
(The number of logs increases over time. At a time, perhaps hundreds of concurrent messages need) Tj
T*
(to be logged. But the question is, are they all important enough to be logged? To solve this,) Tj
T*
(logs have to be structured. We need to decide what to log into the system on the application or) Tj
T*
(logging level.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Use sampling) Tj
T*
(We’ll determine which messages we should log into the system in this approach. Consider a) Tj
T*
(situation where we have lots of messages from the same set of events. For example, there are) Tj
T*
(people commenting on a post, where Person X commented on Person Y’s post, then Person Z) Tj
T*
(commented on Person Y’s post, and so on. Instead of logging all the information, we can use a) Tj
T*
(sampler service that only logs a smaller set of messages from a larger chunk. This way, we can) Tj
T*
(decide on the most important messages to be logged.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: For large systems like Facebook, where billions of events happen per second, it is not) Tj
T*
(viable to log them all. An appropriate sampling threshold and strategy are necessary to) Tj
T*
(selectively pick a representative data set.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What is a scenario where the sampling approach will not work?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Let’s consider an application that processes a financial ATM transaction. It runs various) Tj
T*
(services like fraud detection, expiration time checking, card validation, and many more. If we) Tj
T*
(start to miss out logging of any service, we cannot identify an end-to-end flow that affects) Tj
T*
ET
