T*
(### Use categorization) Tj
T*
(Let’s look into the logging support provided by various programming languages. For example,) Tj
T*
(there’s log4j and logging in Python. The following severity levels are commonly used in) Tj
T*
(logging:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- DEBUG) Tj
T*
(- INFO) Tj
T*
(- WARNING) Tj
T*
(- ERROR) Tj
T*
(- FATAL/CRITICAL) Tj
T*
(Usually, the production logs are set to print messages with the severity of WARNING and above.) Tj
T*
(But for more detailed flow, the severity levels can be set to DEBUG and INFO levels too.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Structure the logs) Tj
T*
(Applications have the liberty to choose the structure of their log data. For example, an) Tj
T*
(application is free to write to log as binary or text data, but it is often helpful to enforce) Tj
T*
(some structure on the logs. The first benefit of structured logs is better interoperability) Tj
T*
(between log writers and readers. Second, the structure can make the job of a log processing) Tj
T*
(system easier.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: The structuring of logs is a dense topic in itself. We refer the interested learners to) Tj
T*
(the PhD thesis by Ryan Braud titled "Query-based debugging of distributed systems.”) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Points to consider while logging) Tj
T*
(We should be careful while logging. The logging information should only contain the relevant) Tj
T*
(information and not breach security concerns. For secure data, we should log encrypted data. We) Tj
T*
(should consider the following few points while logging:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Avoid logging personally identifiable information \(PII\), such as names, addresses, emails,) Tj
T*
(and so on.) Tj
T*
(- Avoid logging sensitive information like credit card numbers, passwords, and so on.) Tj
T*
(- Avoid excessive information. Logging all information is unnecessary. It only takes up more) Tj
T*
(space and affects performance. Logging, being an I/O-heavy operation, has its performance) Tj
T*
(penalties.) Tj
T*
(- The logging mechanism should be secure and not vulnerable because logs contain the) Tj
T*
(application’s flow, and an insecure logging mechanism is vulnerable to hackers.) Tj
T*
(### Vulnerability in logging infrastructure) Tj
T*
(A zero-day vulnerability in Log4j, a famous logging framework for Java, has been identified) Tj
T*
(recently. Log4j has contained the hidden vulnerability, Log4Shell \(CVE-2021-44228\), since 2013.) Tj
T*
(Apache gave the highest available score, a CVSS severity rating of 10, to Log4Shell. The) Tj
T*
(exploit is simple to execute and affects hundreds of millions of devices. Security experts are) Tj
T*
(convinced that this vulnerability can allow devastating cyberattacks internationally because it) Tj
T*
(can enable attackers to run malicious code and take control of the machine.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Logging/System Design Distributed Logging/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: Distributed Logging) Tj
T*
(## Logging) Tj
T*
(A log file records details of events occurring in a software application. The details may) Tj
T*
(consist of microservices, transactions, service actions, or anything helpful to debug the flow) Tj
T*
(of an event in the system. Logging is crucial to monitor the application’s flow.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Need for logging) Tj
T*
(Logging is essential in understanding the flow of an event in a distributed system. It seems) Tj
T*
(like a tedious task, but upon facing a failure or a security breach, logging helps pinpoint) Tj
T*
ET
