545 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 544 0 R >>
endobj
546 0 obj
<< /Length 3649 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(- Best-effort ordering) Tj
T*
(- Strict ordering) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(In a queue, the order of messages is implicitly associated with the incoming messages. Once the) Tj
T*
(messages are put in a queue, the same order is followed in the consumption and processing of) Tj
T*
(these messages.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For concurrent producers putting messages in the same queue, the order is not well defined) Tj
T*
(until producers provide order information—for example, timestamps or sequence numbers. Without) Tj
T*
(any ordering information, the queue puts messages in the queue in whatever order they arrive at) Tj
T*
(the service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For concurrent consumers fetching messages from the same queue, ordering can again become a) Tj
T*
(complicated issue. While the queue can hand over messages one after the other in the same order) Tj
T*
(as they were entered in the queue, two consumers almost concurrently processing two messages) Tj
T*
(might need an application-specific ordering mechanism. The queue might help by tagging a) Tj
T*
(message’s ordering information, sequence number or timestamp, while handing out a message from) Tj
T*
(the queue.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(### Best-effort ordering) Tj
T*
(With the best-effort ordering approach, the system puts the messages in a specified queue in) Tj
T*
(the same order that they’re received.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For example, as shown in the following figure, the producer sends four messages, A, B, C, and) Tj
T*
(D, in the same order as illustrated. Due to network congestion or some other issue, message B) Tj
T*
(is received after message D. Hence, the order of messages is A, C, D, and B at the receiving) Tj
T*
(end. Therefore, in this approach, the messages will be put in the queue in the same order they) Tj
T*
(were received instead of the order in which they were produced on the client side.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Best-effort ordering: Messages are placed in a queue in the same order that they’re received) Tj
T*
(and not in the order they were sent]\(./ordering.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Strict ordering) Tj
T*
(The strict ordering technique preserves the ordering of messages more rigorously. Through this) Tj
T*
(approach, messages are placed in a queue in the order that they’re produced.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Before putting messages in a queue in the correct sequence, it’s crucial to have a mechanism to) Tj
T*
(identify the order in which the messages were produced on the client side. Often, a unique) Tj
T*
(identifier or time-stamp is used to mark a message when it’s produced.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Who’ll be responsible for providing the sequence numbers?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The system provides essential libraries or APIs to the client to give sequence numbers to the) Tj
T*
(messages produced at the client side.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(One of the following three approaches can be used for ordering incoming messages:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Monotonically increasing numbers: One way to order incoming messages is to assign) Tj
T*
(monotonically increasing numbers to messages on the server side. When the first message) Tj
T*
(arrives, the system assigns it a number, such as 1. It then assigns the number 2 to the second) Tj
T*
(message, and so on.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(However, there are potential drawbacks to this approach. First, when a burst of requests is) Tj
T*
(received, it acts as a bottleneck that affects the system’s performance because the system has) Tj
T*
(to assign an ID in a specified sequence to a message while the other messages wait for their) Tj
T*
(turn.) Tj
T*
