() Tj
T*
(Second, it still doesn’t tackle the problem that arises when a message is received before the) Tj
T*
(one that’s produced earlier at the client side. Because of this, it doesn’t guarantee that it) Tj
T*
(will generate the correct order for the messages produced at the client side.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Causality-based sorting at the server side: Keeping in view the drawbacks of using) Tj
T*
(monotonically increasing numbers, another approach that can be used for time-stamping and) Tj
T*
(ordering of incoming messages is causality-based sorting. In this approach, messages are sorted) Tj
T*
(based on the time stamp that was produced at the client side and are put in a queue) Tj
T*
(accordingly. The major drawback of this approach is that for multiple client sessions, the) Tj
T*
(service can’t determine the order in terms of wall-clock time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Using time stamps based on synchronized clocks: To tackle the potential issues that arise) Tj
T*
(with both of the approaches described above, we can use another appropriate method to assign) Tj
T*
(time stamps to messages that’s based on synchronized clocks. In this approach, the time stamp) Tj
T*
(\(ID\) provided to each message through a synchronized clock is unique and in the correct) Tj
T*
(sequence of production of messages. We can tag a unique process identifier with the time stamp) Tj
T*
(to make the overall message identifier unique and tackle the situation when two concurrent) Tj
T*
(sessions ask for a time stamp at the exact same time. Moreover, with this approach, the server) Tj
T*
(can easily identify delayed messages based on the time stamp and wait for the delayed messages.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As we discussed in the section on the sequencer building block, we can get sequence numbers) Tj
T*
(that fulfill double duty as sequence numbers and globally synchronized wall clock time stamps.) Tj
T*
(Using this approach, our service can globally order messages across client sessions as well.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To conclude, the most appropriate mechanism to provide a unique ID or time stamp to incoming) Tj
T*
(messages, from among the three approaches described above, involves the use of synchronized) Tj
T*
(clocks.) Tj
T*
(#### Sorting) Tj
T*
(Once messages are received at the server side, we need to sort them based on their time stamps.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, we use an appropriate online sorting algorithm for this purpose.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Online sorting means that we need to keep some data sorted as new data keeps coming in.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Suppose that a message sent earlier arrives late due to a network delay. What would be the) Tj
T*
(proper approach to handle such a situation?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The simple solution in such cases is to reorder the queue. Two scenarios can arise from this.) Tj
T*
(First, reordering puts the messages in the correct order. Second, we’ve already handed out) Tj
T*
(newer messages to the consumers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If an old message comes when we’ve already handed out a newer message, we put it in a special) Tj
T*
(queue and the client handles that situation. If it were a best-effort queue, we could just put) Tj
T*
(such a message at the head of the queue.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
