(### High-level design) Tj
T*
(Before diving deep into the design, let’s assume the following points to make the discussion) Tj
T*
(more simple and easy to understand. In the upcoming material, we discuss how the following) Tj
T*
(assumptions enable us to eliminate the problems in a single-server solution to the messaging) Tj
T*
(queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Queue data is replicated using either a primary-secondary or quorum-like system inside a) Tj
T*
(cluster \(read through the Data Replication lesson for more details\). Our service can use data) Tj
T*
(partitioning if the queue gets too long to fit on a server. We can use a consistent hashing-) Tj
T*
(like scheme for this purpose, or we may use a key-value store where the key might be the) Tj
T*
(sequence numbers of the messages. In that case, each shard is appropriately replicated \(refer) Tj
T*
(to the Partition lesson for more details on this\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. We also assume that our system can auto-expand and auto-shrink the resources as per the need) Tj
T*
(to optimally utilize resources.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following figure demonstrates a high-level design of a distributed messaging queue that’s) Tj
T*
(composed of several components.) Tj
T*
() Tj
