(## Distributed Messaging Queue/Evaluation of a Distributed Messaging Queue’s Design/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Evaluation of a Distributed Messaging Queue’s Design) Tj
T*
(We completed the process of designing a distributed messaging queue. Now, let’s analyze whether) Tj
T*
(the design met the functional and non-functional requirements of a distributed messaging queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- Queue creation and deletion: When a request for a queue is received at the frontend, the) Tj
T*
(queue is created with all the necessary details provided by the client after undergoing some) Tj
T*
(essential checks. The corresponding cluster manager assigns servers to the newly created queue) Tj
T*
(and updates the information in the metadata stores and caches through a metadata service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Similarly, the queue is deleted when the client doesn’t need it anymore. The responsible) Tj
T*
(cluster manager deallocates the space occupied by the queue and, consequently, deletes the data) Tj
T*
(from all the metadata stores and caches.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(How do we handle messages that can’t be processed—here meaning consumed—after maximum) Tj
T*
(processing attempts by the consumer?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(A special type of queue, called a dead-letter queue, can be provided to handle messages that) Tj
T*
(aren’t consumed after the maximum number of processing attempts have been made by the consumer.) Tj
T*
(This type of queue is also used for keeping messages that can’t be processed successfully due) Tj
T*
(to the following factors:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The messages intended for a queue that doesn’t exist anymore.) Tj
T*
(- The queue length limit is exceeded, although this would rarely occur with our current design.) Tj
T*
(- The message expires due to per-message time to live \(TTL\).) Tj
T*
(A dead-letter queue is also important for determining the cause of failure and for identifying) Tj
T*
(faults in the system.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Send and receive messages: Producers can deliver messages to specific queues once they are) Tj
T*
(created. At the backend, receiving messages are sorted based on time stamps to preserve their) Tj
T*
(order and are placed in the queue. Similarly, a consumer can retrieve messages from a specified) Tj
T*
(queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(When a message is received from a producer for a specific queue, the frontend identifies the) Tj
T*
(primary host or cluster, depending on the replication model, where the queue resides. The) Tj
T*
(request is then forwarded to the corresponding entity and put in the queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Message deletion: Primarily, two options are used to delete a message from a queue.) Tj
T*
