() Tj
T*
(  - The first option is to not delete a message after it’s consumed. However, in this case, the) Tj
T*
(consumer is responsible for keeping track of what’s consumed. For this, we need to maintain the) Tj
T*
(order of messages in the queue and keep track of a message within a queue. A job can then) Tj
T*
(delete the message when the expiration conditions are met. Apache Kafka mostly uses this idea) Tj
T*
(where multiple processes can consume a message.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  - The second approach also doesn’t delete a message after it’s consumed. However, it’s made) Tj
T*
(invisible for some time via an attribute—for example, visibility_timeout. This way, the other) Tj
T*
(consumers are unable to get messages that have already been consumed. The message is then) Tj
T*
(deleted by the consumer via an API call.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In both cases, the message being retrieved by the consumer is only deleted by the consumer. The) Tj
T*
(reason behind this is to provide high durability if a consumer can’t process a message due to) Tj
T*
(some failure. In such a case, in the absence of a delete call, the consumer can retrieve the) Tj
T*
(message again when it comes back.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Moreover, this approach also provides at-least-once delivery semantic. For example, when a) Tj
T*
(worker fails to process the message, another worker can retrieve the message after it becomes) Tj
T*
(visible in the queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(at-least-once delivery semantic: In this type of delivery semantic, a message can be delivered) Tj
T*
(more than once. Most distributed systems implement this type of delivery semantic because it is) Tj
T*
(easier to implement. An underlying assumption is that the application will take care of) Tj
T*
(possible redundant deliveries and processing \(usually, duplicates do not hurt an application) Tj
T*
(because processing is idempotent\).) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What happens when the visibility timeout of a specific message expires and the consumer is) Tj
T*
(still busy processing the message?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The message becomes visible, and another worker can receive the message, thereby duplicating) Tj
T*
(the processing. To avoid such a situation, we ensure that the application sets a safe threshold) Tj
T*
(for visibility timeout.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- Durability: To achieve durability, the queues’ metadata is replicated on different nodes.) Tj
T*
(Similarly, when a message is received, it’s replicated in the queues that reside on different) Tj
T*
(nodes. Therefore, if a node fails, other nodes can be used to deliver or retrieve messages.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Scalability: Our design components, such as front-end servers, metadata servers, caches,) Tj
T*
(back-end clusters, and more are horizontally scalable. We can add to or remove their capacity) Tj
T*
(to match our needs. The scalability can be divided into two dimensions:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
( - Increase in the number of messages: When the number of messages touches a specific) Tj
T*
(limit—say, 80%—the specified queue is expanded. Similarly, the queue is shrunk when the number) Tj
T*
(of messages drops below a certain threshold.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
( - Increase in the number of queues: With an increasing number of queues, the demand for more) Tj
T*
(servers also increases, in which case the cluster manager is responsible for adding extra) Tj
T*
(servers. We commission nodes so that there is performance isolation between different queues.) Tj
T*
(An increased load on one queue shouldn’t impact other queues.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Availability: Our data components, metadata and actual messages, are properly replicated) Tj
T*
(inside or outside the data center, and the load balancer routes traffic around failed nodes.) Tj
T*
