T*
() Tj
T*
(- Performance: For better performance we use caches, data replication, and partitioning, which) Tj
T*
(reduces the data reads and writes time. Moreover, the best effort ordering strategy for) Tj
T*
(ordering messages is there to use to increase the throughput and lower the latency when it’s) Tj
T*
(necessary. In the case of strict ordering, we also suggest time-window based sorting to) Tj
T*
(potentially reduce the latency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(We discussed many subtleties in designing a FIFO queue in a distributed setting. We saw that) Tj
T*
(there is a trade-off between strict message production, message extraction orders, and) Tj
T*
(achievable throughput and latency. Relaxed ordering gives us a higher throughput and lower) Tj
T*
(latency. Asking for strict ordering forces the system to do extra work to enforce wall-clock or) Tj
T*
(causality-based ordering. We use different data stores with appropriate replication and) Tj
T*
(partitioning to scale with data. This design exercise highlights that a construct, a producer-) Tj
T*
(consumer queue, that’s simple to realize in a single-OS based system becomes much more) Tj
T*
(difficult in a distributed setting.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
