/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(A queue size is the maximum number of messages a queue can contain. For example, Amazon’s) Tj
T*
(standard queue size is unlimited. However, for inflight messages, the size is limited to) Tj
T*
(120,000.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Generally, most popular services like Amazon Simple Queue Service \(SQS\) and Microsoft Messaging) Tj
T*
(Queue \(MSMQ\) allow a maximum size of 256KB and 4MB, respectively.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(How do we send messages larger than the predefined size?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Various approaches are used to send messages larger than the specified allowed size. For) Tj
T*
(example, one approach is to divide the message into small chunks at the sender’s side and) Tj
T*
(combine it at the receiver’s side. However, this approach incurs extra complexity on both the) Tj
T*
(sender and receiver sides.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Amazon SQS provides extended libraries through which a user can send a message containing a) Tj
T*
(reference to a message payload in Amazon S3 by making calls to several APIs, including) Tj
T*
(AmazonSQSExtendedClient, ExtendedClientConfiguration, and so on. Similarly, Microsoft MSMQ) Tj
T*
(provides an MQSendLargeMessage API to send a larger message than 4MB as an XML document.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Therefore, we will overlook this discussion by assuming that our system supports one of the) Tj
T*
(strategies followed by Amazon or Microsoft.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(Our design of a distributed messaging queue should adhere to the following non-functional) Tj
T*
(requirements:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Durability: The data received by the system should be durable and shouldn’t be lost.) Tj
T*
(Producers and consumers can fail independently, and a queue with data durability is critical to) Tj
T*
(make the whole system work, because other entities are relying on the queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Scalability: The system needs to be scalable and capable of handling the increased load,) Tj
T*
(queues, producers, consumers, and the number of messages. Similarly, when the load reduces, the) Tj
T*
(system should be able to shrink the resources accordingly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Availability: The system should be highly available for receiving and sending messages. It) Tj
T*
(should continue operating uninterrupted, even after the failure of one or more of its) Tj
T*
(components.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Performance: The system should provide high throughput and low latency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Single-server messaging queue) Tj
T*
(Before we embark on our journey to map out the design of a distributed messaging queue, we) Tj
T*
(should recall how queues are used within a single server where the producer and consumer) Tj
T*
(processes are also on the same node. A producer or consumer can access a single-server queue by) Tj
T*
(acquiring the locking mechanism to avoid data inconsistency. The queue is considered a critical) Tj
T*
(section where only one entity, either the producer or consumer, can access the data at a time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(However, several aspects restrain us from using the single-server messaging queue in today’s) Tj
T*
(distributed systems paradigm. For example, it becomes unavailable to cooperating processes,) Tj
T*
(producers and consumers, in the event of hardware or network failures. Moreover, performance) Tj
T*
(takes a major hit as contention on the lock increases. Furthermore, it is neither scalable nor) Tj
T*
(durable.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
