T*
(or queues metadata.) Tj
T*
(- Load balancers are used to direct incoming requests to servers where the metadata is stored.) Tj
T*
(In our discussion on messaging queues, we focused on their functional and non-functional) Tj
T*
(requirements. Before moving on to the process of designing a distributed messaging queue, it’s) Tj
T*
(essential for us to discuss some key considerations and challenges that may affect the design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Messaging Queue/System Design The Distributed Messaging Queue/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: The Distributed Messaging Queue) Tj
T*
(## What is a messaging queue?) Tj
T*
(A messaging queue is an intermediate component between the interacting entities known as) Tj
T*
(producers and consumers. The producer produces messages and places them in the queue, while the) Tj
T*
(consumer retrieves the messages from the queue and processes them. There might be multiple) Tj
T*
(producers and consumers interacting with the queue at the same time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Here is an illustration of two applications interacting via a single messaging queue:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([An example of two applications interacting via a single messaging queue]\(./mq.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Motivation) Tj
T*
(A messaging queue has several advantages and use cases.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Improved performance: Improved performance: A messaging queue enables asynchronous) Tj
T*
(communication between the two interacting entities, producers and consumers, and eliminates) Tj
T*
(their relative speed difference. A producer puts messages in the queue without waiting for the) Tj
T*
(consumers. Similarly, a consumer processes the messages when they become available. Moreover,) Tj
T*
(queues are often used to separate out slower operations from the critical path and, therefore,) Tj
T*
(help reduce client-perceived latency. For example, instead of waiting for a specific task) Tj
T*
(that’s taking a long time to complete, the producer process sends a message, which is kept in a) Tj
T*
(queue if there are multiple requests, for the required task and continues its operations. The) Tj
T*
(consumer can notify us about the fate of the processing, whether a success or failure, by using) Tj
T*
(another queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Better reliability: The separation of interacting entities via a messaging queue makes the) Tj
T*
(system more fault tolerant. For example, a producer or consumer can fail independently without) Tj
T*
(affecting the others and restart later. Moreover, replicating the messaging queue on multiple) Tj
T*
(servers ensures the system’s availability if one or more servers are down.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Granular scalability: Asynchronous communication makes the system more scalable. For example,) Tj
T*
(many processes can communicate via a messaging queue. In addition, when the number of requests) Tj
T*
(increases, we distribute the workload across several consumers. So, an application is in full) Tj
T*
(control to tweak the number of producer or consumer processes according to its current need.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Easy decoupling: A messaging queue decouples dependencies among different entities in a) Tj
T*
(system. The interacting entities communicate via messages and are kept unaware of each other’s) Tj
T*
(internal working mechanisms.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Rate limiting: Messaging queues also help absorb any load spikes and prevent services from) Tj
T*
(becoming overloaded, acting as a rudimentary form of rate limiting when there is a need to) Tj
T*
(avoid dropping any incoming request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Priority queue: Multiple queues can be used to implement different priorities—for example,) Tj
T*
(one queue for each priority—and give more service time to a higher priority queue.) Tj
T*
(### Messaging queue use cases) Tj
T*
ET
