(## Monitoring: metrics and alerting) Tj
T*
(A good monitoring system needs to clearly define what to measure and in what units \(metrics\).) Tj
T*
(The monitoring system also needs to define threshold values of all metrics and the ability to) Tj
T*
(inform appropriate stakeholders \(alerts\) when values are out of acceptable ranges. Knowing the) Tj
T*
(state of our infrastructure and systems ensures service stability. The support team can respond) Tj
T*
(to issues more quickly and confidently if they have access to information on the health and) Tj
T*
(performance of the deployments. Monitoring systems that collect measurements, show data, and) Tj
T*
(send warnings when something appears wrong are helpful for the support team.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To further understand metrics, alerts, and their connection with monitoring, we’ll go over) Tj
T*
(their significance, their potential benefits, and the data we might want to keep track of.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What are the conventional approaches to handle failures in IT infrastructure?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The two conventional approaches to handling IT infrastructure failures are the reactive and) Tj
T*
(proactive approaches.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the reactive approach, corrective action is taken after the failure occurs. In this) Tj
T*
(approach, even if DevOps takes quick action to find the cause of the error and promptly handle) Tj
T*
(the failures, it causes downtime. As a result, there will be system downtime in the reactive) Tj
T*
(approach, which is generally undesirable for continuously running applications.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In a proactive approach, proactive actions are taken before failure occurs. Therefore, it) Tj
T*
(prevents downtimes and associated losses. The proactive approach works on predicting system) Tj
T*
(failures to take corrective action to avoid the failure. This approach offers better) Tj
T*
(reliability by preventing downtime.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In modern services, completely avoiding problems is not possible. Something is always failing) Tj
T*
(inside huge data centers and network deployments. The goal is to find the impending problems) Tj
T*
(early on and design systems in such a way that service faults are invisible to the end users.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Metrics) Tj
T*
(Metrics objectively define what we should measure and what units will be appropriate. Metric) Tj
T*
(values provide an insight into the system at any point in time For example, a web server’s) Tj
T*
(ability to handle a certain amount of traffic per second or its ability to join a pool of web) Tj
T*
(servers are examples of high-level data correlated with a component’s specific purpose or) Tj
T*
(activity. Another example can be measuring network performance in terms of throughput \(megabits) Tj
T*
(per second\) and latency \(round-trip time\). We need to collect values of metrics with minimal) Tj
T*
(performance penalty. We may use user-perceived latency or the amount of computational resources) Tj
T*
(to measure this penalty.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Values that track how many physical resources our operating system uses can be a good starting) Tj
T*
(point. If we have a monitoring system in place, we don’t have to do much additional work to get) Tj
T*
(data regarding processor load, CPU statistics like cache hits and misses, RAM usage by OS and) Tj
T*
(processes, page faults, disc space, disc read and write latencies, swap space usage, and so on.) Tj
T*
(Metrics provided by many web servers, database servers, and other software help us determine) Tj
T*
(whether everything is running smoothly or not.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
