(### Populate the metrics) Tj
T*
(The metrics should be logically centralized for global monitoring and alerting purposes.) Tj
T*
(Fetching metrics is crucial to the monitoring system. Metrics can either be pushed or pulled) Tj
T*
(into a monitoring system, depending on the preference of the user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Here, we confront a fundamental design challenge now: Do we utilize push or pull? Should the) Tj
T*
(server proactively send the metrics’ values out, or should it only expose an endpoint and wait) Tj
T*
(reactively for an inquiry?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In pull strategy, each monitored server merely needs to store the metrics in memory and send) Tj
T*
(them to an exposed endpoint. The exposed endpoint allows the monitoring application to fetch) Tj
T*
(the metrics itself. Servers sending too much data or sending data too frequently can’t overload) Tj
T*
(the monitoring system. The monitoring system will pull data as per its own schedule.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In other situations, though, pushing may be beneficial, such as when a firewall prevents the) Tj
T*
(monitoring system from accessing the server directly. The monitoring system has the ability to) Tj
T*
(adjust a global configuration about the data to be collected and the interval at which servers) Tj
T*
(and switches should push the data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Push and pull terminologies might be confusing. Whenever we discuss push or pull strategy,) Tj
T*
(we’ll consider it from the monitoring system’s perspective. That is, either the system will) Tj
T*
(pull the metrics values from the applications, or the metrics will be pushed to the monitoring) Tj
T*
(system. To avoid confusion, we’ll stick to the monitoring system’s viewpoint.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
