T*
(# Design of a Distributed Search) Tj
T*
(## High-level design) Tj
T*
(Let’s shape the overall design of a distributed search system before getting into a detailed) Tj
T*
(discussion. There are two phases of such a system, as shown in the illustration below. The) Tj
T*
(offline phase involves data crawling and indexing in which the user has to do nothing. The) Tj
T*
(online phase consists of searching for results against the search query by the user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([High-level design of a distributed search system]\(./design.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The crawler collects content from the intended resource. For example, if we build a search) Tj
T*
(for a YouTube application, the crawler will crawl through all of the videos on YouTube and) Tj
T*
(extract textual content for each video. The content could be the title of the video, its) Tj
T*
(description, the channel name, or maybe even the video’s annotation to enable an intelligent) Tj
T*
(search based not only on the title and description but also on the content of that video. The) Tj
T*
(crawler formats the extracted content for each video in a JSON document and stores these JSON) Tj
T*
(documents in a distributed storage.) Tj
T*
(- The indexer fetches the documents from a distributed storage and indexes these documents) Tj
T*
(using MapReduce, which runs on a distributed cluster of commodity machines. The indexer uses a) Tj
T*
(distributed data processing system like MapReduce for parallel and distributed index) Tj
T*
(construction. The constructed index table is stored in the distributed storage.) Tj
T*
(- The distributed storage is used to store the documents and the index.) Tj
T*
(- The user enters the search string that contains multiple words in the search bar.) Tj
T*
(- The searcher parses the search string, searches for the mappings from the index that are) Tj
T*
(stored in the distributed storage, and returns the most matched results to the user. The) Tj
T*
ET
