(## Detailed discussion) Tj
T*
(Since the indexer is the core component in a search system, we discussed an indexing technique) Tj
T*
(and the problems associated with centralized indexing in the previous lesson. In this lesson,) Tj
T*
(we consider a distributed solution for indexing and searching.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Distributed indexing and searching) Tj
T*
(Let’s see how we can develop a distributed indexing and searching system. We understand that) Tj
T*
(the input to an indexing system is the documents we created during crawling. To develop an) Tj
T*
(index in a distributed fashion, we employ a large number of low-cost machines \(nodes\) and) Tj
T*
(partition or divide the documents based on the resources they have. All the nodes are) Tj
T*
(connected. A group of nodes is called a cluster.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(For performing distributed indexing, the machines in the cluster typically have dual-processor) Tj
T*
(x86 processors running Linux, with 2–4 GB of memory per machine. It is not necessary that all) Tj
T*
(machines are of the same specification, though they should be somewhat comparable. The) Tj
T*
(MapReduce framework is smart enough to give more work to stronger machines.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We use numerous small nodes for indexing to achieve cost efficiency. This process requires us) Tj
T*
(to partition or split the input data \(documents\) among these nodes. However, a key question) Tj
T*
(needs to be addressed: How do we perform this partitioning?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The two most common techniques used for data partitioning in distributed indexing are these) Tj
T*
(below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Document partitioning: In document partitioning, all the documents collected by the web) Tj
T*
(crawler are partitioned into subsets of documents. Each node then performs indexing on a subset) Tj
T*
(of documents that are assigned to it.) Tj
T*
(- Term partitioning: The dictionary of all terms is partitioned into subsets, with each subset) Tj
T*
(residing at a single node. For example, a subset of documents is processed and indexed by a) Tj
T*
(node containing the term “search.”) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
