BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
([Types of data partitioning in a distributed search]\(./types.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In term partitioning, a search query is sent to the nodes that correspond to the query terms.) Tj
T*
(This provides more concurrency because a stream of search queries with different query terms) Tj
T*
(will be served by different nodes. However, term partitioning turns out to be a difficult task) Tj
T*
(in practice. Multiword queries necessitate sending long mapping lists between groups of nodes) Tj
T*
(for merging, which can be more expensive than the benefits from the increased concurrency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Mapping lists: A list of documents in which the term appeared with the frequency of the term in) Tj
T*
(each document.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In document partitioning, each query is distributed across all nodes, and the results from) Tj
T*
(these nodes are merged before being shown to the user. This method of partitioning necessitates) Tj
T*
(less inter-node communication. In our design, we use document partitioning.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Following document partitioning, let’s look into a distributed design for index construction) Tj
T*
(and querying, which is shown in the illustration below. We use a cluster that consists of a) Tj
T*
(number of low-cost nodes and a cluster manager. The cluster manager uses a MapReduce) Tj
T*
(programming model to parallelize the index’s computation on each partition. MapReduce can work) Tj
T*
(on significantly larger datasets that are difficult to be handled by a single large server.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Distributed indexing and searching in a parallel fashion on multiple nodes in a cluster of) Tj
T*
(commodity machines]\(./search_and_index.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The system described above works as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Indexing**) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- We have a document set already collected by the crawler.) Tj
T*
(- The cluster manager splits the input document set into N number of partitions, where N is) Tj
T*
(equal to three in the illustration above. The size of each partition is decided by the cluster) Tj
T*
(manager given the size of the data, the computation, memory limits, and the number of nodes in) Tj
T*
(the cluster. All the nodes may not be available for various reasons. The cluster manager) Tj
T*
(monitors the health of each node through periodic heartbeats. To assign a document to one of) Tj
T*
(the N partitions, a hashing function can be utilized.) Tj
T*
(- After making partitions, the cluster manager runs indexing algorithms for all the N) Tj
T*
(partitions simultaneously on the N number of nodes in a cluster. Each indexing process produces) Tj
T*
(a tiny inverted index, which is stored on the node’s local storage. In this way, we produce N) Tj
T*
(tiny inverted indices rather than one large inverted index.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Searching**) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- In the search phase, when a user query comes in, we run parallel searches on each tiny) Tj
T*
(inverted index stored on the nodes’ local storage generating N queries.) Tj
T*
(- The search result from each inverted tiny index is a mapping list against the queried term) Tj
T*
(\(we assume a single word/term user query\). The merger aggregates these mapping lists.) Tj
T*
(- After aggregating the mapping lists, the merger sorts the list of documents from the) Tj
T*
(aggregated mapping list based on the frequency of the term in each document.) Tj
T*
(- The sorted list of documents is returned to the user as a search result. The documents are) Tj
T*
(shown in sorted \(ascending\) order to the user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: We’ve designed a search system where we utilized a distributed system and parallelized) Tj
T*
(the indexing and searching process. This helped us handle large datasets by working on the) Tj
T*
(smaller partitions of documents. It should be noted that both searching and indexing are) Tj
T*
(performed on the same node. We refer to this idea as colocation.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
