T*
(The proposed design works, and we can replicate it across the globe in various data centers to) Tj
T*
(facilitate all users. Thus, we can achieve the following advantages:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Our design will not be subject to a single point of failure \(SPOF\).) Tj
T*
(- Latency for all users will remain small.) Tj
T*
(- Maintenance and upgrades in individual data centers will be possible.) Tj
T*
(- Scalability \(serving more users per second\) of our system will be improved.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Replication) Tj
T*
(We make replicas of the indexing nodes that produce inverted indices for the assigned) Tj
T*
(partitions. We can answer a query from several sets of nodes with replicas. The overall concept) Tj
T*
(is simple. We continue to use the same architecture as before, but instead of having only one) Tj
T*
(group of nodes, we have R groups of nodes to answer user queries. R is the number of replicas.) Tj
T*
(The number of replicas can expand or shrink based on the number of requests, and each group of) Tj
T*
(nodes has all the partitions required to answer each query.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each group of nodes is hosted on different availability zones for better performance and) Tj
T*
(availability of the system in case a data center fails.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: A load balancer component is necessary to spread the queries across different groups of) Tj
T*
(nodes and retry in case of any error.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Availability zones \(AZs\) are isolated locations within data center regions where public cloud) Tj
T*
(services are launched and operated. Regions are geographic locations where data centers for) Tj
T*
(public cloud service providers are located.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Replication factor and replica distribution) Tj
T*
(Generally, a replication factor of three is enough. A replication factor of three means three) Tj
T*
(nodes host the same partition and produce the index. One of the three nodes becomes the primary) Tj
T*
(node, while the other two are replicas. Each of these nodes produces indexes in the same order) Tj
T*
(to converge on the same state.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To illustrate, let’s divide the data, a document set, into four partitions. Since the) Tj
T*
(replication factor is three, one partition will be hosted by three nodes. We’ll assume that) Tj
T*
(there are two availability zones \(AZ1 and AZ2\). And in each availability zone, we have two) Tj
T*
(nodes. Each node acts as a primary for only one partition \(For example, Node 1 in AZ1 is the) Tj
T*
(primary node for partition P1\). The three copies \(pink, blue, and purple\) for a partition are) Tj
T*
(shared between the two AZ) Tj
T*
(AZ instances so that two copies are in one zone and the third copy is in another zone. Three) Tj
T*
(colors represent three replicas of each partition. For example, the following is true for) Tj
T*
(partition P4:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The first replica, represented by the color pink, is placed in Node 2 of AZ2) Tj
T*
(- The second replica, represented by the color blue, is placed in Node 1 of AZ2) Tj
T*
(- The third replica, represented by the color purple, is placed in Node 2 of AZ1) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each group in the illustration below consists of one replica from all of the four partitions) Tj
T*
(\(P1, P2, P3, P4\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The replica distribution: Each node contains one primary partition and two) Tj
T*
(replicas]\(./replica_distribution.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the above illustration, the primary replica for P_1 is indicated by the dark purple color,) Tj
T*
(the primary replica for P_2 is represented by the dark blue color, and the primary replica for) Tj
T*
(P_3 and P_4 is represented by the dark pink color.) Tj
T*
