(#### Inverted index) Tj
T*
(An inverted index is a HashMap-like data structure that employs a document-term matrix. Instead) Tj
T*
(of storing the complete document as it is, it splits the documents into individual words. After) Tj
T*
(this, the document-term matrix identifies unique words and discards frequently occurring words) Tj
T*
(like “to,” “they,” “the,” “is,” and so on. Frequently occurring words like those are called) Tj
T*
(terms. The document-term matrix maintains a term-level index through this identification of) Tj
T*
(unique words and deletion of unnecessary terms.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For each term, the index computes the following information:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- The list of documents in which the term appeared.) Tj
T*
(- The frequency with which the term appears in each document.) Tj
T*
(- The position of the term in each document.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Inverted Index) Tj
T*
(Term                     Mapping \( [doc], [freq], [[loc]\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
