T*
(Inverted index is one of the most popular index mechanisms used in document retrieval. It) Tj
T*
(enables efficient implementation of boolean, extended boolean, proximity, relevance, and many) Tj
T*
(other types of search algorithms.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Advantages of using an inverted index**) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- An inverted index facilitates full-text searches.) Tj
T*
(- An inverted index reduces the time of counting the occurrence of a word in each document at) Tj
T*
(the run time because we have mappings against each term.) Tj
T*
(Disadvantages of using an inverted index) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- There is storage overhead for maintaining the inverted index along with the actual documents.) Tj
T*
(However, we reduce the search time.) Tj
T*
(- Maintenance costs \(processing\) on adding, updating, or deleting a document. To add a) Tj
T*
(document, we extract terms from the document. Then, for each extracted term, we either add a) Tj
T*
(new row in the inverted index or update an existing one if that term already has an entry in) Tj
T*
(the inverted index. Similarly, for deleting a document, we conduct processing to find the) Tj
T*
(entries in the inverted index for the deleted document’s terms and update the inverted index) Tj
T*
(accordingly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Boolean searching is based on a symbolic logic method established by George Boole, an English) Tj
T*
(mathematician. It allows you to limit, broaden, or define your search by combining words and) Tj
T*
(phrases with the words AND, OR, and NOT \(also known as Boolean operators\). Source: Wikipedia) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Extended Boolean search overcomes the disadvantages of the Boolean search. The main) Tj
T*
(disadvantage of the boolean search is that it doesn’t incorporate term weights in boolean) Tj
T*
(queries, producing either too small or too big result set. Extended boolean search makes use of) Tj
T*
(partial matching and term weights. It assigns a score to the similarity between queries and) Tj
T*
(documents. This way, a document may be relevant if it matches part of the searched terms and is) Tj
T*
(returned as a result, whereas it was not in the Boolean search. Source: Wikipedia\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The proximity search finds documents wherein two or more term occurrences that are separately) Tj
T*
(matching are within a certain distance, where distance is the number of intermediate words or) Tj
T*
(characters. Source: Wikipedia\)) Tj
T*
