(operations on the same machine can lead to an imbalance, and it results in scalability issues.) Tj
T*
(2. Index recomputation: We assume that each replica will compute the index individually, which) Tj
T*
(leads to inefficient usage of resources. Furthermore, index computation is a resource-intensive) Tj
T*
(task with possibly hundreds of stages of pipelined operations. Thus, recomputing the same index) Tj
T*
(over different replicas requires powerful machines. Instead, the logical approach is to compute) Tj
T*
(the index once and replicate it across availability zones.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Because of these key reasons, we’ll look at an alternative approach for distributed indexing) Tj
T*
(and searching.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Solution) Tj
T*
(Rather than recomputing the index on each replica, we compute the inverted index on the primary) Tj
T*
(node only. Next, we communicate the inverted index \(binary blob/file\) to the replicas. The key) Tj
T*
(benefit of this approach is that it avoids using the duplicated amount of CPU and memory for) Tj
T*
(indexing on replicas.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What are the disadvantages of the above-proposed solution?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Since the inverted index will be transferred to the replicas, this will introduce a) Tj
T*
(transmission latency to copy the inverted index file because the size of the index file can be) Tj
T*
(very large.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(When the primary node receives new indexing operations, the inverted index file changes. Each) Tj
T*
(replica needs to fetch the latest version of the file after a certain amount of indexing) Tj
T*
(operations reaches a defined threshold.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Separate the indexing and search) Tj
T*
(With the advent of networking and virtualization technologies, cloud computing has emerged as a) Tj
T*
(successful technology. We have access to massive amounts of bandwidth \(up to 100 Gbps\) and) Tj
T*
(scalable distributed storage in such a technology. These advancements allow for a strong) Tj
T*
(separation between indexing and search without the negative consequence of indexing latency.) Tj
T*
(Because of this isolation, indexing wouldn’t affect search scalability and vice versa. Also,) Tj
T*
(instead of recomputing the index on the replica nodes, which wastes resources, we can just) Tj
T*
(replicate the index files.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll use these technologies to redesign our distributed indexing and searching system. There) Tj
T*
(are three components involved in this search system design:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Indexer: It consists of a group of nodes to compute the index.) Tj
T*
(2. Distributed storage: This system is used to store partitions and the computed index.) Tj
T*
(3. Searcher: It consists of a number of nodes to perform searching.) Tj
T*
(The illustration below depicts the generation and transfer of an inverted index between an) Tj
T*
(indexer and a searcher node:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The indices produced by the indexing nodes are stored on the distributed storage, and the) Tj
T*
(nodes involved in the search reads indices from the distributed storage to produce a result for) Tj
T*
(the user's query]\(./query.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the above illustration, a single node is shown for each indexing and searching operation.) Tj
T*
(But, in reality, there would be an N number of nodes in the indexing phase, one node per) Tj
T*
(partition \(set of documents\), that produces inverted indices. The inverted index is stored in) Tj
T*
(the form of binary files on the nodes’ local storage. Caching these blob files will result in) Tj
T*
(performance improvement. These binary files are also pushed to a distributed storage. In the) Tj
T*
(case of a hardware failure, a new searcher or indexer machine is added, and a copy of the data) Tj
T*
(is retrieved from the distributed storage.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(When the upload is complete, the searcher nodes download the index files. Depending upon user) Tj
T*
(search patterns, the searching nodes will maintain a cache of frequently asked queries and) Tj
T*
