(## Indexing explained) Tj
T*
(Until now, we have explained the development of a highly scalable and performant design using) Tj
T*
(low-cost nodes. However, we are unaware of the internals of the indexing nodes. In this) Tj
T*
(section, we’ll learn how indexing is performed with a MapReduce distributed model and parallel) Tj
T*
(processing framework.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The MapReduce framework is implemented with the help of a cluster manager and a set of worker) Tj
T*
(nodes categorized as Mappers and Reducers. As indicated by its name, MapReduce is composed of) Tj
T*
(two phases:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. The Map phase) Tj
T*
(2. The Reduction phase) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Furthermore, the input to MapReduce is a number of partitions, or set of documents, whereas its) Tj
T*
(output is an aggregated inverted index.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s understand the purpose of the above components:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Cluster manager: The manager initiates the process by assigning a set of partitions to) Tj
T*
(Mappers. Once the Mappers are done, the cluster manager assigns the output of Mappers to) Tj
T*
(Reducers.) Tj
T*
(- Mappers: This component extracts and filters terms from the partitions assigned to it by the) Tj
T*
(cluster manager. These machines output inverted indexes in parallel, which serve as input to) Tj
T*
(the Reducers.) Tj
T*
(- Reducers: The reducer combines mappings for various terms to generate a summarized index.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The cluster manager ensures that all worker nodes are efficiently utilized in the cluster. The) Tj
T*
(MapReduce is built to work under partial failures. If one node fails, it reschedules the work) Tj
T*
(on another node.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Note that the Reducers cannot start as long as the Mappers are working. This means that the) Tj
T*
(cluster manager can use the same node as a Mapper as well as a Reducer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The slides below depict a simplified setup of how MapReduce can be used to generate an inverted) Tj
T*
(index:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Indexing]\(./indexing\)) Tj
