(## Distributed Search/System Design The Distributed Search/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: The Distributed Search) Tj
T*
(## Why do we need a search system?) Tj
T*
(Nowadays, we see a search bar on almost every website. We use that search bar to pick out) Tj
T*
(relevant content from the enormous amount of content available on that website. A search bar) Tj
T*
(enables us to quickly find what we’re looking for. For example, there are plenty of courses) Tj
T*
(present on the Educative website. If we didn’t have a search feature, users would have to) Tj
T*
(scroll through many pages and read the name of each course to find the one they’re looking for.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s take another example. There are billions of videos uploaded and stored on YouTube.) Tj
T*
(Imagine if YouTube didn’t provide us with a search bar. How would we find a specific video) Tj
T*
(among the millions of videos that have been posted on YouTube over the years? It would take) Tj
T*
(months to navigate through all of those videos and find the one we need. Users find it) Tj
T*
(challenging to find what they’re looking for simply by scrolling around.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Search bar]\(./searchBar.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Search engines are an even bigger example. We have billions of websites on the Internet. Each) Tj
T*
(website has many web pages and there is plenty of content on each of these web pages. With so) Tj
T*
(much content, the Internet would practically be useless without search engines, and users would) Tj
T*
(end up lost in a sea of irrelevant data. Search engines are, essentially, filters for the) Tj
T*
(massive amount of data available. They let users quickly obtain information that is of true) Tj
T*
(interest without having to sift through too many unnecessary web pages.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Behind every search bar, there is a search system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## What is a search system?) Tj
T*
(A search system is a system that takes some text input, a search query, from the user and) Tj
T*
(returns the relevant content in a few seconds or less. There are three main components of a) Tj
T*
(search system, namely:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A crawler, which fetches content and creates documents.) Tj
T*
(- An indexer, which builds a searchable index.) Tj
T*
(- A searcher, which responds to search queries by running the search query on the index created) Tj
T*
(by the indexer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(For a search engine, a document consists of the text extracted from a web page. In a movie) Tj
T*
(store’s web page, a document could be a JSON object containing titles, descriptions, and other) Tj
T*
(metadata of the videos upon which we want to perform search queries. The documents could be) Tj
T*
(JSON or any other suitable format. Documents are stored on a distributed storage like S3 or) Tj
T*
(HDFS.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The three phases of a search system]\(./3phases.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Note: We have a separate chapter dedicated to the explanation of the crawler component. In this) Tj
T*
(chapter, we’ll focus on indexing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## How will we design a distributed search system?) Tj
T*
(We divided the design of a distributed search system into five lessons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Requirements: In this lesson, we list the functional and non-functional requirements of a) Tj
T*
(distributed search system. We also estimate our system’s resources, such as servers, storage,) Tj
T*
(and the bandwidth needed to serve a number of queries.) Tj
T*
(2. Indexing: This lesson provides us with background knowledge on the process of indexing with) Tj
T*
(the help of an example. After discussing indexing, we also look into a centralized architecture) Tj
T*
(of distributed search systems.) Tj
T*
(3. Initial design: This lesson consists of the high-level design of our system, its API, and) Tj
T*
