(requirements.) Tj
T*
(Let’s start by understanding the requirements of designing a distributed search system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Task Scheduler/Design Considerations of a Distributed Task Scheduler/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Design Considerations of a Distributed Task Scheduler) Tj
T*
(## Queueing) Tj
T*
(A distributed queue is a major building block used by a scheduler. The simplest scheduling) Tj
T*
(approach is to push the task into the queue on a first-come, first-served basis. If there are) Tj
T*
(10,000 nodes \(resources\) in a cluster \(cloud\), the task scheduler quickly extracts tasks from) Tj
T*
(the queue and schedules them on the nodes. But, if all the resources are currently busy, then) Tj
T*
(tasks will need to wait in the queue, and small tasks might need to wait longer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This scheduling mechanism can affect the reliability of the system, availability of the system,) Tj
T*
(and priority of tasks. There could be cases where we want urgent execution of a task—for) Tj
T*
(example, a task that notifies a user that their account was accessed from an unrecognized) Tj
T*
(device. So, we can’t rely only on the first-come, first-serve to schedule tasks. Instead, we) Tj
T*
(categorize the tasks and set appropriate priorities. We have the following three categories for) Tj
T*
(our tasks:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Tasks that can’t be delayed.) Tj
T*
(- Tasks that can be delayed.) Tj
T*
(- Tasks that need to be executed periodically \(for example, every 5 minutes, or every hour, or) Tj
T*
(every day\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Multiple queues based on the task categories]\(./multiqueues.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Our system ensures that tasks in non-urgent queues are not starved. As soon as some task’s) Tj
T*
(delay limit is about to be reached, it is moved to the urgent tasks queue so that it gets) Tj
T*
(service. We’ll see how the task scheduler implements priorities later in this lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s explore some parameters that help the scheduler efficiently utilize resources and provide) Tj
T*
(reliable service to the users.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Execution cap) Tj
T*
(Some tasks take very long to execute and occupy the resource blocking other tasks. The) Tj
T*
(execution cap is an important parameter to consider while scheduling tasks. If we completely) Tj
T*
(allocate a resource to a single task and wait for that task’s completion, some tasks might not) Tj
T*
(halt because of a bug in the task script that doesn’t let it finish its execution. We let the) Tj
T*
(clients set the execution cap for their tasks. After that specified time, we should stop task) Tj
T*
(execution, release the resource, and allocate it to the next task in the queue. If the task) Tj
T*
(execution stops due to the execution cap limit, our system notifies the respective clients of) Tj
T*
(these instances. The client needs to do appropriate remedial actions for such cases.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If clients don’t set the execution cap, the scheduler uses its default upper bound on the) Tj
T*
(maximum allowed time to kill the tasks. Suppose a task actually takes longer—for example, if we) Tj
T*
(are training a machine learning model. In that case, the scheduler might need to pause and) Tj
T*
(resume a task many times to accommodate other tasks. It wouldn’t be fair to the short task that) Tj
T*
(has to wait for two days to use a resource for two seconds.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cloud providers can’t let a task execute for an unlimited time for a basic \(free\) account,) Tj
T*
(because using their resources costs a certain fee to the providers. To handle such cases,) Tj
T*
(clients are informed about maximum usage limits so that they can handle long task execution.) Tj
T*
(For example, clients may design their task in such a way that they checkpoint after some time) Tj
T*
(and load from that state to resume progress in case resources are taken from the client due to) Tj
T*
(usage limit.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
