T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What if a long task is 90% executed, but before it completes, the machine that was executing) Tj
T*
(this task fails?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The task scheduler will re-execute the task on some other machine. Tasks need to be either) Tj
T*
(idempotent, which is discussed later in the lesson, or they should be able to restore their) Tj
T*
(state from a previous checkpoint. Once the state is saved, we can resume that task’s execution) Tj
T*
(on any other machine. This makes our system fault tolerant and saves our resources.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Prioritization) Tj
T*
(There are tasks that need urgent execution. For example, in a social application like Facebook,) Tj
T*
(the users can mark themselves safe during an emergency situation, such as an earthquake. The) Tj
T*
(tasks that carry out this activity should be executed in a timely manner, otherwise this) Tj
T*
(feature would be useless to Facebook users. Sending an email notification to the customers that) Tj
T*
(their account was debited a certain amount of money is another example of tasks that require) Tj
T*
(urgent execution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To prioritize the tasks, the task scheduler maintains a delay tolerance parameter for each task) Tj
T*
(and executes the task close to its delay tolerance. Delay tolerance is the maximum amount of) Tj
T*
(time a task execution could be delayed. The task that has the shortest delay tolerance time is) Tj
T*
(executed first. By using a delay tolerance parameter, we can postpone the tasks with longer) Tj
T*
(delay tolerance values to make room for urgent tasks during peak times.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(How do we determine the value of delay tolerance?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Since there are different categories of tasks in various applications, the application owners) Tj
T*
(or clients can set or automate the values themselves, depending upon the task category. For) Tj
T*
(example, in a social media application like Facebook, we can generate a newsfeed, suggest) Tj
T*
(friends, allow users to mark themselves safe after a disaster, send notifications about a live) Tj
T*
(stream event, and many more. Out of the listed tasks, the priority tasks should be to mark a) Tj
T*
(person safe during an earthquake and send notifications about live stream events. Clients can) Tj
T*
(tighten the delay tolerance values of these tasks down to milliseconds or a few seconds while) Tj
T*
(tasks like suggesting friends can be delayed for days.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The task scheduling system itself can set the delay tolerance value depending on the task) Tj
T*
(category and its severity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There are different costs for different priorities \(there are usually higher costs for high-) Tj
T*
(priority tasks, for example\) so that customers can carefully categorize their tasks.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Resource capacity optimization) Tj
T*
(There could be a time when resources are close to the overload threshold \(for example, above) Tj
T*
(80% utilization\). This is called peak time. The same resource may be idle during off-peak) Tj
T*
(times. So, we have to think about better utilization of the resources during off-peak times and) Tj
T*
(how to keep resources available during peak times.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There are tasks that don’t need urgent execution. For example, in a social application like) Tj
T*
(Facebook, suggesting friends is not an urgent task. We can make a separate queue for tasks like) Tj
T*
(this and execute them in off-peak times. If we consistently have more work to do than the) Tj
T*
(available resources, we might have a capacity problem, and to solve that, we should commission) Tj
T*
(more resources. A cloud provider needs to have a target resources-to-demand ratio. When demand) Tj
T*
(starts increasing, the ratio will move towards 0. If the ratio starts changing over time, the) Tj
T*
ET
