(provider might decide to commission more or fewer resources.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Task idempotency) Tj
T*
(If the task executes successfully, but for some reason the machine fails to send an) Tj
T*
(acknowledgement, the scheduler will schedule the task again. The task is executed again, and we) Tj
T*
(end up with the wrong result, which means the task was non-idempotent. An example of non-) Tj
T*
(idempotence is shown in the following illustration:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([non-idempotent]\(./nonidempotency\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We don’t want the final result to change when executing the task again. This is critical in) Tj
T*
(financial applications while transferring money. We require that tasks are idempotent. An) Tj
T*
(idempotent task produces the same result, no matter how many times we execute it. The execution) Tj
T*
(of an idempotent task is shown in the following illustration:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Idempotence is a characteristic of certain operations in mathematics and computer science that) Tj
T*
(allows them to be applied several times without influencing the outcome.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([idempotent]\(./idempotent\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s make the task of uploading a video to the database an idempotent operation. We don’t want) Tj
T*
(the video to be duplicated in the database in case the uploader didn’t receive the) Tj
T*
(acknowledgment. Idempotency ensures that the video is not duplicated. This property is added in) Tj
T*
(the implementation by the developers where they identify the video by something \(for example,) Tj
T*
(its name\) and overwrite the old one. This way, no matter how many times someone uploads it, the) Tj
T*
(final result is the same. Idempotency enables us to simply re-execute a failed task.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(How should we handle task execution that can never be completed because of an infinite loop in) Tj
T*
(the payload of that task?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(We need to mark and kill such tasks. So, for this purpose, we can set time limits. If it takes) Tj
T*
(more than the specified execution cap value, we can kill the task. But it’s challenging to) Tj
T*
(differentiate between a buggy task and a long task. We can handle it at the application level) Tj
T*
(where the clients take care of the long tasks by saving the state at different times. Clients) Tj
T*
(can also resume from that state if the task scheduler kills that task, assuming that the task) Tj
T*
(contains an infinite loop.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Schedule and execute untrusted tasks) Tj
T*
(Before proceeding, let’s ask ourselves a question: What are the untrusted tasks, and how should) Tj
T*
(we manage them?) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
