T*
(# Design of a Distributed Task Scheduler) Tj
T*
(## Components) Tj
T*
(We can consider scheduling at many levels. We could be asked to design scheduling that is done) Tj
T*
(internally by an organization to run tasks on their own cluster of machines. There, they have) Tj
T*
(to find ample resources and need to decide which task to run first.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(On the other hand, we could also be asked to design scheduling that a cloud provider uses to) Tj
T*
(schedule tasks coming from multiple clients. Cloud providers need to decide which task to run) Tj
T*
(first and which clients to handle first to provide appropriate isolation between different) Tj
T*
(tenants.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(So, in general, the big components of our system are:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Clients: They initiate the task execution.) Tj
T*
(- Resources: The task is executed on these components.) Tj
T*
(- Scheduler: A scheduler performs processes between clients and resources and decides which) Tj
T*
(task should get resources first.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Scheduler putting tasks into a queue for resource allocation]\(./sys.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As shown in the above illustration, it is necessary to put the incoming tasks into a queue. It) Tj
T*
(is because of the following reasons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- We might not have sufficient resources available right now.) Tj
T*
(- There is task dependency, and some tasks need to wait for others.) Tj
T*
(- We need to decouple the clients from the task execution so that they can hand off work to our) Tj
T*
(system. Our system then queues it for execution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s design a task scheduling system that should be able to schedule any task. Often, many) Tj
T*
(tasks are relatively short-lived—from seconds to minutes. For long-running tasks, we might need) Tj
T*
(the ability of periodic checksumming and restoration at the application level to recover from) Tj
T*
(possible failures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s assume that some single server in our fleet can meet the computational needs of each) Tj
T*
(task. For tasks that need many servers, either the application would need to break them down) Tj
T*
(into smaller tasks for our system or employ long-term resource acquisition from the cluster) Tj
T*
(manager.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design) Tj
T*
(When a task comes for scheduling, it should contain the following information with it:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Resource requirements: The requirements include how many CPU cores it needs, how much RAM is) Tj
T*
(required to execute this task, how much disk space is required, what should the disk access) Tj
T*
(rate be \(input/output rate per second, or IOPS\), and how many TCP ports the task needs for the) Tj
T*
