T*
(# Evaluation of a Distributed Task Scheduler's Design) Tj
T*
(## Availability) Tj
T*
(The first component in our design was a rate limiter that is appropriately replicated and) Tj
T*
(ensures availability. Task submission is done by several nodes. If a node that submits a task) Tj
T*
(fails, the other nodes take its place. The queue in which we push the task is also distributed) Tj
T*
(in nature, ensuring availability. We always have resources available because we continuously) Tj
T*
(monitor if we need to add or remove resources. Each component in the design is distributed and) Tj
T*
(makes the overall system available.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Durability) Tj
T*
(We store the tasks in a persistent distributed database and push the tasks into the queue near) Tj
T*
(their execution time. Once a task is submitted, it is in the database until its execution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Scalability) Tj
T*
(Our task scheduler provides scalability because the task submitter is distributed in our) Tj
T*
(design. We can add more nodes to the cluster to submit an increasing number of tasks. The tasks) Tj
T*
(are then saved into a distributed relational database, which is also scalable. The tasks from) Tj
T*
(RDB are then pushed to a distributed queue, which can scale with an increasing number of tasks.) Tj
T*
(We can add more queues for different types of tasks. We can also add more resources depending) Tj
T*
(on the resource-to-demand ratio.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Non-functional requirements fulfilled by our task scheduler system]\(./evaluation.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Fault tolerance) Tj
T*
(A task is not removed from the queue the first time it is sent for execution. If the execution) Tj
T*
(fails, we retry for the maximum number of allowed attempts. If the task contains an infinite) Tj
T*
(loop, we kill the task after some specified time and notify the user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Bounded waiting time) Tj
T*
(We don’t let the users wait for an infinite time. We have a limit on the maximum waiting time.) Tj
T*
(If the limit is reached and we are unable to schedule the task for some reason, we notify the) Tj
T*
(users and ask them to try again.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(We discussed the difference between an OS-level task scheduler and a data center-level task) Tj
T*
(scheduler. We explained that the data center-level task scheduling needs a distributed solution) Tj
T*
(because of multiple tenants and dispersed resources. We learned that the queue is a major) Tj
T*
(building block of a task scheduler. We also used distributed queues where we can scale with an) Tj
T*
(increasing number of tasks to utilize an increasing number of resources.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This lesson helped us to evaluate the issues with the FIFO queue. It was observed that the main) Tj
T*
(job of the task scheduler is to set the priorities of the tasks for which we used a delay) Tj
T*
(tolerance parameter. We discussed how the task scheduler determines the delay tolerance value) Tj
T*
(and used different distributed databases to store task details. We ensured that the dependent) Tj
T*
(tasks are executed in order by running the tasks according to DAG stored in the graph database.) Tj
T*
(Depending upon the number of tasks \(or demand\), we added or removed resources to optimize the) Tj
T*
ET
