T*
(# Requirements of a Distributed Task Scheduler's Design) Tj
T*
(## Requirements) Tj
T*
(Let’s start by understanding the functional and non-functional requirements for designing a) Tj
T*
(task scheduler.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Functional requirements) Tj
T*
(The functional requirements of the distributed task scheduler are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Submit tasks: The system should allow the users to submit their tasks for execution.) Tj
T*
(- Allocate resources: The system should be able to allocate the required resources to each) Tj
T*
(task.) Tj
T*
(- Remove tasks: The system should allow the users to cancel the submitted tasks.) Tj
T*
(- Monitor task execution: The task execution should be adequately monitored and rescheduled if) Tj
T*
(the task fails to execute.) Tj
T*
(- Efficient resource utilization: The resources \(CPU and memory\) must be used efficiently in) Tj
T*
(terms of time, cost, and fairness. Efficiency means that we do not waste resources. For) Tj
T*
(example, if we allocate a heavy resource to a light task that can easily be executed on a cheap) Tj
T*
(resource, it means that we have not efficiently utilized our resources. Fairness is all) Tj
T*
(tenants’ ability to get the resources with equally likely probability in a certain cost class.) Tj
T*
(- Release resources: After successfully executing a task, the system should take back the) Tj
T*
(resources assigned to the task.) Tj
T*
(- Show task status: The system should show the users the current status of the task.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Functional requirements of the distributed task scheduler]\(./fr.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(The non-functional requirements of the distributed task scheduler are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Availability: The system should be highly available to schedule and execute tasks.) Tj
T*
(- Durability: The tasks received by the system should be durable and should not be lost.) Tj
T*
(- Scalability: The system should be able to schedule and execute an ever-increasing number of) Tj
T*
(tasks per day. Fault-tolerance: The system must be fault-tolerant by providing services) Tj
T*
(uninterrupted despite faults in one or more of its components.) Tj
T*
(- Bounded waiting time: This is how long a task needs to wait before starting execution. We) Tj
T*
(must not execute tasks much later than expected. Users shouldn’t be kept on waiting for an) Tj
T*
(infinite time. If the waiting time for users crosses a certain threshold, they should be) Tj
T*
(notified.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Non-functional requirements of distributed task scheduler]\(./nfr.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(So far in this lesson, we have learned about task schedulers in general and distinguished) Tj
T*
(between centralized and distributed task schedulers. Lastly, we listed the requirements of the) Tj
T*
(distributed task scheduler system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Building blocks we will use) Tj
T*
(We’ll utilize the following building blocks in the design of our task scheduling system:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Building blocks of a task scheduler]\(./bb.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Rate limiter is required to limit the number of tasks so that our system is reliable.) Tj
T*
(- A sequencer is needed to uniquely identify tasks.) Tj
T*
(- Database\(s\) are used to store task information.) Tj
T*
(- A distributed queue is required to arrange tasks in the order of execution.) Tj
T*
(- Monitoring is essential to check the health of the resources and to detect failed tasks to) Tj
T*
(provide reliable service to the users.) Tj
T*
ET
