(task scheduling system according to these requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Distributed Task Scheduler/System Design The Distributed Task Scheduler/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: The Distributed Task Scheduler) Tj
T*
(## What is a task scheduler?) Tj
T*
(A task is a piece of computational work that requires resources \(CPU time, memory, storage,) Tj
T*
(network bandwidth, and so on\) for some specified time. For example, uploading a photo or a) Tj
T*
(video on Facebook or Instagram consists of the following background tasks:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Encode the photo or video in multiple resolutions.) Tj
T*
(2. Validate the photo or video to check for content monetization copyrights, and many more.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Content monetization is a way of leveraging content so that a service can profit from it as) Tj
T*
(users consume it.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The successful execution of all the above tasks makes the photo or video visible. However, a) Tj
T*
(photo and video uploader does not need to stop the above tasks to complete.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Another example is when we post a comment on Facebook. We don’t hold the comment poster until) Tj
T*
(that comment is delivered to all the followers. That delivery is delegated to an asynchronous) Tj
T*
(task scheduler to do offline.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In a system, many tasks contend for limited computational resources. A system that mediates) Tj
T*
(between tasks and resources by intelligently allocating resources to tasks so that task-level) Tj
T*
(and system-level goals are met is called a task scheduler.) Tj
T*
(## When to use a task schedular) Tj
T*
(A task scheduler is a critical component of a system for getting work done efficiently. It) Tj
T*
(allows us to complete a large number of tasks using limited resources. It also aids in fully) Tj
T*
(utilizing the system’s resources, provides users with an uninterrupted execution experience,) Tj
T*
(and so on. The following are some of the use cases of task scheduling:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Single-OS-based node: It has many processes or tasks that contend for the node’s limited) Tj
T*
(computational resources. So, we could use a local OS task scheduler that efficiently allocates) Tj
T*
(resources to the tasks. It uses multi-feedback queues to pick some tasks and runs them on some) Tj
T*
(processor.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cloud computing services: Where there are many distributed resources and various tasks from) Tj
T*
(multiple tenants, there is a strong need for a task scheduler to utilize cloud computing) Tj
T*
(resources efficiently and meet tenants’ demands. A local OS task scheduler isn’t sufficient for) Tj
T*
(this purpose because the tasks are in the billions, the source of the tasks is not single, and) Tj
T*
(the resources to manage are not in a single machine. We have to go for a distributed solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Large distributed systems: In this system, many tasks run in the background against a single) Tj
T*
(request by a user. Consider that there are millions to billions of users of a popular system) Tj
T*
(like Facebook, WhatsApp, or Instagram. These systems require a task scheduler to handle) Tj
T*
(billions of tasks. Facebook schedules its tasks against billions of parallel asynchronous) Tj
T*
(requests by its users using Async.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(uninterrupted execution experience: Running periodic tasks without user intervention to) Tj
T*
(initiate the task execution again and again.) Tj
T*
(asynchronous requests: Asynchronous requests are those that are not on a client’s critical path) Tj
T*
(and often have some delay tolerance. Users request these tasks, and the system completes the) Tj
T*
(tasks while the requester does not need to wait for the completion of the work. Notifications) Tj
T*
(about the final state of the task are communicated to the requester some time in the future.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
