(## Distributed task scheduling) Tj
T*
(The process of deciding and assigning resources to the tasks in a timely manner is called task) Tj
T*
(scheduling. The visual difference between an OS-level task scheduler and a data center-level) Tj
T*
(task scheduler is shown in the following illustration:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([An OS-level task scheduler vs. a data center-level task scheduler]) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The OS task scheduler schedules a node’s local tasks or processes on that node’s computational) Tj
T*
(resources. At the same time, the data center’s task scheduler schedules billions of tasks) Tj
T*
(coming from multiple tenants that use the data center’s resources.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Our goal is to design a task scheduler similar to the data center-level task scheduler where) Tj
T*
(the following is considered:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Tasks will come from many different sources, tenants, and sub-systems.) Tj
T*
(- Many resources will be dispersed in a data center \(or maybe across many data centers\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The above two requirements make the task scheduling problem challenging. We’ll design a) Tj
T*
(distributed task scheduler that can handle all these tasks by making it scalable, reliable, and) Tj
T*
(fault-tolerant.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## How will we design a task scheduling system?) Tj
T*
(1. Requirements: We’ll identify the functional and non-functional requirements of a task) Tj
T*
(scheduling system in this lesson.) Tj
T*
(2. Design: This lesson will discuss the system design of our task scheduling system and) Tj
T*
(explores the components of the system and database schema.) Tj
T*
(3. Design considerations: In this lesson, we’ll highlight some design factors, such as task) Tj
T*
(prioritization, resource optimization, and so on.) Tj
T*
(4. Evaluation: We’ll evaluate our design of task scheduler based on our requirements.) Tj
T*
(Let’s start by understanding the requirements of a task scheduling system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
