(## DNS as a distributed system) Tj
T*
(Although the DNS hierarchy facilitates the distributed Internet that we know today, it’s a) Tj
T*
(distributed system itself. The distributed nature of DNS has the following advantages:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(It avoids becoming a single point of failure \(SPOF\).) Tj
T*
(It achieves low query latency so users can get responses from nearby servers.) Tj
T*
(It gets a higher degree of flexibility during maintenance and updates or upgrades. For example,) Tj
T*
(if one DNS server is down or overburdened, another DNS server can respond to user queries.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(There are 13 logical root name servers \(named letter A through M\) with many instances spread) Tj
T*
(throughout the globe. These servers are managed by 12 different organizations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s now go over how DNS is scalable, reliable, and consistent.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Highly scalable) Tj
T*
(Due to its hierarchical nature, DNS is a highly scalable system. Roughly 1,000 replicated) Tj
T*
(instances of 13 root-level servers are spread throughout the world strategically to handle user) Tj
T*
(queries. The working labor is divided among TLD and root servers to handle a query and,) Tj
T*
(finally, the authoritative servers that are managed by the organizations themselves to make the) Tj
T*
(entire system work. As shown in the DNS hierarchy tree above, different services handle) Tj
T*
(different portions of the tree enabling scalability and manageability of the system.) Tj
T*
(### Reliable) Tj
T*
(Three main reasons make the DNS a reliable system:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Caching: The caching is done in the browser, the operating system, and the local name server,) Tj
T*
(and the ISP DNS resolvers also maintain a rich cache of frequently visited services. Even if) Tj
T*
(some DNS servers are temporarily down, cached records can be served to make DNS a reliable) Tj
T*
(system.) Tj
T*
(Server replication: DNS has replicated copies of each logical server spread systematically) Tj
T*
(across the globe to entertain user requests at low latency. The redundant servers improve the) Tj
T*
(reliability of the overall system.) Tj
T*
(Protocol: Although many clients use DNS over unreliable user datagram protocol \(UDP\), UDP has) Tj
T*
(its advantages. UDP is much faster and, therefore, improves DNS performance. Furthermore,) Tj
T*
(Internet service’s reliability has improved since its inception, so UDP is usually favored over) Tj
T*
(TCP. A DNS resolver can resend the UDP request if it didn’t get a reply to a previous one. This) Tj
T*
(request-response needs just one round trip, which provides a shorter delay as compared to TCP,) Tj
T*
(which needs a three-way handshake before data exchange.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What happens if a network is congested? Should DNS continue using UDP?) Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Typically, DNS uses UDP. However, DNS can use TCP when its message size exceeds the original) Tj
T*
(packet size of 512 Bytes. This is because large-size packets are more prone to be damaged in) Tj
T*
(congested networks. DNS always uses TCP for zone transfers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Some clients prefer DNS over TCP to employ transport layer security for privacy reasons.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Consistent) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
