(### The nslookup output) Tj
T*
(The Non-authoritative answer, as the name suggests, is the answer provided by a server that is) Tj
T*
(not the authoritative server of Google. It isn’t in the list of authoritative nameservers that) Tj
T*
(Google maintains. So, where does the answer come from? The answer is provided by second, third,) Tj
T*
(and fourth-hand name servers configured to reply to our DNS query—for example, our university) Tj
T*
(or office DNS resolver, our ISP nameserver, our ISP’s ISP nameserver, and so on. In short, it) Tj
T*
(can be considered as a cached version of Google’s authoritative nameservers response. If we try) Tj
T*
(multiple domain names, we’ll realize that we receive a cached response most of the time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If we run the same command multiple times, we’ll receive the same IP addresses list but in a) Tj
T*
(different order each time. The reason for that is DNS is indirectly performing load balancing.) Tj
T*
(It’s an important term that we’ll gain familiarity with in the coming lessons.) Tj
T*
(### The dig output) Tj
T*
(The Query time: 4 msec represents the time it takes to get a response from the DNS server. For) Tj
T*
(various reasons, these numbers may be different in our case.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The 300 value in the ANSWER SECTION represents the number of seconds the cache is maintained in) Tj
T*
(the DNS resolver. This means that Google’s ADNS keeps a TTL value of five minutes\(300sec/60\)) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
