(called name servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Resource records**: The DNS database stores domain name to IP address mappings in the form of) Tj
T*
(resource records \(RR\). The RR is the smallest unit of information that users request from the) Tj
T*
(name servers. There are different types of RRs. The table below describes common RRs. The three) Tj
T*
(important pieces of information are type, name, and value. The name and value change depending) Tj
T*
(upon the type of the RR.) Tj
T*
([Resource records]\(./common_types_of_rr.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Caching**: DNS uses caching at different layers to reduce request latency for the user.) Tj
T*
(Caching plays an important role in reducing the burden on DNS infrastructure because it has to) Tj
T*
(cater to the queries of the entire Internet.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Hierarchy**: DNS name servers are in a hierarchical form. The hierarchical structure allows) Tj
T*
(DNS to be highly scalable because of its increasing size and query load. In the next lesson,) Tj
T*
(we’ll look at how a tree-like structure is used to manage the entire DNS database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
