(### Consistent Hashing) Tj
T*
(Consistent hashing is an effective way to manage the load over the set of nodes. In consistent) Tj
T*
(hashing, we consider that we have a conceptual ring of hashes from 0 to n-1, where n is the) Tj
T*
(number of available hash values. We use each node’s ID, calculate its hash, and map it to the) Tj
T*
(ring. We apply the same process to requests. Each request is completed by the next node that it) Tj
T*
(finds by moving in the clockwise direction in the ring.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Whenever a new node is added to the ring, the immediate next node is affected. It has to share) Tj
T*
(its data with the newly added node while other nodes are unaffected. It’s easy to scale since) Tj
T*
(we’re able to keep changes to our nodes minimal. This is because only a small portion of) Tj
T*
(overall keys need to move. The hashes are randomly distributed, so we expect the load of) Tj
T*
(requests to be random and distributed evenly on average on the ring.) Tj
T*
([Consistent Hashing]\(./consistent_hashing\)) Tj
T*
(The primary benefit of consistent hashing is that as nodes join or leave, it ensures that a) Tj
T*
(minimal number of keys need to move. However, the request load isn’t equally divided in) Tj
T*
(practice. Any server that handles a large chunk of data can become a bottleneck in a) Tj
T*
(distributed system. That node will receive a disproportionately large share of data storage and) Tj
T*
(retrieval requests, reducing the overall system performance. As a result, these are referred to) Tj
T*
(as hotspots.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As shown in the figure below, most of the requests are between the N4 and N1 nodes. Now, N1 has) Tj
T*
(to handle most of the requests compared to other nodes, and it has become a hotspot. That means) Tj
T*
(non-uniform load distribution has increased load on a single server.) Tj
T*
