(```) Tj
T*
(We’ve made the proposed design of key-value storage scalable. The next task is to make our) Tj
T*
(system highly available.) Tj
T*
(## Data replication) Tj
T*
(We have various methods to replicate the storage. It can be either a primary-secondary) Tj
T*
(relationship or a peer-to-peer relationship.) Tj
T*
(### Primary-secondary approach) Tj
T*
(In a primary-secondary approach, one of the storage areas is primary, and other storage areas) Tj
T*
(are secondary. The secondary replicates its data from the primary. The primary serves the write) Tj
T*
(requests while the secondary serves read requests. After writing, there’s a lag for) Tj
T*
(replication. Moreover, if the primary goes down, we can’t write into the storage, and it) Tj
T*
(becomes a single point of failure.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Does the primary-secondary approach fulfill the requirements of the key-value store that we) Tj
T*
(defined in the System Design: The Key-value Store lesson?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(One of our requirements is that we need the ability to always write. This approach is good for) Tj
T*
(the always read option. However, this approach doesn’t include the ability to always write) Tj
T*
(because it will overload the primary storage. Moreover, if a primary server fails, we need to) Tj
T*
(upgrade a secondary to a primary. The availability of write will suffer as we won’t allow) Tj
T*
(writes during the switch-over time.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(### Peer-to-peer approach) Tj
T*
(In the peer-to-peer approach, all involved storage areas are primary, and they replicate the) Tj
T*
(data to stay updated. Both read and write are allowed on all nodes. Usually, it’s inefficient) Tj
T*
(and costly to replicate in all n) Tj
T*
(n nodes. Instead, three or five is a common choice for the number of storage nodes to be) Tj
T*
(replicated.) Tj
T*
([Peer-to-peer relationship]\(./p2p.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll use a peer-to-peer relationship for replication. We’ll replicate the data on multiple) Tj
T*
(hosts to achieve durability and high availability. Each data item will be replicated at n) Tj
T*
(n hosts, where n is a parameter configured per instance of the key-value store. For example, if) Tj
T*
(we choose n to be 5, it means we want our data to be replicated to five nodes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each node will replicate its data to the other nodes. We’ll call a node coordinator that) Tj
T*
(handles read or write operations. It’s directly responsible for the keys. A coordinator node is) Tj
T*
(assigned the key “K.” It’s also responsible for replicating the keys to n-1) Tj
T*
(n−1 successors on the ring \(clockwise\). These lists of successor virtual nodes are called) Tj
T*
(preference lists. To avoid putting replicas on the same physical nodes, the preference list can) Tj
T*
(skip those virtual nodes whose physical node is already in the list.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s consider the illustration given below. We have a replication factor, n, set to 3. For the) Tj
T*
(key “K,” the replication is done on the next three nodes: B, C, and D. Similarly, for key “L,”) Tj
T*
(the replication is done on nodes C, D, and E.) Tj
T*
([Replication in key-value store]\(./kv.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Key-value Store/System Design The Key-value Store/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: The Key-value Store) Tj
T*
(## Introduction to key-value stores) Tj
T*
(Key-value stores are distributed hash tables \(DHTs\). A key is generated by the hash function) Tj
T*
ET
