(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Many applications might not require a rich programming model provided by a traditional) Tj
T*
(relational database management system \(RDBMS\). Using RDBMS for such applications is often) Tj
T*
(expensive in terms of cost and performance.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## How will we design a key-value store?) Tj
T*
(We’ve divided the key-value system design into the following four lessons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Design a Key-value Store: We’ll define the requirements of a key-value store and design the) Tj
T*
(API.) Tj
T*
(- Ensure Scalability and Replication: We’ll learn to achieve scalability using consistent) Tj
T*
(hashing and replicate the partitioned data.) Tj
T*
(- Versioning Data and Achieving Configurability: We’ll learn to resolve conflicts that occur) Tj
T*
(due to changes made by more than one entity, and we’ll make our system more configurable for) Tj
T*
(different use cases.) Tj
T*
(- Enable Fault Tolerance and Failure Detection: We’ll learn to make a key-value store fault) Tj
T*
(tolerant and how to detect failures in the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Key-value Store/Versioning Data and Achieving Configurability/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Versioning Data and Achieving Configurability) Tj
T*
(## Data versioning) Tj
T*
(When network partitions and node failures occur during an update, an object’s version history) Tj
T*
(might become fragmented. As a result, it requires a reconciliation effort on the part of the) Tj
T*
(system. It’s necessary to build a way that explicitly accepts the potential of several copies) Tj
T*
(of the same data so that we can avoid the loss of any updates. It’s critical to realize that) Tj
T*
(some failure scenarios can lead to multiple copies of the same data in the system. So, these) Tj
T*
(copies might be the same or divergent. Resolving the conflicts among these divergent histories) Tj
T*
(is essential and critical for consistency purposes.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The version history contains the details of objects that have been modified.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
([Data Versioning]\(./version\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(To handle inconsistency, we need to maintain causality between the events. We can do this using) Tj
T*
(the timestamps and update all conflicting values with the value of the latest request. But time) Tj
T*
(isn’t reliable in a distributed system, so we can’t use it as a deciding factor.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Another approach to maintaining causality effectively is by using vector clocks. A vector clock) Tj
T*
(is a list of \(node, counter\) pairs. There’s a single vector clock for every version of an) Tj
T*
(object. If two objects have different vector clocks, we’re able to tell whether they’re) Tj
T*
(causally related or not \(more on this in a bit\). Unless one of the two changes is reconciled,) Tj
T*
ET
