T*
() Tj
T*
(### Compromise with vector clocks limitations) Tj
T*
(The size of vector clocks may increase if multiple servers write to the same object) Tj
T*
(simultaneously. It’s unlikely to happen in practice because writes are typically handled by one) Tj
T*
(of the top n nodes in a preference list.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For example, if there are network partitions or multiple server failures, write requests may be) Tj
T*
(processed by nodes not in the top n nodes in the preference list. As a result we can have a) Tj
T*
(long version like this: \([A,10], [B,4], [C,1], [D,2], [E,1], [F,3], [G,5], [H,7], [I,2], [J,2],) Tj
T*
([K,1], [L,1]\). It’s a hassle to store and maintain such a long version history.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can limit the size of the vector clock in these situations. We employ a clock truncation) Tj
T*
(strategy to store a timestamp with each \(node, counter\) pair to show when the data item was) Tj
T*
(last updated by the node. Vector clock pairs are purged when the number of \(node, counter\)) Tj
T*
(pairs exceeds a predetermined threshold \(let’s say 10\). Because the descendant linkages can’t) Tj
T*
(be precisely calculated, this truncation approach can lead to a lack of efficiency in) Tj
T*
(reconciliation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## The get and put operations) Tj
T*
(One of our functional requirements is that the system should be configurable. We want to) Tj
T*
(control the trade-offs between availability, consistency, cost-effectiveness, and performance.) Tj
T*
(So, let’s achieve configurability by implementing the basic get and put functions of the key-) Tj
T*
(value store.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Every node can handle the get \(read\) and put \(write\) operations in our system. A node handling) Tj
T*
(a read or write operation is known as a coordinator. The coordinator is the first among the top) Tj
T*
(n nodes in the preference list.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There can be two ways for a client to select a node:) Tj
T*
(- We route the request to a generic load balancer.) Tj
T*
(- We use a partition-aware client library that routes requests directly to the appropriate) Tj
T*
(coordinator nodes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Both approaches have their benefits. The client isn’t linked to the code in the first approach,) Tj
T*
(whereas lower latency is achievable in the second. The latency is lower due to the reduced) Tj
T*
(number of hops because the client can directly go to a specific server.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s make our service configurable by having an ability where we can control the trade-offs) Tj
T*
(between availability, consistency, cost-effectiveness, and performance. We can use a) Tj
T*
(consistency protocol similar to those used in quorum systems.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s take an example. Say n in the top n of the preference list is equal to 3. It means three) Tj
T*
(copies of the data need to be maintained. We assume that nodes are placed in a ring. Say A, B,) Tj
T*
(C, D, and E is the clockwise order of the nodes in that ring. If the write function is) Tj
T*
(performed on node A, then the copies of that data will be placed on B and C. This is because B) Tj
T*
(and C are the next nodes we find while moving in a clockwise direction of the ring.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A quorum is the minimum number of votes that a distributed transaction has to obtain in order) Tj
T*
(to be allowed to perform an operation in a distributed system. A quorum-based technique is) Tj
T*
(implemented to enforce consistent operation in a distributed system. \(Wikipedia\)) Tj
T*
