T*
(## Usage of r and w) Tj
T*
(Now, consider two variables, r and w. The r means the minimum number of nodes that need to be) Tj
T*
(part of a successful read operation, while w is the minimum number of nodes involved in a) Tj
T*
(successful write operation. So if r=2, it means our system will read from two nodes when we) Tj
T*
(have data stored in three nodes. We need to pick values of r and w such that at least one node) Tj
T*
(is common between them. This ensures that readers could get the latest-written value. For that,) Tj
T*
(we’ll use a quorum-like system by setting r + w > n.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following table gives an overview of how the values of n, r and w,  affect the speed of) Tj
T*
(reads and writes:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Value Effects on Reads and Writes) Tj
T*
(n             r             w          Description) Tj
T*
(3             2             1          It won't be allowed as it violates our constraint r + w) Tj
T*
(> n .) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3             2             2          It will be allowed as it fulfills constraints.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3             3             1          It will provide speedy writes and slower reads since) Tj
T*
(readers need to go to all n replicas for a value.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3             1             3          It will provide speedy reads from any node but slow) Tj
T*
(writes since we now need to write to all n nodes synchronously.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Let’s say n = 3, which means we have three nodes where the data is copied to. Now, for w = 2,) Tj
T*
(the operation makes sure to write in two nodes to make this request successful. For the third) Tj
T*
(node, the data is updated asynchronously.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Usage]\(./usage\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In this model, the latency of a get operation is decided by the slowest of the r replicas. The) Tj
T*
(reason is that for the larger value of r, we focus more on availability and compromise) Tj
T*
(consistency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The coordinator produces the vector clock for the new version and writes the new version) Tj
T*
(locally upon receiving a put\(\) request for a key. The coordinator sends n highest-ranking nodes) Tj
T*
(with the updated version and a new vector clock. We consider a write successful if at least w-1) Tj
T*
(nodes respond. Remember that the coordinate writes to itself first, so we get w writes in) Tj
T*
(total.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Requests for a get\(\) operation are made to the n highest-ranked reachable nodes in a preference) Tj
T*
(list for a key. They wait for r answers before returning the results to the client.) Tj
T*
(Coordinators return all dataset versions that they regard as unrelated if they get several) Tj
T*
(datasets from the same source \(divergent histories that need reconciliation\). The conflicting) Tj
T*
(versions are then merged, and the resulting key’s value is rewritten to override the previous) Tj
T*
(versions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(By now, we’ve fulfilled the scalability, availability, conflict-resolution, and configurable) Tj
T*
(service requirements. The last requirement is to have a fault-tolerant system. Let’s discuss) Tj
T*
(how we’ll achieve it in the next lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
