(We’ll also understand how load balancers are connected to form a hierarchy, sharing work across) Tj
T*
(different tiers of LBs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Algorithms of load balancers) Tj
T*
(Load balancers distribute client requests according to an algorithm. Some well-known algorithms) Tj
T*
(are given below:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Round-robin scheduling: In this algorithm, each request is forwarded to a server in the pool in) Tj
T*
(a repeating sequential manner.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Weighted round-robin: If some servers have a higher capability of serving clients’ requests,) Tj
T*
(then it’s preferred to use a weighted round-robin algorithm. In a weighted round-robin) Tj
T*
(algorithm, each node is assigned a weight. LBs forward clients’ requests according to the) Tj
T*
(weight of the node. The higher the weight, the higher the number of assignments.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Least connections: In certain cases, even if all the servers have the same capacity to serve) Tj
T*
(clients, uneven load on certain servers is still a possibility. For example, some clients may) Tj
T*
(have a request that requires longer to serve. Or some clients may have subsequent requests on) Tj
T*
(the same connection. In that case, we can use algorithms like least connections where newer) Tj
T*
(arriving requests are assigned to servers with fewer existing connections. LBs keep a state of) Tj
T*
(the number and mapping of existing connections in such a scenario. We’ll discuss more about) Tj
T*
(state maintenance later in the lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Least response time: In performance-sensitive services, algorithms such as least response time) Tj
T*
(are required. This algorithm ensures that the server with the least response time is requested) Tj
T*
(to serve the clients.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(IP hash: Some applications provide a different level of service to users based on their IP) Tj
T*
(addresses. In that case, hashing the IP address is performed to assign users’ requests to) Tj
T*
(servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(URL hash: It may be possible that some services within the application are provided by specific) Tj
T*
(servers only. In that case, a client requesting service from a URL is assigned to a certain) Tj
T*
(cluster or set of servers. The URL hashing algorithm is used in those scenarios.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(There are other algorithms also, like randomized or weighted least connections algorithms.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Static versus dynamic algorithms) Tj
T*
(Algorithms can be static or dynamic depending on the machine’s state. Let’s look at each of the) Tj
T*
(categories individually:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Static algorithms don’t consider the changing state of the servers. Therefore, task assignment) Tj
T*
(is carried out based on existing knowledge about the server’s configuration. Naturally, these) Tj
T*
(algorithms aren’t complex, and they get implemented in a single router or commodity machine) Tj
T*
(where all the requests arrive.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Dynamic algorithms are algorithms that consider the current or recent state of the servers.) Tj
T*
(Dynamic algorithms maintain state by communicating with the server, which adds a communication) Tj
T*
(overhead. State maintenance makes the design of the algorithm much more complicated.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Dynamic algorithms require different load balancing servers to communicate with each other to) Tj
T*
(exchange information. Therefore, dynamic algorithms can be modular because no single entity) Tj
T*
(will do the decision-making. Although this adds complexity to dynamic algorithms, it results in) Tj
T*
(improved forwarding decisions. Finally, dynamic algorithms monitor the health of the servers) Tj
T*
(and forward requests to active servers only.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: In practice, dynamic algorithms provide far better results because they maintain a state) Tj
T*
(of serving hosts and are, therefore, worth the effort and complexity.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
