T*
(### Stateful versus stateless LBs) Tj
T*
(While static and dynamic algorithms are required to consider the health of the hosting servers,) Tj
T*
(a state is maintained to hold session information of different clients with hosting servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If the session information isn’t kept at a lower layer \(distributed cache or database\), load) Tj
T*
(balancers are used to keep the session information. Below, we describe two ways of handling) Tj
T*
(session maintenance through LBs:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Stateful) Tj
T*
(Stateless) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Stateful load balancing) Tj
T*
(As the name indicates, stateful load balancing involves maintaining a state of the sessions) Tj
T*
(established between clients and hosting servers. The stateful LB incorporates state information) Tj
T*
(in its algorithm to perform load balancing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Essentially, the stateful LBs retain a data structure that maps incoming clients to hosting) Tj
T*
(servers. Stateful LBs increase complexity and limit scalability because session information of) Tj
T*
(all the clients is maintained across all the load balancers. That is, load balancers share) Tj
T*
(their state information with each other to make forwarding decisions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Stateful load balancing]\(./stateful.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Stateless load balancing) Tj
T*
(Stateless load balancing maintains no state and is, therefore, faster and lightweight.) Tj
T*
(Stateless LBs use consistent hashing to make forwarding decisions. However, if infrastructure) Tj
T*
(changes \(for example, a new application server joining\), stateless LBs may not be as resilient) Tj
T*
(as stateful LBs because consistent hashing alone isn’t enough to route a request to the correct) Tj
T*
(application server. Therefore, a local state may still be required along with consistent) Tj
T*
(hashing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Stateless load balancers using hash buckets to map requests to end servers]\(./hash.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Therefore, a state maintained across different load balancers is considered as stateful load) Tj
T*
(balancing. Whereas, a state maintained within a load balancer for internal use is assumed as) Tj
T*
(stateless load balancing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Types of load balancers) Tj
T*
(Depending on the requirements, load balancing can be performed at the network/transport and) Tj
T*
(application layer of the open systems interconnection \(OSI\) layers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Layer 4 load balancers: Layer 4 refers to the load balancing performed on the basis of) Tj
T*
(transport protocols like TCP and UDP. These types of LBs maintain connection/session with the) Tj
T*
(clients and ensure that the same \(TCP/UDP\) communication ends up being forwarded to the same) Tj
T*
(back-end server. Even though TLS termination is performed at layer 7 LBs, some layer 4 LBs also) Tj
T*
(support it.) Tj
T*
(Layer 7 load balancers: Layer 7 load balancers are based on the data of application layer) Tj
T*
(protocols. It’s possible to make application-aware forwarding decisions based on HTTP headers,) Tj
T*
(URLs, cookies, and other application-specific data—for example, user ID. Apart from performing) Tj
T*
(TLS termination, these LBs can take responsibilities like rate limiting users, HTTP routing,) Tj
T*
(and header rewriting.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Layer 7 load balancers are smart in terms of inspection. However layer 4 load balancers) Tj
T*
(are faster in terms of processing. For instance, Google has developed a high-performance) Tj
T*
(distributed load balancer called Maglev for their cloud platform.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
