<< /Length 3803 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Load balancer deployment) Tj
T*
(We discussed the trade-offs of load balancing performed at different OSI layers. In practice,) Tj
T*
(however, a single layer LB isn’t enough for a large data center. In fact, multiple layers of) Tj
T*
(load balancers coordinate to take informed forwarding decisions. A traditional data center may) Tj
T*
(have a three-tier LB as shown below:) Tj
T*
([Three-tier load balancer in a typical data center]\(./3tiers.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Tier-0 and tier-1 LBs) Tj
T*
(If DNS can be considered as the tier-0 load balancer, equal cost multipath \(ECMP\) routers are) Tj
T*
(the tier-1 LBs. From the name of ECMP, it’s evident that this layer divides incoming traffic on) Tj
T*
(the basis of IP or some other algorithm like round-robin or weighted round-robin. Tier-1 LBs) Tj
T*
(will balance the load across different paths to higher tiers of load balancers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(ECMP routers play a vital role in the horizontal scalability of the higher-tier LBs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Tier-2 LBs) Tj
T*
(The second tier of LBs include layer 4 load balancers. Tier-2 LBs make sure that for any) Tj
T*
(connection, all incoming packets are forwarded to the same tier-3 LBs. To achieve this goal, a) Tj
T*
(technique like consistent hashing can be utilized. But in case of any changes to the) Tj
T*
(infrastructure, consistent hashing may not be enough. Therefore, we have to maintain a local or) Tj
T*
(global state as we’ll see in the coming sections of the lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Tier-2 load balancers can be considered the glue between tier-1 and tier-3 LBs. Excluding) Tj
T*
(tier-2 LBs could result in erroneous forwarding decisions in case of failures or dynamic) Tj
T*
(scaling of LBs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Tier-3 LBs) Tj
T*
(Layer 7 LBs provide services at tier 3. Since these LBs are in direct contact with the back-end) Tj
T*
(servers, they perform health monitoring of servers at HTTP level. This tier enables scalability) Tj
T*
(by evenly distributing requests among the set of healthy back-end servers and provides high) Tj
T*
(availability by monitoring the health of servers directly. This tier also reduces the burden on) Tj
T*
(end-servers by handling low-level details like TCP-congestion control protocols, the discovery) Tj
T*
(of Path MTU \(maximum transmission unit\), the difference in application protocol between client) Tj
T*
(and back-end servers, and so on. The idea is to leave the computation and data serving to the) Tj
T*
(application servers and effectively utilize load balancing commodity machines for trivial) Tj
T*
(tasks. In some cases, layer 7 LBs are at the same level as the service hosts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(TCP makes use of a congestion window to estimate the number of in-flight packets in a) Tj
T*
(connection to avoid network congestion. Two well-known TCP congestion protocols are Tahoe and) Tj
T*
(Reno.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Path MTU is the largest packet size that a source can send on the network beyond which packet) Tj
T*
(fragmentation will happen. This number is equal to the smallest MTU on the network path.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(To summarize, tier 1 balances the load among the load balancers themselves. Tier 2 enables a) Tj
T*
(smooth transition from tier 1 to tier 3 in case of failures, whereas tier 3 does the actual) Tj
T*
(load balancing between back-end servers. Each tier performs other tasks to reduce the burden on) Tj
T*
(end-servers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Practical example) Tj
T*
(Let’s look at an example where requests from a client come in and get forwarded to different) Tj
T*
(application servers based on the application data inside the client’s network packets.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
