stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
([Practical example]\(./example1.jpg\)) Tj
T*
([Practical example]\(./example2.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s look at the illustration above in the following steps:) Tj
T*
(1. R1 indicates request 1 coming through one of the ECMP routers \(tier-1 LBs\).) Tj
T*
(2. ECMP routers forward R1 to any of the three available tier-2 LBs using a round-robin) Tj
T*
(algorithm. Tier-2 LBs take a hash of the source IP address\(IPc\) and forward the packet to the) Tj
T*
(next tier of LBs.) Tj
T*
(3. Tier-3, upon receiving the packet, offloads TLS and reads the HTTP\(S\) data. By observing the) Tj
T*
(requested URL, it forwards the request to the server handling requests for slides.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(R2 takes the same path but a different end-server because the requested URL contains document) Tj
T*
(instead of slides. Tier-3 LBs are preconfigured to forward requests to application servers) Tj
T*
(based on the application data. For instance, a typical HAProxy server can have the following) Tj
T*
(configuration in tier-3 LBs:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(mode HTTP                               //define which mode the LB will work on, TCP for tier-2) Tj
T*
(LBs) Tj
T*
(acl slidesApp path_end -i /presentation //define a category of applications if the path ends in) Tj
T*
(/presentation) Tj
T*
(use_backend slidesServers if slidesApp  // use a set of backend servers if the request arrives) Tj
T*
(for slidesApp) Tj
T*
(backend slidesServers                   // listing servers serving slidesApp) Tj
T*
(server slides1 192.168.12.1:80          //using slides1 server to serve slidesApp.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                                                             HAProxy sample configuration for) Tj
T*
(layer 7 load balancers) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(After a request reaches a back-end server, should the response be routed back through each tier) Tj
T*
(of the load balancers?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(No, the server can send the response directly to the routers \(tier-1 LBs\) through tier-3 LBs,) Tj
T*
(which can forward the response from the data center. Such a response is called direct routing) Tj
T*
(\(DR\) or direct server return \(DSR\).) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
(Why don’t the servers directly send the response to the routers \(tier-1 LBs\) instead of tier-3) Tj
T*
(LBs?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Tier-3 LBs maintain some state of connection—for example, SSL encryption/decryption. This is) Tj
T*
(necessary to give clients a seamless experience.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 3) Tj
T*
(According to you, which tier of LBs is more prone to bugs?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Tier 3 has more complexity, which makes it more prone to bugs.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
