stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(and more.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Load Balancer/Global and Local Load Balancing/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Global and Local Load Balancing) Tj
T*
(## Introduction) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(From the previous lesson, it may seem like load balancing is performed only within the data) Tj
T*
(center. However, load balancing is required at a global and a local scale. Let’s understand the) Tj
T*
(function of each of the two:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Global server load balancing \(GSLB\): GSLB involves the distribution of traffic load across) Tj
T*
(multiple geographical regions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Local load balancing: This refers to load balancing achieved within a data center. This type of) Tj
T*
(load balancing focuses on improving efficiency and better resource utilization of the hosting) Tj
T*
(servers in a data center.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s understand each of the two techniques below.) Tj
T*
(## Global server load balancing) Tj
T*
(GSLB ensures that globally arriving traffic load is intelligently forwarded to a data center.) Tj
T*
(For example, power or network failure in a data center requires that all the traffic be) Tj
T*
(rerouted to another data center. GSLB takes forwarding decisions based on the users’ geographic) Tj
T*
(locations, the number of hosting servers in different locations, the health of data centers,) Tj
T*
(and so on.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the next lesson, we’ll also learn how GSLB offers automatic zonal failover. GSLB service can) Tj
T*
(be installed on-premises or obtained through Load Balancing as a Service \(LBaaS\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The illustration below shows that the GSLB can forward requests to three different data) Tj
T*
(centers. Each local load balancing layer within a data center will maintain a control plane) Tj
T*
(connection with the GSLB providing information about the health of the LBs and the server farm.) Tj
T*
(GSLB uses this information to drive traffic decisions and forward traffic load based on each) Tj
T*
(region’s configuration and monitoring information.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Usage of global load balancing to send user requests to different regions]\(./global_local.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now, we’ll discuss how the domain name system \(DNS\) can perform GSLB.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(LBaaS is a cloud-based load balancing technique that follows a pay-per-use model. LBaaS offers) Tj
T*
(the flexibility of automatic scaling up or down depending upon the traffic load.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(### Load balancing in DNS) Tj
T*
(We understand that DNS can respond with multiple IP addresses for a DNS query. In the lesson on) Tj
T*
(DNS, we discussed that it’s possible to do load balancing through DNS while looking at the) Tj
T*
(output of nslookup. DNS uses a simple technique of reordering the list of IP addresses in) Tj
T*
(response to each DNS query. Therefore, different users get a reordered IP address list. It) Tj
T*
(results in users visiting a different server to entertain their requests. In this way, DNS) Tj
T*
(distributes the load of requests on different data centers. This is performing GSLB. In) Tj
T*
(particular, DNS uses round-robin to perform load balancing as shown below:) Tj
T*
([Load balancing in DNS]\(./lb\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As shown above, round-robin in DNS forwards clients to data centers in a strict circular order.) Tj
T*
(However, round-robin has the following limitations:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
681 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 680 0 R >>
endobj
682 0 obj
<< /Length 3712 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Different ISPs have a different number of users. An ISP serving many customers will provide the) Tj
T*
(same cached IP to its clients, resulting in uneven load distribution on end-servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Because the round-robin load-balancing algorithm doesn’t consider any end-server crashes, it) Tj
T*
(keeps on distributing the IP address of the crashed servers until the TTL of the cached entries) Tj
T*
(expires. Availability of the service, in that case, can take a hit due to DNS-level load) Tj
T*
(balancing.) Tj
T*
(Despite its limitations, round-robin is still widely used by many DNS service providers.) Tj
T*
(Furthermore, DNS uses short TTL for cached entries to do effective load balancing among) Tj
T*
(different data centers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: DNS isn’t the only form of GSLB. Application delivery controllers \(ADCs\) and cloud-based) Tj
T*
(load balancing \(discussed later\) are better ways to do GSLB.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Application delivery controllers \(ADCs\) are part of the application delivery network \(ADN\).) Tj
T*
(They can be considered the superset of LBs offering various services, including load balancing.) Tj
T*
(The primary task of ADCs is to perform web acceleration to reduce the load from the server) Tj
T*
(farm. Some well-known services between layers 3 and 7 include caching, SSL offloading,) Tj
T*
(proxy/reverse proxy services, IP traffic optimization, and many more. ADCs also implement GSLB.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### The need for local load balancers) Tj
T*
(DNS plays a vital role in balancing the load, but it suffers from the following limitations:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The small size of the DNS packet \(512 Bytes\) isn’t enough to include all possible IP addresses) Tj
T*
(of the servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There’s limited control over the client’s behavior. Clients may select arbitrarily from the) Tj
T*
(received set of IP addresses. Some of the received IP addresses may belong to busy data) Tj
T*
(centers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Clients can’t determine the closest address to establish a connection with. Although DNS) Tj
T*
(geolocation and anycasting-like solutions can be implemented, they aren’t trivial solutions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In case of failures, recovery can be slow through DNS because of the caching mechanism,) Tj
T*
(especially when TTL values are longer.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(To solve some of the above problems, we need another layer of load balancing in the form of) Tj
T*
(local LB. In the next lesson, we’ll discuss different details about local load balancers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## What is local load balancing?) Tj
T*
(Local load balancers reside within a data center. They behave like a reverse proxy and make) Tj
T*
(their best effort to divide incoming requests among the pool of available servers. Incoming) Tj
T*
(clients’ requests seamlessly connect to the LB that uses a virtual IP address \(VIP\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Reverse proxy is a component at the edge of the server-side network that sits between the) Tj
T*
(server and the outside world. It can provide features like privacy, security, caching, etc.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A VIP is an address that has no physical machine to correspond to. In fact, a number of) Tj
T*
(machines will use the same Virtual IP address. The failure of an interface on a network results) Tj
T*
(in the loss of the packets sent to it. However, using VIP, packets are automatically rerouted) Tj
T*
(to a failover machine. From the outside, a VIP will appear as a single machine. Internally,) Tj
T*
(however, a group of machines will use the same address.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
683 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 682 0 R >>
endobj
684 0 obj
<< /Length 3438 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(DNS queries are usually responded with VIPs.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Can DNS be considered a global server load balancer \(GSLB\)?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Yes, there are actually two ways of doing global traffic management \(GTM\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(GTM through ADCs: Some ADCs implement GSLB. In that case, ADCs have a real-time view of the) Tj
T*
(hosting servers and forward requests based on the health and capacity of the data center.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(GTM through DNS: DNS does GSLB by analyzing the IP location of the client. For each user) Tj
T*
