stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(- Lack of user imitation: Such probes might not represent a typical user behavior to explain) Tj
T*
(how a typical user will use the service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: The initial design is based on active probing.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Improve the design) Tj
T*
(Instead of using a prober on vantage points, we can embed the probers into the actual) Tj
T*
(application instead. We’ll have the following two components:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Agent: This is a prober embedded in the client application that sends the appropriate service) Tj
T*
(reports about any failures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Collector: This is a report collector independent of the primary service. It’s made) Tj
T*
(independent to avoid the situations where client agents want to report an error to the failed) Tj
T*
(service. We summarize errors reports from collectors and look for spikes in the errors graph to) Tj
T*
(see client-side issues.) Tj
T*
(The following illustration shows how an agent reaches an independent collector when primary) Tj
T*
(service isn’t reachable:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Evaluating the agent's report to see a spike in errors]\(./evaluate.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(These collectors are a hierarchy of big data processing systems. We can place them near the) Tj
T*
(client network, and over time, we can accumulate these statistics from all such localized) Tj
T*
(sites. We’ll use online stream processing systems to make such a system near real-time. If) Tj
T*
(we’re mainly looking for summary statistics, our system can tolerate the loss of some error) Tj
T*
(reports. Some reports will be relative to the overall user population. We might say 1% of) Tj
T*
(service users are “some.” If we don’t want to lose any reports, we’ll need to design a system) Tj
T*
(with more care, which will be more expensive.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now, we’ll solve the following concerns:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Can a user activate and deactivate client-side reports?) Tj
T*
(- How do client-side agents reach collectors under faulty conditions?) Tj
T*
(- How will we protect user privacy?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Activate and deactivate reports) Tj
T*
(We’ll use a custom HTML header to send appropriate information to the collectors. Though a) Tj
T*
(client accesses the service via a browser, a specific browser should know about this feature to) Tj
T*
(appropriately fill in the header information in the HTTP requests. For organizations that make) Tj
T*
(browsers and provide services \(for example, Chromium-based browsers\), such features can be) Tj
T*
(incorporated and standardized over time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Another solution can be to use a client-side application that the service controls, and then we) Tj
T*
(can easily include such headers over HTTP.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The client can fill in the request header if the client has already consented to that. The) Tj
T*
(service can then reply with appropriate values for the policy and collection endpoints.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Activate and deactivate reports]\(./activate_deactivate.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Reach collectors under faulty conditions) Tj
T*
(The collectors need to be in a different failure domain from the web service endpoint that) Tj
T*
(we’re trying to monitor. The client side can try various collectors in different failure) Tj
T*
(domains until one works. We can see a similar pattern in the following examples. At times, we) Tj
T*
(refer to such a phenomenon as being outside the blast radius of a fault.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If we want to see the reachability of an IP, we host the service on a different IP. If we) Tj
T*
(monitor the availability of a domain, we host the collector on a different domain. And if we) Tj
T*
(want to detect that an autonomous system route isn’t hijacked, we host the service in a) Tj
T*
ET
