(## Failures due to a routing bug) Tj
T*
(Let’s look at a real-world example of an error that impacted a large number of service) Tj
T*
(customers, but the service wasn’t readily aware of it.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(One of Google’s peer ISPs accidentally announced Internet routes that it wasn’t supposed to. As) Tj
T*
(a result, the traffic of many of Google’s customers started routing through unintended ISPs and) Tj
T*
(wasn’t reaching Google. Clients were frustrated because they weren’t able to reach Google,) Tj
T*
(while Google might have been unaware of such problems right away because these issues didn’t) Tj
T*
(happen on its infrastructure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([BGP leak]\(./bgp_leak.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The above leak isn’t unique. Similar issues keep arising. Another such leakage happened on) Tj
T*
(April 16, 2021, when an AS mistakenly announced over 30,000 BGP prefixes. This resulted in a 13) Tj
T*
(times spike in the inbound traffic to their network. However, an increase in influx was) Tj
T*
(observed, and the problem was solved.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The impacted services’ monitoring systems might not catch the above events readily. Monitoring) Tj
T*
(such situations is crucial so that the application remains available for all of its customers.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, in the next lessons, we’ll go through methods that help us to monitor the situations) Tj
T*
(mentioned above.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
