(## Data collector) Tj
T*
(We need a monitoring system to update us about our several data centers. We can stay updated if) Tj
T*
(the information about our processes reaches us, which is possible through logging. We’ll choose) Tj
T*
(a pull strategy. Then, we’llextract our relevant metrics from the logs of the application. As) Tj
T*
(discussed in our logging design, we used a distributed messaging queue. The message in the) Tj
T*
(queue has the service name, ID, and a short description of the log. This will help us identify) Tj
T*
(the metric and its information for a specific service. Exposing the relevant metrics to the) Tj
T*
(data collector is necessary for monitoring any service so that our data collector can get the) Tj
T*
(metrics from the service and store them into the time-series database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A real-world example of a monitoring system based on the pull-based approach is DigitalOcean.) Tj
T*
(It monitors millions of machines that are dispersed globally.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(DigitalOcean, Inc. is an American cloud infrastructure provider that allows developers to) Tj
T*
(deploy and scale applications that run simultaneously on multiple computers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What are some drawbacks of using a push-based approach?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The push-based monitoring tool collects metric data from the applications and servers and sends) Tj
T*
(it to a central collection platform. Each microservice sends its metrics to the monitoring) Tj
T*
(system, resulting in a heavy traffic load on the infrastructure. This is how monitoring might) Tj
T*
(become a bottleneck for business operations. The monitoring can be near real-time. However, if) Tj
T*
(appropriate care isn’t taken, it can overwhelm the infrastructure with continual push requests) Tj
T*
(from all services, resulting in network floods. We must also install daemons on each of these) Tj
T*
(targets to send metrics to the monitoring server, which requires additional work.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Service discoverer) Tj
T*
(The data collector is responsible for fetching metrics from the services it monitors. This way,) Tj
T*
(the monitoring system doesn’t need to keep a track of services. Instead, it can find them using) Tj
T*
(discoverer service. We’ll save the relative information of the services we have to monitor.) Tj
T*
(We’ll use a service discovery solution and integrate with several platforms and tools,) Tj
T*
(including EC2, Kubernetes, and Consul. This will allow us to discover which services we have to) Tj
T*
(monitor. Similar dynamic discovery can be used for newly commissioned hardware.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s add our newly identified component to our existing design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
