([Detailed design of monitoring system]\(./dashboard.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Our all-in-one monitoring service works for actively tracking systems and services. It collects) Tj
T*
(and stores data, and it supports searches, graphs, and alerts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Pros) Tj
T*
(- The design of our monitoring service ensures the smooth working of the operations and keeps) Tj
T*
(an eye on signs of impending problems.) Tj
T*
(- Our design avoids overloading the network traffic by fetching the data itself.) Tj
T*
(- The monitoring service provides higher availability.) Tj
T*
(## Cons) Tj
T*
(- The system seems scalable, but managing more servers to monitor can be a problem. For) Tj
T*
(example, we have a dedicated server responsible for running the monitoring service. It can be a) Tj
T*
(single point of failure \(SPOF\). To cater to SPOF, we can have a failover server for our) Tj
T*
(monitoring system. Then, we also need to maintain consistency between actual and failover) Tj
T*
(servers. However, such a design will also hit a scalability ceiling as the number of servers) Tj
T*
(further increase.) Tj
T*
(- Monitoring collects an enormous amount of data 24/7, and keeping it forever might not be) Tj
T*
(feasible. We need a policy and mechanisms to delete unwanted data periodically to efficiently) Tj
T*
(utilize the resources.) Tj
T*
(Let’s think of a way to overcome the problems with our monitoring service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Improving our design) Tj
T*
(We want to improve our design so that our system can scale better and decide what data to keep) Tj
T*
(and what to delete. Let’s see how the push-based approach works. In a push-based approach, the) Tj
T*
(application pushes its data to the monitoring system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Push-based monitoring system]\(./push.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We used a pull-based strategy to avoid network congestion. This also allows the applications to) Tj
T*
(be free of the aspect that they have to send the relevant monitoring data of to the system.) Tj
T*
(Instead, the monitoring system fetches or pulls the data itself. To cater to scaling needs, we) Tj
T*
(need to apply a push-based approach too. We’ll use a hybrid approach by combining our pull-) Tj
T*
(based strategy with the push-based strategy.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll keep using a pull-based strategy for several servers within a data center. We’ll also) Tj
T*
(assign several monitoring servers for hundreds or thousands of servers within a data) Tj
T*
(center—let’s say one server monitoring 5,000 servers. We’ll call them secondary monitoring) Tj
T*
(servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now, we’ll apply the push-based strategy. The secondary monitoring systems will push their data) Tj
T*
(to a primary data center server. The primary data center server will push its data to a global) Tj
T*
(monitoring service responsible for checking all the data centers spread globally.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll use blob storage to store our excessive data, apply elastic search, and view our relevant) Tj
T*
(stats using a visualizer. As our servers or data centers increase, we’ll add more monitoring) Tj
T*
(systems. The design for this is given below.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Using a hierarchy of systems for scaling is a common design pattern in system design. By) Tj
T*
(increasing nodes on a level or introducing additional levels in the hierarchy, we get the) Tj
T*
(ability to scale according to our current needs.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
([Monitoring systems pull the data from various servers and then push the data to the data) Tj
T*
