(## Non-functional System Characteristics/Availability/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Availability) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## What is availability?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Measuring availability) Tj
T*
(Mathematically, availability, A, is a ratio. The higher the A value, the better. We can also) Tj
T*
(write this up as a formula:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A \(in percent\) = \(total time - amount of time service was down\)/total time * 100) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([the nines]\(./nine_availability.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We measure availability as a number of nines. The following table shows how much downtime is) Tj
T*
(permitted when we’re using a given number of nines.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Availability and service providers) Tj
T*
(Each service provider may start measuring availability at different points in time. Some cloud) Tj
T*
(providers start measuring it when they first offer the service, while some measure it for) Tj
T*
(specific clients when they start using the service. Some providers might not reduce their) Tj
T*
(reported availability numbers if their service was not down for all the clients. The planned) Tj
T*
(downtimes are excluded. Downtime due to cyberattacks might not be incorporated into the) Tj
T*
(calculation of availability. Therefore, we should carefully understand how a specific provider) Tj
T*
(calculates their availability numbers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Non-functional System Characteristics/Fault Tolerance/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Fault Tolerance) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## What is fault tolerance?) Tj
T*
(Real-world, large-scale applications run hundreds of servers and databases to accommodate) Tj
T*
(billions of users’ requests and store significant data. These applications need a mechanism) Tj
T*
(that helps with data safety and eschews the recalculation of computationally intensive tasks by) Tj
T*
(avoiding a single point of failure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Fault tolerance refers to a system’s ability to execute persistently even if one or more of its) Tj
T*
(components fail. Here, components can be software or hardware. Conceiving a system that is) Tj
T*
(hundred percent fault-tolerant is practically very difficult.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s discuss some important features for which fault-tolerance becomes a necessity.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
705 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 704 0 R >>
endobj
706 0 obj
<< /Length 4181 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
(Availability focuses on receiving every client’s request by being accessible 24/7.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reliability is concerned with responding by taking specified action on every client’s request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Fault tolerance techniques) Tj
T*
(Failure occurs at the hardware or software level, which eventually affects the data. Fault) Tj
T*
(tolerance can be achieved by many approaches, considering the system structure. Let’s discuss) Tj
T*
(the techniques that are significant and suitable for most designs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Replication) Tj
T*
(One of the most widely-used techniques is replication-based fault tolerance. With this) Tj
T*
(technique, we can replicate both the services and data. We can swap out failed nodes with) Tj
T*
(healthy ones and a failed data store with its replica. A large service can transparently make) Tj
T*
(the switch without impacting the end customers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We create multiple copies of our data in separate storage. All copies need to update regularly) Tj
T*
(for consistency when any update occurs in the data. Updating data in replicas is a challenging) Tj
T*
(job. When a system needs strong consistency, we can synchronously update data in replicas.) Tj
T*
(However, this reduces the availability of the system. We can also asynchronously update data in) Tj
T*
(replicas when we can tolerate eventual consistency, resulting in stale reads until all replicas) Tj
T*
(converge. Thus, there is a trade-off between both consistency approaches. We compromise either) Tj
T*
(on availability or on consistency under failures—a reality that is outlined in the CAP theorem.) Tj
T*
(#### Checkpointing) Tj
T*
(Checkpointing is a technique that saves the system’s state in stable storage when the system) Tj
T*
(state is consistent. Checkpointing is performed in many stages at different time intervals. The) Tj
T*
(primary purpose is to save the computational state at a given point. When a failure occurs in) Tj
T*
(the system, we can get the last computed data from the previous checkpoint and start working) Tj
T*
(from there.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Checkpointing also comes with the same problem that we have in replication. When the system has) Tj
T*
(to perform checkpointing, it makes sure that the system is in a consistent state, meaning that) Tj
T*
(all processes are stopped. This type of checkpointing is known as synchronous checkpointing. On) Tj
T*
(the other hand, checkpointing in an inconsistent state leads to data inconsistency problems.) Tj
T*
(Let’s look at the illustration below to understand the difference between a consistent and an) Tj
T*
(inconsistent state:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Consistent state: The illustration above shows no communication among the processes when the) Tj
T*
(system performs checkpointing. All the processes are sending or receiving messages before and) Tj
T*
(after checkpointing. This state of the system is called a consistent state.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Inconsistent state: The illustration also displays that processes communicate through messages) Tj
T*
(when the system performs checkpointing. This indicates an inconsistent state, because when we) Tj
T*
(get a previously saved checkpoint, Process i will have a message \(m_1\) and Process j will have) Tj
T*
(no record of message sending.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Non-functional System Characteristics/Maintainability/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Maintainability) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## What is maintainability?) Tj
T*
(Besides building a system, one of the main tasks afterward is keeping the system up and running) Tj
T*
(by finding and fixing bugs, adding new functionalities, keeping the system’s platform updated,) Tj
T*
(and ensuring smooth system operations. One of the salient features to define such requirements) Tj
T*
(of an exemplary system design is maintainability. We can further divide the concept of) Tj
T*
(maintainability into three underlying aspects:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Operability: This is the ease with which we can ensure the system’s smooth operational running) Tj
T*
(under normal circumstances and achieve normal conditions under a fault.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
