<< /Length 3158 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Lucidity: This refers to the simplicity of the code. The simpler the code base, the easier it) Tj
T*
(is to understand and maintain it, and vice versa.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Modifiability: This is the capability of the system to integrate modified, new, and unforeseen) Tj
T*
(features without any hassle.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Measuring maintainability) Tj
T*
(Maintainability, M, is the probability that the service will restore its functions within a) Tj
T*
(specified time of fault occurrence. M measures how conveniently and swiftly the service regains) Tj
T*
(its normal operating conditions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For example, suppose a component has a defined maintainability value of 95% for half an hour.) Tj
T*
(In that case, the probability of restoring the component to its fully active form in half an) Tj
T*
(hour is 0.95.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Maintainability gives us insight into the system’s capability to undergo repairs and) Tj
T*
(modifications while it’s operational.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(We use \(mean time to repair\) MTTR as the metric to measure M.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(MTTR=Total Maintainance Time / Total Number of Repairs) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In other words, MTTR is the average amount of time required to repair and restore a failed) Tj
T*
(component. Our goal is to have as low a value of MTTR as possible.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Maintainability and reliability) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Maintainability can be defined more clearly in close relation to reliability. The only) Tj
T*
(difference between them is the variable of interest. Maintainability refers to time-to-repair,) Tj
T*
(whereas reliability refers to both time-to-repair and the time-to-failure. Combining) Tj
T*
(maintainability and reliability analysis can help us achieve availability, downtime, and uptime) Tj
T*
(insights.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Non-functional System Characteristics/Reliability/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Reliability) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## What is reliability?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reliability, R, is the probability that the service will perform its functions for a specified) Tj
T*
(time. R measures how the service performs under varying operating conditions.) Tj
T*
(We often use mean time between failures \(MTBF\) and mean time to repair \(MTTR\) as metrics to) Tj
T*
(measure R.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(MTBF = total elapsed time-sum of down time / total number of failures) Tj
T*
(MTTR = total maintainance time / total number of repairs) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(\(We strive for a higher MTBF value and a lower MTTR value.\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Reliability and availability) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reliability and availability are two important metrics to measure compliance of service to) Tj
T*
(agreed-upon service level objectives \(SLO\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The measurement of availability is driven by time loss, whereas the frequency and impact of) Tj
T*
(failures drive the measure of reliability. Availability and reliability are essential because) Tj
T*
(they enable the stakeholders to assess the health of the service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reliability \(R\) and availability \(A\) are two distinct concepts, but they are related.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
