() Tj
T*
(## Non-functional System Characteristics/Scalability/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Scalability) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## What is scalability?) Tj
T*
(Scalability is the ability of a system to handle an increasing amount of workload without) Tj
T*
(compromising performance. A search engine, for example, must accommodate increasing numbers of) Tj
T*
(users, as well as the amount of data it indexes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The workload can be of different types, including the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(• Request workload: This is the number of requests served by the system.) Tj
T*
(• Data/storage workload: This is the amount of data stored by the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Dimensions) Tj
T*
(Here are the different dimensions of scalability:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(• Size scalability: A system is scalable in size if we can simply add additional users and) Tj
T*
(resources to it.) Tj
T*
(• Administrative scalability: This is the capacity for a growing number of organizations or) Tj
T*
(users to share a single distributed system with ease.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(• Geographical scalability: This relates to how easily the program can cater to other regions) Tj
T*
(while maintaining acceptable performance constraints. In other words, the system can readily) Tj
T*
(service a broad geographical region, as well as a smaller one.) Tj
T*
(## Different approaches of scalability) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Vertical scalability—scaling up) Tj
T*
(Vertical scaling, also known as “scaling up,” refers to scaling by providing additional) Tj
T*
(capabilities \(for example, additional CPUs or RAM\) to an existing device. Vertical scaling) Tj
T*
(allows us to expand our present hardware or software capacity, but we can only grow it to the) Tj
T*
(limitations of our server. The dollar cost of vertical scaling is usually high because we might) Tj
T*
(need exotic components to scale up.) Tj
T*
(### Horizontal scalability—scaling out) Tj
T*
(Horizontal scaling, also known as “scaling out,” refers to increasing the number of machines in) Tj
T*
(the network. We use commodity nodes for this purpose because of their attractive dollar-cost) Tj
T*
(benefits. The catch here is that we need to build a system such that many nodes could) Tj
T*
(collectively work as if we had a single, huge server.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Pub-sub/Design of a Pub-sub System/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Design of a Pub-sub System) Tj
T*
(## First design) Tj
T*
(In the previous lesson, we discussed that a producer writes into topics, and consumers) Tj
T*
(subscribe to a topic to read messages from that topic. Since new messages are added at the end) Tj
T*
(of the queue, we can use distributed messaging queues for topics.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The components we’ll need have been listed below:) Tj
T*
(- Topic queue: Each topic will be a distributed messaging queue so we can store the messages) Tj
T*
(sent to us from the producer. A producer will write their messages to that queue.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
