() Tj
T*
(- Database: We’ll use a relational database that will store the subscription details. For) Tj
T*
(example, we need to store which consumer has subscribed to which topic so we can provide the) Tj
T*
(consumers with their desired messages. We’ll use a relational database since our consumer-) Tj
T*
(related data is structured and we want to ensure our data integrity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Message director: This service will read the message from the topic queue, fetch the) Tj
T*
(consumers from the database, and send the message to the consumer queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Consumer queue: The message from the topic queue will be copied to the consumer’s queue so) Tj
T*
(the consumer can read the message. For each consumer, we’ll define a separate distributed) Tj
T*
(queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Subscriber: When the consumer requests a subscription to a topic, this service will add an) Tj
T*
(entry into the database.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The consumer will subscribe to a topic, and the system will add the subscriber’s details to the) Tj
T*
(database. The producer will write into the topics, and the message director will read the) Tj
T*
(message from the queue, fetch the details to whom it should add the message, and send it to) Tj
T*
(them. The consumers will consume the message from their queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: We’ll use fail-over services for the message director and subscriber to guard against) Tj
T*
(failures.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Using the distributed messaging queue]\(./using.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Using the distributed messaging queues makes our design simple. However, the huge number of) Tj
T*
(queues needed is a significant concern. If we have millions of subscribers for thousands of) Tj
T*
(topics, defining and maintaining millions of queues is expensive. Moreover, we’ll copy the same) Tj
T*
(message for a topic in all subscriber queues, which is unnecessary duplication and takes up) Tj
T*
(space.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(Is there a way to avoid maintaining a separate queue for each reader?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(In messaging queues, the message disappears after the reader consumes it. So, what if we add a) Tj
T*
(counter for each message? The counter value decrements as a subscriber consumes the message. It) Tj
T*
(does not delete the message until the counter becomes zero. Now, we don’t need to keep a) Tj
T*
(separate queue for each reader.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
(What is the problem with the previous approach?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The unread messages can become a bottleneck if we use the conventional queue API. For example,) Tj
T*
(if 9 out of 10 readers have consumed the message present at the start of the queue, then that) Tj
T*
(message won’t be deleted until the tenth consumer has also consumed the message, and the first) Tj
T*
(nine consumers won’t be able to move forward.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll need to change the storage interface so that consumers can independently consume data.) Tj
T*
(Our system will need to keep sufficient metadata and track what information each consumer has) Tj
T*
(consumed and delete a message when the information has been consumed by all consumers. It) Tj
T*
(resembles with reference count mechanism in Linux’s hard link of files.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
