(## Second design) Tj
T*
(Let’s consider another approach to designing a pub-sub system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### High-level design) Tj
T*
(At a high level, the pub-sub system will have the following components:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Broker: This server will handle the messages. It will store the messages sent from the) Tj
T*
(producer and allow the consumers to read them.) Tj
T*
(- Cluster manager: We’ll have numerous broker servers to cater to our scalability needs. We) Tj
T*
(need a cluster manager to supervise the broker’s health. It will notify us if a broker fails.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Storage: We’ll use a relational database to store consumer details, such as subscription) Tj
T*
(information and retention period.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Consumer manager: This is responsible for managing the consumers. For example, it will verify) Tj
T*
(if the consumer is authorized to read a message from a certain topic or not.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Besides these components, we also have the following design considerations:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Acknowledgment: An acknowledgment is used to notify the producer that the received message) Tj
T*
(has been stored successfully. The system will wait for an acknowledgment from the consumer if) Tj
T*
(it has successfully consumed the message.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Retention time: The consumers can specify the retention period time of their messages. The) Tj
T*
(default will be seven days, but it is configurable. Some applications like banking applications) Tj
T*
(require the data to be stored for a few weeks as a business requirement, while an analytical) Tj
T*
(application might not need the data after consumption.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([High-level design of a pub-sub system]\(./high_level_design.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s understand the role of each component in detail.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Broker) Tj
T*
(The broker server is the core component of our pub-sub system. It will handle write and read) Tj
T*
(requests. A broker will have multiple topics where each topic can have multiple partitions) Tj
T*
(associated with it. We use partitions to store messages in the local storage for persistence.) Tj
T*
(Consequently, this improves availability. Partitions contain messages encapsulated in segments.) Tj
T*
(Segments help identify the start and end of a message using an offset address. Using segments,) Tj
T*
(consumers consume the message of their choice from a partition by reading from a specific) Tj
T*
(offset address. The illustration below depicts the concept that has been described above.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([A depiction of how messages are stored within segments inside a) Tj
T*
(partition]\(./how_messages_are_stored.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As we know, a topic is a persistent sequence of messages stored in the local storage of the) Tj
T*
(broker. Once the data has been added to the topic, it cannot be modified. Reading and writing a) Tj
T*
(message from or to a topic is an I/O task for computers, and scaling such tasks is challenging.) Tj
T*
(This is the reason we split the topics into multiple partitions. The data belonging to a single) Tj
T*
(topic can be present in numerous partitions. For example, let’s assume have Topic A and we) Tj
T*
(allocate three partitions for it. The producers will send their messages to the relevant topic.) Tj
T*
(The messages received will be sent to various partitions on basis of the round-robin algorithm.) Tj
T*
(We’ll use a variation of round-robin: weighted round-robin. The following slides show how the) Tj
T*
(messages are stored in various partitions belonging to a single topic.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Broker]\(./how_broker_works\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(Strict ordering ensures that the messages are stored in the order in which they are produced.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
