(How can we ensure strict ordering for our messages?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(We’ll assign each partition a unique ID, partition_ID. The user can provide the partition_ID) Tj
T*
(while writing into the system. In this way, the messages will be sent to the specified) Tj
T*
(partition, and the ordering will be strict. Our API call to write into the pub-sub system looks) Tj
T*
(like this:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(write\(topic_ID, partition_ID, message\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If the user does not provide the partition_ID, we’ll use the weighted round-robin algorithm to) Tj
T*
(decide which message has to be sent to which partition.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(It might seem strange to give the ability to choose a partition to the client of pub-sub.) Tj
T*
(However, such a facility can be the basis from where clients can get data for some specific) Tj
T*
(time period—for example, getting data from yesterday. For simplicity, we won’t include time-) Tj
T*
(based reading in our design.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
(What problems can arise if all partitions are on the same broker?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(If the broker fails or dies, all the messages in the partitions will be lost. To avoid this, we) Tj
T*
(need to make sure that the partitions are spread on different brokers.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 3) Tj
T*
(Why can’t we use blob stores like S3 to keep messages, instead of the broker’s local storage?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Blob stores like S3 are not optimized for writing and reading short-sized data. If our data is) Tj
T*
(geo-replicated, the above problem is exacerbated.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Therefore, we used the server’s local persistent store with append-based writing. Traditional) Tj
T*
(hard disks are specially tuned to provide good write performance with writing to contiguous) Tj
T*
(tracks or sectors. Reading throughput and latency is also good for contiguous regions of the) Tj
T*
(disk because it allows extensive data caching.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 4) Tj
T*
(If we use a round-robin algorithm to send messages to a partition, how does the system know) Tj
T*
(where to look when it is time to read?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Our system will need to keep appropriate metadata persistently. This metadata will keep) Tj
T*
(mappings between the logical index of segment or messages to the server identity or partition) Tj
T*
(identifier.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We’ll discuss consumer manager later in the lesson, which will keep the required information.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Multiple topics]\(./multiple_topics\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We discussed that a message will be stored in a segment. We’ll identify each segment using an) Tj
T*
(offset. Since these are immutable records, the readers are independent and they can read) Tj
T*
(messages anywhere from this file using the necessary API functions. The following slides show) Tj
T*
