50 780 Td
(# Design of a Rate Limiter) Tj
T*
(## High-level design) Tj
T*
(A rate limiter can be deployed as a separate service that will interact with a web server, as) Tj
T*
(shown in the figure below. When a request is received, the rate limiter suggests whether the) Tj
T*
(request should be forwarded to the server or not. The rate limiter consists of rules that) Tj
T*
(should be followed by each incoming request. These rules define the throttling limit for each) Tj
T*
(operation. Let’s go through a rate limiter rule from Lyft, which has open-sourced its rate) Tj
T*
(limiting component.) Tj
T*
(```yaml) Tj
T*
(domain: messaging) Tj
T*
(descriptors:) Tj
T*
(   -key: message_type) Tj
T*
(    value: marketing) Tj
T*
(    rate_limit:) Tj
T*
(               unit: day) Tj
T*
(               request_per_unit: 5) Tj
T*
(                            Rate-limiting rules from Lyft) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([High-level design]\(./design\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the above rate-limiting rule, the unit is set to day and the request_per_unit is set to 5.) Tj
T*
(These parameters define that the system can allow five marketing messages per day.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Detailed design) Tj
T*
(The high-level design given above does not answer the following questions:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Where are the rules stored?) Tj
T*
(How do we handle requests that are rate limited?) Tj
T*
(In this section, we’ll first expand the high-level architecture into several other essential) Tj
T*
(components. We’ll also explain each component in detail, as shown in the following figure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The rate limiter accepts or rejects requests based on throttle rules]\(./detailed_design.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s discuss each component that is present in the detailed design of a rate limiter.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Rule database: This is the database, consisting of rules defined by the service owner. Each) Tj
T*
(rule specifies the number of requests allowed for a particular client per unit of time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Rules retriever: This is a background process that periodically checks for any modifications to) Tj
T*
(the rules in the database. The rule cache is updated if there are any modifications made to the) Tj
T*
(existing rules.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Throttle rules cache: The cache consists of rules retrieved from the rule database. The cache) Tj
T*
(serves a rate-limiter request faster than persistent storage. As a result, it increases the) Tj
T*
(performance of the system. So, when the rate limiter receives a request against an ID \(key\), it) Tj
T*
(checks the ID against the rules in the cache.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Decision-maker: This component is responsible for making decisions against the rules in the) Tj
T*
(cache. This component works based on one of the rate-limiting algorithms that are discussed in) Tj
T*
(the next lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Client identifier builder: This component generates a unique ID for a request received from a) Tj
T*
(client. This could be a remote IP address, login ID, or a combination of several other) Tj
T*
(attributes, due to which a sequencer can’t be used here. This ID is considered as a key to) Tj
T*
(store the user data in the key-value database. So, this key is passed to the decision-maker for) Tj
T*
(further service decisions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In case the predefined limit is crossed, APIs return an HTTP response code 429 Too Many) Tj
T*
(Requests, and one of the following strategies is applied to the request:) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
729 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 728 0 R >>
endobj
730 0 obj
<< /Length 4045 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
(Drop the request and return a specific response to the client, such as “too many requests” or) Tj
T*
(“service unavailable.”) Tj
T*
(If some requests are rate limited due to a system overload, we can keep those requests in a) Tj
T*
(queue to be processed later.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Request processing) Tj
T*
(When a request is received, the client identifier builder identifies the request and forwards) Tj
T*
(it to the decision-maker. The decision-maker determines the services required by request, then) Tj
T*
(checks the cache against the number of requests allowed, as well as the rules provided by the) Tj
T*
(service owner. If the request does not exceed the count limit, it is forwarded to the request) Tj
T*
(processor, which is responsible for serving the request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The decision-maker takes decisions based on the throttling algorithms. The throttling can be) Tj
T*
(hard, soft, or elastic. Based on soft or elastic throttling, requests are allowed more than the) Tj
T*
(defined limit. These requests are either served or kept in the queue and served later, upon the) Tj
T*
(availability of resources. Similarly, if hard throttling is used, requests are rejected, and a) Tj
T*
(response error is sent back to the client.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(In the event of a failure, a rate limiter is unable to perform the task of throttling. In these) Tj
T*
(scenarios, should the request be accepted or rejected?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(In such a scenario, the proposed system should adhere to the non-functional requirements,) Tj
T*
(including availability and fault tolerance. So, in case of failure, the rate limiter will be) Tj
T*
(unable to perform. However, the default decision would be not to throttle any request. The) Tj
T*
(reason for this is that we would have many rate limiters at a different service level. Even if) Tj
T*
(there is no other rate limiter, the load balancer performs this task at a certain level, as) Tj
T*
(explained earlier.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Race condition) Tj
T*
(There is a possibility of a race condition in a situation of high concurrency request patterns.) Tj
T*
(It happens when the “get-then-set” approach is followed, wherein the current counter is) Tj
T*
(retrieved, incremented, and then pushed back to the database. While following this approach,) Tj
T*
(some additional requests can come through that could leave the incremented counter invalid.) Tj
T*
(This allows a client to send a very high rate of requests, bypassing the rate-limiting) Tj
T*
(controls. To avoid this problem, the locking mechanism can be used, where one process can) Tj
T*
(update the counter at a time while others wait for the lock to be released. Since this approach) Tj
T*
(can cause a potential bottleneck, it significantly degrades performance and does not scale) Tj
T*
(well.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Another method that could be used is the “set-then-get” approach, wherein a value is) Tj
T*
(incremented in a very performant fashion, avoiding the locking approach. This approach works if) Tj
T*
(there’s minimum contention. However, one might use other approaches where the allowed quota is) Tj
T*
(divided into multiple places and divide the load on them, or use sharded counters to scale an) Tj
T*
(approach.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: We can use sharded counters for rate-limiting under the highly concurrent nature of) Tj
T*
(traffic. By increasing the number of shards, we reduce write contention. Since we have to) Tj
T*
(collect counters from all shards, our reading may slow down.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### A rate limiter should not be on the client’s critical path) Tj
T*
(Let’s assume a real-time scenario where millions of requests hit the front-end servers. Each) Tj
T*
(request will retrieve, update, and push back the count to the respective cache. After all these) Tj
T*
(operations, the request is sent forward to be served. This approach could cause latency if) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
731 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 730 0 R >>
endobj
732 0 obj
<< /Length 3464 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(there is a high number of requests. To avoid numerous computations in the client’s critical) Tj
T*
(path, we should divide the work into offline and online parts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Initially, when a client’s request is received, the system will just check the respective) Tj
T*
(count. If it is less than the maximum limit, the system will allow the client’s request. In the) Tj
T*
(second phase, the system updates the respective count and cache offline. For a few requests,) Tj
T*
(this won’t have any effect on the performance, but for millions of requests, this approach) Tj
T*
(increases performance significantly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s understand the online and offline updates approach with an example. In the following set) Tj
T*
(of illustrations, when a request is received, its ID is forwarded to the rate limiter that will) Tj
T*
(check the condition if\(request\(ID\).Count <= Max. Limit by retrieving data from the cache. For) Tj
T*
(simplicity, assume that one of the requests ID is 101, that is, request\(ID\) = 101. The) Tj
T*
(following table shows the number of requests made by each client and the maximum number of) Tj
T*
(requests per unit time that a client can make.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Request ID     Maximum Limit    Count) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(100            5                 4) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(101            5                 3) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If the condition is true, the rate limiter will first respond back to the front-end server with) Tj
T*
(an Allowed signal. The corresponding count and other relevant information are updated offline) Tj
T*
(in the next steps. The rate limiter writes back the updated data in the cache. Following this) Tj
T*
(approach reduces latency and avoids the contention that incoming requests could have caused.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Rate limiter]\(./critical_path\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: We’ve seen a form of rate limiting in TCP network protocol, where the recipient can) Tj
T*
(throttle the sender by advertising the size of the window \(the outstanding data a recipient is) Tj
T*
(willing to receive\). The sender sends the minimum value of either the congestion window or the) Tj
T*
(advertised window. Many network traffic shapers use similar mechanisms to provide preferential) Tj
T*
(treatment to different network flows.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(In this lesson, we discussed the design of a rate limiter in distributed systems. Let’s analyze) Tj
T*
(the non-functional requirements we promised in the previous lesson.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Availability: If a rate limiter fails, multiple rate limiters will be available to handle the) Tj
T*
(incoming requests. So, a single point of failure is eliminated.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Low latency: Our system retrieves and updates the data of each incoming request from the) Tj
T*
(cache instead of the database. First, the incoming requests are forwarded if they do not exceed) Tj
T*
(the rate limit, and then the cache and database are updated.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Scalability: The number of rate limiters can be increased or decreased based on the number of) Tj
T*
(incoming requests within the defined limit.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now, our system provides high availability, low latency, and scalability in light of the above) Tj
T*
(discussion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Rate Limiter/Quiz on the Rate Limiter’s Design/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
