() Tj
T*
(## Rate Limiter/Rate Limiter Algorithms/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Rate Limiter Algorithms) Tj
T*
(## Algorithms for rate limiting) Tj
T*
(The task of a rate limiter is directed by highly efficient algorithms, each of which has) Tj
T*
(distinct advantages and disadvantages. However, there is always a choice to choose an algorithm) Tj
T*
(or combination of algorithms depending on what we need at a given time. While different) Tj
T*
(algorithms are used in addition to those below, we’ll take a look at the following popular) Tj
T*
(algorithms.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Token bucket) Tj
T*
(- Leaking bucket) Tj
T*
(- Fixed window counter) Tj
T*
(- Sliding window log) Tj
T*
(- Sliding window counter) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Token bucket algorithm) Tj
T*
(This algorithm uses the analogy of a bucket with a predefined capacity of tokens. The bucket is) Tj
T*
(periodically filled with tokens at a constant rate. A token can be considered as a packet of) Tj
T*
(some specific size. Hence, the algorithm checks for the token in the bucket each time we) Tj
T*
(receive a request. There should be at least one token to process the request further.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The flow of the token bucket algorithm is as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Assume that we have a predefined rate limit of R and the total capacity of the bucket is C) Tj
T*
(1. The algorithm adds a new token to the bucket after every 1/R seconds.) Tj
T*
(2. The algorithm discards the new incoming tokens when the number of tokens in the bucket is) Tj
T*
(equal to the total capacity C of the bucket.) Tj
T*
(3. If there are N incoming requests and the bucket has at least N tokens, the tokens are) Tj
T*
(consumed, and requests are forwarded for further processing.) Tj
T*
(4. If there are N incoming requests and the bucket has a lower number of tokens, then the) Tj
T*
(number of requests accepted equals the number of available tokens in the bucket.) Tj
T*
(The following illustration represents the working of the token bucket algorithm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([How the token bucket algorithm works]\(./token_bucket.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following illustration demonstrates how token consumption and rate-limiting logic work. In) Tj
T*
(this example, the capacity of the bucket is three, and it is refilled at a rate of three tokens) Tj
T*
(per minute.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Token bucket]\(./token_bucket\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Essential parameters) Tj
T*
(We require the following essential parameters to implement the token bucket algorithm:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Bucket capacity\(C\): The maximum number of tokens that can reside in the bucket.) Tj
T*
(- Rate limit \(R\): The number of requests we want to limit per unit time.) Tj
T*
(- Refill rate \(1/R\): The number of tokens put into the bucket per unit time.) Tj
T*
(- Requests count \(N\): This parameter tracks the number of incoming requests and compares them) Tj
T*
(with the bucket’s capacity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Advantages) Tj
T*
(- This algorithm can cause a burst of traffic as long as there are enough tokens in the bucket.) Tj
T*
(- It is space efficient. The memory needed for the algorithm is nominal due to limited states.) Tj
T*
(#### Disadvantages) Tj
T*
(- From the implementation perspective, a lock might require taking a token from the bucket that) Tj
T*
(can increase the request’s processing delay if contention on the lock increases.) Tj
T*
(- Choosing an optimal value for the essential parameters is a difficult task.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
735 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 734 0 R >>
endobj
736 0 obj
<< /Length 4059 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(### The leaking bucket algorithm) Tj
T*
(The leaking bucket algorithm is a variant of the token bucket algorithm with slight) Tj
T*
(modifications. Instead of using tokens, the leaking bucket algorithm uses a bucket to contain) Tj
T*
(incoming requests and processes them at a constant outgoing rate. This algorithm uses the) Tj
T*
(analogy of a water bucket leaking at a constant rate. Similarly, in this algorithm, the) Tj
T*
(requests arrive at a variable rate. The algorithm process these requests at a constant rate in) Tj
T*
(a first-in-first-out \(FIFO\) order.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s look at how the leaking bucket algorithm works in the illustration below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Essential parameters) Tj
T*
(The leaking bucket algorithm requires the following parameters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Bucket capacity \(C\): This determines the maximum capacity of the bucket. The algorithm will) Tj
T*
(discard the incoming requests when the bucket reached its maximum limit of C.) Tj
T*
(- Inflow rate \(R_{in}\): This parameter shows the inflow rate of requests. This is a varying) Tj
T*
(quantity that depends on the application and nature of requests. We use this parameter to find) Tj
T*
(the initial capacity of the bucket.) Tj
T*
(- Outflow rate \(R_{out}\): This determines the number of requests processed per unit time.) Tj
T*
(#### Advantages) Tj
T*
(- Due to a constant outflow rate \(R_{out}\), it avoids the burst of requests, unlike the token) Tj
T*
(bucket algorithm.) Tj
T*
(- This algorithm is also space efficient since it requires just three states: inflow rate) Tj
T*
(\(R_{in}\), outflow rate \(R_{out}\), and bucket capacity \(C\).) Tj
T*
(- Since requests are processed at a fixed rate, it is suitable for applications with a stable) Tj
T*
(outflow rate.) Tj
T*
(#### Disadvantages) Tj
T*
(- A burst of requests can fill the bucket, and if not processed in the specified time, recent) Tj
T*
(requests can take a hit.) Tj
T*
(- Determining an optimal bucket size and outflow rate is a challenge.) Tj
T*
(### Fixed window counter algorithm) Tj
T*
(This algorithm divides the time into fixed intervals called windows and assigns a counter to) Tj
T*
(each window. When a specific window receives a request, the counter is incremented by one. Once) Tj
T*
(the counter reaches its limit, new requests are discarded in that window.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As shown in the below figure, a dotted line represents the limit in each window. If the counter) Tj
T*
(is lower than the limit, forward the request; otherwise, discard the request.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Fixed window counter algorithm: Discard the request exceeding the limit]\(./fixed_window.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There is a significant problem with this algorithm. A burst of traffic greater than the allowed) Tj
T*
(requests can occur at the edges of the window. In the below figure, the system allows a maximum) Tj
T*
(of ten requests per minute. However, the number of requests in the one-minute window from 01:30) Tj
T*
(to 02:30 is 20, which is greater than the allowed number of requests.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Edge case problem in the fixed window counter algorithm. The number of requests in one minute) Tj
T*
(from 01:30 to 02:30 exceeds the predefined limit of 10 requests per minute]\(./edge_cases.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Essential parameters) Tj
T*
(The fixed window counter algorithm requires the following parameters:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Window size \(W\): It represents the size of the time window. It can be a minute, an hour, or any) Tj
T*
(other suitable time slice.) Tj
T*
(Rate limit \(R\): It shows the number of requests allowed per time window.) Tj
T*
(Requests count \(N\): This parameter shows the number of incoming requests per window. The) Tj
T*
(incoming requests are allowed if N is less than or equal to R.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Advantages) Tj
T*
(- It is also space efficient due to constraints on the rate of requests.) Tj
T*
(- As compared to token bucket-style algorithms \(that discard the new requests if there aren’t) Tj
T*
