(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In this lesson, we explored various popular rate-limiting algorithms. We also shed light on the) Tj
T*
(advantages and disadvantages of these algorithms. Each of these algorithms can be deployed) Tj
T*
(based on the user choice and the type of use case.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Rate Limiter/Requirements of a Rate Limiter’s Design/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Requirements of a Rate Limiter’s Design) Tj
T*
(## Requirements) Tj
T*
(Our focus in this lesson is to design a rate limiter with the following functional and non-) Tj
T*
(functional requirements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- To limit the number of requests a client can send to an API within a time window.) Tj
T*
(- To make the limit of requests per window configurable.) Tj
T*
(- To make sure that the client gets a message \(error or notification\) whenever the defined) Tj
T*
(threshold is crossed within a single server or combination of servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- Availability: Essentially, the rate limiter protects our system. Therefore, it should be) Tj
T*
(highly available.) Tj
T*
(- Low latency: Because all API requests pass through the rate limiter, it should work with a) Tj
T*
(minimum latency without affecting the user experience.) Tj
T*
(- Scalability: Our design should be highly scalable. It should be able to rate limit an) Tj
T*
(increasing number of clients’ requests over time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Types of throttling) Tj
T*
(A rate limiter can perform three types of throttling.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Hard throttling: This type of throttling puts a hard limit on the number of API requests.) Tj
T*
(So, whenever a request exceeds the limit, it is discarded.) Tj
T*
(2. Soft throttling: Under soft throttling, the number of requests can exceed the predefined) Tj
T*
(limit by a certain percentage. For example, if our system has a predefined limit of 500) Tj
T*
(messages per minute with a 5% exceed in the limit, we can let the client send 525 requests per) Tj
T*
(minute.) Tj
T*
(3. Elastic or dynamic throttling: In this throttling, the number of requests can cross the) Tj
T*
(predefined limit if the system has excess resources available. However, there is no specific) Tj
T*
(percentage defined for the upper limit. For example, if our system allows 500 requests per) Tj
T*
(minute, it can let the user send more than 500 requests when free resources are available.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Linux operating systems provide a kernel feature known as cgroups \(control groups\) that limits,) Tj
T*
(accounts for, and isolates the resources—CPU time, system memory, disk storage, I/O, and) Tj
T*
(network bandwidth—of a collection of processes. By using cgroups, the system administrator can) Tj
T*
(monitor, deny access to specific resources, and reconfigure the cgroups dynamically on a) Tj
T*
(running system. The cgroups feature provides the following benefits through which the system) Tj
T*
(administrator gains a fine-grain control of the system resources:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Resource limiting: Using this feature, a restriction can be imposed on groups not to exceed a) Tj
T*
(configured memory limit and file system cache.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Prioritization: Through this feature, some groups can be prioritized to use a larger share of) Tj
T*
(CPU cycles or disk I/O throughput.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Accounting: This feature is used to measure a group’s resource usage, which could also be) Tj
T*
(used for billing purposes.) Tj
T*
ET
