T*
(## Where to place the rate limiter) Tj
T*
(There are three different ways to place the rate limiter.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. On the client side: It is easy to place the rate limiter on the client side. However, this) Tj
T*
(strategy is not safe because it can easily be tampered with by malicious activity. Moreover,) Tj
T*
(the configuration on the client side is also difficult to apply in this approach.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. On the server side: As shown in the following figure, the rate limiter is placed on the) Tj
T*
(server-side. In this approach, a server receives a request that is passed through the rate) Tj
T*
(limiter that resides on the server.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Rate limiter placed at the server side]\(./rate_limiter_server.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. As middleware: In this strategy, the rate limiter acts as middleware, throttling requests to) Tj
T*
(API servers as shown in the following figure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Rate limiter as middleware]\(./rate_limiter_middleware.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Placing a rate limiter is dependent on a number of factors and is a subjective decision, based) Tj
T*
(on the organization’s technology stack, engineering resources, priorities, plan, goals, and so) Tj
T*
(on.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Many modern services use APIs to provide their functionality to the clients. API) Tj
T*
(endpoints can be a good vantage point to rate limit the incoming client traffic because all) Tj
T*
(traffic passes through them.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Two models for implementing a rate limiter) Tj
T*
(One rate limiter might not be enough to handle enormous traffic to support millions of users.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, a better option is to use multiple rate limiters as a cluster of independent nodes.) Tj
T*
(Since there will be numerous rate limiters with their corresponding counters \(or their rate) Tj
T*
(limit\), there are two ways to use databases to store, retrieve, and update the counters along) Tj
T*
(with the user information.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. A rate limiter with a centralized database: In this approach, rate limiters interact with a) Tj
T*
(centralized database, preferably Redis or Cassandra. The advantage of this model is that the) Tj
T*
(counters are stored in centralized databases. Therefore, a client can’t exceed the predefined) Tj
T*
(limit. However, there are a few drawbacks to this approach. It causes an increase in latency if) Tj
T*
(an enormous number of requests hit the centralized database. Another extensive problem is the) Tj
T*
(potential for race conditions in highly concurrent requests \(or associated lock contention\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. A rate limiter with a distributed database: Using an independent cluster of nodes is another) Tj
T*
(approach where the rate-limiting state is in a distributed database. In this approach, each) Tj
T*
(node has to track the rate limit. The problem with this approach is that a client could exceed) Tj
T*
(a rate limit—at least momentarily, while the state is being collected from everyone—when) Tj
T*
(sending requests to different nodes \(rate-limiters\). To enforce the limit, we must set up) Tj
T*
(sticky sessions in the load balancer to send each consumer to exactly one node. However, this) Tj
T*
(approach lacks fault tolerance and poses scaling problems when the nodes get overloaded.) Tj
T*
ET
