T*
(Aside from the above two concepts, another problem is whether to use a global counter shared by) Tj
T*
(all the incoming requests or individual counters per user. For example, the token bucket) Tj
T*
(algorithm can be implemented in two ways. In the first method, all requests can share the total) Tj
T*
(number of tokens in a single bucket, while in the second method, individual buckets are) Tj
T*
(assigned to users. The choice of using shared or separate counters \(or buckets\) depends on the) Tj
T*
(use case and the rate-limiting rules.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(Can a rate limiter be used as a load balancer?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Load balancers prevent too many requests from being forwarded to an application server. They) Tj
T*
(either reject the request based on a limit or send the request to a queue for later processing.) Tj
T*
(However, the load balancer is unbiased towards incoming requests by treating them equally. For) Tj
T*
(example, let’s assume that our web service exposes several operations. Some of these operations) Tj
T*
(are fast, and some are slow. A request for slow operations takes more time and processing power) Tj
T*
(than fast operations. The load balancer doesn’t know the cost of such operations. Therefore, if) Tj
T*
(we aim to limit the number of requests for a particular operation, we should do it on the) Tj
T*
(application server rather than load balancer level.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
(Assume a scenario in which a client intends to send requests for a particular service using two) Tj
T*
(virtual machines \(VMs\), where one is using a VPN to a different region. Suppose that the) Tj
T*
(throttling identifier works based on user credentials. Therefore, the user ID would be the same) Tj
T*
(for both sessions. Moreover, let’s assume that requests from different VMs can hit different) Tj
T*
(data centers. How would the throttling work to prevent the user from exceeding the rate limit) Tj
T*
(in this scenario?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(To rate limit the incoming requests, we have two different choices to place the rate limiter.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Rate limiter per data center: One way to throttle the incoming requests from the user is to use) Tj
T*
(rate limiting per data centers. Each datac enter will have its own rate-limiter, which limits) Tj
T*
(the incoming requests. In this approach, the rate \(count or rate limit\) is relatively lower.) Tj
T*
(Therefore, a limited number of requests are allowed per unit time. Moreover, this approach) Tj
T*
(provides lower latency since the requests are normally directed to the nearest data centers) Tj
T*
(located geographically. Often, latency within a data center is less than one millisecond and) Tj
T*
(multiple redundant paths are available in case of some link failure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A shared rate limiter across data centers: Another approach is to use a shared rate limiter) Tj
T*
(across multiple data centers. This way, requests received from both VMs will be throttled by) Tj
T*
(the single rate limiter. The number of requests allowed in this case is higher. However, this) Tj
T*
(approach is relatively slower, as prior to directing a request to any nearest data center, it) Tj
T*
(will pass through the shared rate limiter. Latency is often high and variable across) Tj
T*
(geographically distributed data centers and not a lot of redundant paths are available.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
