(## Sequencer/Design of a Unique ID Generator/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Design of a Unique ID Generator) Tj
T*
(In the previous lesson, we saw that we need unique identifiers for many use cases, such as) Tj
T*
(identifying objects \(for example, Tweets, uploaded videos, and so on\) and tracing the execution) Tj
T*
(flow in a complex web of services. Now, we’ll formalize the requirements for a unique) Tj
T*
(identifier and discuss three progressively improving designs to meet our requirements.) Tj
T*
(## Requirements for unique identifiers) Tj
T*
(The requirements for our system are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Uniqueness: We need to assign unique identifiers to different events for identification) Tj
T*
(purposes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Scalability: The ID generation system should generate at least a billion unique IDs per day.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Availability: Since multiple events happen even at the level of nanoseconds, our system) Tj
T*
(should generate IDs for all the events that occur.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- 64-bit numeric ID: We restrict the length to 64 bits because this bit size is enough for many) Tj
T*
(years in the future. Let’s calculate the number of years after which our ID range will wrap) Tj
T*
(around.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total numbers available = 2^{64} = 1.8446744 x 10^{19}) Tj
T*
(Estimated number of events per day = 1 billion = 10^{9}) Tj
T*
(Number of events per year = 365 billion = 365 x 10^{9}) Tj
T*
(Number of years to deplete identifier range = 2^64 / \(365 x 10^9\) = 50,539,024.8595 years) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(64 bits should be enough for a unique ID length considering these calculations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s dive into the possible solutions for the problem mentioned above.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## First solution: UUID) Tj
T*
(A straw man solution for our design uses UUIDs \(universally unique IDs\). This is a 128-bit) Tj
T*
(number and it looks like 123e4567e89b12d3a456426614174000) Tj
T*
(123e4567e89b12d3a456426614174000 in hexadecimal. It gives us about 10^{38} numbers. UUIDs have) Tj
T*
(different versions. We opt for version 4, which generates a pseudorandom number.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Straw man solution: Often a simple solution whose primary purpose is to facilitate discussion) Tj
T*
(towards better solutions.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Each server can generate its own ID and assign the ID to its respective event. No coordination) Tj
T*
(is needed for UUID since it’s independent of the server. Scaling up and down is easy with UUID,) Tj
T*
(and this system is also highly available. Furthermore, it has a low probability of collisions.) Tj
T*
(The design for this approach is given below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Generating a unique ID using the UUID approach]\(./uuid.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Cons) Tj
T*
(Using 128-bit numbers as primary keys makes the primary-key indexing slower, which results in) Tj
T*
(slow inserts. A workaround might be to interpret an ID as a hex string instead of a number.) Tj
T*
(However, non-numeric identifiers might not be suitable for many use cases. The ID isn’t of) Tj
T*
(64-bit size. Moreover, there’s a chance of duplication. Although this chance is minimal, we) Tj
T*
(can’t claim UUID to be deterministically unique. Additionally, UUIDs given to clients over time) Tj
T*
(might not be monotonically increasing. The following table summarizes the requirements we have) Tj
T*
(fulfilled using UUID:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(               Requirements Filled with UUID) Tj
T*
(                  Unique      Scalable     Available      64-bit numeric ID) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
