<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 752 0 R >>
endobj
754 0 obj
<< /Length 3947 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Any server can claim a range when it needs it for the first time or if it runs out of the) Tj
T*
(range. Suppose a server has a range, and now it keeps the start of the range in a local) Tj
T*
(variable. Whenever a request for an ID is made, it provides the local variable value to the) Tj
T*
(requestor and increments the value by one.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s say server 1 claims the number range 300,001 to 400,000. After this range claim, the user) Tj
T*
(ID 300,001 is assigned to the first request. The server then returns 300,002 to the next user,) Tj
T*
(incrementing its current position within the range. This continues until user ID 400,000 is) Tj
T*
(released by the server. The application server then queries the central server for the next) Tj
T*
(available range and repeats this process.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This resolves the problem of the duplication of user IDs. Each application server can respond) Tj
T*
(to requests concurrently. We can add a load balancer over a set of servers to mitigate the load) Tj
T*
(of requests.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We use a microservice called range handler that keeps a record of all the taken and available) Tj
T*
(ranges. The status of each range can determine if a range is available or not. The state—that) Tj
T*
(is, which server has what range assigned to it—can be saved on a replicated storage.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This microservice can become a single point of failure, but a failover server acts as the) Tj
T*
(savior in that case. The failover server hands out ranges when the main server is down. We can) Tj
T*
(recover the state of available and unavailable ranges from the latest checkpoint of the) Tj
T*
(replicated store.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Design of the range handler microservice]\(./range_handler.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Pros) Tj
T*
(This system is scalable, available, and yields user IDs that have no duplicates. Moreover, we) Tj
T*
(can maintain this range in 64 bits, which is numeric.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Cons) Tj
T*
(We lose a significant range when a server dies and can only provide a new range once it’s live) Tj
T*
(again. We can overcome this shortcoming by allocating shorter ranges to the servers, although) Tj
T*
(ranges should be large enough to serve identifiers for a while.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The following table sums up what this approach fulfills for us:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(                     Requirements Filled by These Three Options) Tj
T*
(                        Unique            Scalable      Available      64-bit numeric ID) Tj
T*
(  Using UUID             ✖️                ✔️              ✔️             ✖️) Tj
T*
(Using a database         ✖️                ✖️              ✔️             ✔️) Tj
T*
(Using a range handler    ✔️                ✔️              ✔️             ✔️) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We developed a solution that provides us with a unique ID, which we can assign to various) Tj
T*
(events and even use as a primary key. But what if we add a requirement that the ID is time) Tj
T*
(sortable too?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Sequencer/System Design Sequencer/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# System Design: Sequencer) Tj
T*
(## Motivation) Tj
T*
(There can be millions of events happening per second in a large distributed system. Commenting) Tj
T*
(on a post on Facebook, sharing a Tweet, and posting a picture on Instagram are just a few) Tj
T*
(examples of such events. We need a mechanism to distinguish these events from each other. One) Tj
T*
(such mechanism is the assignment of globally unique IDs to each of these events.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Assigning a primary key to an entry in a database is another use case of a unique ID. Usually,) Tj
T*
(the auto-increment feature in databases fulfills this requirement. However, that feature won’t) Tj
T*
(work for a distributed database, where different nodes independently generate the identifiers.) Tj
T*
ET
