(For this case, we need a unique ID generator that acts as a primary key in a distributed) Tj
T*
(setting—for example, a horizontally-sharded table.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A unique ID helps us identify the flow of an event in the logs and is useful for debugging. A) Tj
T*
(real-world example of unique ID usage is Facebook’s end-to-end performance tracing and analysis) Tj
T*
(system, Canopy. Canopy uses TraceID to identify an event uniquely across the execution path) Tj
T*
(that may potentially perform hundreds of microservices to fulfill one user request.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Canopy: An End-to-End Performance Tracing and Analysis System) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
([Assigning a unique TraceID to each event]\(./seq.jpg\)) Tj
T*
(## How do we design a sequencer?) Tj
T*
(We’ve divided the sequencer’s comprehensive design into the following two lessons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Design of a Unique ID Generator: After enlisting the requirements of the design, we discuss) Tj
T*
(three ways to generate unique IDs: using UUID, using a database, and using a range handler.) Tj
T*
(2. Unique IDs with Causality: In this lesson, we incorporate an additional factor of time in) Tj
T*
(the generation of IDs and explain the process by taking causality into consideration.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Unique IDs are important for identifying events and objects within a distributed system.) Tj
T*
(However, designing a unique ID generator within a distributed system is challenging. In the) Tj
T*
(next lesson, let’s look at the requirements for a distributed unique ID generation system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Sequencer/Unique IDs with Causality/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# Unique IDs with Causality) Tj
T*
(## Causality) Tj
T*
(In the previous lesson,we generated unique IDs to differentiate between various events. Apart) Tj
T*
(from having unique identifiers for events, we’re also interested in finding the sequence of) Tj
T*
(these events. Let’s consider an example where Peter and John are two Twitter users. John posts) Tj
T*
(a comment \(event A\), and Peter replies to John’s comment \(event B\). Event B is dependent on) Tj
T*
(event A and can’t happen before it. The events are not concurrent here.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can also have concurrent events—that is, two events that occur independently of each other.) Tj
T*
(For example, if Peter and John comment on two different Tweets, there’s no happened-before) Tj
T*
(relationship or causality between them. It’s essential to identify the dependence of one event) Tj
T*
(over the other but not in the case of concurrent events.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: The scenario described above can also be handled by assigning a unique ID and encoding) Tj
T*
(the dependence of events using a social graph. We might also use a separate time data structure) Tj
T*
(and a simple unique ID. However, we want a unique ID to do double duty—provide unique) Tj
T*
(identification and also help with the causality of events.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
([visualization]\(./nc\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Some applications need the events to have unique identifiers and carry any relevant causality) Tj
T*
(information. An example of this is giving an identifier to the concurrent writes of a key into) Tj
T*
(a key-value store to implement the last-write-wins strategy.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can either use logical or physical clocks to infer causality. Some systems have additional) Tj
T*
(requirements where we want event identifiers’ causality to map wall-clock time. An example of) Tj
T*
(this is a financial application that complies with the European MiFID regulations. MiFID) Tj
T*
(requires clocks to be within 100 microseconds of UTC to detect anomalies during high-) Tj
T*
(volume/high-speed market trades.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: There are many subtleties associated with logical or physical clocks. We can refer to the) Tj
T*
(text below titled “Time in a Distributed System” to refresh our concepts of time.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
