(We use time to determine the sequence of events in our life. For example, if Sam took a bath at) Tj
T*
(6 a.m. and ate breakfast at 7:00 a.m., we can determine that Sam took a bath before breakfast) Tj
T*
(by the time stamps of each event. Time stamps, therefore, can be used to maintain causality.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(                                    Optional Revision: Time in a Distributed System) Tj
T*
(Physical clocks) Tj
T*
(There are often two types of physical clocks available in a computer: the time-of-day clock and) Tj
T*
(monotonic counters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The time-of-day clock) Tj
T*
(- This usually has lower resolution in comparison to monotonic counters.) Tj
T*
(- Network Time Protocol \(NTP\) can move the clock forward or backward, so it’s not always) Tj
T*
(monotonic.) Tj
T*
(- It may or may not incorporate leap seconds.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Monotonic counters) Tj
T*
(- Monotonic counters usually have higher resolution than time-of-day clocks.) Tj
T*
(- Monotonic counters should be used for the duration between two events rather than for the) Tj
T*
(time.) Tj
T*
(- These aren’t meaningful across different nodes. For instance, even on the same server with) Tj
T*
(multiple processors, there can be a different counter per processor. The application needs to) Tj
T*
(be careful when using counters from different processors.) Tj
T*
(- The NTP might adjust it without violating monotonicity.) Tj
T*
(- The NTP can only speed up or slow down the counter rate of change by up to 0.05%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reasons for clock drift) Tj
T*
(Physical clocks drift over time due to many reasons:) Tj
T*
(- Temperature differences) Tj
T*
(- The equipment’s age) Tj
T*
(- Manufacturing defects) Tj
T*
(- Virtualized clocks) Tj
T*
(For instance, a server with a clock drift of 200 parts per million implies a six millisecond) Tj
T*
(drift if synced every 30 seconds, or a 17 seconds drift if re-synced every 24 hours.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A study showed that on the public Internet, an NTP couldn’t get clock accuracy better than 35) Tj
T*
(ms, and it can spike to one second when there’s congestion on the network. Otherwise, NTPs use) Tj
T*
(multiple time servers and discards outliers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The trade-off: complexity and cost versus clock accuracy) Tj
T*
(It’s possible to keep clock drift consistently small using GPS and atomic clocks, careful) Tj
T*
(deployment, and monitoring. However, such a system comes with an added dollar cost, as well as) Tj
T*
(increased system complexity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Logical clocks) Tj
T*
(- Lamport clocks provide us with happened-before relationships. If event A happens before event) Tj
T*
(B, then the clock value of A will be less than the clock value of B. There’s a subtle point) Tj
T*
(that given any two clock values of two events from any two servers, we can’t compare them to) Tj
T*
(infer the happened-before relationship because those two events can be concurrent \(meaning not) Tj
T*
(causally related\).) Tj
T*
(- We can use vector clocks to infer the happened-before relationships using the clock values.) Tj
T*
(For this, we’ll need a counter per participating entity in the vector.) Tj
T*
(- We should note that happened-before might not mean that two events were causally related. It) Tj
T*
(might be the case that one event happened before the other. Usually, we need application-level) Tj
T*
(context on top of the happened-before mechanism to infer real causality.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Use UNIX time stamps) Tj
T*
(UNIX time stamps are granular to the millisecond and can be used to distinguish different) Tj
T*
(events. We have an ID-generating server that can generate one ID in a single millisecond. Any) Tj
T*
ET
