/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(request to generate a unique ID is routed to that server, which returns a time stamp and then) Tj
T*
(returns a unique ID. The ability to generate an ID in milliseconds allows us to generate a) Tj
T*
(thousand identifiers per second. This means we can get 24 \(hour\)* 60 \(min/hour\) * 60 \(sec/min\)) Tj
T*
(* 1000 \(ID/sec\) = 86400000 IDs) Tj
T*
(24\(hour\)∗60\(min/hour\)∗60\(sec/min\)∗1000\(ID/sec\)=86400000IDs in a day. That’s less than a billion) Tj
T*
(per day.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Connect to the following terminal to view the UNIX time stamp in milliseconds.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Our system works well with generating IDs, but it poses a crucial problem. The ID-generating) Tj
T*
(server is a single point of failure \(SPOF\), and we need to handle it. To cater to SPOF, we can) Tj
T*
(add more servers. Each server generates a unique ID for every millisecond. To make the overall) Tj
T*
(identifier unique across the system, we attach the server ID with the UNIX time stamp. Then, we) Tj
T*
(add a load balancer to distribute the traffic more efficiently. The design of a unique ID) Tj
T*
(generator using a UNIX time stamps is given below:) Tj
T*
([Using the time stamp as an ID]\(./timestamps.jpg\)) Tj
T*
(### Pros) Tj
T*
