(## Using logical clocks) Tj
T*
(We can utilize logical clocks \(Lamport and vector clocks\) that need monotonically increasing) Tj
T*
(identifiers for events.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Lamport clocks) Tj
T*
(In Lamport clocks, each node has its counter. All of the system’s nodes are equipped with a) Tj
T*
(numeric counter that begins at zero when first activated. Before executing an event, the) Tj
T*
(numeric counter is incremented by one. The message sent from this event to another node has the) Tj
T*
(counter value. When the other node receives the message, it first updates its logical clock by) Tj
T*
(taking the maximum of its clock value. Then, it takes the one sent in a message and then) Tj
T*
(executes the message.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Lamport clocks provide a unique partial ordering of events using the happened-before) Tj
T*
(relationship. We can also get a total ordering of events by tagging unique node/process) Tj
T*
(identifiers, though such ordering isn’t unique and will change with a different assignment of) Tj
T*
(node identifiers. However, we should note that Lamport clocks don’t allow us to infer causality) Tj
T*
(at the global level. This means we can’t simply compare two clock values on any server to infer) Tj
T*
(happened-before relationship. Vector clocks overcome this shortcoming.) Tj
T*
(### Vector clocks) Tj
T*
(Vector clocks maintain causal history—that is, all information about the happened-before) Tj
T*
(relationships of events. So, we must choose an efficient data structure to capture the causal) Tj
T*
(history of each event.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
