(## Summary) Tj
T*
(We want to avoid duplicate identifiers. Consider what will happen if duplicate payment or) Tj
T*
(purchase orders are generated.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(UUIDs provide probabilistic guarantees about the keys’ non-collision. Deterministically getting) Tj
T*
(non-collision guarantees might need consensus among different distributed entities or stores) Tj
T*
(and read from the replicated store.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(As key length becomes large, it often causes slower tuple updates in a database. Therefore,) Tj
T*
(identifiers should be big enough but not too big.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Often, it’s desirable that no one is able to guess the next ID. Otherwise, undesirable data) Tj
T*
(leaks can happen, and the organization’s competitors may learn how many orders were processed) Tj
T*
(in a day by simply looking at order IDs. Adding a few random numbers to the bits of the) Tj
T*
(identifier make it hard to guess, although this comes at a performance cost.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can use simple counters for generating unique IDs if we don’t want to relate ID to time.) Tj
T*
(Fetching time stamps is slower than simple counters.) Tj
T*
(We can just use simple counters for generating unique IDs if we don’t want to relate ID to) Tj
T*
(time. Fetching time stamps is slower than simple counters, though this requires that we store) Tj
T*
(generated IDs persistently. The counter needs to be stored in the database. Storage comes with) Tj
T*
(its own issues. These include multiple concurrent writes becoming overwhelming for the database) Tj
T*
(and the database being the single point of failure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For some distributed databases, such as Spanner, it can hurt to generate monotonically) Tj
T*
(increasing or decreasing IDs. Google reports the following: “In fact, using monotonically) Tj
T*
(increasing \(or decreasing\) values as row keys does not follow best practices in Spanner because) Tj
T*
(it creates hotspots in the database, leading to a reduction in performance.”) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Note: Globally ordering events is an expensive procedure. A feature that was fast and simple in) Tj
T*
(a centralized database \(auto-increment based ID\) becomes slow and complicated in its) Tj
T*
(distributed counterpart due to some fundamental constraints \(such as consensus, which is) Tj
T*
(difficult among remote entities\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
