(# Detailed Design of Sharded Counters) Tj
T*
(## Detailed design) Tj
T*
(We’ll now discuss the three primary functionalities of the sharded counter–creation, write, and) Tj
T*
(read–in detail. We’ll answer many important questions by using Twitter as an example. These) Tj
T*
(questions include:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- How many shards should be created against each new tweet?) Tj
T*
(- How will the shard value be incremented for a specific tweet?) Tj
T*
(- What will happen in the system when read requests come from the end users?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Sharded counter creation) Tj
T*
(As we discussed earlier, when a user posts a tweet on Twitter, the \\createCounter API is) Tj
T*
(called. The system creates multiple counters for each newly created post by the user. The) Tj
T*
(following is the list of main counters created against each new tweet:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Tweet like counter) Tj
T*
(- Tweet reply counter) Tj
T*
(- Tweet retweet counter) Tj
T*
(- Tweet view counter in case a tweet contains video) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Now, the question is how does the system decide the number of shards in each counter? The) Tj
T*
(number of shards is critical for good performance. If the shard count is small for a specific) Tj
T*
(write workload, we face high write contention, which results in slow writes. On the other hand,) Tj
T*
(if the shard count is too high for a particular write profile, we encounter a higher overhead) Tj
T*
(on the read operation. The reason for slower reads is because of the collection of values from) Tj
T*
(different shards that might reside on different nodes inside geographically distributed data) Tj
T*
(centers. The reading cost of a counter value rises linearly with the number of shards because) Tj
T*
(the values of all shards of a respective counter are added. The writes scale linearly as we add) Tj
T*
(new shards due to increasing requests. Therefore, there is a trade-off between making writes) Tj
T*
(quick versus read performance. We’ll discuss how we can improve read performance later.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The decision about the number of shards depends on many factors that collectively try to) Tj
T*
(predict the write load on a specific counter in the short term. For tweets, these factors) Tj
T*
(include follower count. The tweet of a user with millions of followers gets more shards than a) Tj
T*
(user with few followers on Twitter because there is a possibility that their tweets will get) Tj
T*
(many, often millions, of likes. Sometimes, a celebrity tweet includes one or more hashtags. The) Tj
T*
(system also creates the sharded counter for this hashtag because it has a high chance of being) Tj
T*
(marked as a trend.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Many human-centered activities often have a long-tailed activity pattern, where many people are) Tj
T*
(concentrated on a relatively small set of activities. Perhaps shortened attention spans might) Tj
T*
(be playing a role here. It means that after some time, the flurry of likes will die down, and) Tj
T*
(we might not need as many shards now for a counter as were once needed. Similarly, our initial) Tj
T*
(prediction for future writes might turn out to be wrong, and we might need more shards to) Tj
T*
(handle write requests. We require that our system can dynamically expand or shrink the number) Tj
T*
(of shards based on the current need.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We need to monitor the write load for all the shards to appropriately route requests to) Tj
T*
(specific shards, possibly using load balancers. Such a feedback mechanism can also help us) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
