(decide when to close down some of the shards for a counter and when to add additional shards.) Tj
T*
(This process does not only provide good performance for the end user but also utilizes our) Tj
T*
(resources at near-optimal levels.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What happens when a user with just a few followers has a post go viral on Twitter?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(The system needs to detect such cases where a counter unexpectedly starts getting very high) Tj
T*
(write traffic. We’ll dynamically increase the number of shards of the affected counter to) Tj
T*
(mitigate the situation.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Burst of writes requests) Tj
T*
(As we mentioned earlier, millions of users interact with our example celebrity’s tweet, which) Tj
T*
(eventually sends a burst of write requests to the system. The system assigns each write request) Tj
T*
(to the available shards of the specified counter of the particular tweet. How does the system) Tj
T*
(select these shards operating on different computational units \(nodes\) to assign the write) Tj
T*
(requests? We can use three approaches to do this:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Round-robin selection) Tj
T*
(One way to solve the above problem is to use a round-robin selection of shards. For example,) Tj
T*
(let’s assume the number of shards is 100. The system starts with shard_1, then shard_2, and) Tj
T*
(continues until it reaches shard_100. Usually, round-robin work allocation either overloads or) Tj
T*
(underutilizes resources because scheduling is done without considering the current load) Tj
T*
(conditions. However, if each request is similar \(and roughly needs the same time to serve\), a) Tj
T*
(round-robin approach can be used and is attractive for its simplicity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the following illustrations, we assume that user requests are first handed out to an) Tj
T*
(appropriate server by the load balancer. Then, each such server uses its own round-robin) Tj
T*
(scheduling to use a shard:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Round-robin technique]\(./rr\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Random selection) Tj
T*
(Another simple approach can be to uniformly and randomly select a shard for writing. The) Tj
T*
(challenge with both round-robin selection and random selection is with variable load changes on) Tj
T*
(the nodes \(where shards are hosted\). It is hard to appropriately distribute the load on) Tj
T*
(available shards. Load variability on nodes is common because a physical node is often being) Tj
T*
(used for multiple purposes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(#### Metrics-based selection) Tj
T*
(The third approach is shard selection based on specific metrics. For example, a dedicated node) Tj
T*
(\(load balancer\) manages the selection of the shards by reading the shards’ status. The below) Tj
T*
(slides go over how sharded counters are created:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Metrics]\(./metrics\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Manage read requests) Tj
T*
(When the user sends the read request, the system will aggregate the value of all shards of the) Tj
T*
(specified counter to return the total count of the feature \(such as like or reply\).) Tj
T*
(Accumulating values from all the shards on each read request will result in low read throughput) Tj
T*
(and high read latency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The decision of when the system will sum all shards values is also very critical. If there is) Tj
T*
(high write traffic along with reads, it might be virtually impossible to get a real current) Tj
T*
(value because by the time we report a read value to the client, it will have already changed.) Tj
T*
(So, periodically reading all the shards of a counter and caching it should serve most of the) Tj
T*
(use cases. By reducing the accumulation period, we can increase the accuracy of read values.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
