(Can you think of a use case where sharded counters with the above-mentioned consistency model) Tj
T*
(might not be suitable?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(We might not use shared counters with a relaxed consistency model where we need strong) Tj
T*
(consistency. An example can be read-then-write scenarios where we first need to get the) Tj
T*
(accurate value of something before deciding to modify it \(actually, such a scenario will need) Tj
T*
(transaction support\).) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Using sharded counters for the Top K problem) Tj
T*
(This section will discuss how we can use sharded counters to solve a real-world problem known) Tj
T*
(as the Top K problem. We’ll continue to use the real-time application Twitter as an example,) Tj
T*
(where calculating trends is one of the Top K problems for Twitter. Here, K represents the) Tj
T*
(number of top trends. Many users use various hashtags in their tweets. It is a huge challenge) Tj
T*
(to manage millions of hashtags’ counts to show them in individual users’ trends timelines based) Tj
T*
(on their locality.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The sharded counter is the key to the above problem. As discussed earlier, on Twitter, the) Tj
T*
(system creates the counters for each hashtag and decides the shard count according to the) Tj
T*
(user’s followers who used the hashtag in the tweet. When users on Twitter use the same hashtag) Tj
T*
(again in their tweet, the count maintains the same counter created initially on the first use) Tj
T*
(of that hashtag.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Twitter shows trends primarily based on the popularity of the specific hashtag in a user’s) Tj
T*
(region. Twitter will maintain a separate counter for each discussed metric with a global) Tj
T*
(hashtag counter. Let’s discuss each of the metrics below:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Region-wise hashtag count indicates the number of tweets with the same hashtag used within a) Tj
T*
(specific geographical region. For example, thousands of tweets with the same tags from New York) Tj
T*
(City suggest that users in the New York area may see this hashtag in their trends timeline.) Tj
T*
(- A time window indicates the amount of time during which tweets with specific tags are posted.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The system calculates the 24-hour count of the specified hashtag]\(./topk.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Below is more detail on the above illustration:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The global hashtag counter represents the total of all location-based counters.) Tj
T*
(Location-based counters represent their current count when the system reaches the set threshold) Tj
T*
(in a specified time and the hashtag becomes a trend for some users. For example, Twitter sets) Tj
T*
(10,000 as a threshold. When location-based hashtag counts reach 10,000, Twitter shows these) Tj
T*
(hashtags in the trends timeline of the users of the respective country where the hashtag is) Tj
T*
(being used. The specified hashtag may be displayed worldwide if counts increase in all) Tj
T*
(countries.) Tj
T*
(Next, we’ll discuss Top K tweets in a user’s homepage timeline. The Top K tweets include) Tj
T*
(accounts the user is following, tweets the user has liked, and retweets of accounts the user) Tj
T*
(follows. Tweets get priority in Top K problems based on follower count and time. Twitter also) Tj
T*
(shows promoted tweets and some tweets of accounts the user doesn’t follow in the user’s home) Tj
T*
(timeline, depending on the tweet’s popularity and location.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Twitter also uses other metrics to optimize Top K selection, but we’ve discussed the) Tj
T*
(leading metrics here.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(## Placement of sharded counters) Tj
T*
(An important concern is where we should place shared counters. Should they reside on the same) Tj
T*
(nodes as application servers, in separate nodes in the data center, in nodes of CDN at the edge) Tj
T*
