(of a network near the end users? The exact answer to this question depends on our specific use) Tj
T*
(case. For Twitter, we can compute counts by placing sharded counters near the user, which can) Tj
T*
(also help to handle heavy hitter and Top K problems efficiently.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(Should we lock all shards of a counter before accumulating their values?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(No. Reads can happen concurrently with writes without the need for an across-shards lock. This) Tj
T*
(lock will decimate the write performance, the original reason we used sharded counters. Under a) Tj
T*
(relaxed consistency model, where the value of a counter might not reflect the exact current) Tj
T*
(value, there is no need for simultaneous read locks across all shards.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(However, depending on the specific use case, such a mechanism might be used when reads) Tj
T*
(frequency is very low.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reads can store counter values in appropriate data stores and rely on the respective data) Tj
T*
(stores for read scalability. The Cassandra store can be used to maintain views, likes,) Tj
T*
(comments, and many more counts of the users in the specified region. These counts represent the) Tj
T*
(last computed sum of all shards of a particular counter.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(When users generate a timeline, read requests are forwarded to the nearest servers, and then) Tj
T*
(the persisted values in the store can be used to respond. This storage also helps to show the) Tj
T*
(region-wise Top K trends. The list of local Top K trends is sent to the application server, and) Tj
T*
(then the application server sorts all the lists to make a list of global Top K trends.) Tj
T*
(Eventually, the application server sends all counters’ details to the cache.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We also need storage for the sharded counters, which store all information about them with) Tj
T*
(their metadata. The Redis or Memcache servers can play a vital role here. For example, each) Tj
T*
(tweet’s unique ID can become the key, and the value of this key can be a counter ID, or a list) Tj
T*
(of counters’ IDs \(like counter, reply counter, and so on\). Furthermore, each counter ID has its) Tj
T*
(own key-value store where the counter \(for example, a likes counter\) ID is a key and the value) Tj
T*
(is a list of assigned shards.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The job of identifying the relevant counter and mapping all write requests to the appropriate) Tj
T*
(counter in sharded counters can be done in parallel. We map the all-write request to the) Tj
T*
(appropriate counter, and then each counter chooses a shard randomly based on some metrics to do) Tj
T*
(increments and decrements. In contrast, we reduce periodically to aggregate the value of all) Tj
T*
(shards of the particular counter. Then, these counter values can be stored in the Cassandra) Tj
T*
(store. The slides below help illustrate these points:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Placement]\(./placement\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Evaluation of the sharded counters) Tj
T*
(This section will evaluate the sharded counters and explain how sharded counters will increase) Tj
T*
(performance by providing high availability and scalability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Availability) Tj
T*
(A single counter for any feature \(such as like, view, or reply\) has a high risk of a single) Tj
T*
(point of failure. Sharded counters eliminate a single point of failure by running many shards) Tj
T*
(for a particular counter. The system remains available even if some shards go down or suffer a) Tj
T*
(fault. This way, sharded counters provide high availability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Scalability) Tj
T*
(Sharded counters allow high horizontal scaling as needed. Shards running on additional nodes) Tj
T*
(can be easily added to the system to scale up our operation capacity. Eventually, these) Tj
T*
(additional shards also increase the system’s performance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
