(### Reliability) Tj
T*
(Another primary purpose of the sharded counters is to reduce the massive write request by) Tj
T*
(mapping each write request to a particular shard. Each write request is handled when it comes,) Tj
T*
(and there is no request waiting in the queue. Due to this, the hit ratio increases, and the) Tj
T*
(system’s reliability also increases. Furthermore, the system periodically saves the computed) Tj
T*
(counts in stable storage—Cassandra, in this case.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Conclusion) Tj
T*
(Nowadays, sharded counters are a key player in improving the overall performance of giant) Tj
T*
(services. They provide high scalability, availability, and reliability. Sharded counters solved) Tj
T*
(significant issues, including the heavy hitters and Top K problems, that are very common in) Tj
T*
(large-scale applications.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Sharded Counters/High-level Design of Sharded Counters/README.md) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(# High-level Design of Sharded Counters) Tj
T*
(## High-level solution sketch) Tj
T*
(Managing millions of tweet likes requires many counters operating on many nodes. To manage) Tj
T*
(these counters, we need an efficient system that can provide high performance and scalability) Tj
T*
(as the number of users grows.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(What will happen when a single tweet on Twitter gets a million likes, and the application) Tj
T*
(server receives a write request against each like to increment the relevant counter? These) Tj
T*
(millions of requests are eventually serialized in a queue for data consistency. Such) Tj
T*
(serialization is one way to deal with concurrent activity, though at the expense of added) Tj
T*
(delay. Real-time applications want to keep the quality of experience high by providing as) Tj
T*
(minimum as possible latency for the end user.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s see the illustration below to understand this problem:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Celebrity vs. common people tweet]\(./twitter.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A single counter for each tweet posted by a celebrity is not enough to handle millions of) Tj
T*
(users. The solution to this problem is a sharded counter, also known as a distributed counter,) Tj
T*
(where each counter has a specified number of shards as needed. These shards run on different) Tj
T*
(computational units in parallel. We can improve performance and reduce contention by balancing) Tj
T*
(the millions of write requests across shards.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(First, a write request is forwarded to the specified tweet counter when the user likes that) Tj
T*
(tweet. Then, the system chooses an available shard of the specified tweet counter to increment) Tj
T*
(the like count. Let’s look at the illustration below to understand sharded counters having) Tj
T*
(specified shards:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Counters and their shards working on different computational units]\(./counters.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the above illustration, the total number of shards per counter is \(N+1\). We’ll use an) Tj
T*
(appropriate value for N according to our needs. Let’s discuss an example to understand how) Tj
T*
(sharded counters handle millions of write and read requests for a single post.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let’s assume that a famous YouTube channel with millions of subscribers uploads a new video.) Tj
T*
(The server receives a burst of write requests for video views from worldwide users. First, a) Tj
T*
(new counter initiates for a newly uploaded video. The server forwards the request to the) Tj
T*
(corresponding counter, and our system chooses the shard randomly and updates the shard value,) Tj
T*
(which is initially zero. In contrast, when the server receives read requests, it adds the) Tj
T*
(values of all the shards of a counter to get the current total.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can use a sharded counter for every scenario where we need scalable counting \(such as) Tj
T*
(Facebook posts and YouTube videos\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## API design for sharded counters) Tj
T*
ET
