(threads, there’s a need to move to more powerful CPU and memory servers. This will reduce the) Tj
T*
(total number of servers and the threads required by each server to communicate across the) Tj
T*
(fleet. Having fewer servers means that each server maintains fewer threads. Amazon is adding) Tj
T*
(fine-grained alarming for thread consumption in the service.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Front-end fleet changes: Several changes are required to radically improve the cold start) Tj
T*
(time for the front-end fleet. Moreover, the front-end server cache needs to be moved to a) Tj
T*
(dedicated fleet.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Avoiding recurrent failures: To avoid recurrent failures in the future, extensive AWS) Tj
T*
(services, like CloudWatch and others, must be moved to a separate, partitioned front-end fleet.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question) Tj
T*
(What possible measures should Amazon have adopted to safeguard against the kind of failures) Tj
T*
(they faced in Kinesis Data Streams?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(Possible solutions) Tj
T*
(- Dividing the region into independent failure domains would have reduced the blast radius of) Tj
T*
(the event and made it possible for the production team to quickly recover from the problem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- They should have a system like Facebook’s Resource Allowance System for capacity reservation) Tj
T*
(at the time of planned and unplanned events.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Building an application across multiple clouds or AWS regions would have made it easier for) Tj
T*
(the affected customers to recover quickly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- There’s a need to uncouple services to an extreme extent to eliminate cross-dependency) Tj
T*
(issues.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Failures in a complex system are inevitable. However, some important services like the status) Tj
T*
(dashboard should be hosted on different servers, either inside the service or in some third-) Tj
T*
(party’s infrastructure.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
