() Tj
T*
([Facebook’s high-level architecture]\(./arch\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Analysis) Tj
T*
(Some of the key takeaways from the series of events shown above are the following:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- From common activity to catastrophe: The withdrawal or addition of the network routes is a) Tj
T*
(relatively common activity. The confluence of bugs \(first a faulty configuration, and then a) Tj
T*
(bug in an audit tool not able to detect such a problem\) triggered a chain of events, which) Tj
T*
(resulted in cascading failures. A cascading failure is when one failure can trigger another) Tj
T*
(failure, ultimately bringing the whole system down.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Reasons for slow restoration: It might seem odd that it took six hours to restore the) Tj
T*
(service. Wasn’t it easy to reannounce the withdrawn routes? At the scale that Facebook is) Tj
T*
(operating on, rarely is anything done manually, and there are automated systems to perform) Tj
T*
(changes. The internal tools probably relied on the DNS infrastructure, and when all data) Tj
T*
(centers are offline from the backbone, it would have been virtually impossible to use those) Tj
T*
(tools. Manual intervention would have been necessary. Manually bootstrapping a system of this) Tj
T*
(scale isn’t easy. The usual physical and digital security mechanisms that were in place made it) Tj
T*
(a slow process to intervene manually.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Low probability events can occur: In retrospect, it might seem odd that authoritative DNS) Tj
T*
(systems disconnect themselves if internal data centers aren’t accessible. This is another) Tj
T*
(example where a very rare event, such as none of the data centers being accessible, happened,) Tj
T*
(triggering another event.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Pitfalls of automation: Facebook has been an early advocate of automating network) Tj
T*
(configuration changes, effectively saying that software can do a better job of running the) Tj
T*
(network than humans, who are more prone to errors. However, software can have bugs, such as) Tj
T*
(this one.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Lessons learned) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Ready operations team: There can be a hidden single point of failure in complex systems. The) Tj
T*
(best defense against such faults is to have the operations team ready for such an occurrence) Tj
T*
(through regular training. Thinking clearly under high-stress situations becomes necessary to) Tj
T*
(deal with such events.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Simple system design: As systems get bigger, they become more complex, and they have emergent) Tj
T*
(behaviors. To understand the overall behavior of the system, it might not be sufficient to) Tj
T*
(understand only the behavior of its components. Cascading failures can arise. This is one) Tj
T*
(reason to keep the system design as simple as possible for the current needs and evolve the) Tj
T*
(design slowly. Unfortunately, there’s no perfect solution to deal with this problem. We should) Tj
T*
(accept the possibility of such failures, perform continuous monitoring, have the ability to) Tj
T*
(solve issues when they arise, and learn from the failures to improve the system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Contingency plan: Some third-party services rely on Facebook for single sign-on. When the) Tj
T*
(outage occurred, third-party services were up and running, but their clients were unable to use) Tj
T*
(them because Facebook’s login facility was also unavailable. This is another example of) Tj
T*
(assuming that some service will always remain available and of a hidden single point of) Tj
T*
(failure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Hosting DNS to independent third-party providers: There are a few services that are so) Tj
T*
(robustly designed and perfected over time that their clients start assuming that the service is) Tj
T*
(and will always be 100% available. The DNS is one such service, and it has been very carefully) Tj
T*
(crafted. Designers often assume that it will never fail. Hosting DNS to independent third-party) Tj
T*
(providers might be a way to guard against such problems. DNS allows multiple authoritative) Tj
T*
(servers, and an organization might have many at different places. Although, we should note that) Tj
T*
(DNS at Facebook’s scale isn’t simple, is tightly connected to their backbone infrastructure,) Tj
T*
ET
