(want.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Complex systems: Systems are complex, and they usually have emergent properties where the sum) Tj
T*
(of system components is more complex than the individual pieces.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Types of failure in distributed systems) Tj
T*
(Most modern services are designed in such a way that failures are contained, and others might) Tj
T*
(be localized for some users. Let’s explore the types of failures we can observe in the) Tj
T*
(distributed systems.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- System failure: A software or hardware failure is the most common cause of system failure.) Tj
T*
(The contents in the primary memory are lost when a system fails. However, the data in secondary) Tj
T*
(storage or replicas remains unaffected. The system reboots during such a breakdown.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Method failure: Such failures suspend the working of distributed systems. It may also make) Tj
T*
(the system execute the processes incorrectly or enter a deadlock state.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Communication medium failure: Such failures occur when one component or service of a system) Tj
T*
(can’t reach the other internal or external entities.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Secondary storage failure: In such failures, the secondary storage or replicas are down. The) Tj
T*
(data in these nodes becomes inaccessible, so primary nodes need to generate another replica to) Tj
T*
(ensure reliability and fault tolerance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Vantage points) Tj
T*
(Something is always failing for large services. It’s preferred to have a graceful degradation) Tj
T*
(so that only a small portion of users are impacted for a short time. Therefore, we need) Tj
T*
(globally dispersed vantage points to independently see the service status.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: Many independent services, such as Downdetector, exist for crowd-sourced problem) Tj
T*
(reporting. It’s interesting to note that if we visit such a service and see the status of our) Tj
T*
(favorite applications, we’ll see that there’s always someone in the world facing some service) Tj
T*
(problem.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Types of failure in a distributed system]\(./types.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Importance of independent service providers) Tj
T*
(One of the design goals of the original Internet was to provide resilience so that if one part) Tj
T*
(fails, the rest can still operate.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(With the emergence of a handful of service providers over the last decade, critics have raised) Tj
T*
(concerns about such centralization and the potential impacts of failures. See “It’s time to) Tj
T*
(decentralize the internet, again: What was distributed is now centralized by Google, Facebook,) Tj
T*
(etc” by Bruce Davie. Most companies provide some kind of dashboard to enable users to see the) Tj
T*
(service status. However, some failures might even knock out these dashboards. Affected) Tj
T*
(companies then communicate to their customers via services like Twitter to announce the) Tj
T*
(updates. Independent third-party services are valuable for failure detection and status) Tj
T*
(dissemination.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Note: The broader concept here is to use independent failure domains. A failure domain is a) Tj
T*
(concept where anything failing inside a domain or network shouldn’t affect other components and) Tj
T*
(services in other domains. At times, we say that two domains are independent if they’re outside) Tj
T*
(the blast radius of each other.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Importance of independent service]\(./independence.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In the following lessons, we’ll discuss the failures of well-known services by giant companies,) Tj
T*
(the causes of failures, and what mitigation techniques can be used to avoid these failures.) Tj
T*
ET
