T*
(# Design Improvements of a Web Crawler) Tj
T*
(## Introduction) Tj
T*
(This lesson gives us a detailed rundown of the improvements that are required to enhance the) Tj
T*
(functionality, performance, and security of our web crawler design. We have divided this lesson) Tj
T*
(into two sections:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Functionality and performance enhancement design improvements—extensibility and multi-worker) Tj
T*
(architecture.) Tj
T*
(2. Security-enhancement design improvements—crawler traps.) Tj
T*
(Let’s dive into these sections.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Design improvements) Tj
T*
(Our current design is simplistic and has some inherent shortcomings and challenges. Let’s) Tj
T*
(highlight them one by one and make some adjustments to our design along the way.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Shortcoming: Currently, our design supports the HTTP protocol and only extracts textual) Tj
T*
(content. This leads to the question of how we can extend our crawler to facilitate multiple) Tj
T*
(communication protocols and extract various types of files.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Adjustment: Since we have two separate components for serving communication handling and) Tj
T*
(extracting, HTML Fetcher and Extractor, let’s discuss their modifications one by one.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. HTML Fetcher: We have only discussed the HTTP module in this component so far because of the) Tj
T*
(widely-used HTTP URLs scheme. We can easily extend our design to incorporate other) Tj
T*
(communication protocols like File Transfer Protocol \(FTP\). The workflow will then have an) Tj
T*
(intermediary step where the crawler invokes the concerned communication module based on the) Tj
T*
(URL’s scheme. The subsequent steps will remain the same.) Tj
T*
(2. Extractor: Currently, we only extract the textual content from the downloaded document) Tj
T*
(placed in the Document Input Stream \(DIS\). This document contains other file types as well, for) Tj
T*
(example, images and videos. If we wish to extract other content from the stored document, we) Tj
T*
(need to add new modules with functionalities to process those media types. Since we use a blob) Tj
T*
(store for content storage, storing the newly-extracted content comprising text, images, and) Tj
T*
(videos won’t be a problem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The extensibility of the HTML fetcher and extractor]\(./extensibility.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Shortcoming: The current design doesn’t explain how the multi-worker concept integrates into) Tj
T*
(this system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Adjustment: Every worker needs a URL to work on from the priority queue. Different websites) Tj
T*
(require different times for workers to finish crawling, so each worker will dequeue a new URL) Tj
T*
(upon its availability.) Tj
T*
(There are several ways to achieve this multi-worker architecture for our system. Some of them) Tj
T*
(are as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Domain level log: The crawler assigns one whole domain to a worker. All the URLs branching) Tj
T*
(out of the initial URL are the responsibility of the same crawler. We ensure this by caching) Tj
T*
(the hash of the hostname against the worker ID for guaranteed future assignment to the same) Tj
T*
(worker. This also helps us avoid any redundant crawling of that domain’s web pages by any) Tj
T*
(worker.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This approach is best-suited for achieving reverse URL indexing, which involves traversing the) Tj
T*
(web address directories in a reversed order. It ensures the storage efficiency of the URL) Tj
T*
(storing process for later use and prevents extensive repetitive string matching processes for) Tj
T*
