(duplication testing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Range division: The range of URLs given to each crawler; the crawler distributes a range of) Tj
T*
(URLs from the priority queue among workers to avoid clashes. Like the previous approach, the) Tj
T*
(crawler must hash the range associated with each worker.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Per URL crawling: Queue all the URLs; a worker takes a URL and enqueues the subsequently) Tj
T*
(found URLs into the priority queue. Those new URLs are readily available to crawl through for) Tj
T*
(other workers. To avoid enqueuing multiple similar links that direct to the same web page, we) Tj
T*
(calculate the checksum of the canonicalized URL.) Tj
T*
(## Crawler traps) Tj
T*
(A crawler trap is a URL or a set of URLs that cause indefinite crawler resource exhaustion.) Tj
T*
(This section dedicates itself to the classification, identification, and prevention of crawler) Tj
T*
(traps.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Classification) Tj
T*
(There can be many classification schemes for crawler traps, but let’s classify them based on) Tj
T*
(the URL scheme.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mostly, web crawler traps are the result of poor website structuring, such as:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- URLs with query parameters: These query parameters can hold an immense amount of values, all) Tj
T*
(while generating a large number of useless web pages for a single domain:) Tj
T*
(HTTP://www.abc.com?query.) Tj
T*
(- URLs with internal links: These links redirect in the same domain and can create an infinite) Tj
T*
(cycle of redirection between the web pages of a single domain, making the crawler crawl the) Tj
T*
(same content over and over again.) Tj
T*
(- URLs with infinite calendar pages: These have never-ending combinations of web pages based on) Tj
T*
(the varying date values, and can create a large number of pointless web pages for a single) Tj
T*
(domain.) Tj
T*
(- URLs for the dynamic content generation: These are query-based and can generate a massive) Tj
T*
(number of web pages based on the dynamic content resulting from these queries. Such URLs might) Tj
T*
(become a never-ending crawl on a single domain.) Tj
T*
(- URLs with repeated/cyclic directories: They form an indefinite loop of redirections. For) Tj
T*
(example, HTTP://www.abc.com/first/second/first/second/....) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([Classification of web crawler traps]\(./classification.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mostly, the crawler traps are unintended because of the poor structuring of the website.) Tj
T*
(Interestingly, crawler traps can also be placed intentionally to dynamically generate an) Tj
T*
(endless series of web pages with the pure purpose of exhausting a crawler’s bandwidth. These) Tj
T*
(traps severely impact the throughput of a crawler and hamper its performance.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Throughput: useful content crawled in a time period) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(These crawler traps might be detrimental to the crawler, but they also seriously affect the) Tj
T*
(website’s SEO ranking.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Identification) Tj
T*
(It’s essential to categorize the crawling traps to correctly identify them and think of design) Tj
T*
(adjustments accordingly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There are mainly two layers to the process of identifying a crawler trap:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Analyzing the URL scheme: The URLs with a poor URL structure, for example, those with cyclic) Tj
T*
(directories: HTTP://www.abc.com/first/second/first/second/... will create crawler traps. Hence,) Tj
T*
(filtering such URLs beforehand will rescue our crawler resources.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Analyzing the total number of web pages against a domain: An impossibly large number of web) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
