(# Requirements of a Web Crawler's Design) Tj
T*
(## Requirements) Tj
T*
(Let’s highlight the functional and non-functional requirements of a web crawler.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Functional requirements) Tj
T*
(These are the functionalities a user must be able to perform:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Crawling: The system should scour the WWW, spanning from a queue of seed URLs provided) Tj
T*
(initially by the system administrator.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 1) Tj
T*
(Where do we get these seed URLs from?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(There are two possible ways to create or gather seed URLs:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We can manually create them) Tj
T*
(We can scan the IP addresses for the presence of web servers) Tj
T*
(These seed URLs must be of good quality.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Question 2) Tj
T*
(Why are good quality seed URLs important and what happens if the seed quality is not good?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(When we model WWW as a graph where URLs link one node to another, our goal is to discover as) Tj
T*
(much of it as possible during the crawl.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Using a low-quality seed might limit the discovery to just a fraction of the WWW graph.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
823 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 822 0 R >>
endobj
824 0 obj
<< /Length 3106 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
(Question 3) Tj
T*
(How do we select seed URLs for crawling?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Answer) Tj
T*
(There are multiple approaches to selecting seed URLs. Some of them are:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Location-based: We can have different seed URLs depending on the location of the crawler.) Tj
T*
(Category-based: Depending on the type of content we need to crawl, we can have various sets of) Tj
T*
(seed URLs.) Tj
T*
(Popularity-based: This is the most popular approach. It combines both the aforementioned) Tj
T*
(approaches. It groups the seed URLs based on hot topics in a specific area.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Storing: The system should be able to extract and store the content of a URL in a blob store.) Tj
T*
(This makes that URL and its content processable by the search engines for indexing and ranking) Tj
T*
(purposes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Scheduling: Since crawling is a process that’s repeated, the system should have regular) Tj
T*
(scheduling to update its blob stores’ records.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Non-functional requirements) Tj
T*
(- Scalability: The system should inherently be distributed and multithreaded, because it has to) Tj
T*
(fetch hundreds of millions of web documents.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Extensibility: Currently, our design supports HTTP\(S\) communication protocol and text files) Tj
T*
(storage facilities. For augmented functionality, it should also be extensible for different) Tj
T*
(network communication protocols, able to add multiple modules to process, and store various) Tj
T*
(file formats.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Consistency: Since our system involves multiple crawling workers, having data consistency) Tj
T*
(among all of them is necessary.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Performance: The system should be smart enough to limit its crawling to a domain, either by) Tj
T*
(time spent or by the count of the visited URLs of that domain. This process is called self-) Tj
T*
(throttling. The URLs crawled per second and the throughput of the content crawled should be) Tj
T*
(optimal.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Websites usually host a robot.txt file, which communicates domain-specified limitations to the) Tj
T*
(crawler. The crawler should adhere to these limitations by all means.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Improved user interface—customized scheduling: Besides the default recrawling, which is a) Tj
T*
(functional requirement, the system should also support the functionality to perform non-routine) Tj
T*
(customized crawling on the system administrator’s demands.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Resource estimation) Tj
T*
(We need to estimate various resource requirements for our design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(**Assumptions**) Tj
T*
(These are the assumptions we’ll use when estimating our resource requirements:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- There are a total of 5 billion web pages.) Tj
T*
(- The text content per webpage is 2070 KB.) Tj
T*
(- The metadata for one web page is 500 Bytes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(A study suggests that the average size of a webpage content is 2070KB \(2.07MB\) based on 892) Tj
T*
(processed websites.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
