826 0 obj
<< /Length 3029 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
12 TL
50 780 Td
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(The metadata consists of a webpage title and description of the web page showing its purpose.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Storage estimation) Tj
T*
(The collective storage required to store the textual content of 5 billion web pages is:) Tj
T*
(Total storage per crawl=5 Billion×\(2070 KB+500B\)=10.35PB) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The total storage required by the web crawler system]\(./storage.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Traversal time) Tj
T*
(Since the traversal time is just as important as the storage requirements, let’s calculate the) Tj
T*
(approximate time for one-time crawling. Assuming that the average HTTP traversal per webpage is) Tj
T*
(60 ms, the time to traverse all 5 billion pages will be:) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(Webpage traversal is a function of the page size. Smaller web pages take less than 60 ms, and) Tj
T*
(large pages take longer; however, the number 60 ms is the average traversal time.) Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Total traversal time=5 Billion×60 ms=0.3 Billion seconds=9.5 years) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(It’ll take approximately 9.5 years to traverse the whole Internet while using one instance of) Tj
T*
(crawling, but we want to achieve our goal in one day. We can accomplish this by designing our) Tj
T*
(system to support multi-worker architecture and divide the tasks among multiple workers running) Tj
T*
(on different servers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Number of servers estimation for multi-worker architecture) Tj
T*
(Let’s calculate the number of servers required to finish crawling in one day. Assume that there) Tj
T*
(is only one worker per server.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(No. of days required by 1 server to complete the task=9.5 years×365 days≈3468 days) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(One server takes 3,468 days to complete the task.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(How many servers would we need to complete this same task in one day?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(We would need 3,468 servers to complete the same task in just one day.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The number of servers required for the web crawler system]\(./servers.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(```) Tj
T*
(If there are a n number of threads per server, we’ll divide 3,468 by n. For example, if one) Tj
T*
(server is capable of executing ten threads at a time, then the number of servers is reduced to) Tj
T*
(3468/10 ≈ 347servers) Tj
T*
( ```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Bandwidth estimation) Tj
T*
(Since we want to process 10.35PB of data per day the total bandwidth required would be:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(10.35PB/86400sec≈120GB/sec≈960Gb/sec) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(960Gb/sec is the total required bandwidth. Now, assume that the task is distributed equally) Tj
T*
(among 3468 servers to accomplish the task in one day. Thus, the per server bandwidth would be:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(\(960Gb/sec\) / 3468 servers ≈277Mb/sec per server) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([The total bandwidth required for the web crawler system]\(./bandwidth.jpg\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let's play around with the initial assumptions and see how the estimates change in the) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
